window.DATA = {"groups": [{"id": "family", "title": "The Prophet's ﷺ Family & Household", "sub": "Wives — the Mothers of the Believers — children, grandchildren, uncles, aunts, cousins and the freed members of his blessed household."}, {"id": "ten", "title": "The Ten Promised Paradise", "sub": "Al-ʿAsharah al-Mubashsharah — the ten whom the Prophet ﷺ named in a single gathering as people of Paradise."}, {"id": "early-mecca", "title": "Early Meccan Converts & the Persecuted", "sub": "The first believers of Dār al-Arqam, the tortured and the steadfast, and the emigrants to Abyssinia."}, {"id": "ansar", "title": "The Anṣār — Helpers of Madinah", "sub": "The Aws and Khazraj who pledged at ʿAqabah, sheltered the Muhājirūn, and carried the faith at Badr, Uḥud and beyond."}, {"id": "quraysh-late", "title": "Later Converts of Quraysh", "sub": "Those who fought against Islam for years — then became some of its greatest swords and servants."}, {"id": "beyond", "title": "From Every Horizon — Chiefs, Seekers & Late Arrivals", "sub": "Persians and rabbis, tribal kings and desert questioners: proof that this message was for all mankind."}, {"id": "context", "title": "Around the Story — Kin & Adversaries", "sub": "Not Companions — but you cannot master the sirah without knowing them. Included for context."}], "people": [{"id": "khadijah-bint-khuwaylid", "name": "Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "arabic": "خديجة بنت خويلد", "kunya_titles": "al-Ṭāhirah; Mother of the Believers; first believer", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ first wife, mother of most of his children, and the first person to believe in him.", "story": "A noblewoman of the Quraysh, twice widowed, Khadījah hired the young Muhammad ﷺ for her trade caravan after hearing of his honesty, then extended a marriage proposal through a friend; he ﷺ was 25, and they remained monogamous for 25 years (Ch. 4). She gifted him her servant Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, whom he came to love as a son (Ch. 4). When the Revelation first descended and the Prophet ﷺ raced home with a palpitating heart crying 'Cover me!', it was Khadījah who calmed him with words that encapsulated his character: 'Allah will never disgrace you—you maintain family ties, you are truthful, you host guests, and you carry out all types of good.' She then took him to her cousin Waraqah ibn Nawfal, who confirmed his Prophethood (Ch. 5). She was the first person—man or woman—to accept Islam (Ch. 6). Jibrīl conveyed to her glad tidings of a house in Paradise with no noise or struggle. She died in the Year of Sorrow, three days after Abū Ṭālib; the Prophet ﷺ personally shrouded and buried her, and his love for her endured so strongly that ʿĀʾishah confessed she was never jealous of any wife except Khadījah, whom she never met (Ch. 9). al-Ṣallābī devotes a section to the Prophet's ﷺ loyalty to her (pp. 147–148): he would slaughter a sheep and send portions to Khadījah's friends in her memory, and when her sister Hālah later visited Medina, the mere sound of a voice so like Khadījah's moved him; he also welcomed with honour an old woman who 'would visit us during the lifetime of Khadījah,' saying that honouring old friendship is part of faith (p. 1251).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: hired the Prophet ﷺ for trade, then married him at 25; 25 years of monogamous marriage", "Ch. 5: consoled him after the first Revelation and took him to Waraqah ibn Nawfal", "Ch. 6: the first convert to Islam", "Ch. 4: given glad tidings of a house in Paradise with no noise or struggle", "Ch. 9: died in the Year of Sorrow; the Prophet ﷺ mourned her for the rest of his life"], "badges": [], "death": "10th year of Prophethood (Year of Sorrow), Mecca", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 106–110, 143–148, 519"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. Year of Sorrow · 3 BH", "sallabi": {"text": "Both her previous husbands having died, Khadeejah bint Khuwailid ؓ was a widow. She was known for her noble character, and the people of the Quraish would call her, “The Pure and Chaste One.” She ؓ was also very wealthy; she would engage men to do business on her behalf. After she heard about the truthfulness and trustworthiness of Muhammad ﷺ, she proposed that he do business for her in Ash-Sham, promising to give him more than she gave to any other man who did business for her.", "page": 106}, "who_ar": "أَوَّلُ زَوْجَاتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَأُمُّ أَكْثَرِ أَوْلَادِهِ، وَأَوَّلُ مَنْ آمَنَ بِهِ."}, {"id": "sawdah-bint-zamah", "name": "Sawdah bint Zamʿah", "arabic": "سودة بنت زمعة", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ second wife, the widow he married after Khadījah's death to care for his household.", "story": "After Khadījah's death left the Prophet ﷺ caring for three daughters alone, Khawlah bint Ḥakīm gently noted how withdrawn he had become and proposed two candidates: 'If you want an unmarried lady, then ʿĀʾishah is ideal, and if you want a widowed lady, then Sawdah is ideal.' As ʿĀʾishah was still too young, the Prophet ﷺ gave Khawlah permission to approach Sawdah; she and her father approved, and the marriage took place in Mecca (Ch. 9). Sawdah thus became the woman who managed the Prophet's ﷺ home and daughters through the final, hardest years of Mecca and the Hijrah. The book preserves a very human moment from after Badr: entering her home—where the Prophet ﷺ had ordered the prisoners of war to be personally hosted—she saw the Qurashī chief Suhayl ibn ʿAmr sitting captive and, in a momentary lapse of old tribal shame, blurted, 'O Abū Yazīd, you surrendered?! Why did you not die an honourable death?' She only realised her words when the Prophet ﷺ, beside her, said, 'O Sawdah, are you stoking him to fight against Allah and His Messenger?'—and she swore she had not known what she was saying (Ch. 14). In later life she famously gifted her allotted day to ʿĀʾishah. She died in Medina during ʿUmar's or Muʿāwiyah's era, with 54 AH commonly given. al-Ṣallābī notes (p. 516) that Sawdah was herself a returnee from the Abyssinian emigration — widowed when her husband al-Sakrān ibn ʿAmr died after their return to Mecca — and records that she was the first woman the Prophet ﷺ married after Khadījah, a marriage of consolation and honour for a bereaved believer.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: married the Prophet ﷺ after Khadījah's death upon Khawlah bint Ḥakīm's suggestion", "Ch. 14: her momentary lapse before the captive Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, and the Prophet's ﷺ gentle correction"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 54 AH, Medina (also reported late in ʿUmar's caliphate)", "after_sirah": "Sawdah spent her long widowhood in Medina in quiet worship and open-handed charity, rarely leaving her home in obedience to the verse 'and remain in your homes'. ʿĀʾishah, the co-wife she had gifted her day to, said of her: 'There is no woman I would rather be than Sawdah bint Zamʿah.' The majority report, via Ibn Abī Khaythama, places her death in Shawwāl 54 AH during Muʿāwiyah's era, though al-Bukhārī recorded that she died late in ʿUmar's caliphate.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 516", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 54 AH", "who_ar": "ثَانِيَةُ زَوْجَاتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَالْأَرْمَلَةُ الَّتِي تَزَوَّجَهَا بَعْدَ وَفَاةِ خَدِيجَةَ لِتَقُومَ عَلَى شَأْنِ بَيْتِهِ."}, {"id": "aishah-bint-abi-bakr", "name": "ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr", "arabic": "عائشة بنت أبي بكر", "kunya_titles": "Umm ʿAbdillāh; al-Ṣiddīqah; Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of Abū Bakr, the Prophet's ﷺ most beloved wife, and the greatest female scholar of this Ummah.", "story": "Her marriage was one of only two commanded by Allah: the Prophet ﷺ saw her in a dream, carried in a silk cloth, and was told, 'This will be your wife' (Ch. 9). The contract was made in Mecca and the marriage consummated years later in Medina. The book calls her the greatest female scholar in Islamic history, whose narrations—on his humble bedding, his gentleness ('whatever is gentle is beautiful'), the first Revelation, and countless rulings—illuminate every chapter (Ch. 1). Her greatest trial was the Slander (al-Ifk): left behind by the caravan, escorted back innocently by Ṣafwān, then slandered by the hypocrites while she lay sick and unaware. Abandoned to fend for herself, she declared she would say only what the father of Yūsuf said: 'Beautiful patience!'—and Allah revealed her innocence from above the seven heavens in verses recited until the Day of Judgement (Ch. 18). When the wives united in requesting more of this world, Allah gave them the choice, and ʿĀʾishah answered instantly: 'What is there to consult? I choose Allah and His Messenger' (Ch. 28). The Prophet ﷺ spent his final illness in her home and died with his head between her neck and chest, her miswāk-softened saliva in his mouth—a blessing she treasured all her life (Ch. 30). She taught the Ummah for nearly five more decades. al-Ṣallābī notes (p. 942) that the Prophet ﷺ would run footraces with her — and of the two races preserved in the ḥadīth books, ʿĀʾishah won one.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: her marriage commanded by Allah through the Prophet's ﷺ dream", "Ch. 18: the Slander (al-Ifk) and her vindication by Revelation", "Ch. 28: chose Allah and His Messenger without hesitation when the choice was revealed", "Ch. 30: nursed the Prophet ﷺ in his final illness; he died on her lap, in her house, on her day", "Ch. 1: her narrations of his character—his bedding, his hunger, his gentleness"], "badges": ["Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 58 AH, Medina (also reported 57 AH) — buried at al-Baqīʿ", "after_sirah": "For nearly half a century she was the reference point of the Ummah: over two thousand narrations, and senior Companions and jurists alike sending their hardest questions to her door. She died in Ramaḍān 58 AH during Muʿāwiyah's era and, at her own insistence, was buried at night in al-Baqīʿ with the other wives rather than beside the Prophet ﷺ; Abū Hurayrah led her funeral prayer.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 940–943, 1324–1343, 1983", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 58 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The contract of the Messenger of Allah’s marriage to ‘Aishah ؓ was finalized when the latter was six years old, when Khadeejah ؓ had already died. The Prophet ﷺ then consummated the marriage in Al-Madeenah, when ‘Aishah ؓ was nine years old, in the month of Shawwaal, during the first year of the Islamic calendar.", "page": 940}, "who_ar": "بِنْتُ أَبِي بَكْرٍ، وَأَحَبُّ زَوْجَاتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ إِلَيْهِ، وَأَعْلَمُ نِسَاءِ هَذِهِ الْأُمَّةِ."}, {"id": "hafsah-bint-umar", "name": "Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar", "arabic": "حفصة بنت عمر", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers; custodian of the written muṣḥaf", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and wife of the Prophet ﷺ from 3 AH.", "story": "Widowed young, Ḥafṣah became the subject of one of the sīrah's most touching episodes of companionship: her father ʿUmar offered her hand to ʿUthmān, who politely declined, then to Abū Bakr, who would not even respond. Hurt, ʿUmar complained to the Prophet ﷺ, who answered, 'Ḥafṣah will marry a person better than ʿUthmān, and ʿUthmān will marry a person better than Ḥafṣah'—and married her himself in Shaʿbān 3 AH. Abū Bakr later explained his silence: he had already heard the Prophet ﷺ mention her and would not disclose his secret (Ch. 15). When Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays returned from Abyssinia, it was with her friend Ḥafṣah that she reunited (Ch. 21). Strong-willed like her father, Ḥafṣah was—with ʿĀʾishah—at the forefront when the wives requested more of this world's comforts; ʿUmar's dramatic narration of the month-long separation that followed, and his tearful sight of the Prophet's ﷺ bare room, is one of the book's great scenes (Ch. 28). In the final illness she urged that her father lead the prayer in place of Abū Bakr, but the Prophet ﷺ insisted on Abū Bakr (Ch. 30). After his passing, the written copy of the Qurʾān compiled under Abū Bakr was entrusted to her keeping. Her learning shows in an exchange al-Ṣallābī records (pp. 1519–1520): when the Prophet ﷺ said in her presence that none of the people of the Riḍwān Pledge would enter the Fire, Ḥafṣah countered with the verse 'There is not one of you but will pass over it' — and he ﷺ answered her with its continuation, 'Then We shall save those who feared Allah.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: married the Prophet ﷺ in 3 AH after ʿUthmān and Abū Bakr declined ʿUmar's offer", "Ch. 21: reunion with her friend Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays after Khaybar", "Ch. 28: at the centre of the marital dispute and the month of separation, narrated by ʿUmar", "Ch. 30: asked that ʿUmar lead the prayer during the final illness"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 45 AH, Medina (also reported 41 AH)", "after_sirah": "Jibrīl himself testified for her: 'She is much-fasting, much-praying, and she is your wife in Paradise.' She guarded the ṣuḥuf of the Qurʾān compiled under her father's watch until her death — lending them to ʿUthmān for the standard copies and receiving them back — and died in Shaʿbān 45 AH during Muʿāwiyah's era, buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1085–1086, 1519–1520", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 45 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "I spent a number of nights (waiting), and then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed to her. I married her off to him, after which Abu Bakr met me and said, ‘Perhaps you became angry at me when you offered me Hafsah and I did not give you any response.’ I said, ‘Yes (that is true).’", "page": 1085}, "who_ar": "بِنْتُ عُمَرَ بْنِ الْخَطَّابِ، وَزَوْجَةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مُنْذُ السَّنَةِ الثَّالِثَةِ لِلْهِجْرَةِ."}, {"id": "zaynab-bint-khuzaymah", "name": "Zaynab bint Khuzaymah", "arabic": "زينب بنت خزيمة", "kunya_titles": "Umm al-Masākīn (Mother of the Poor); Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Wife of the Prophet ﷺ for only a few months; widow of the first battlefield martyr of Islam.", "story": "Zaynab bint Khuzaymah was married to Ṭufayl ibn al-Ḥārith, who fell in the Battle of Badr as the first martyr in Islam. She was not from the Quraysh at all, but from the tribe of Hawāzin—a woman of a foreign tribe, widowed by the first martyr of the Ummah. The book highlights the profound message in the Prophet's ﷺ decision to offer her his own hand in marriage: a public declaration that the families of the martyrs would never be abandoned, but honoured and cared for at the very highest level (Ch. 15). He married her about a month after his marriage to Ḥafṣah, in 3 AH. Her time in the Prophet's ﷺ household was heartbreakingly brief—only a few months before she died of natural causes, making her, alongside Khadījah, one of only two of his wives to die in his lifetime. Yet her legacy was already secure long before her marriage: she was famous throughout her life for constantly feeding the poor and the orphaned children, earning her the beautiful title by which history remembers her—Umm al-Masākīn, the Mother of the Poor (Ch. 15). (al-Ṣallābī, p. 1245, records her previous husband as ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh, martyred at Uḥud, places the marriage in Ramaḍān about thirty months after the Hijrah, and her death nine months later; he confirms that her title Umm al-Masākīn was earned already in the days before Islam.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: married the Prophet ﷺ in 3 AH as the widow of the first martyr in Islam", "Ch. 15: known as Umm al-Masākīn for feeding the poor and orphans; died a few months after the marriage"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 4 AH, Medina — some eight months after her marriage (also reported 3 AH)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1245", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "who_ar": "مَكَثَتْ زَوْجَةً لِلنَّبِيِّ ﷺ أَشْهُرًا قَلِيلَةً فَقَطْ، وَهِيَ أَرْمَلَةُ أَوَّلِ شَهِيدٍ قُتِلَ فِي مَيْدَانِ الْقِتَالِ فِي الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "umm-salamah", "name": "Umm Salamah bint Abī Umayyah", "arabic": "أم سلمة هند بنت أبي أمية", "kunya_titles": "Hind bint Ḥudhayfah (Abī Umayyah) ibn al-Mughīrah; Mother of the Believers; of 'The People of Two Emigrations'", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Noblewoman of Makhzūm, emigrant to Abyssinia and Medina, wife of the Prophet ﷺ renowned for her wisdom.", "story": "Hind bint Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Mughīrah was among the first emigrants to Abyssinia with her husband Abū Salamah, and she narrated the Muslims' anxiety there during the Negus's war (Ch. 7). Her Hijrah to Medina was among the most painful in the sīrah: her clan tore her infant son from her and separated her from her husband, and for a year she wept daily in the desert until a kind clansman relented; she then crossed the desert to Medina alone (Ch. 12). When Abū Salamah died of his Uḥud wounds in 4 AH, she recited the duʿāʾ he had taught her from the Prophet ﷺ—'O Allah, recompense me in my calamity and replace it with that which is better'—while wondering who could ever be better than Abū Salamah. Refusing Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, she accepted the Prophet ﷺ himself, who answered her three worries about jealousy, age, and children with matchless grace (Ch. 17). Her wisdom shone brightest at Ḥudaybiyyah: when the grieving Companions did not respond to his command, she advised the Prophet ﷺ to shave his own head first—and they all rushed to follow (Ch. 20). At the Conquest, she carried her half-brother's plea for mercy to the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 25). She outlived all the wives, dying around 61 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: early emigrant to Abyssinia; narrated the Muslims' fear during the Negus's war", "Ch. 12: her harrowing year of separation before emigrating to Medina alone", "Ch. 17: widowed after Uḥud; married the Prophet ﷺ, who answered her three hesitations beautifully", "Ch. 20: her decisive counsel to the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥudaybiyyah", "Ch. 25: interceded (unsuccessfully at first) for her brother and the Prophet's ﷺ cousins at the Conquest"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 61 AH, Medina (also reported 59 and 62 AH) — the last of the Mothers of the Believers to die", "after_sirah": "She lived a full half-century after the Prophet ﷺ as one of the most prolific women narrators of ḥadīth — 378 narrations — and her home remained a station of knowledge and counsel through five caliphates. She lived long enough for the news of Karbalāʾ to reach Medina, and the grief for Ḥusayn — whom she had helped raise — is said to have hastened her end; she died around 61 AH, the last of the Mothers of the Believers, and was buried at al-Baqīʿ. al-Ṣallābī confirms her as the last of the Prophet's ﷺ wives to die, in 61 AH (p. 1252), and adds from Tafsīr al-Manār the wisdoms of the marriage — among them goodwill toward her powerful clan of Makhzūm (p. 1251). (al-Ṣallābī counts 388 aḥādīth in her Musnad, p. 1252.)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 617–619, 1245–1252, 1537", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 61 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "My husband, my son, and I were thus divided. I would go out every morning to Al-Abtah (a place that is situated between Makkah and Minaa), and I would sit there and cry until the evening. I continued doing that for an entire year - or for almost an entire year.", "page": 617}, "who_ar": "شَرِيفَةُ بَنِي مَخْزُومٍ، هَاجَرَتْ إِلَى الْحَبَشَةِ ثُمَّ إِلَى الْمَدِينَةِ، وَهِيَ زَوْجُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الْمَعْرُوفَةُ بِحِكْمَتِهَا."}, {"id": "juwayriyyah-bint-al-harith", "name": "Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith", "arabic": "جويرية بنت الحارث", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of the chieftain of the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq; her marriage freed her entire tribe.", "story": "Daughter of al-Ḥārith, chieftain of the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, Juwayriyyah was captured in the campaign against her tribe. ʿĀʾishah narrated that she was stunningly beautiful—no one saw her without being captivated. Exercising her right under the Shariah, she arranged to purchase her own freedom and approached the Prophet ﷺ for a loan; he offered her something greater: marriage, with her freedom as her dowry (Ch. 17). What followed is one of the most remarkable chain reactions in the sīrah. The Anṣār, hearing that the Prophet ﷺ had married into the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, declared, 'How can we have the Prophet's in-laws as captives?'—and freed every single captive. Her father came to ransom her, but the Prophet ﷺ deferred to Juwayriyyah's own choice; she chose to remain with him, and her father was so awed that he embraced Islam, followed by his entire tribe. The Prophet ﷺ reinstated al-Ḥārith as leader and returned the tribe's property. ʿĀʾishah said, 'I do not know of any lady that brought more blessings to her tribe than Juwayriyyah' (Ch. 17). She was known for abundant worship: the Prophet ﷺ once found her still supplicating in the same spot hours after Fajr, and taught her the mighty words of tasbīḥ that outweigh hours of dhikr (Ch. 17).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: captured at al-Muraysīʿ; the Prophet ﷺ married her with her freedom as her dowry", "Ch. 17: the Anṣār freed every captive of the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq for her sake; her father and whole tribe embraced Islam", "Ch. 17: her long hours of supplication and the tasbīḥ the Prophet ﷺ taught her"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 50 AH, Medina (also reported 56 AH)", "after_sirah": "She lived on in Medina exactly as the book last shows her: long hours on her prayer mat, much fasting, much dhikr. She died in Rabīʿ al-Awwal 50 AH during Muʿāwiyah's era, around sixty-five years of age; Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, governor of Medina, led her funeral prayer, and she was buried at al-Baqīʿ. al-Ṣallābī records (p. 1312) that seven of her ḥadīth survive in the compilations — related from her by Ibn ʿAbbās, Kurayb, Mujāhid and others — and confirms her death in 50 AH (or, it is said, 56 AH).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1309–1312", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 50 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "People soon found out that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had married Juwairiyyah bint Al-Haarith ؓ, and they began to say (about the people of Banu Al-Mustaliq), ‘These are the in-laws of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ (i.e., so it is not befitting for them to remain as captives and slaves).’ They immediately freed everyone that was in their possession; and so through his marriage to her, the people of one-hundred households from the Banu Al-Mustaliq tribe gained their freedom.", "page": 1309}, "who_ar": "بِنْتُ سَيِّدِ بَنِي الْمُصْطَلِقِ، أَعْتَقَ زَوَاجُهَا قَبِيلَتَهَا كُلَّهَا."}, {"id": "zaynab-bint-jahsh", "name": "Zaynab bint Jaḥsh", "arabic": "زينب بنت جحش", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ cousin, wed to him by decree of Allah revealed in Sūrah al-Aḥzāb.", "story": "Zaynab was the Prophet's ﷺ cousin, a noblewoman first married—at his insistence and by Divine instruction that a believer has no choice when Allah and His Messenger decree a matter—to his freed slave and adopted son, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah. The marriage was riddled with conflict, and despite the Prophet's ﷺ counsel to 'keep your wife and fear Allah,' Zayd eventually divorced her. Then Allah Himself revealed: 'We gave her to you in marriage, so that there would be no blame on the believers for marrying the ex-wives of their adopted sons' (Ch. 17). Hers was thus one of only two marriages commanded directly by Allah—contracted without dowry or witnesses because it was ordained from above the heavens, decisively abolishing the pagan taboo around adopted kinship. ʿĀʾishah and Anas both remarked that if the Prophet ﷺ were ever to conceal a verse, it would have been this one—yet he conveyed it in full (Ch. 17). Her nobility of soul shone during the Slander: ʿĀʾishah acknowledged Zaynab as her only rival in the Prophet's ﷺ affection, yet when questioned, Zaynab refused to lower her rival, saying, 'I protect my ears and eyes from sin; by Allah, I only know good of ʿĀʾishah' (Ch. 18). She died in 20 AH, the first of the Mothers of the Believers to pass away after him ﷺ, famed for her charity.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: her marriage to Zayd, the divorce, and her marriage to the Prophet ﷺ decreed in the Qurʾān", "Ch. 18: her integrity during the Slander—refusing to speak anything but good of ʿĀʾishah"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 20 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "The Prophet ﷺ had told his wives that the first to follow him would be 'the one with the longest hand'; they measured their hands with a stick until Zaynab's death revealed his meaning — the longest hand was the most generous. She worked leather with her own hands and gave everything away, and when ʿUmar sent her stipend she distributed it entirely before nightfall. ʿUmar led her funeral prayer in 20 AH, and she was buried at al-Baqīʿ. al-Ṣallābī confirms the 'longest hand' episode (p. 1426) — noting that her birth-name had been Barrah, which the Prophet ﷺ himself changed to Zaynab — and records that she died in 20 AH at the age of fifty-three (p. 1446).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1425–1427, 1446", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 20 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Zainab ؓ was among the first group of people who migrated to Al-Madeenah, and she was a very pious woman. During the day, she fasted; and during the night, she stood up to pray; and she was generous to others and frequently gave charity to the poor and needy.", "page": 1425}, "who_ar": "بِنْتُ عَمَّةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، زَوَّجَهُ اللهُ إِيَّاهَا بِأَمْرٍ أَنْزَلَهُ فِي سُورَةِ الْأَحْزَابِ."}, {"id": "umm-habibah", "name": "Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān", "arabic": "أم حبيبة رملة بنت أبي سفيان", "kunya_titles": "Ramlah; Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of Abū Sufyān who chose Islam over her father's leadership of the opposition; married the Prophet ﷺ from Abyssinia.", "story": "Ramlah bint Abī Sufyān embraced Islam while her father remained the standard-bearer of Meccan opposition, and she emigrated to Abyssinia in the early, desperate years. Widowed in a foreign land, she received an honour unique in the sīrah: the Prophet ﷺ was so keen to secure her hand that he sent an envoy across the sea to Abyssinia, proposing marriage and appointing the Negus himself as her guardian, since she had no male Muslim relative present. The Negus rejoiced on the Prophet's ﷺ behalf, threw a lavish celebration for the Muslims of Abyssinia, and showered her with royal gifts—yet she did not meet her husband for a full year, until the emigrants returned to Medina after Khaybar (Ch. 21). The book records her finest hour before the Conquest of Mecca: when Abū Sufyān came to Medina to salvage the broken treaty and entered his daughter's room, she rolled up the Prophet's ﷺ bedding rather than let her still-pagan father sit upon it. A decade of separation had not diluted her loyalty: Allah and His Messenger came before the most powerful man of Quraysh, her own father (Ch. 25). Her father and family later embraced Islam at the Conquest. She died in Medina around 44 AH. al-Ṣallābī also relates (p. 83) that she once offered to share her rare closeness: she asked the Prophet ﷺ to marry her own sister, the daughter of Abū Sufyān, saying that of all women she would most love her sister to share with her in goodness — and he ﷺ gently explained that this is not permissible.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: married to the Prophet ﷺ while in Abyssinia, with the Negus as guardian of the contract", "Ch. 25: folded away the Prophet's ﷺ bed from her pagan father Abū Sufyān before the Conquest"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "d. 44 AH, Medina (also reported 42 AH)", "after_sirah": "She lived into the caliphate of her brother Muʿāwiyah, narrating some sixty-five ḥadīth — among them the beloved promise of a house in Paradise for whoever prays twelve voluntary rakʿahs daily. On her deathbed she called for ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah and asked that any co-wife's hurt between them be forgiven, leaving this world with a clean heart in 44 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 516, 1679", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 44 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "While some of the Prophet’s Companions ﷺ were in Abyssinia, one among them, Umm Habeebah ؓ, married the Prophet ﷺ. The actual marriage contract took place while she was in Abyssinia, a fact that is established in the books of Sunnah. Abu Daawood related in his Sunan with an authentic chain that Umm Habeebah was married to ‘Ubaidullah ibn Jahsh, who died in the land of Abyssinia.", "page": 516}, "who_ar": "بِنْتُ أَبِي سُفْيَانَ الَّتِي آثَرَتِ الْإِسْلَامَ عَلَى زَعَامَةِ أَبِيهَا لِلْمُعَارَضَةِ، وَتَزَوَّجَهَا النَّبِيُّ ﷺ وَهِيَ بِالْحَبَشَةِ."}, {"id": "safiyyah-bint-huyayy", "name": "Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy", "arabic": "صفية بنت حيي", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of the Banū Naḍīr chieftain Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab; became a Mother of the Believers after Khaybar.", "story": "Ṣafiyyah was the daughter of Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, chieftain of the Banū Naḍīr who had relocated to Khaybar. Captured after the battle and initially allocated to Diḥyah al-Kalbī, a Companion pointed out that the daughter of a chieftain befitted none but the Prophet ﷺ. He called her and offered two choices with complete freedom: accept Islam and stay with him, or remain on her faith and be freed and returned to her people. Her answer stunned those who heard it: 'O Messenger of Allah, I desired to accept Islam and believed in you before you even called me to Islam. I love Allah and His Messenger more than I love my freedom and more than returning to my people.' He immediately freed her and married her (Ch. 21). To those sceptical of so sudden a transformation, the book relates her own explanation: as a child she overheard her father admit that Muhammad ﷺ was indeed the awaited Messenger the Jews were expecting—yet vow, 'I will be his enemy for as long as I live.' That confession planted the seed of īmān in her heart; she saw that they knew the truth and refused it out of pride (Ch. 21). She lived as a devout Mother of the Believers until around 50 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: captured at Khaybar; chose Islam and the Prophet ﷺ over freedom and her people", "Ch. 21: the childhood conversation of her father Ḥuyayy that planted īmān in her heart"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 50 AH, Medina (also reported 36 or 52 AH)", "after_sirah": "Her loyalty never wavered: during the siege of ʿUthmān she laid a plank from her house to his and carried food and water across to the beleaguered Caliph. She died around 50 AH during Muʿāwiyah's era and was buried at al-Baqīʿ, leaving a third of her estate — by one report — to a nephew who had remained on her old faith, generous to her kin to the last. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1603–1604) that when a servant went to ʿUmar claiming she loved the Sabbath and kept ties with the Jews, she answered, 'I have not loved Saturday since Allah replaced it for me with Friday; and as for the Jews, they are my blood relatives, and that is why I join ties with them' — then freed the very servant who had slandered her. He confirms her death in Ramaḍān 50 AH, or, it is said, 52 AH (p. 1604).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1599–1604", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 50 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Next, the Prophet ﷺ consummated his marriage to her, after which he ﷺ asked, “What prompted you to refuse to make camp the first time?” She ؓ replied, “As we were still near the Jews, I feared for you (for your life) (and so I wanted to get farther away from them before we made camp).” The Prophet ﷺ was pleased by her answer and immediately began to hold her in high regard.", "page": 1599}, "who_ar": "ابْنَةُ حُيَيِّ بْنِ أَخْطَبَ سَيِّدِ بَنِي النَّضِيرِ، صَارَتْ مِنْ أُمَّهَاتِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ بَعْدَ خَيْبَرَ."}, {"id": "maymunah-bint-al-harith", "name": "Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith", "arabic": "ميمونة بنت الحارث", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Believers", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The last woman the Prophet ﷺ married, wed during the Qaḍāʾ ʿUmrah of 7 AH.", "story": "When the Muslims returned to Mecca for the make-up ʿUmrah stipulated by the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, the Prophet's ﷺ uncle al-ʿAbbās suggested that he marry his own sister-in-law, Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, a widow. The book pauses here to note the inclusive culture of the Companions: there was simply no taboo attached to a woman's previous marital status. The Prophet ﷺ agreed, and al-ʿAbbās served as her walī (Ch. 23). A famous fiqh discussion attaches to her marriage: Ibn ʿAbbās narrated that the Prophet ﷺ married her while in the state of iḥrām, while Maymūnah herself narrated that the marriage took place after his ʿUmrah was complete; there is consensus, in any case, that it was consummated outside Mecca after the ʿUmrah (Ch. 23). The Prophet ﷺ even used the wedding as a diplomatic opening, asking the Quraysh to extend his three days in Mecca and inviting them to a celebratory feast; they curtly refused—'We have no need for your food'—so he honoured the treaty, left the city, and consummated the marriage at the camp outside Mecca, at Sarif (Ch. 23). Through her, al-ʿAbbās and Khālid ibn al-Walīd became in-laws of the Prophet ﷺ. Decades later she died and was buried, by a striking decree of Allah, at that same spot of Sarif, around 51 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 23: married during the Qaḍāʾ ʿUmrah at al-ʿAbbās's suggestion, with al-ʿAbbās as her walī", "Ch. 23: the iḥrām discussion between Ibn ʿAbbās's and her own narrations", "Ch. 23: the rejected wedding feast and the Prophet's ﷺ honouring of the treaty"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 51 AH, Sarif near Mecca (also reported 61 AH)", "after_sirah": "ʿĀʾishah said of her: 'She was among the most God-fearing of us and the most devoted to family ties.' Returning from a journey around 51 AH, she fell ill and died at Sarif — the very spot of her wedding camp — and her nephew ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās led her funeral prayer there, reminding the bearers to carry the Mother of the Believers gently. al-Ṣallābī confirms (p. 1634) her death and burial at Sarif, the very place of her wedding camp. (He there calls her the last of the Prophet's ﷺ wives to die, though he elsewhere gives that distinction to Umm Salamah, p. 1252.)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1633–1634", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 51 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "In response to their request, the Prophet ﷺ left and consummated his marriage to Maimoonah ؓ at a place called Sarif, which is situated near At-Tan’eem. Maimoonah ؓ was the last woman to marry the Prophet ﷺ, and she ؓ was the last of his wives to die. It is interesting to note that she died at Sarif and was buried there, the very place where, years earlier, she had consummated her marriage to the Prophet ﷺ.", "page": 1633}, "who_ar": "آخِرُ مَنْ تَزَوَّجَ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ مِنَ النِّسَاءِ، تَزَوَّجَهَا فِي عُمْرَةِ الْقَضَاءِ سَنَةَ سَبْعٍ لِلْهِجْرَةِ."}, {"id": "mariyyah-al-qibtiyyah", "name": "Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah", "arabic": "مارية القبطية", "kunya_titles": "Māriyyah bint Shamʿūn; Umm Ibrāhīm", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Coptic lady gifted by the Muqawqis of Egypt; mother of the Prophet's ﷺ son Ibrāhīm.", "story": "Māriyyah bint Shamʿūn, famously known as al-Qibṭiyyah (the Copt), was a Christian servant of noble origin whom Jurayj ibn Mīnā, the Muqawqis of Egypt, gifted to the Prophet ﷺ along with her sister Sīrīn and vast gifts of gold; the Prophet ﷺ gifted Sīrīn to the poet Ḥassān ibn Thābit and kept Māriyyah for himself (Ch. 28). In Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH she bore him his son Ibrāhīm, and he ﷺ entered the mosque beaming, announcing that he had named the boy after his father, Prophet Ibrāhīm (Ch. 28). She embraced Islam, was honoured as the mother of the Prophet's ﷺ child, and died in Medina around 16 AH during ʿUmar's caliphate.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: gifted to the Prophet ﷺ by the Muqawqis of Egypt with her sister Sīrīn", "Ch. 28: gave birth to Ibrāhīm in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 16 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "After the Prophet ﷺ died, Abū Bakr and then ʿUmar personally saw to her maintenance, honouring her station as the mother of his son. She died in Muḥarram 16 AH; ʿUmar himself gathered the people for her funeral prayer and she was buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1627, 1808", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 16 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Muqawqis continued to disbelieve even after the Prophet ﷺ personally invited him to embrace Islam in a letter he wrote to him. The Prophet ﷺ still accepted the gifts Al-Muqawqis sent to him. The gifts Al-Muqawqis sent were a mule, some clothes, and two female slaves, one of whom was Maariyyah Al-Qibtiyyah ؓ.", "page": 1627}, "who_ar": "الْقِبْطِيَّةُ الَّتِي أَهْدَاهَا الْمُقَوْقِسُ عَظِيمُ مِصْرَ، وَأُمُّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ ابْنِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"id": "fatimah-bint-muhammad", "name": "Fāṭimah bint Muhammad", "arabic": "فاطمة بنت محمد", "kunya_titles": "al-Zahrāʾ; leader of the women of Paradise", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ beloved youngest daughter, wife of ʿAlī, mother of Ḥasan and Ḥusayn, and the only child to outlive him.", "story": "Of the Prophet's ﷺ seven children, only Fāṭimah outlived him (Ch. 1). She appears at his side from the earliest days: named individually in his public warning to his kin—'O Fāṭimah bint Muhammad, save yourself from the fire of Hell, for I will not be able to help you on the Day of Judgement' (Ch. 6)—and, as a young girl, wiping the camel entrails that the mockers of Quraysh threw upon her prostrating father. Married to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, she lived in exemplary simplicity: when she complained of her chores and asked for a servant, the Prophet ﷺ refused so long as Ahl al-Ṣuffah went hungry, and she never asked again (Ch. 13). Through her came his grandsons Ḥasan and Ḥusayn, the leaders of the youth of Paradise (Ch. 17). Before the Conquest, when Abū Sufyān desperately begged her to have little Ḥasan grant him protection, she answered with quiet firmness that no one can offer protection against the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 25). In his final illness she wept, 'How painful is the suffering of my father!' and he consoled her: 'O Fāṭimah, your father will not suffer after today.' At his passing she cried, 'O my father! You have answered the call of your Lord!' (Ch. 30). She followed him six months later, in 11 AH. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1086–1087) that ʿAlī's entire dowry for her was an old armour worth some four dirhams, and her trousseau from her father ﷺ was some velvet, a canteen, and a leather pillow stuffed with sweet idhkhir grass; and that at the mubāhalah with the Christians of Najrān it was Fāṭimah, ʿAlī, Ḥasan and Ḥusayn whom the Prophet ﷺ brought to invoke Allah's judgement (p. 1932).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: named in the Prophet's ﷺ warning to his nearest kin", "Ch. 13: asked for a servant; taught contentment while Ahl al-Ṣuffah starved", "Ch. 17: mother of Ḥasan and Ḥusayn", "Ch. 25: rebuffed Abū Sufyān's plea for protection through five-year-old Ḥasan", "Ch. 30: her grief and consolation at her father's ﷺ deathbed"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 11 AH, Medina — six months after the Prophet ﷺ", "after_sirah": "Her six remaining months were quiet and grief-heavy; the secret her father ﷺ had whispered — that she would be the first of his family to join him — was the one thing that had made her laugh through her tears at his deathbed. She died in Ramaḍān 11 AH, washed and buried at night at al-Baqīʿ by ʿAlī, in keeping with her own wish for modesty even in death.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1086–1088, 1932, 1971–1972", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 11 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Alee’s female servant found out that Faatimah ؓ was ready to get married and that one or more proposals had been made for her. She said to ‘Alee ؓ, “Did you know that someone went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and made a proposal to marry Faatimah?” ‘Alee ؓ said, “No.” She said, “Indeed, a proposal has been made for her. Then what prevents you from going to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so that he can marry you (to her).”", "page": 1086}, "who_ar": "ابْنَةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الصُّغْرَى الْحَبِيبَةُ إِلَيْهِ، وَزَوْجَةُ عَلِيٍّ، وَأُمُّ الْحَسَنِ وَالْحُسَيْنِ، وَالْوَحِيدَةُ مِنْ وَلَدِهِ الَّتِي بَقِيَتْ بَعْدَهُ."}, {"id": "ruqayyah-bint-muhammad", "name": "Ruqayyah bint Muhammad", "arabic": "رقية بنت محمد", "kunya_titles": "wife of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, wife of ʿUthmān, twice an emigrant, who died the day of the victory of Badr.", "story": "Ruqayyah was among the daughters born to the Prophet ﷺ and Khadījah in the blessed years before Prophethood (Ch. 4). Married to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, she shared with him the honour of being among the very first emigrants to Abyssinia—twelve men and four women who fled with nothing but their faith, rich Companions turned poor overnight for the sake of Allah (Ch. 7). She later emigrated again to Medina. When the call to Badr came, Ruqayyah lay severely ill, and the Prophet ﷺ himself instructed ʿUthmān to remain behind and nurse her; for this ʿUthmān was counted among those who received a full share of the spoils and reward despite not attending (Ch. 14). Allah decreed a tragic juxtaposition that the book dwells upon: on the very day Zayd ibn Ḥārithah galloped into Medina on al-Qaṣwāʾ proclaiming the impossible victory at Badr, ʿUthmān was returning from the cemetery having just buried her. The single most joyous day in the history of Islam to that point was, in the Prophet's ﷺ own household, a day of loss—as if Allah were reminding His Messenger that this world will always remain a test, and unadulterated happiness belongs only to the Hereafter (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia with her husband ʿUthmān", "Ch. 14: fatally ill during Badr; ʿUthmān kept behind to nurse her by the Prophet's ﷺ command", "Ch. 14: buried on the very day the news of the victory of Badr reached Medina"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "2 AH, Medina — the day news of Badr arrived", "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "who_ar": "ابْنَةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَزَوْجُ عُثْمَانَ، هَاجَرَتِ الْهِجْرَتَيْنِ، وَتُوُفِّيَتْ يَوْمَ النَّصْرِ بِبَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "umm-kulthum-bint-muhammad", "name": "Umm Kulthūm bint Muhammad", "arabic": "أم كلثوم بنت محمد", "kunya_titles": "wife of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "Daughter of the Prophet ﷺ and Khadījah; the second of his daughters to marry ʿUthmān.", "story": "Umm Kulthūm is named among the children of the Prophet ﷺ and Khadījah—Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthūm, and Fāṭimah—of whom three daughters died in his lifetime (Ch. 4). After her sister Ruqayyah's death, she was married to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, fulfilling the Prophet's ﷺ words to ʿUmar that 'ʿUthmān will marry a person better than Ḥafṣah' (Ch. 15); for marrying two daughters of the Prophet ﷺ, ʿUthmān earned the title Dhū al-Nūrayn, Possessor of the Two Lights. She died childless in 9 AH, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed over her grave.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: named among the Prophet's ﷺ children by Khadījah", "Ch. 15: the Prophet's ﷺ promise that ʿUthmān would marry 'a person better than Ḥafṣah'"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 9 AH, Medina", "beyond_sources": ["Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 9 AH", "who_ar": "ابْنَةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَخَدِيجَةَ، وَهِيَ ثَانِيَةُ بَنَاتِهِ الَّتِي تَزَوَّجَهَا عُثْمَانُ."}, {"id": "zaynab-bint-muhammad", "name": "Zaynab bint Muhammad", "arabic": "زينب بنت محمد", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The eldest daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, who suffered a violent attack during her emigration to Medina.", "story": "Zaynab was the eldest of the daughters born to the Prophet ﷺ and Khadījah (Ch. 4). She remained in Mecca after the Hijrah, married to Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ, and when she finally set out to emigrate, Habbār ibn al-Aswad attacked her camel, causing her to fall and miscarry the Prophet's ﷺ grandchild—a crime that placed Habbār on the short kill-list at the Conquest of Mecca, though he later negotiated his forgiveness (Ch. 25). Weakened by her injuries, she died in 8 AH, mourned deeply by her father ﷺ. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1024) that after Badr she sent, as ransom for her captive husband Abū al-ʿĀṣ, the very necklace Khadījah had given her at her wedding; the sight of it moved the Prophet ﷺ deeply, and with the Companions' consent Abū al-ʿĀṣ was freed and the necklace returned — against his pledge to let Zaynab emigrate, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah being sent to escort her from Baṭn Yaʾjaj outside Mecca.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: the eldest of the Prophet's ﷺ daughters", "Ch. 25: Habbār ibn al-Aswad caused her fall and miscarriage during her emigration"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 8 AH, Medina", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1023–1025", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When the Prophet ﷺ saw the necklace, many memories were certainly rekindled in his mind - memories of his daughter and of his wife. Nonetheless, he ﷺ didn’t order his Companions ؓ to free Abul-’Aas without taking the ransom money, but instead asked them to do so if they wanted to, for he ﷺ did not want to take away from them their right to the ransom money.", "page": 1025}, "who_ar": "كُبْرَى بَنَاتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، تَعَرَّضَتْ لِاعْتِدَاءٍ عَنِيفٍ فِي أَثْنَاءِ هِجْرَتِهَا إِلَى الْمَدِينَةِ."}, {"id": "ibrahim-ibn-muhammad", "name": "Ibrāhīm ibn Muhammad", "arabic": "إبراهيم بن محمد", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ infant son by Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah, whose death drew his father's most famous tears.", "story": "Ibrāhīm was born in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH to Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah. The Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque with a beaming smile: 'Last night a baby boy was born to me, and I shall call him the name of my father, Ibrāhīm' (Ch. 28). Less than a year and a half later, the child fell deathly sick. At his deathbed the Prophet ﷺ kissed him, smelled him, and wept. When ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf remarked in surprise, 'Even you are weeping!', he ﷺ replied, 'O Ibn ʿAwf, this is mercy,' then spoke the words that have taught the Ummah how to grieve ever since: 'Indeed the eyes shed tears and the heart grieves, but we will not say except what pleases our Lord. O Ibrāhīm! We are indeed grieved by your separation' (Ch. 28). Within hours of his death the sun eclipsed, and the people whispered that even the sun grieved for the Prophet's son. His integrity absolute, the Prophet ﷺ gathered them and corrected it at once: 'The Sun and the Moon are two of the signs of Allah—they do not eclipse for anyone's birth or death' (Ch. 28). Anas said, 'Had Ibrāhīm lived, he would have been a righteous Prophet'—but Allah had decreed the finality of His Messenger's Prophethood. Ibrāhīm was the sixth of seven children the Prophet ﷺ buried.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: born to Māriyyah in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH; named after Prophet Ibrāhīm", "Ch. 28: died in infancy; the Prophet's ﷺ tears of mercy at his deathbed", "Ch. 28: the eclipse sermon—'they do not eclipse for anyone's birth or death'"], "badges": [], "death": "10 AH, Medina — in infancy, under a year and a half old", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 10 AH", "who_ar": "ابْنُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الرَّضِيعُ مِنْ مَارِيَةَ الْقِبْطِيَّةِ، الَّذِي ذَرَفَ أَبُوهُ عَلَى مَوْتِهِ أَشْهَرَ دُمُوعِهِ."}, {"id": "hasan-ibn-ali", "name": "Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī", "arabic": "الحسن بن علي", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad; leader of the youth of Paradise", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ first grandson, son of ʿAlī and Fāṭimah, who would later unite the Ummah.", "story": "Ḥasan was born in Shaʿbān 5 AH, a beacon of joy amidst a sea of trials. ʿAlī had named him Ḥarb (War), but the Prophet ﷺ said, 'No, he is not War; he is Beauty (Ḥasan)'—and gave the adhān in his ear, from which most rulings of the ʿaqīqah are derived (Ch. 17). The book overflows with the Prophet's ﷺ affection for his grandsons: he prolonged his prostration in prayer rather than disturb the toddler climbing on his back, and once interrupted the Friday sermon itself to scoop up the two boys tripping over their little red garments, reciting, 'Your wealth and children are a test' (Ch. 17). He ﷺ declared, 'Ḥasan and Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth in Paradise, and their father is even better than them' (Ch. 17). Even Abū Sufyān recognised the child's station, begging Fāṭimah before the Conquest to let five-year-old Ḥasan grant him protection (Ch. 25). Ḥasan most resembled the Prophet ﷺ in appearance. Decades later, true to his grandfather's ﷺ legacy of mercy, he relinquished the caliphate in 41 AH to reconcile the two great warring camps of Muslims—the 'Year of Unity'—and died in Medina around 49 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: born Shaʿbān 5 AH; renamed from Ḥarb to Ḥasan by the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 17: the prolonged prostration and interrupted sermon out of love for him", "Ch. 17: 'leaders of the youth in Paradise'", "Ch. 25: Abū Sufyān begged Fāṭimah for the five-year-old's protection"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 49 AH (also reported 50 AH), Medina — buried at al-Baqīʿ", "after_sirah": "Pledged as Caliph in Kufa after his father's assassination in 40 AH, he handed the office to Muʿāwiyah within months — fulfilling to the letter his grandfather's ﷺ prophecy in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī: 'This son of mine is a sayyid, and through him Allah will reconcile two great camps of the Muslims.' He then returned to Medina and spent his last decade in worship and boundless charity, giving away his entire fortune twice. He died in 49 or 50 AH, by most reports poisoned, and was buried beside his mother's people at al-Baqīʿ. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1253) that the Prophet ﷺ insisted on giving the ʿaqīqah for his grandson himself, slaughtering two rams, and had Fāṭimah shave the newborn's head and give silver equal to the weight of his hair in charity; he later stood the boy beside him at the mubāhalah with the Christians of Najrān (p. 1932). (al-Ṣallābī, citing al-Qurṭubī and al-Nawawī, places his birth in Shaʿbān 4 AH, p. 1252.)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1252–1253, 1932", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 49 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Alee ibn Abee Taalib said, “When Al-Hasan was born, I named him Harb, but then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and said, ‘Show me my son. What have you named him?’ I said, ‘Harb.’ He ﷺ said, ‘No, instead he is Hasan.’”", "page": 1252}, "who_ar": "أَوَّلُ أَحْفَادِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، ابْنُ عَلِيٍّ وَفَاطِمَةَ، الَّذِي وَحَّدَ الْأُمَّةَ فِيمَا بَعْدُ."}, {"id": "husayn-ibn-ali", "name": "Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī", "arabic": "الحسين بن علي", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; leader of the youth of Paradise", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ second grandson, son of ʿAlī and Fāṭimah, the martyr of Karbalāʾ.", "story": "Ḥusayn was born ten months after his brother, and his very name is a gift of the Prophet ﷺ: Ḥusayn is the diminutive of Ḥasan—'the little Ḥasan'—after the Prophet ﷺ replaced ʿAlī's chosen war-name with names of beauty (Ch. 17). He shared fully in his grandfather's ﷺ boundless affection: one of the two toddlers who climbed on the Prophet ﷺ in prostration while he patiently prolonged it, and one of the two boys in red garments for whom he ﷺ paused the Friday sermon, picking them both up before the congregation (Ch. 17). Of him and his brother the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Ḥasan and Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth in Paradise, and their father is even better than them' (Ch. 17). Through Ḥusayn descends the noble line of the Ahl al-Bayt; his son ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn (Zayn al-ʿĀbidīn) is quoted in the book teaching that the sīrah was taught like a sūrah of the Qurʾān (Ch. 1). The book also notes with sorrow how later Islamic history devolved into sectarian strife surrounding the Prophet's ﷺ grandchildren (Ch. 28). Ḥusayn was martyred at Karbalāʾ in 61 AH, sealing with his blood his rank among the greatest of this Ummah. al-Ṣallābī records (p. 1252), from al-Wāqidī, that Fāṭimah conceived Ḥusayn only fifty nights after Ḥasan's birth, and that at the mubāhalah with the Christians of Najrān the Prophet ﷺ brought ʿAlī, Fāṭimah, Ḥasan and Ḥusayn — the family over whom he asked Allah's judgement (p. 1932).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: born ten months after Ḥasan; named 'the little Ḥasan' by the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 17: cherished in the Prophet's ﷺ prayer and sermon episodes; 'leaders of the youth in Paradise'", "Ch. 1: his son ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn quoted on how the sīrah was taught"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "61 AH — martyred at Karbalāʾ on the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ (10 Muḥarram)", "after_sirah": "After decades of quiet dignity in Medina, he refused to give allegiance to Yazīd and set out toward Kufa at its people's invitation. Intercepted at Karbalāʾ and denied even water of the Euphrates, he fell on the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ, 10 Muḥarram 61 AH, with some seventy of his family and companions — a wound the Ummah has never ceased to grieve.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1252, 1932", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 61 AH", "who_ar": "سِبْطُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الثَّانِي، وَابْنُ عَلِيٍّ وَفَاطِمَةَ، وَشَهِيدُ كَرْبَلَاءَ."}, {"id": "hamzah-ibn-abd-al-muttalib", "name": "Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "حمزة بن عبد المطلب", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿUmārah; the Lion of Allah; Sayyid al-Shuhadāʾ (leader of the martyrs)", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ uncle and foster-brother, champion warrior of Islam, martyred and mutilated at Uḥud.", "story": "Ḥamzah's conversion was truly unique: returning from a hunt, he learned that Abū Jahl had publicly abused his nephew, and with his blood boiling he struck Abū Jahl with his bow and declared for Islam almost in defiance—then, after a night of turmoil, testified with certainty: 'I testify that you are truthful; I do not ever want to return to my old faith.' He became the most senior convert of his time, and the persecuted Muslims marched publicly to the Kaʿbah in two rows, one led by Ḥamzah and one by ʿUmar (Ch. 8). At Badr he stepped forward for the opening duel and killed his opponent without suffering a scratch, then finished ʿUtbah when ʿUbaydah fell wounded (Ch. 14). At Uḥud he fought so ferociously that Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim promised his Abyssinian slave Waḥshī freedom for Ḥamzah's life alone; Waḥshī's javelin struck him from behind—the only way, the book notes, that Ḥamzah could ever have been killed—and Hind mutilated his body, chewing his liver (Ch. 16). The Prophet ﷺ sobbed over his mutilated body, and Allah revealed the command of patient restraint (Ch. 16). Years later the Prophet ﷺ could not bear to look at Waḥshī even after accepting his Islam—such was his love for the Lion of Allah, the leader of the martyrs. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1132) that his sister Ṣafiyyah brought two garments to shroud him; finding beside him a slain Anṣārī mutilated as he had been, with no shroud of his own, the Companions drew lots and wrapped each martyr in one of the two.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: converted in defence of the Prophet ﷺ, striking Abū Jahl with his bow", "Ch. 8: led one of the two rows in the first public march to the Kaʿbah", "Ch. 14: champion of the opening duel at Badr", "Ch. 16: martyred at Uḥud by Waḥshī; his body mutilated by Hind", "Ch. 16: the Prophet's ﷺ profound grief and the Revelation commanding restraint"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 362, 1001, 1129–1135"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Hamzah ؓ, another one of the Prophet's uncles, showed a strong display of family loyalty when Abu Jahl cursed the Prophet ﷺ. Hamzah, showing no fear, struck Abu Jahl with his bow, causing a fracture; he then said to Abu Jahl, “Do you curse Muhammad while I am upon his religion. Return the blow (I delivered to you) if you are able to do so!”", "page": 362}, "who_ar": "عَمُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَأَخُوهُ مِنَ الرَّضَاعَةِ، وَبَطَلُ الْإِسْلَامِ الْمِقْدَامُ، اسْتُشْهِدَ وَمُثِّلَ بِهِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "al-abbas-ibn-abd-al-muttalib", "name": "al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "العباس بن عبد المطلب", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Faḍl; 'the uncle of a man is like his father'", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ uncle, protector in Mecca, 'the final Emigrant', and ancestor of the Abbasid line.", "story": "Al-ʿAbbās threads through the whole sīrah. Not yet Muslim, he accompanied his nephew to the secret pledge of ʿAqabah out of pure familial love, scrutinising the unknown men of Yathrib and speaking on the Prophet's ﷺ behalf (Ch. 11). Forced out to Badr with Quraysh, he was named among those not to be killed; a short, slight Anṣārī captured the physically imposing warrior—with the help, the Prophet ﷺ revealed, of a noble Angel (Ch. 14). Before Uḥud, it was his secret message that gave the Prophet ﷺ two days' warning of the Quraysh advance (Ch. 16). His formal conversion is debated—before Khaybar, before the Conquest, or long concealed in Mecca at the Prophet's ﷺ behest—but Allah honoured him as the last person ever counted among the Emigrants, met at Juḥfah as the army marched to Mecca (Ch. 25). He escorted Abū Sufyān to the Prophet ﷺ and secured his safety (Ch. 25). At Ḥunayn, when the army broke, it was al-ʿAbbās's famously loud voice that the Prophet ﷺ used to rally the Companions back (Ch. 26). At the Farewell Hajj the Prophet ﷺ nullified the usury owed to him first of all (Ch. 29). The Prophet ﷺ preached, 'Do not irritate my uncle, for the uncle of a man is like his father,' and in the drought of 18 AH ʿUmar chose him to pray for rain on behalf of all (Ch. 25). al-Ṣallābī records (pp. 1021–1023) that the Prophet ﷺ, refusing any shadow of nepotism, made al-ʿAbbās pay a higher ransom than the other Badr prisoners — then, on al-Ṣallābī's reading, al-ʿAbbās returned to Mecca a concealed Muslim and for years passed the Quraysh's secrets to Medina with amazing skill, proclaiming his Islam openly only hours before the Conquest.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: accompanied and vouched for the Prophet ﷺ at the pledge of ʿAqabah", "Ch. 14: captured at Badr by an Anṣārī aided by an Angel; ransomed", "Ch. 16: sent the secret warning of the Quraysh march before Uḥud", "Ch. 25: 'the final Emigrant'; escorted Abū Sufyān to safety at the Conquest", "Ch. 26: rallied the fleeing army at Ḥunayn with his mighty voice", "Ch. 29-30: usury owed him nullified at the Farewell Hajj; tended the Prophet ﷺ in his final illness"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 32 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "Under the caliphs he was the honoured elder of the Ummah: ʿUmar and ʿUthmān would not ride past without dismounting for the Prophet's ﷺ uncle. He lost his sight in his final years, and died in Rajab 32 AH at about eighty-eight; ʿUthmān led his funeral prayer and he was buried at al-Baqīʿ. Two of his descendants would later found the dynasty that bore his name.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1021–1023, 1691–1695", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Amazed at what he had just heard, Al-’Abbaas said, “By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, verily, I know that you are indeed the Messenger of Allah. No one other than me and Umm Fadl knew about that matter, so count towards my payment, O Messenger of Allah, the twenty Ooqiyyahs (a measurement of wealth) that was with me and that you seized (during the Battle of Badr).” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “That is something that Allah took from you and gave to us.”", "page": 1021}, "who_ar": "عَمُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَحَامِيهِ بِمَكَّةَ، وَ«آخِرُ الْمُهَاجِرِينَ»، وَجَدُّ بَنِي الْعَبَّاسِ."}, {"id": "safiyyah-bint-abd-al-muttalib", "name": "Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "صفية بنت عبد المطلب", "kunya_titles": "aunt of the Prophet ﷺ; mother of Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ paternal aunt, full sister of Ḥamzah, and mother of his disciple Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām.", "story": "Ṣafiyyah was the Prophet's ﷺ paternal aunt and the mother of Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, making Zubayr—of whom the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Every Prophet has his disciples, and my disciple is Zubayr'—his direct cousin (Ch. 6). When the Prophet ﷺ stood on Mount Ṣafā and warned his kin one by one, he called her by name: 'O Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, aunt of the Prophet, save yourself from the fire of Hell, for I will not be able to help you on the Day of Judgement' (Ch. 6). She embraced Islam, emigrated to Medina, and is remembered in the wider sīrah literature for her courage during the Battle of the Trench. She died in Medina during ʿUmar's caliphate. al-Ṣallābī devotes a section to her patience at Uḥud (p. 1132): striding to the field despite the Prophet's ﷺ concern, she was met by her son al-Zubayr, declared that news of Ḥamzah's death had already reached her, and handed over two garments to shroud her brother — one of which went by lot to a mutilated Anṣārī who had none.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: named in the Prophet's ﷺ warning to his nearest kin at Ṣafā", "Ch. 6: identified as the mother of Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, the Prophet's ﷺ disciple"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 20 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "The wider literature remembers her as the first Muslim woman to kill an enemy in battle: during the Trench, when a Jewish scout prowled the fortress sheltering the women, the aging Ṣafiyyah took a tent-pole and felled him herself. She mourned her nephew ﷺ in elegies the sources preserve, and died in Medina around 20 AH, in her early seventies, during ʿUmar's caliphate; she was buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1132, 1401–1403", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 20 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "She then pushed me in my chest, (which was not surprising) for she certainly was a tough and hardy woman. She said, ‘May you have no earth underneath you (to keep you standing)! Get away from me!’ I said, ‘Verily, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has commanded you (to stop).’ She stopped and took out two garments that she had with her and said, ‘Here are two garments that I have brought for my brother, Hamzah, for news of his death has indeed reached me. So enshroud him in them.’", "page": 1132}, "who_ar": "عَمَّةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَشَقِيقَةُ حَمْزَةَ، وَأُمُّ حَوَارِيِّهِ الزُّبَيْرِ بْنِ الْعَوَّامِ."}, {"id": "jafar-ibn-abi-talib", "name": "Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib", "arabic": "جعفر بن أبي طالب", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; al-Ṭayyār, 'the one with two wings in Paradise'", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ cousin, spokesman of the emigrants before the Negus, and martyred commander of Muʾtah.", "story": "Jaʿfar, elder brother of ʿAlī, led the emigrants to Abyssinia and vindicated the Prophet's ﷺ choice in one of the sīrah's most celebrated speeches. Refusing to prostrate to the Negus, he stood with a firm chest and described how Islam had lifted his people from idolatry, immorality, and oppression, then recited from Sūrah Maryam until the Negus wept; pressed about ʿĪsā, he answered with wisdom that preserved both truth and their asylum (Ch. 7). He remained in Abyssinia over a decade, returning just after Khaybar—when the Prophet ﷺ kissed his forehead and said, 'I don't know which pleases me more: conquering Khaybar or the return of Jaʿfar!' (Ch. 21). When ʿAlī, Zayd, and Jaʿfar disputed the honour of raising Ḥamzah's orphaned daughter, the Prophet ﷺ told him, 'You resemble me most in both looks and manners,' and awarded her to him since her maternal aunt was his wife (Ch. 23). At Muʾtah he was second-in-command; when Zayd fell, Jaʿfar took the banner and fought one of the most vicious fights ever seen until both his arms were severed and his body bore over 50 wounds, none in his back. The Prophet ﷺ announced, 'I saw Jaʿfar in Paradise—Allah had given him two wings to replace his two arms' (Ch. 24). al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1661) that upon his martyrdom the Prophet ﷺ went to his wife Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, called for Jaʿfar's children, kissed each one and wept, then told the Companions: 'Do not forget to prepare food for the family of Jaʿfar, for indeed they are preoccupied with the matter of their companion.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: spokesman before the Negus; recited Sūrah Maryam until the king wept", "Ch. 21: returned from Abyssinia to the Prophet's ﷺ kiss and famous welcome", "Ch. 23: awarded the care of Ḥamzah's daughter—'you resemble me in looks and manners'", "Ch. 24: martyred at Muʾtah carrying the banner; given two wings in Paradise"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Martyr"], "death": "8 AH — martyred at Muʾtah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 509–512, 1592, 1661"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ja’far ibn Abee Taalib ؓ was wisely chosen to lead the Muslims in Abyssinia and to speak on their behalf to An-Najaashee, for someone of at least equal intelligence to ‘Amr ibn Al-‘Aas was needed to debate him before the king. Ja’far ؓ was an ideal representative of the Muslims for various reasons. First, having lived with the Prophet ﷺ under the same roof, Ja’far ؓ was very close to him, and probably knew him ﷺ better than any other Muslim in Abyssinia.", "page": 509}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَخَطِيبُ الْمُهَاجِرِينَ بَيْنَ يَدَيِ النَّجَاشِيِّ، وَالْأَمِيرُ الَّذِي اسْتُشْهِدَ يَوْمَ مُؤْتَةَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-abbas", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās", "arabic": "عبد الله بن عباس", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-ʿAbbās; Ḥabr al-Ummah (the Sage of the Ummah); Turjumān al-Qurʾān", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ young cousin, son of al-ʿAbbās, who became the master interpreter of the Qurʾān.", "story": "Son of al-ʿAbbās and cousin of the Prophet ﷺ, Ibn ʿAbbās was still a boy at the Prophet's ﷺ death, yet his narrations are woven through this entire book: he described how the Prophet ﷺ would raise his voice in nothing but truth (Ch. 6), preserved the accounts of the early tortures answered by Saʿīd ibn Jubayr (Ch. 6), transmitted the events of Badr, and recorded rulings from the marriage of his aunt Maymūnah in iḥrām (Ch. 23) to the fate of the Army of the Elephant discussed with ʿUmar. His genius shone in the book's closing chapters: when the Caliph ʿUmar quizzed the senior Companions on the meaning of Sūrah al-Naṣr, none knew the answer except the young ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, who explained that it was Allah's indication to the Prophet ﷺ that his time on Earth was ending (Ch. 30). Decades later, he and ʿUthmān both cited the Prophet's ﷺ words when the Khārijites arose (Ch. 28). Blessed by the Prophet's ﷺ own duʿāʾ for understanding of the Book, he became known as the Sage of the Ummah and the Interpreter of the Qurʾān, teaching until he died in Ṭāʾif in 68 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: narrator of the Prophet's ﷺ character and the early persecution", "Ch. 23: narrated the fiqh discussion of Maymūnah's marriage in iḥrām", "Ch. 30: alone among the Companions in explaining Sūrah al-Naṣr as the Prophet's ﷺ approaching death"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 68 AH, Ṭāʾif", "after_sirah": "He became exactly what the Prophet's ﷺ duʿāʾ had asked: the fountainhead of tafsīr for all later generations, teaching in Medina, Basra — which he governed for ʿAlī — and finally Mecca. Blind in his last years, he withdrew to Ṭāʾif and died there in 68 AH at about seventy; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah led his funeral prayer and said, 'Today the sage of this Ummah has died.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1724–1725", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 68 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ibn ‘Abbaas ؓ related that ‘Umar ؓ used to take him to gatherings that the venerable fighters of Badr would attend. Some of them were bothered by the fact that ‘Umar would bring along with him someone who was so young. One day, they said to ‘Umar ؓ, “Why do you bring him to us, when we have children just like him (that we can bring as well)?”", "page": 1724}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الصَّغِيرُ، وَابْنُ الْعَبَّاسِ، الَّذِي صَارَ تُرْجُمَانَ الْقُرْآنِ."}, {"id": "abu-sufyan-ibn-al-harith", "name": "Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "أبو سفيان بن الحارث بن عبد المطلب", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ paternal cousin and childhood companion who mocked him for two decades, then embraced Islam at the Conquest.", "story": "A paternal cousin raised alongside the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith became one of the most brazen mockers of Islam, writing the most scathing poems against him. As the army approached Mecca he panicked and intercepted the Prophet ﷺ pleading for mercy; the Prophet ﷺ at first refused—'I have no need for either of them'—testing his sincerity. Raising his son's hand, he swore he would wander the desert and die if not admitted, and the Prophet's ﷺ gentle heart softened. As Abū Sufyān recited his poem of repentance—'A guide whom I repelled at every turn, but he continued to guide me to Allah'—the Prophet ﷺ struck his chest, saying, 'Yes, by Allah, you repelled me at every turn.' He accepted Islam and lived the rest of his life as a pious Muslim serving the faith (Ch. 25).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: intercepted the Prophet ﷺ before the Conquest, was tested, then forgiven and accepted Islam"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 20 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He spent his final years in Medina in worship and self-reproach for the lost decades, and the sources record that he dug his own grave at al-Baqīʿ days before he died — telling his family not to weep, for he had not sinned since his Islam. He died around 20 AH in ʿUmar's caliphate, and ʿUmar prayed over him. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1692) that in his account it was Umm Salamah who first spoke on the cousins' behalf, and that only a short while after his acceptance of Islam he proved his worth at Ḥunayn, achieving exemplary feats on the battlefield while so many fled.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1691–1692", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 20 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Despite the seriousness of their crimes, the Prophet ﷺ forgave them, and both men embraced Islam. Abu Sufyaan ibn Al-Haarith ؓ atoned for his past poems by composing a beautiful poem in which he praised the Prophet ﷺ and spoke of how he had become guided by him. And it was only a short while later, during the Hunain Battle, that Abu Sufyaan ibn Al-Haarith proved his worth, achieving exemplary feats on the battlefield.", "page": 1691}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَرَفِيقُ صِبَاهُ، هَجَاهُ عِشْرِينَ سَنَةً ثُمَّ أَسْلَمَ عَامَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "zayd-ibn-harithah", "name": "Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "arabic": "زيد بن حارثة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Usāmah; al-Ḥibb (the Beloved of the Prophet ﷺ); once known as Zayd ibn Muhammad", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ freed slave and once adopted son; the only Companion named in the Qurʾān; martyred commander of Muʾtah.", "story": "Kidnapped from Yemen as a boy and sold into slavery, Zayd was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ by Khadījah. When his grieving father finally found him and offered ransom, the Prophet ﷺ let Zayd choose freely—and Zayd chose the Prophet ﷺ over his own family: 'You are more than a father and uncle combined.' The Prophet ﷺ took him to the Kaʿbah, freed him, and adopted him, and he was called Zayd ibn Muhammad until Sūrah al-Aḥzāb abolished adoption-naming (Ch. 4). He is the only Companion mentioned by name in the Qurʾān—an honour not even Abū Bakr received (Ch. 4). He alone accompanied the Prophet ﷺ on the perilous journey to Ṭāʾif (Ch. 9), and it was Zayd who galloped into Medina on al-Qaṣwāʾ announcing the victory of Badr (Ch. 14). He married Umm Ayman, and their son Usāmah was born in the Prophet's ﷺ own house (Ch. 4). The Prophet ﷺ sent him on at least ten expeditions, always as commander—no one ever commanded Zayd. At Muʾtah he led the army against the Romans and was the first of the three commanders to be martyred (Ch. 24). ʿĀʾishah declared, 'If Zayd had been alive when the Prophet ﷺ died, he would have been chosen as Caliph' (Ch. 4). al-Ṣallābī notes (p. 155) that Zayd — Ḥibb al-Nabī, the beloved of the Prophet ﷺ — was the first freed slave to embrace Islam.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: chose the Prophet ﷺ over his own father; freed and adopted at the Kaʿbah", "Ch. 9: sole companion of the Prophet ﷺ at Ṭāʾif", "Ch. 14: announced the victory of Badr to Medina", "Ch. 24: commander at Muʾtah; martyred bearing the banner", "Ch. 4: the only Companion named in the Qurʾān (al-Aḥzāb, 37)"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "8 AH — martyred at Muʾtah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 155–156, 529–531, 1654–1658"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "But by that time, Zaid had gotten to know the wonderful character of the Prophet ﷺ, and Zaid’s love for the Prophet ﷺ was so great that he chose him over his own father. Zaid said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, “I would never choose anyone over you. To me, you rank equal to a father and an uncle.”", "page": 155}, "who_ar": "مَوْلَى النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَدَعِيُّهُ الَّذِي تَبَنَّاهُ حِينًا، وَالصَّحَابِيُّ الْوَحِيدُ الْمُسَمَّى فِي الْقُرْآنِ، وَأَمِيرُ مُؤْتَةَ الشَّهِيدُ."}, {"id": "usamah-ibn-zayd", "name": "Usāmah ibn Zayd", "arabic": "أسامة بن زيد", "kunya_titles": "al-Ḥibb ibn al-Ḥibb ('the Beloved, son of the Beloved')", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "Son of Zayd and Umm Ayman, born and raised in the Prophet's ﷺ house; the 18-year-old commander of his final army.", "story": "Usāmah was born in the house of the Prophet ﷺ to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and Umm Ayman, and grew up as family. He was so beloved that Companions wanting something from the Prophet ﷺ would ask Usāmah to ask on their behalf, for he ﷺ could not resist his requests; he was nicknamed 'the Prophet's beloved, son of the Prophet's beloved' (Ch. 4). During the Slander, he was one of the two men the Prophet ﷺ consulted, and he strongly defended ʿĀʾishah's innocence (Ch. 18). After Muʾtah, the Prophet ﷺ teared up upon seeing Usāmah, who so closely resembled his martyred father (Ch. 24). At the Farewell Hajj, Usāmah rode behind the Prophet ﷺ on his descent from ʿArafah (Ch. 29). Then came his defining honour: passing over Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, the Prophet ﷺ appointed 18-year-old Usāmah to command the army to the Roman frontier, telling him, 'Go to where your father was killed'—channelling his grief into resolve. When news of the Prophet's ﷺ illness came, he camped outside Medina and finally rushed back; too weak to speak, the Prophet ﷺ pointed to him and then to the sky: 'Go, and Allah is with you' (Ch. 30). ʿUmar as Caliph paid him a higher stipend than his own son, 'because he was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah than you' (Ch. 4). al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1738) that when the family of the Makhzūmī woman due the ḥadd for theft sought an intercessor, everyone agreed that only Usāmah, so close to the Prophet ﷺ, could dare ask — and his reluctant plea drew the immortal reply: 'By the One Who has the soul of Muhammad in His Hand, were Fāṭimah bint Muhammad to steal, I would cut off her hand.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: born in the Prophet's ﷺ house; 'the Beloved, son of the Beloved'", "Ch. 18: consulted during the Slander; defended ʿĀʾishah", "Ch. 29: rode behind the Prophet ﷺ at the Farewell Hajj", "Ch. 30: appointed at 18 to command the final army; the Prophet's ﷺ silent farewell gesture"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 54 AH, al-Jurf near Medina (also reported 58/59 AH)", "after_sirah": "Abū Bakr's first act as Caliph was to dispatch Usāmah's army exactly as the Prophet ﷺ had commanded — walking beside the mounted young commander and refusing to let him dismount. Usāmah returned victorious, but never again raised a sword at anyone who said lā ilāha illa Allāh: he withdrew completely from the civil wars, living at Wādī al-Qurā and then Medina, and died at al-Jurf around 54 AH, late in Muʿāwiyah's era.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1327, 1738, 1974–1975", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 54 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When revelation (concerning my affair) was slow in coming, the Prophet ﷺ summoned ‘Alee ibn Abee Taalib and Usaamah ibn Zaid, seeking their counsel as to whether he should divorce his wife (i.e., me). Usaamah pointed out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what he knew, in terms of the innocence of his family (i.e., of his wives) and how loving they were. He said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, (she) is your family (i.e., your wife), and we know only good things (about her).’", "page": 1327}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ زَيْدٍ وَأُمِّ أَيْمَنَ، وُلِدَ وَنَشَأَ فِي بَيْتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَأَمَّرَهُ عَلَى آخِرِ جُيُوشِهِ وَهُوَ ابْنُ ثَمَانِيَ عَشْرَةَ سَنَةً."}, {"id": "umm-ayman", "name": "Umm Ayman (Barakah)", "arabic": "أم أيمن بركة", "kunya_titles": "Barakah; 'my mother after my mother'", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "Servant of the Prophet's ﷺ mother Āminah who cared for him from birth; wife of Zayd and mother of Usāmah.", "story": "Barakah, known as Umm Ayman, was the servant of the Prophet's ﷺ mother Āminah, and remained part of his household from his infancy—he ﷺ is well known to have honoured her as 'my mother after my mother'. She was married to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and their son, the famous Usāmah ibn Zayd, was born in the house of the Prophet ﷺ himself (Ch. 4); the book notes her motherhood of Usāmah when describing his closeness to the Prophet ﷺ during the events of the Slander (Ch. 18). An early believer and emigrant, she lived to grieve the Prophet's ﷺ passing, dying in Medina shortly afterwards.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: servant of Āminah; wife of Zayd and mother of Usāmah, born in the Prophet's ﷺ house", "Ch. 18: mentioned as the mother of Usāmah, the Prophet's ﷺ trusted confidant"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 11 AH, Medina — months after the Prophet ﷺ (also reported c. 24 AH, early in ʿUthmān's caliphate)", "after_sirah": "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim preserves her last famous scene: Abū Bakr and ʿUmar visited her as the Prophet ﷺ used to, and found her weeping — not for him, she said, for what is with Allah is better for His Messenger, but 'because revelation from the heavens has ceased' — and the two Caliphs wept with her. One report has her dying five or six months after the Prophet ﷺ; another that she lived until days after ʿUmar's assassination, dying early in ʿUthmān's caliphate. al-Ṣallābī records (p. 83) that she was an Abyssinian maid of the Prophet's ﷺ father ʿAbdullāh who nursed the newborn after his father's death, that she served water to the wounded at Uḥud (p. 1173), and he confirms the first report of her death — five months after the Prophet ﷺ (p. 83).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 82–83, 1173, 1985", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Usd al-Ghāba", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 11 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "As for Umm Aiman, she was a maid who belonged to ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Abdul-Muttalib, and she was from Abyssinia. When the Prophet ﷺ was born, and after his father had died, Umm Aiman nursed the Prophet ﷺ. Then, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew older, he ﷺ freed her and married her off to Zaid ibn Haarithah ؓ. The Prophet ﷺ died first, and then Umm Aiman ؓ died five months later.", "page": 82}, "who_ar": "خَادِمَةُ آمِنَةَ أُمِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، حَضَنَتْهُ مُنْذُ وِلَادَتِهِ، وَهِيَ زَوْجُ زَيْدٍ وَأُمُّ أُسَامَةَ."}, {"id": "halimah-al-sadiyyah", "name": "Ḥalīmah al-Saʿdiyyah", "arabic": "حليمة السعدية", "kunya_titles": "of the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr; foster mother of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "family", "tier": "major", "who": "The Bedouin wet-nurse of the Banū Saʿd who nursed the Prophet ﷺ in infancy and witnessed his first miracles.", "story": "Ḥalīmah of the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr came to Mecca in the annual custom of taking infants to nurse in the eloquent desert air. No family wanted the orphan child—payment customarily came from the father—and finding herself without any infant, she took Muhammad ﷺ. The blessings began at once: her milk flowed abundantly for him and his milk-brother, her old she-camel's udder filled, and her slow donkey outpaced the whole caravan. Her husband declared, 'Ḥalīmah, by Allah, I think that you have brought a blessed soul.' The miracles continued so consistently through his two-year tenure that she pleaded with Āminah to extend the stay (Ch. 3). It ended with the awesome event of the opening of his chest: Jibrīl came to the four-year-old, removed a black slither from his heart—'This is Shayṭān's portion in you'—washed it in Zamzam in a golden cup, and sealed it. The frightened Ḥalīmah returned him to his mother (Ch. 3). Her bond with his household endured: at Ḥunayn, over half a century later, her daughter Shaymāʾ—his milk-sister—was found among the captives, and the Prophet ﷺ freed and honoured her for their shared childhood (Ch. 26).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 3: chose the orphan infant no one wanted and was overwhelmed with blessings", "Ch. 3: witnessed the aftermath of the opening of the Prophet's ﷺ chest and returned him to Āminah", "Ch. 26: her daughter Shaymāʾ freed and honoured by the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥunayn"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 83–88, 1795"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "After we arrived in Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was presented to each one of us, and each one of us disliked (taking) him. We said, ‘He is an orphan, and it is the father who is kind and generous to the wet nurse. (In the case of this orphan) what can we expect his mother, uncle, or grandfather to do for us?’ Each one of my female companions took an infant (except for me). When I found no other child, I returned to him (the Prophet ﷺ) and took him.", "page": 83}, "who_ar": "الْمُرْضِعَةُ الْبَدَوِيَّةُ مِنْ بَنِي سَعْدٍ، أَرْضَعَتِ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ فِي صِغَرِهِ وَشَهِدَتْ أَوَائِلَ مُعْجِزَاتِهِ."}, {"id": "atikah-bint-abd-al-muttalib", "name": "ʿĀtikah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "عاتكة بنت عبد المطلب", "kunya_titles": "aunt of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ paternal aunt whose dream foretold the destruction of Quraysh at Badr; her acceptance of Islam is differed upon.", "story": "Three days before Ḍamḍam's frantic arrival in Mecca, ʿĀtikah dreamt of a crier racing on a camel, shouting from the Kaʿbah and Mount Abū Qubays, 'O traitors! Meet your death in three days!', then toppling a boulder that splintered into every house in Mecca. Her brother al-ʿAbbās urged secrecy but himself let it slip, and soon all Mecca gossiped about the dream; Abū Jahl mocked, 'Since when did the children of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib get a female prophetess?' On the third day Ḍamḍam arrived screaming for help—calling Quraysh, exactly as ʿĀtikah had foreseen, to their wealth and in truth to their death at Badr (Ch. 14). It should be noted that scholars differ over whether ʿĀtikah ever accepted Islam; she is included here for her role in the book's narrative, and Allah knows best her end.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: her vivid dream before Badr that shook Mecca and was fulfilled to the day"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 965"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Certain members of the Banu Haashim clan saw dreams that had the effect of further weakening the morale of the Quraish. For example, ‘Aatikah bint ‘Abdul-Muttalib saw a dream in which a man threw a rock from on top of Mount Abu Qubais in Makkah. The rock crumbled into pieces that entered into every house of the Quraish, which of course was interpreted to mean that death was soon going to afflict all of Quraish’s families.", "page": 965}, "who_ar": "عَمَّةُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الَّتِي أَنْذَرَتْ رُؤْيَاهَا بِمَصْرَعِ قُرَيْشٍ بِبَدْرٍ، وَقَدِ اخْتُلِفَ فِي إِسْلَامِهَا."}, {"id": "barirah", "name": "Barīrah", "arabic": "بريرة", "kunya_titles": "mawlāh of ʿĀʾishah", "group": "family", "tier": "minor", "who": "The slave-woman whom ʿĀʾishah purchased in order to free; loyal servant of the Prophet's ﷺ household.", "story": "Barīrah was a former slave whom ʿĀʾishah purchased for the very purpose of setting her free, and in appreciation and loyalty she remained close to ʿĀʾishah, serving her as a free woman within the Prophet's ﷺ household (Ch. 18). During the Slander, when ʿAlī suggested questioning her maidservant, the Prophet ﷺ called Barīrah forward, and her nervous 'confession'—that the young ʿĀʾishah's only fault was dozing off and letting the goat eat the dough—comically underscored ʿĀʾishah's complete purity and innocence (Ch. 18). In the books of fiqh she is also the source of foundational rulings on manumission and a freed woman's choice in marriage.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 18: freed by ʿĀʾishah; her testimony of ʿĀʾishah's innocence during the Slander"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1327"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned for Bareerah to come to him, and when she came, he ﷺ said, ‘O Bareerah, have you seen anything suspicious (about ‘Aishah)?’ She said, ‘No, by the One Who has sent you with the truth. The thing that I most find fault with her is that she is a young woman who sleeps, leaving her family’s dough unwatched, and the sheep (that is fed in the house) comes and eats it (i.e., negligence, because of her youth, is the greatest fault I find in her).’", "page": 1327}, "who_ar": "الْأَمَةُ الَّتِي اشْتَرَتْهَا عَائِشَةُ لِتُعْتِقَهَا، وَخَادِمَةُ بَيْتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الْوَفِيَّةُ."}, {"id": "abu-bakr-al-siddiq", "name": "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "arabic": "أبو بكر الصديق", "kunya_titles": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Quḥāfah; al-Ṣiddīq — 'The Truthful'; 'the second of the two in the cave'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ closest friend and confidant, the first free adult male to accept Islam, and the first Rightly-Guided Caliph.", "story": "The Ummah is unanimous that Abū Bakr is the best of all Companions. The Prophet ﷺ said, \"I am not allowed to choose a khalīl, but if I were allowed, I would choose Abū Bakr,\" and testified that when everyone else hesitated, \"it was only Abū Bakr who believed me\" — he alone accepted Islam without a moment's doubt (Ch. 6). A stream of early converts, including five of the Ten Promised Paradise, embraced Islam at his hand (Ch. 6). He pulled ʿUqbah off the Prophet ﷺ as he was being strangled at the Kaʿbah, crying, \"Are you going to kill a man for saying my Lord is Allah?\" (Ch. 7). When he instantly affirmed the Night Journey — \"If he said that, then it is true\" — he earned the title al-Ṣiddīq (Ch. 10). Chosen as the Prophet's ﷺ companion for the Hijrah, he wept from joy, then whispered fearfully in Cave Thawr and heard, \"What do you think about two men whose third is Allah?\" (Ch. 12). He defied ʿUrwah at Ḥudaybiyyah and steadied ʿUmar (Ch. 20), gave his entire wealth at Tabūk (Ch. 27), and led the prayers in the Prophet's ﷺ final illness. At the calamity itself, he alone stood firm — \"Whoever used to worship Muhammad, know that Muhammad ﷺ has died\" — and was given the pledge at Saqīfah as the first Caliph (Ch. 30). He led the Apostasy Wars and ordered the Qur'an's compilation (Ch. 13), dying in 13 AH. al-Ṣallābī preserves the scene at Tabūk (p. 1817): asked what he had left for his family after bringing everything he owned, Abū Bakr replied, \"I left for them Allah and His Messenger.\"", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: the only person to accept Islam without any hesitation; the Prophet ﷺ would have taken him as his khalīl", "Ch. 7: rescued the Prophet ﷺ from ʿUqbah's strangling; returned Ibn al-Dughinnah's protection, saying 'I accept the protection of Allah'", "Ch. 10: named al-Ṣiddīq for instantly believing the Isrā' and Miʿrāj", "Ch. 12: companion of the cave on the Hijrah — 'Do not worry, for Allah is certainly with us'", "Ch. 18: resumed his stipend to Misṭaḥ after the Slander, seeking Allah's forgiveness", "Ch. 30: led the prayers in the Prophet's ﷺ final illness, steadied the Ummah at his death, and was elected Caliph at Saqīfah"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Rightly-Guided Caliph"], "death": "d. 13 AH, Medina, as Caliph — buried beside the Prophet ﷺ", "after_sirah": "His caliphate lasted just twenty-seven months, yet it saved Islam itself. He fell ill in Jumādā al-Ākhirah 13 AH and died at sixty-three — the same age as his beloved ﷺ — asking to be shrouded in his old washed garments, for 'the living need new clothes more than the dead.' ʿĀʾishah's room opened once more, and he was buried with his head at the Prophet's ﷺ shoulder, companion in the cave and companion in the grave.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 158–160, 385, 659", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 13 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Abu Bakr ؓ was the first free man to embrace Islam, and even before the advent of Islam, he was one of the closest friends of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Throughout their long years of friendship, Abu Bakr ؓ became very much acquainted with the noble character of the Prophet ﷺ, which is why it is not much of a surprise that he embraced Islam without hesitating in the least.", "page": 158}, "who_ar": "أَقْرَبُ أَصْحَابِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ إِلَيْهِ وَمَوْضِعُ سِرِّهِ، وَأَوَّلُ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ مِنَ الرِّجَالِ الْأَحْرَارِ، وَأَوَّلُ الْخُلَفَاءِ الرَّاشِدِينَ."}, {"id": "umar-ibn-al-khattab", "name": "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "arabic": "عمر بن الخطاب", "kunya_titles": "Abū Ḥafṣ; al-Fārūq — 'The Distinguisher'; one of the Prophet's ﷺ 'Two Ministers'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Fierce early opponent turned mighty defender of Islam, the Prophet's ﷺ close confidant and father-in-law, and the second Rightly-Guided Caliph.", "story": "ʿUmar set out sword in hand to kill the Prophet ﷺ, was redirected to his own sister Fāṭimah and her husband Saʿīd ibn Zayd, struck her in rage — then, shamed by her bleeding courage, read the parchment of Qur'an and marched to Dār al-Arqam declaring, \"I came to accept Islam!\" The house erupted in takbīr; Ibn Masʿūd said, \"We have ever remained in glory since the day ʿUmar accepted Islam,\" and the Prophet ﷺ named him al-Fārūq (Ch. 8). ʿAlī recalled that no one made Hijrah publicly except ʿUmar (Ch. 12). His dream confirmed the adhān (Ch. 13); at Badr he stood boldly in counsel and argued for executing the prisoners (Ch. 14); he gave his daughter Ḥafṣah in marriage to the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 15) and answered Abū Sufyān's boasts at Uḥud (Ch. 16). At Ḥudaybiyyah he questioned the treaty — \"Are you not the Messenger of Allah?\" — and spent a lifetime of good deeds in regret (Ch. 20). He gave half his wealth at Tabūk and vowed, \"I will never compete with Abū Bakr again\" (Ch. 27). Shattered at the Prophet's ﷺ death, he collapsed at Abū Bakr's recitation, then was first to pledge allegiance at Saqīfah (Ch. 30). As Caliph he conquered Persia — seating Surāqah on Kisrā's throne (Ch. 12) — and Jerusalem, and was assassinated in 23 AH. al-Ṣallābī records Ibn Masʿūd's fuller tribute (p. 497): \"The Islam of ʿUmar was a conquest, his migration was a victory, and his caliphate was a mercy — we would not pray beside the Kaʿbah until ʿUmar embraced Islam.\"", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: his dramatic conversion — his sister Fāṭimah's steadfastness, the parchment of Ṭā-Hā, and his takbīr-raising entry into Dār al-Arqam", "Ch. 12: the only Companion to make the Hijrah publicly, daring the Quraysh to stop him", "Ch. 13: his dream confirming the wording of the adhān", "Ch. 15: married his daughter Ḥafṣah to the Prophet ﷺ after ʿUthmān and Abū Bakr declined", "Ch. 20: his anguished questioning at Ḥudaybiyyah and lifelong expiation for it", "Ch. 30: collapsed at the news of the Prophet's ﷺ death; first to place his hand in Abū Bakr's at Saqīfah"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Rightly-Guided Caliph", "Martyr"], "death": "23 AH — assassinated in Medina as Caliph; buried beside the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr", "after_sirah": "After ten years of a caliphate that reshaped the world, he was stabbed at Fajr prayer in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 23 AH by Abū Luʾluʾah, a Magian slave. Dying, he sent his son to ʿĀʾishah to beg the spot beside his two companions — 'and if she refuses, bury me with the ordinary Muslims at al-Baqīʿ.' She gave up the place she had kept for herself, and ʿUmar was laid beside the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr, having appointed the six-man shūrā that chose the next Caliph.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 497–499, 1534", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 23 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ibn Mas’ood ؓ said, “Verily, the Islam of ‘Umar ؓ was a conquest, his migration (to Al-Madeenah) was a victory, and his caliphate was a mercy (to the Muslims). Verily, we would not pray beside the Ka’bah until ‘Umar ؓ embraced Islam. Then, when he embraced Islam, he fought with the Quraish and then prayed beside the Ka’bah, and we prayed (there) with him.”", "page": 497}, "who_ar": "عَدُوٌّ شَدِيدٌ لِلْإِسْلَامِ فِي أَوَّلِهِ ثُمَّ مُدَافِعٌ عَظِيمٌ عَنْهُ، وَصَفِيُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَصِهْرُهُ، وَثَانِي الْخُلَفَاءِ الرَّاشِدِينَ."}, {"id": "uthman-ibn-affan", "name": "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "arabic": "عثمان بن عفان", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; Dhū al-Nūrayn — 'Possessor of the Two Lights'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "One of the Prophet's ﷺ closest friends, husband of two of his daughters, and the third Rightly-Guided Caliph.", "story": "ʿUthmān accepted Islam at Abū Bakr's hand among the earliest converts; the Prophet ﷺ said of him, \"Even the Angels are shy in his presence\" (Ch. 6). Married to the Prophet's ﷺ daughter Ruqayyah, he was among the first twelve men to emigrate to Abyssinia, taking her with him (Ch. 7). At Badr the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to stay behind nursing the gravely ill Ruqayyah; he received a full share of the spoils, yet heard Medina's victory celebrations as he returned from burying her (Ch. 14). He later married her sister Umm Kulthūm, earning the title Dhū al-Nūrayn. At Ḥudaybiyyah he was sent as envoy to Mecca — \"more noble in their eyes,\" said ʿUmar — and when rumours spread that he had been killed, the Prophet ﷺ took the Pledge of Divine Acceptance, clasping his own left hand in his right and declaring, \"This is for ʿUthmān!\" ʿUthmān refused to perform ṭawāf while the Muslims were barred: \"By Allah, if I stayed in Mecca for one year, I would not perform ṭawāf until the Prophet ﷺ did so first\" (Ch. 20). He single-handedly financed the Army of Hardship for Tabūk — camels, gold, and more — until the Prophet ﷺ declared, \"ʿUthmān has no sin after what he has done today\" (Ch. 27). As third Caliph he standardised the muṣḥaf; besieged by rebels citing that very ḥadīth in his defence (Ch. 27), he was martyred in 35 AH. al-Ṣallābī details (pp. 1815–1816) how at Tabūk he rose three times as the Prophet ﷺ kept exhorting the people — pledging one hundred, then two hundred, then three hundred fully equipped camels — then brought a thousand dinars which the Prophet ﷺ turned over in his hands, repeating that nothing Ibn ʿAffān did after that day could harm him.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: early convert at Abū Bakr's hand — 'Even the Angels are shy in his presence'", "Ch. 7: led the first emigration to Abyssinia with his wife Ruqayyah, daughter of the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 14: kept back from Badr to nurse Ruqayyah; buried her the very day of the victory", "Ch. 20: envoy to Mecca at Ḥudaybiyyah; the false rumour of his death triggered Bayʿat al-Riḍwān, where the Prophet ﷺ pledged on his behalf", "Ch. 25: sheltered his foster-brother ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd at the Conquest and won him the Prophet's ﷺ pardon", "Ch. 27: equipped the Army of Hardship — 'nothing can harm ʿUthmān after what he has done today'"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Abyssinia", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Rightly-Guided Caliph", "Martyr"], "death": "35 AH — martyred in Medina during the siege of his home", "after_sirah": "Through twelve years of caliphate he expanded the Prophet's ﷺ Mosque, launched Islam's first navy, and sent the standardised muṣḥaf to every province. When the rebels finally broke in, in Dhū al-Ḥijjah 35 AH, the old man of over eighty forbade his supporters to shed Muslim blood in his defence, and was killed fasting, with the Qurʾān open before him — its pages stained by his blood. He was buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 493, 1515, 1815–1816", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 35 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "It seems that they did not travel as one group, for it is related that ‘Uthmaan ibn ‘Affaan ؓ and his wife, Ruqayyah (daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ) ؓ, were the first among them to migrate. It is interesting to note that not a single one of the migrants was a slave, even though it was the slaves - such as Bilaal ؓ, Khabbaab ؓ, and ‘Aamir - who were tortured more severely than anyone else.", "page": 493}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَقْرَبِ أَصْحَابِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ إِلَيْهِ، وَزَوْجُ ابْنَتَيْهِ، وَثَالِثُ الْخُلَفَاءِ الرَّاشِدِينَ."}, {"id": "ali-ibn-abi-talib", "name": "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "arabic": "علي بن أبي طالب", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Ḥasan; Abū Turāb; 'to me as Hārūn was to Mūsā'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ cousin and son-in-law, raised in his household, the first child to accept Islam, and the fourth Rightly-Guided Caliph.", "story": "ʿAlī was the first child to accept Islam (Ch. 6). On the Hijrah night he lay in the Prophet's ﷺ bed as a decoy while the assassins waited outside, then stayed behind to return the trusts Meccans had deposited with the Prophet ﷺ before joining him at Qubā' (Ch. 12–13). At Badr he cut down al-Walīd in the opening duel and helped fell ʿUtbah; he recalled that when the fighting grew fierce, the Companions would seek protection through the Prophet ﷺ himself (Ch. 14). At Uḥud he won the opening mubārazah (Ch. 16), and at the Trench he answered ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Wudd's challenge — volunteering three times until the Prophet ﷺ relented — and slew the legendary champion (Ch. 19). He wrote the treaty at Ḥudaybiyyah but could not bring himself to erase \"Messenger of Allah\" (Ch. 20). At Khaybar the Prophet ﷺ promised the banner to \"a man whom Allah and His Messenger love,\" cured ʿAlī's infected eye with his blessed saliva, and sent him forth; ʿAlī defeated the champion Mirḥab (Ch. 21). At Tabūk he was told, \"Are you not content to be to me as Hārūn was to Mūsā, except that there is no Prophet after me?\" (Ch. 27), and at Ghadīr Khumm the Prophet ﷺ declared, \"If I am a person's protector, then ʿAlī is their protector\" (Ch. 29). Husband of Fāṭimah and father of al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, he became fourth Caliph and was assassinated in 40 AH in Kufa. al-Ṣallābī notes (p. 154) that ʿAlī was ten years old at his conversion according to the strongest view — that of al-Ṭabarī and Ibn Isḥāq — the Prophet ﷺ having taken him into his own household years earlier to ease Abū Ṭālib's financial hardship.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: the first child to accept Islam", "Ch. 12: slept in the Prophet's ﷺ bed as a decoy on the Hijrah night, then returned the Meccans' trusts", "Ch. 14: victor in the opening duel at Badr against al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah", "Ch. 19: killed ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Wudd in single combat at the Trench", "Ch. 21: given the banner of Khaybar — 'a man whom Allah and His Messenger love' — his eye healed by the Prophet's ﷺ saliva; defeated Mirḥab", "Ch. 29: honoured at Ghadīr Khumm and sent to proclaim Sūrah al-Tawbah during Abū Bakr's Hajj"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Rightly-Guided Caliph", "Martyr"], "death": "40 AH — assassinated in Kufa as Caliph", "after_sirah": "His caliphate was spent holding the Ummah together through its first civil strife, which he met with a restraint and a grief history has honoured ever since. Struck with a poisoned sword by the Khārijite Ibn Muljam while walking to Fajr in Ramaḍān 40 AH, he died two days later at about sixty-three, commanding that his killer receive one blow only and no mutilation — just as his cousin ﷺ would have wished.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 154, 1586–1587, 1830", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 40 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Shortly after Khadeejah ؓ embraced Islam, ‘Alee ibn Abee Taalib ؓ also entered into the fold of Islam. He was the first child to embrace Islam, being 10 years old at the time, at least according to the strongest view in the matter, two proponents of which are At-Tabaree and Ibn Ishaaq. So even before the Prophet ﷺ received revelation for the first time, ‘Alee ؓ was one of the members of his household.", "page": 154}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَصِهْرُهُ، نَشَأَ فِي بَيْتِهِ، وَأَوَّلُ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ مِنَ الصِّبْيَانِ، وَرَابِعُ الْخُلَفَاءِ الرَّاشِدِينَ."}, {"id": "talhah-ibn-ubaydillah", "name": "Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh", "arabic": "طلحة بن عبيد الله", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muḥammad; 'the living martyr'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert at Abū Bakr's hand and the human shield of Uḥud, who said 'My chest instead of your chest, O Messenger of Allah.'", "story": "Ṭalḥah embraced Islam in the earliest days at the hand of Abū Bakr, one of the young Qurashī nobles who became giants of the Ummah (Ch. 6). Before Badr, the Prophet ﷺ sent him with Saʿīd ibn Zayd to scout the great Quraysh caravan of a thousand camels; Abū Sufyān detected the pair from Medinan date seeds in their camels' dung and rerouted the caravan, so the two missed the battle itself — yet they were among the nine granted a full share of its spoils (Ch. 14). His immortal hour came at Uḥud. When the Muslims were scattered and only ten men ringed the Prophet ﷺ, Ṭalḥah was the first to answer, \"Who will take on these people and be my Companion in Paradise?\" — but the Prophet ﷺ kept him at his side as the Anṣār went forth one by one to martyrdom, until only Ṭalḥah and Saʿd remained. An expert archer, he thwarted the attackers, placing his body before the Prophet ﷺ, saying, \"My chest instead of your chest, O Messenger of Allah,\" and partially blocking with his shield the sword-strike that gashed the Prophet's ﷺ face (Ch. 16); his hand was maimed shielding him, and he is remembered as \"the living martyr.\" Years later, he alone of the Muhājirūn rose to congratulate Kaʿb ibn Mālik when his repentance was accepted — a kindness Kaʿb never forgot (Ch. 27). He was killed at the Battle of the Camel in 36 AH. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1126) that when the wounded Prophet ﷺ could not climb a great boulder on the mountain at Uḥud, Ṭalḥah — his right arm already paralysed by an arrow — crouched beneath and bore him up on his back, and al-Zubayr heard the Prophet ﷺ say, \"Ṭalḥah has made himself deserving (of Paradise).\"", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: among the early converts who accepted Islam at the hand of Abū Bakr", "Ch. 14: scouted the Quraysh caravan with Saʿīd ibn Zayd before Badr; detected by Abū Sufyān through Medinan date seeds; granted a full share of the spoils", "Ch. 16: first to volunteer when the Prophet ﷺ asked 'Who will take on these people and be my Companion in Paradise?'", "Ch. 16: shielded the Prophet ﷺ with his own body and shield — 'My chest instead of your chest, O Messenger of Allah'", "Ch. 20: named among the Ten Promised Paradise, the highest rank of the Companions", "Ch. 27: the only Muhājir to stand, shake hands, and congratulate Kaʿb ibn Mālik on his accepted repentance"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Uḥud", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Martyr"], "death": "36 AH — killed at the Battle of the Camel, near Basra", "after_sirah": "He served on ʿUmar's six-man shūrā and remained one of Medina's most fabulously generous men — nicknamed 'Ṭalḥah of abundant giving'. At the Battle of the Camel in Jumādā 36 AH he was withdrawing from the fighting when a stray arrow — widely reported to have been shot by Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam from his own side — severed the vein of his knee, and he bled to death at about sixty. He was buried in Basra, and ʿAlī grieved to remember the Prophet's ﷺ words that Ṭalḥah and Zubayr would be his neighbours in Paradise.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 159, 1126, 1877", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 36 AH", "who_ar": "أَسْلَمَ قَدِيمًا عَلَى يَدِ أَبِي بَكْرٍ، وَكَانَ تُرْسَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ، الْقَائِلَ: «نَحْرِي دُونَ نَحْرِكَ يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ»."}, {"id": "al-zubayr-ibn-al-awwam", "name": "Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām", "arabic": "الزبير بن العوام", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; the ḥawārī (disciple) of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ cousin, son of Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, of whom he ﷺ said: 'Every Prophet has his disciples, and my disciple is Zubayr.'", "story": "Zubayr accepted Islam as a youth at Abū Bakr's hand, among the first converts. A direct cousin of the Prophet ﷺ through his mother Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, he was honoured with the words, \"Every Prophet has his disciples, and my disciple is Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām\" (Ch. 6). He joined the first emigration to Abyssinia (Ch. 7), and when war engulfed that land, it was Zubayr who went out to observe the battle for the anxious refugees, returning to the city crying, \"Rejoice, for the Negus has won!\" (Ch. 7). He fought at Badr, and at Uḥud eagerly volunteered for the Prophet's ﷺ sword — though Abū Dujānah won it by pledging to give it its due, and Zubayr, following him into battle, conceded he was true to his vow (Ch. 16). During the terror of the Trench, when the Prophet ﷺ needed a man to infiltrate the Banū Qurayẓah and verify their treachery, Zubayr came forth and sneaked in alone — the occasion on which the \"disciple\" ḥadīth was proclaimed; days later he threw armoured Nawfal into the trench, prompting a bystander to praise his \"amazing sword\" (Ch. 19). He investigated Ḥāṭib's letter with ʿAlī (Ch. 25), carried a banner at the Conquest of Mecca after Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah (Ch. 25), and stood with the Prophet's ﷺ family preparing his burial (Ch. 30). He was killed in 36 AH after withdrawing from the Battle of the Camel. al-Ṣallābī preserves (pp. 999–1000) Zubayr's own account of his Badr duel — felling ʿUbaydah ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, armoured so completely that only his eyes showed, with a spear-thrust to the eye — and relates how the captured armour was asked of him in turn by the Prophet ﷺ, then Abū Bakr, ʿUmar and ʿUthmān.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: among the first converts — 'Every Prophet has his disciples, and my disciple is Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām'", "Ch. 7: emigrated to Abyssinia and brought the refugees news of the Negus's victory — 'Rejoice, for the Negus has won!'", "Ch. 16: at Uḥud, foremost in volunteering for the Prophet's ﷺ sword", "Ch. 19: sneaked alone into the Banū Qurayẓah at the Trench to confirm their betrayal", "Ch. 25: entrusted with intercepting Ḥāṭib's letter and given a banner at the Conquest of Mecca", "Ch. 30: among the Prophet's ﷺ close family who prepared his blessed body for burial"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Abyssinia", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān", "Martyr"], "death": "36 AH — killed at Wādī al-Sibāʿ after leaving the Battle of the Camel", "after_sirah": "On the field of the Camel, ʿAlī reminded him privately of the Prophet's ﷺ words that Zubayr would one day fight ʿAlī wrongfully — and Zubayr, remembering, dropped his sword and left the battle rather than raise it against him. He was pursued to Wādī al-Sibāʿ by ʿAmr ibn Jurmūz, who killed him as he prayed and carried the sword to ʿAlī expecting reward; ʿAlī wept over the sword that had 'so long defended the Prophet ﷺ' and said, 'Give the killer of the son of Ṣafiyyah tidings of the Fire.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 159, 999–1000, 1367", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 36 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Az-Zubair ibn Al-‘Awwaam ؓ said, “On the Day of Badr, I met ‘Ubaidah ibn Sa’eed ibn Al-‘Aas (on the battlefield). He was covered completely in armour, so that only his eyes could be seen. His Kunyaa (a kind of name) was Abu Dhaat Al-Karish. He said, ‘I am Abu Dhaat Al-Karish,’ after which I attacked him with an ‘Anazah (a weapon that resembles a staff); I stabbed him in his eye, and he died.”", "page": 999}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمَّةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَابْنُ صَفِيَّةَ بِنْتِ عَبْدِ الْمُطَّلِبِ، قَالَ فِيهِ ﷺ: «إِنَّ لِكُلِّ نَبِيٍّ حَوَارِيًّا، وَإِنَّ حَوَارِيَّ الزُّبَيْرُ»."}, {"id": "abdurrahman-ibn-awf", "name": "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf", "arabic": "عبد الرحمن بن عوف", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muḥammad; the only person ever to lead the Prophet ﷺ in prayer", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert and legendary merchant-philanthropist who arrived in Medina penniless and rebuilt a fortune from the marketplace.", "story": "The oldest of the early batch who accepted Islam at Abū Bakr's hand (Ch. 6), ʿAbdurraḥmān emigrated to Abyssinia (Ch. 7) and then to Medina with nothing. When his Anṣārī brother-in-faith Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ offered him half of everything — a wife and a garden — he replied with immortal dignity, \"May Allah bless you. Just direct me towards the marketplace,\" and traded his way back to being one of Medina's wealthiest men, serving the Prophet ﷺ in every way he could (Ch. 6, 13); their pairing is the textbook example of the mu'ākhāh brotherhood (Ch. 13). At Badr he hoped for strong warriors beside him and found two slight teenagers — who then hunted down and felled Abū Jahl before his eyes; and his old business partner Umayyah ibn Khalaf clung to him for protection, in vain, as justice found him through Bilāl (Ch. 14). At Tabūk came his singular honour: with the Prophet ﷺ delayed, the Companions put ʿAbdurraḥmān forward for Fajr; the Prophet ﷺ joined in the second rakʿah and motioned him to remain — making him the only person ever to lead the Prophet ﷺ in prayer (Ch. 27). When Ibrāhīm lay dying, it was he who gently questioned the Prophet's ﷺ tears and received the reply, \"O Ibn ʿAwf, this is mercy\" (Ch. 28). He died wealthy and generous in 32 AH in Medina. al-Ṣallābī devotes a study (pp. 1460–1465) to his command of the seven-hundred-strong expedition to Dūmat al-Jandal, where the Kalb chief al-Aṣbagh ibn ʿAmr embraced Islam at his hands and married his daughter to him — the first time, al-Ṣallābī notes, that Islamic rule was applied outside Medina.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: among the first converts at Abū Bakr's hand — 'Just direct me towards the marketplace'", "Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia", "Ch. 13: his brotherhood with Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ, the model of the mu'ākhāh", "Ch. 14: flanked at Badr by the two youths who killed Abū Jahl; his former partner Umayyah ibn Khalaf begged him for protection", "Ch. 27: led the Prophet ﷺ himself in Fajr prayer at Tabūk — a unique honour", "Ch. 28: at Ibrāhīm's death, heard the Prophet ﷺ say, 'O Ibn ʿAwf, this is mercy'"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Abyssinia", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "d. 32 AH, Medina (also reported 31 AH) — buried at al-Baqīʿ", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar entrusted him with the deciding voice on the six-man shūrā, and he withdrew his own candidacy to broker ʿUthmān's election — the Ummah's honest broker to the end. His generosity became legend: caravans of seven hundred camels given away in a day, seven hundred of the veterans of Badr remembered in his will, and enormous provision for the Mothers of the Believers, of whom ʿĀʾishah prayed, 'May Allah give him to drink from Salsabīl.' He died in 32 AH and was buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 771, 1460–1465", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Abdur-Rahmaan ؓ said, “I have no need of any of that. Is there any marketplace in which there is (good) business?” Sa’d said, “(Yes,) the marketplace of Qainuqaa’.” Qainuqaa’ was the name of a Jewish tribe, and the said marketplace was ascribed to them.", "page": 771}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، وَالتَّاجِرُ الْجَوَادُ الْمَشْهُورُ، قَدِمَ الْمَدِينَةَ صِفْرَ الْيَدَيْنِ فَبَنَى ثَرْوَتَهُ مِنَ السُّوقِ."}, {"id": "sad-ibn-abi-waqqas", "name": "Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ", "arabic": "سعد بن أبي وقاص", "kunya_titles": "Abū Isḥāq; the first to shoot an arrow for the sake of Allah", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Teenage early convert and master archer, the only man for whom the Prophet ﷺ said, 'May my mother and father be ransomed for you.'", "story": "Saʿd was the youngest of the early converts at Abū Bakr's hand, around sixteen. His mother went on hunger strike to force him back to idolatry, withering away until he declared, \"O my mother... even if you had one thousand lives, I would let you lose them one after the other before renouncing my faith\" — and Allah revealed Qur'an about his ordeal (Ch. 6). On one of the first expeditions from Medina he made history by shooting the first arrow for the sake of Allah (Ch. 13). A veteran of Badr, his greatest day was Uḥud: when the Muslims scattered, he and Ṭalḥah stood among the last defenders beside the Prophet ﷺ in the mountain crevice. Both expert archers, they thwarted wave after wave, and the Prophet ﷺ handed Saʿd arrows saying, \"May my mother and father be ransomed for you. Fire away, O Saʿd!\" — the highest expression of respect in Arabic, and the first and only time the Prophet ﷺ ever said it to anyone; Saʿd proudly cherished the unique honour all his life (Ch. 16). He stood at Ḥudaybiyyah among those Allah was pleased with, and is named in this book among the Ten Promised Paradise (Ch. 20). He later commanded the Muslim armies at Qādisiyyah that felled Persia, governed Kufa, and was the last of the Ten to die, around 55 AH at al-ʿAqīq near Medina. al-Ṣallābī adds (pp. 885–886) that in that first skirmish — sixty Muhājirūn under ʿUbaydah ibn al-Ḥārith facing two hundred Quraysh riders — Saʿd stood firm shooting arrows to cover his companions' orderly withdrawal.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: converted at sixteen; withstood his mother's hunger strike — verses of Qur'an revealed about him", "Ch. 13: shot the first arrow in the history of Islam", "Ch. 16: one of the last two Companions defending the Prophet ﷺ at Uḥud", "Ch. 16: 'May my mother and father be ransomed for you. Fire away, O Saʿd!' — a phrase the Prophet ﷺ said to no one else", "Ch. 20: named among the Ten Promised Paradise, the highest rank of the Companions"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "d. 55 AH, al-ʿAqīq near Medina (also reported 54–58 AH) — the last of the Ten to die", "after_sirah": "The archer of Uḥud became the conqueror of an empire: at Qādisiyyah in 15 AH his armies broke Persia, and he founded the garrison city of Kufa and governed it for ʿUmar, who also named him to the shūrā of six. In the civil wars he refused to fight any Muslim, retiring to his estate at al-ʿAqīq — 'until you bring me a sword that can tell a believer from a disbeliever.' He died there around 55 AH, the last of the Ten and of the Muhājirūn to die, asking to be shrouded in the very cloak he had worn at Badr; he was buried at al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 402–403, 885–886, 1126", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 55 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Then she spent another day and night without eating anything, and when she woke up on the following morning, she was extremely weak. When I saw that, I said, ‘O my mother, you know, by Allah, that were you to have 100 souls, and they all departed one soul at a time, still nothing would make me abandon my religion. So if you want, eat; and if you want, don’t eat.’", "page": 402}, "who_ar": "غُلَامٌ مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَرَامٍ مَاهِرٌ، وَهُوَ الرَّجُلُ الَّذِي لَمْ يَقُلِ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ لِغَيْرِهِ: «فِدَاكَ أَبِي وَأُمِّي»."}, {"id": "said-ibn-zayd", "name": "Saʿīd ibn Zayd", "arabic": "سعيد بن زيد", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Aʿwar; son of the ḥanīf Zayd ibn ʿAmr", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert in whose home ʿUmar's conversion began; husband of ʿUmar's sister Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb.", "story": "Saʿīd was the son of Zayd ibn ʿAmr, one of the rare ḥanīfs who abandoned idolatry before revelation to search for the religion of Ibrāhīm (Ch. 2) — and the son fulfilled what the father sought, accepting Islam in its earliest days with his wife Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb. Their home became the unlikely stage for one of the sīrah's turning points: ʿUmar, marching to kill the Prophet ﷺ, was redirected to their house, where Khabbāb was secretly teaching them Qur'an. ʿUmar burst in demanding answers; when he stepped forward to strike Saʿīd, Fāṭimah intervened and was bloodied instead, and their courage under his fists broke ʿUmar's rage. He read the parchment of Sūrah Ṭā-Hā and went straight to Dār al-Arqam to embrace Islam (Ch. 8). Before Badr, the Prophet ﷺ chose Saʿīd and Ṭalḥah to scout the enormous Quraysh caravan; Abū Sufyān sniffed out the pair from Medinan date seeds in the camel dung and diverted his caravan, so Saʿīd missed the battle itself — yet he was among the nine granted a full share of its spoils (Ch. 14). He witnessed Uḥud, the Trench, and the Pledge of Divine Acceptance, and this book counts him among the Ten Promised Paradise, the highest rank of the Companions (Ch. 20). He lived long, famously ascetic and avoiding leadership, and died around 51 AH at al-ʿAqīq, buried in Medina. al-Ṣallābī relates from al-Wāqidī (p. 1886) that when Allah accepted the repentance of the three who stayed behind from Tabūk, it was Saʿīd who hurried to Banū Wāqif to give Hilāl ibn Umayyah the glad tidings — and Hilāl fell straight to the ground in prostration.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 2: heir of his father Zayd ibn ʿAmr, the ḥanīf who searched for the religion of Ibrāhīm", "Ch. 8: learning Qur'an from Khabbāb with his wife Fāṭimah when ʿUmar stormed in — their steadfastness sparked ʿUmar's conversion", "Ch. 14: scouted the thousand-camel Quraysh caravan with Ṭalḥah before Badr", "Ch. 14: among the nine given a full share of Badr's spoils despite being absent on duty", "Ch. 20: named among the Ten Promised Paradise, the highest rank of the Companions"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "d. 51 AH, al-ʿAqīq; buried in Medina (also reported 50–52 AH)", "after_sirah": "He fought at Yarmūk and briefly held Damascus, then begged to be relieved of governorship — jihād and worship, not office, were his love. Medina remembered his fearsome answered prayers: when Arwā bint Aws falsely accused him of seizing her land, he prayed, 'O Allah, if she is lying, blind her eye and kill her in her land' — and so it happened, exactly. He died at al-ʿAqīq around 51 AH; Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Ibn ʿUmar carried him to his grave in Medina.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 160, 167, 1886", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 51 AH", "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، وَفِي بَيْتِهِ كَانَ مَبْدَأُ إِسْلَامِ عُمَرَ، وَهُوَ زَوْجُ أُخْتِهِ فَاطِمَةَ بِنْتِ الْخَطَّابِ."}, {"id": "abu-ubaydah-ibn-al-jarrah", "name": "Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ", "arabic": "أبو عبيدة بن الجراح", "kunya_titles": "ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ; 'the Trustee (Amīn) of this Ummah'", "group": "ten", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert famed for trustworthiness and humility, who sacrificed his own teeth extracting the metal rings from the Prophet's ﷺ cheek at Uḥud.", "story": "An early convert of the Meccan years, Abū ʿUbaydah faced at Badr the most searing test of faith in the book: his own idolatrous father hunted him across the battlefield, and when finally cornered, Abū ʿUbaydah in self-defence killed him. As murmurs spread and grief pressed upon him, Allah revealed the verse that those who truly believe in Allah do not retain loyalty to those who wage war on Him — even fathers and sons (Ch. 14). At Uḥud, when the battle ended with an arrow and metal rings of the Prophet's ﷺ helmet still embedded in his blessed face, Abū ʿUbaydah begged Abū Bakr for the honour of removing them; the metal was lodged too deep for fingers, so he bit down and dislodged each piece, losing a tooth for each — and would smile proudly ever after that his teeth were sacrificed for the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 16). Sent with reinforcements to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ at Dhāt al-Salāsil — commanding even Abū Bakr and ʿUmar — he graciously stepped down when ʿAmr insisted on overall command, saying the Prophet ﷺ had ordered him not to fall into dispute: a masterclass in leading from behind (Ch. 24). He carried the central banner at the Conquest of Mecca (Ch. 25). At Saqīfah, Abū Bakr held up his hand alongside ʿUmar's as worthy of the Caliphate (Ch. 30). Known as the Trustee of this Ummah, he later governed Syria and died in the plague of ʿAmwās in 18 AH. al-Ṣallābī recounts the scene behind the title (p. 1932): when Najrān's delegation asked for a trustworthy man to collect what their treaty owed, the Companions lifted their heads hoping to be chosen, and the Prophet ﷺ said, \"Stand up, O Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ... Here is the trustworthy one of this nation.\"", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: at Badr, forced to kill his own father in self-defence — Qur'an revealed affirming loyalty to Allah above kinship", "Ch. 16: extracted the metal rings from the Prophet's ﷺ cheek at Uḥud with his teeth, losing two of his own", "Ch. 20: named among the Ten Promised Paradise, the highest rank of the Companions", "Ch. 24: led reinforcements at Dhāt al-Salāsil and humbly ceded command to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ — 'even if you disobey me, I will not disobey you'", "Ch. 25: bore the middle group's banner at the Conquest of Mecca", "Ch. 30: one of the two men Abū Bakr proposed for the Caliphate at Saqīfah"], "badges": ["Ten Promised Paradise", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "18 AH — died in the plague of ʿAmwās, Syria; buried in the Jordan Valley", "after_sirah": "As supreme commander in Syria he received the surrender of Damascus and stood beside ʿUmar at the gates of Jerusalem. When the plague of ʿAmwās swept the land in 18 AH he refused ʿUmar's summons to safety — 'I am in the army of the Muslims and will not desert them' — and died among his soldiers at fifty-eight, buried in the Jordan Valley. ʿUmar said on his own deathbed: 'If Abū ʿUbaydah were alive, I would have appointed him Caliph.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1127, 1452–1453, 1932", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 18 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Along with three-hundred riders, Abu ‘Ubaidah ibn Al-Jarraah ؓ was sent along the route of the seashore to look for, and perhaps overtake, one of Quraish’s trading caravans. On the way, however, Abu Ubaidah’s men began to run out of food; Abu Ubaidah ordered them to hand over all of the food they had with them, so that he could assess their situation based on the quantity of food that was left.", "page": 1452}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، اشْتُهِرَ بِالْأَمَانَةِ وَالتَّوَاضُعِ، ضَحَّى بِثَنِيَّتَيْهِ حِينَ نَزَعَ حَلْقَتَيِ الْمِغْفَرِ مِنْ وَجْنَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "bilal-ibn-rabah", "name": "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ", "arabic": "بلال بن رباح", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; muʾadhdhin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Abyssinian slave among the first seven to embrace Islam; the most iconic of the persecuted believers and the first muʾadhdhin of the Ummah.", "story": "Ibn Masʿūd counted Bilāl among the first seven who embraced Islam, and of them all, only Bilāl never once said a word to please his torturers (Ch. 6). His master, Umayyah ibn Khalaf, would drag him through the streets with a rope around his neck and pin him in the desert sun under a massive burning rock — rocks so hot, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ recalled, that meat could be cooked on them — while Bilāl cried out 'One; One!', proclaiming the Oneness of Allah (Ch. 6). Abū Bakr freed him, and his honour matched his struggles: when the adhān was legislated in Medina, the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Stand up, O Bilāl! You have the loudest voice,' making him the first muʾadhdhin of Islam (Ch. 13). At Badr he came face to face with Umayyah ibn Khalaf and pointed out his old tormentor, who was killed in the fighting (Ch. 14). At the Conquest of Mecca, the Prophet ﷺ had Bilāl climb onto the roof of the Kaʿbah itself and give the adhān — the slave once tortured beneath its idols now calling all of Mecca to Allah (Ch. 25). He later settled in Syria and is buried in Damascus. al-Ṣallābī, who devotes a section to his ordeal (pp. 393–396), records the Prophet's ﷺ words to him: 'Indeed, last night, I heard the sound of your footsteps ahead of me in Paradise.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: named by Ibn Masʿūd among the first seven Muslims; the only one who never yielded under torture", "Ch. 6: dragged through Mecca and crushed under burning rocks by Umayyah ibn Khalaf, crying 'One; One!'", "Ch. 13: appointed the first muʾadhdhin — 'Stand up, O Bilāl! You have the loudest voice'", "Ch. 14: identified Umayyah ibn Khalaf at Badr — 'This is the man who tortured me!'", "Ch. 25: gave the adhān from the roof of the Kaʿbah at the Conquest of Mecca"], "badges": ["Badr", "Muʾadhdhin"], "death": "d. 20 AH, Damascus (also reported 17–18 AH)", "after_sirah": "After the Prophet's ﷺ passing, Medina without him was more than Bilāl could bear: he left for the jihad in Syria and rarely gave the adhān again — when he called it once more on a visit to Medina, the city wept as it had not wept since the Prophet's ﷺ death. He died in Damascus around 20 AH and is buried at the Bāb al-Ṣaghīr cemetery.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 393–396, 732, 1709", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 20 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Bilaal ؓ had no family to protect him. In fact, Bilaal ؓ was hardly considered a human being; his only purpose, according to the mind-set of the Quraish, was to serve and obey, to be bought and sold like livestock. That he should have an opinion in important matters was considered ludicrous, which is why the Quraish, fearing a change in their way of life, became outraged when Bilaal and other slaves embraced Islam.", "page": 393}, "who_ar": "عَبْدٌ حَبَشِيٌّ مِنَ السَّبْعَةِ الْأَوَائِلِ الَّذِينَ أَسْلَمُوا، وَأَشْهَرُ الْمُعَذَّبِينَ فِي اللَّهِ، وَأَوَّلُ مُؤَذِّنٍ لِهَذِهِ الْأُمَّةِ."}, {"id": "ammar-ibn-yasir", "name": "ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir", "arabic": "عمار بن ياسر", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Yaqẓān; 'the son of Sumayyah'", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Son of Islam's first two martyrs; among the first seven Muslims, tortured until he outwardly renounced his faith — occasioning the Qur'anic concession for the coerced.", "story": "ʿAmmār was among the first seven to embrace Islam alongside his father Yāsir and mother Sumayyah (Ch. 6). The family was tortured in front of one another — a fate worse than the pain itself — and the Prophet ﷺ made a special duʿā' for them: 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise.' After Abū Jahl murdered his father, mother, and older brother Muhammad, fifteen-year-old ʿAmmār could bear no more and outwardly renounced his faith. Distraught, he ran to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked, 'How do you find your heart?' He replied, 'As it always was.' Allah then revealed the verse of Sūrah al-Naḥl excusing those forced while their hearts are firm in faith (Ch. 6). The Prophet ﷺ said, 'His īmān has been filled in his heart all the way to his neck,' and, 'Whenever the son of Sumayyah is faced with two options, he always chooses the most correct of the two.' In later years the Prophet ﷺ entrusted him to burn down Masjid al-Ḍirār, the mosque of the Hypocrites (Ch. 27), and confided to him the identity of the masked Hypocrites who tried to push him ﷺ off a cliff returning from Tabūk (Ch. 27). He was killed at Ṣiffīn in his nineties, fighting alongside ʿAlī.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: among the first seven Muslims; his family tortured together — 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir'", "Ch. 6: forced renunciation under torture; Sūrah al-Naḥl 16:106 revealed excusing the coerced", "Ch. 27: ordered by the Prophet ﷺ to destroy and burn Masjid al-Ḍirār", "Ch. 27: told by the Prophet ﷺ the identity of the Hypocrites behind the cliff ambush after Tabūk"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "37 AH — killed at the Battle of Ṣiffīn, in his nineties", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar appointed him governor of Kufa, and in his nineties he stood with ʿAlī at Ṣiffīn, where he was killed in 37 AH — fulfilling to the letter the Prophet's ﷺ words to him, 'You will be killed by the transgressing party.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 399–401, 1853, 1865", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 37 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "So when Yaasir ؓ, Sumayyah ؓ, ‘Ammaar, and ‘Abdullah ؓ - ‘Ammaar’s brother - embraced Islam, it was the Makhzoom clan that was responsible for inflicting punishment upon Yaasir’s family. The tormenters would take Yaasir’s family out to the hot sands of the desert at high noon and torture them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pass by them as they were being tortured and comfort them by saying, “Patience, O family of Yaasir, for indeed, your place of appointment is Paradise.”", "page": 399}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ أَوَّلِ شَهِيدَيْنِ فِي الْإِسْلَامِ، وَأَحَدُ السَّبْعَةِ الْأَوَائِلِ الَّذِينَ أَسْلَمُوا، عُذِّبَ حَتَّى تَبَرَّأَ بِلِسَانِهِ مِنْ دِينِهِ، فَنَزَلَتْ فِي الْقُرْآنِ رُخْصَةُ الْمُكْرَهِ."}, {"id": "yasir-ibn-amir", "name": "Yāsir ibn ʿĀmir", "arabic": "ياسر بن عامر", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAmmār; the first martyr of Islam", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Yemeni settler in Mecca, husband of Sumayyah and father of ʿAmmār; the first person martyred for Islam.", "story": "Yāsir was among the earliest slave-class converts of Mecca, embracing Islam alongside his wife Sumayyah bint Khayyāṭ and their son ʿAmmār (Ch. 6). Lacking any tribal protection, the family bore the full brunt of Quraysh's savagery: they were tortured in front of each other, a fate the book calls far worse than the physical pain itself. Passing by them, the Prophet ﷺ made his famous duʿā', 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise' (Ch. 6). Yāsir was then brutally killed, becoming the first martyr in Islam — the opening name in the long register of those who gave their lives for lā ilāha illa Allāh. His story, with that of his wife and son, has captured the hearts of Muslims for over a millennium as the very picture of steadfastness under unrelenting, merciless persecution.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: embraced Islam with his wife Sumayyah and son ʿAmmār among the earliest converts", "Ch. 6: the Prophet ﷺ made duʿā' — 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise'", "Ch. 6: brutally killed under torture, becoming the first martyr in Islam"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "Killed under torture in Mecca before the Hijrah — the first martyr of Islam (al-Ṣallābī counts Sumayyah as the first martyr of Islam, with Yāsir martyred in a similar manner shortly after her, p. 400)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 399–400"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Ammaar’s father, Yaasir ؓ, was not a native of Makkah, which helps to explain why he was so vulnerable to persecution at the hands of the Quraish; he was from the Banu ‘Anas, one of the tribes of Yemen. Yaasir ؓ and his two brothers, Al-Haarith and Maalik, came to Makkah in search of one of their brothers. Al-Haarith and Maalik eventually returned to Yemen, whereas Yaasir ؓ settled in Makkah.", "page": 399}, "who_ar": "يَمَانِيٌّ اسْتَوْطَنَ مَكَّةَ، زَوْجُ سُمَيَّةَ وَوَالِدُ عَمَّارٍ، وَأَوَّلُ مَنِ اسْتُشْهِدَ فِي الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "sumayyah-bint-khayyat", "name": "Sumayyah bint Khayyāṭ", "arabic": "سمية بنت خياط", "kunya_titles": "Umm ʿAmmār; the first female martyr of Islam", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Enslaved woman among the first seven Muslims; mother of ʿAmmār and the first woman martyred for Islam.", "story": "Ibn Masʿūd named Sumayyah among the first seven who embraced Islam (Ch. 6). With her husband Yāsir and son ʿAmmār she endured torture designed to break the family by making each watch the others suffer, and it was for them that the Prophet ﷺ made the duʿā', 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise' (Ch. 6). After her husband was killed, Sumayyah — despite her slave status and the certain consequences — grew enraged and bravely rebuked Abū Jahl to his face. He murdered her in the most brutal and inhumane manner, making her the first woman to die for this Ummah (Ch. 6). Her honour lived on in her son: the Prophet ﷺ would praise ʿAmmār as 'the son of Sumayyah' who, faced with two options, always chooses the more correct of the two — her name itself becoming a title of distinction.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: named among the first seven Muslims", "Ch. 6: tortured with her family — 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise'", "Ch. 6: rebuked Abū Jahl despite her slave status and was brutally murdered by him — the first female martyr"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "Killed by Abū Jahl in Mecca before the Hijrah — the first female martyr of Islam (al-Ṣallābī relates that Abū Jahl stabbed her with a spear and counts her the first martyr of Islam outright, p. 400)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 399–400"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "One day, Abu Jahl said to Sumayyah ؓ, “You believe in Muhammad only because you are in love with him for his beauty.” She reproached Abu Jahl as he deserved to be reproached, and in response, he jabbed her in her private area with a spear and killed her. Sumayyah ؓ became the first martyr in Islam, sacrificing the most valuable thing a person has to offer - her very life.", "page": 399}, "who_ar": "أَمَةٌ مِنْ أَوَّلِ سَبْعَةٍ أَسْلَمُوا، وَأُمُّ عَمَّارٍ، وَأَوَّلُ شَهِيدَةٍ فِي الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "khabbab-ibn-al-aratt", "name": "Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt", "arabic": "خباب بن الأرت", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Arab slave of Yemeni origin, of the first ten to embrace Islam; his scarred back became the enduring testimony of Meccan persecution.", "story": "Khabbāb was of the first ten people to embrace Islam (Ch. 6). When his mistress Umm Anmār discovered his faith, she hired a mob to beat him unconscious and would herself hold burning iron against his back. The Prophet ﷺ prayed, 'O Allah, please help Khabbāb against his enemy,' and Umm Anmār later died of the very cauterisation treatment prescribed for her madness (Ch. 6). It was Khabbāb who, though steadfast, once asked the Prophet ﷺ at the Kaʿbah, 'For how long will we be tortured like this? Why don't you ask Allah to help us?' — drawing the reply that those before were combed with iron and sawn in half yet did not waver (Ch. 7). It was also Khabbāb who was secretly teaching Qur'an to Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb and Saʿīd ibn Zayd the night ʿUmar stormed in and left a believer (Ch. 8). Decades later, Caliph ʿUmar asked him about Umm Anmār; Khabbāb silently lifted his shirt, and ʿUmar exclaimed he had never seen a back like it, seating him beside himself in honour. On his deathbed in Kufa, wealthy at last, Khabbāb wept — fearing his comforts had already spent his reward, remembering Ḥamzah, who had not even a full shroud (Ch. 6).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: of the first ten Muslims; tortured by Umm Anmār with burning iron", "Ch. 7: asked the Prophet ﷺ at the Kaʿbah, 'For how long, O Messenger of Allah?'", "Ch. 8: teaching Qur'an to Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb and Saʿīd ibn Zayd on the night of ʿUmar's conversion", "Ch. 6: showed Caliph ʿUmar his scarred back; wept on his deathbed over his late-life luxury"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 37 AH, Kufa (also reported 39 AH)", "after_sirah": "He settled in Kufa after the conquests, and returning from Ṣiffīn, ʿAlī stood at his grave on the outskirts of the city and prayed for the man who had 'accepted Islam willingly, emigrated obediently, and lived as a mujāhid.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 167, 405–408", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 37 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "And their appointed teacher was Khabbaab ibn Al-Arat ؓ. When they met, their main concern was not simply to learn how to recite the Qur’an, but also to actually study the Qur’an, understand its Verses, learn its commands and prohibitions, and apply its teachings.", "page": 167}, "who_ar": "عَبْدٌ عَرَبِيٌّ يَمَانِيُّ الْأَصْلِ، مِنَ الْعَشَرَةِ الْأَوَائِلِ إِسْلَامًا، صَارَ ظَهْرُهُ الْمُشَوَّهُ بِالنُّدُوبِ شَاهِدًا خَالِدًا عَلَى عَذَابِ أَهْلِ مَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-masud", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd", "arabic": "عبد الله بن مسعود", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; Ibn Umm ʿAbd", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "The sixth convert to Islam, shepherd-turned-scholar; first Muslim to recite the Qur'an publicly and one of the Ummah's greatest authorities on the Qur'an.", "story": "A young shepherd of the servant class hired by ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ, Ibn Masʿūd met the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr when they asked him for milk. He refused what was not his to give — and after the Prophet ﷺ drew milk from a dry she-goat by duʿā', he converted on the spot, the sixth person to enter Islam (Ch. 6). When the Companions at Dār al-Arqam debated who would first recite Qur'an publicly, Ibn Masʿūd volunteered despite having no tribe to protect him, saying, 'I put my trust in Allah.' He recited Sūrah al-Raḥmān melodiously at the Kaʿbah until the Quraysh beat him bloody — and offered to do the same the next day (Ch. 6). The Prophet ﷺ said whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an as it was revealed should recite as Ibn Umm ʿAbd does; he took over seventy sūrahs directly from the Prophet's ﷺ mouth (Ch. 6). He transmitted the visions of the Miʿrāj (Ch. 10), and at Badr it was he who found his old tormentor Abū Jahl dying, put his foot on his chest, and finished him with his own sword (Ch. 14). At ʿUmar's death he said, 'We have ever remained in glory since the day ʿUmar accepted Islam' (Ch. 8). al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 409) that at that first meeting the Prophet ﷺ passed his hand over him and said, 'May Allah have mercy on you, for indeed you are a young lad who is muʿallam' — taught and guided to what is correct.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: the she-goat miracle; converted on the spot as the sixth Muslim", "Ch. 6: first to recite the Qur'an publicly — Sūrah al-Raḥmān at the Kaʿbah, beaten for it", "Ch. 10: narrated the Prophet's ﷺ vision at the Sidrah al-Muntahā", "Ch. 14: dispatched Abū Jahl at Badr with Abū Jahl's own sword"], "badges": ["Badr", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 32 AH, Medina (some say 33 AH)", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar sent him to Kufa as its teacher and treasurer with the words, 'I have preferred you over myself,' and his recitation and rulings shaped Kufan scholarship for generations. Recalled to Medina in ʿUthmān's caliphate, he died there and was buried in al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 409–411, 992–994", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Perhaps ‘Abdullah ؓ is best remembered for being the first Companion to openly recite the Qur’an in front of a gathering of polytheists, an amazingly brave feat considering the fact that ‘Abdullah ؓ was not a member of a subtribe in Makkah, but was only allied to one, which means that he ؓ had no family to protect him. What is more, ‘Abdullah ؓ was skinny, with especially thin calves, and not a person who could defend himself against large or muscular attackers.", "page": 409}, "who_ar": "سَادِسُ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ، وَرَاعِي غَنَمٍ صَارَ عَالِمًا، وَأَوَّلُ مُسْلِمٍ جَهَرَ بِالْقُرْآنِ عَلَانِيَةً، وَمِنْ أَعْلَمِ الْأُمَّةِ بِكِتَابِ اللهِ."}, {"id": "al-arqam-ibn-abi-al-arqam", "name": "Al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam", "arabic": "الأرقم بن أبي الأرقم", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; host of Dār al-Arqam", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Makhzūmī youth of the first ten Muslims whose house, Dār al-Arqam, became the secret headquarters of early Islam.", "story": "His given name was al-Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, more famously known as al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam — one of the first ten people to accept Islam (Ch. 6). When the believers grew beyond a dozen and needed somewhere to congregate, the Prophet ﷺ chose his house with striking strategic insight: al-Arqam was from Banū Makhzūm, not Banū Hāshim, so no one would suspect a Makhzūmī of harbouring a Hāshimī; and his house sat in the busy centre of Mecca, letting attendees slip in and out without suspicion. Dār al-Arqam thus became the hub of the Muslims — a centre for teaching, prayer, and strategy (Ch. 6). It was there that the Companions planned the first public recitation of the Qur'an (Ch. 6), and there that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came, sword in hand, and left having declared his Islam to the takbīr of the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 8). Few Companions can claim that the formative institution of the Meccan daʿwah bore their name. He emigrated to Medina, witnessed Badr, and lived on into the era of the Umayyads, dying in Medina. al-Ṣallābī, analysing the choice of the house (pp. 175–176), adds that al-Arqam was only about sixteen when he embraced Islam — no one would think to search an adolescent's home — and that no book records the Quraysh ever discovering the Muslims' meeting place.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: one of the first ten Muslims; his house chosen by the Prophet ﷺ as the secret base of the daʿwah", "Ch. 6: Dār al-Arqam becomes the hub of teaching, prayer, and strategy", "Ch. 8: ʿUmar accepts Islam inside Dār al-Arqam"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. c. 55 AH, Medina (some say 53 AH)", "after_sirah": "He lived quietly in Medina deep into the Umayyad era, past the age of eighty; at his own bequest, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ — his companion from the first days of Dār al-Arqam — led his funeral prayer.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 174–176", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 55 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Arqam ؓ did not openly declare his Islam, so it was highly unlikely that anyone would think that the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions ؓ were meeting one another in his house. Al-Arqam ibn Abee Al-Arqam ؓ was from the Banu Makhzoom clan, the same clan that carried the flag of war (or enmity) against the Banu Haashim clan (of which the Prophet ﷺ was a member).", "page": 174}, "who_ar": "فَتًى مَخْزُومِيٌّ مِنَ الْعَشَرَةِ الْأَوَائِلِ إِسْلَامًا، صَارَتْ دَارُهُ «دَارَ الْأَرْقَمِ» مَقَرًّا سِرِّيًّا لِلْإِسْلَامِ فِي أَوَّلِ عَهْدِهِ."}, {"id": "musab-ibn-umayr", "name": "Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr", "arabic": "مصعب بن عمير", "kunya_titles": "Muṣʿab al-Khayr; 'the first envoy of Islam'", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Pampered aristocrat turned persecuted believer; first envoy to Yathrib, flag-bearer at Badr and Uḥud, and martyr of Uḥud.", "story": "In Jāhiliyyah, Muṣʿab was famous as the most spoilt youth of the Quraysh, doted on by his mother and wearing new garments daily; after Islam, the same parents heartlessly tortured him, and he was equally famous for persevering (Ch. 16). He was among the first emigrants to Abyssinia (Ch. 7). After the First Covenant of ʿAqabah, the Prophet ﷺ sent him as his envoy to Yathrib, where he taught Qur'an, led the prayer, and won forty converts within weeks. His calm daʿwah — 'sit and listen; if you find it agreeable, good, and if not, I will stop' — converted the young chieftains Usayd ibn Huḍayr and Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, after which whole tribes entered Islam; he even oversaw the first Friday prayer in Islam (Ch. 11). He carried the flag at Badr (Ch. 14), where he told his own captured brother's captor to tighten his grip, 'for his mother is wealthy.' At Uḥud he held the Muslim flag until his right arm was severed, then his left, then clasped it between the stumps of his arms until he was killed (Ch. 16). The man once draped in finery left this world with a single garment too short to cover his body in burial. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 403–404) that he first kept his Islam secret, until ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah saw him praying and informed his mother and clansmen, who seized and imprisoned him; he remained a prisoner until he escaped to join the first migration to Abyssinia.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia", "Ch. 11: first envoy to Yathrib; converted Usayd ibn Huḍayr and Saʿd ibn Muʿādh; first Friday prayer", "Ch. 14: flag-bearer at Badr; the exchange with his captive brother Abū ʿAzīz", "Ch. 16: martyred at Uḥud holding the flag with severed arms; buried in a shroud too short for his body"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 403–404, 585–587, 1136–1137"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Mus’ab’s life began to change when he heard that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was inviting people to embrace Islam. Mus’ab ؓ went to him ﷺ in the house of Al-Arqam and ended up embracing Islam; however, fearing mistreatment from his mother and fellow clansmen, Mus’ab, like some other early converts, kept his acceptance of Islam a secret. And so he would secretly visit the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.", "page": 403}, "who_ar": "فَتًى مُتْرَفٌ مِنَ الْأَشْرَافِ صَارَ مُؤْمِنًا مُضْطَهَدًا، وَهُوَ أَوَّلُ سَفِيرٍ إِلَى يَثْرِبَ، وَحَامِلُ اللِّوَاءِ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ وَأُحُدٍ، وَشَهِيدُ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "suhayb-al-rumi", "name": "Ṣuhayb al-Rūmī", "arabic": "صهيب بن سنان الرومي", "kunya_titles": "Abū Yaḥyā; al-Rūmī ('the Roman')", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Iraqi-born former captive of the Byzantines, among the first seven Muslims; ransomed his entire wealth to make hijrah — 'a profitable trade'.", "story": "Ṣuhayb was not actually Roman: of Iraqi descent, he was captured by a Byzantine force as a child and enslaved in Rome, later escaping to Arabia where ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān bought him in Mecca (Ch. 6). Ibn Masʿūd counted him among the first seven Muslims, and though his master did not torture him, he did not stop the likes of Abū Jahl from doing so (Ch. 6). When Ṣuhayb set out for Medina, the Quraysh cornered him on the outskirts. He drew his bow and swore every arrow would find flesh before they touched him — so they demanded his wealth and even his horse instead. He gave up everything and became the only Companion ever to perform hijrah on foot, reaching the Prophet ﷺ at Qubā' dehydrated and crawling. The Prophet ﷺ personally wiped the dust from him, smiling and repeating three times, 'Your trade was profitable, O Ṣuhayb' — knowledge only Jibrīl could have brought. Allah revealed of him, 'And there are those who would sell themselves for Allah's pleasure' (Ch. 6). When Abū Jahl demanded the Prophet ﷺ expel Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, and ʿAmmār, Allah revealed the command never to dismiss those who invoke their Lord morning and evening (Ch. 6). ʿUmar later chose him to lead the prayer during the shūrā after his death.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: among the first seven Muslims; persecuted by Abū Jahl", "Ch. 6: surrendered all his wealth to the Quraysh — the only Companion to make hijrah on foot", "Ch. 6: 'Your trade was profitable, O Ṣuhayb' — Qur'an 2:207 revealed about him", "Ch. 6: named with Bilāl and ʿAmmār in Abū Jahl's demand for expulsion, answered by Revelation"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 38 AH, Medina (some say 39 AH)", "after_sirah": "The dying ʿUmar's choice of Ṣuhayb to lead the prayer during the shūrā was itself an honour with no parallel — and it was Ṣuhayb who led the funeral prayer over ʿUmar himself. He died in Medina and was buried in al-Baqīʿ, around seventy years old.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 625–627", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 38 AH", "who_ar": "وُلِدَ بِالْعِرَاقِ وَسَبَتْهُ الرُّومُ، وَهُوَ مِنَ السَّبْعَةِ الْأَوَائِلِ إِسْلَامًا، افْتَدَى نَفْسَهُ بِمَالِهِ كُلِّهِ لِيُهَاجِرَ، فَقِيلَ: «رَبِحَ الْبَيْعُ»."}, {"id": "uthman-ibn-mazun", "name": "ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn", "arabic": "عثمان بن مظعون", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Sā'ib", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert and ascetic of the first Abyssinia emigration who renounced pagan protection to share his companions' suffering.", "story": "ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn was among the first wave of emigrants to Abyssinia — twelve men and four women of the noble houses of Quraysh (Ch. 7). When false rumours of Quraysh's conversion drew the emigrants home, he secured the protection of his powerful friend al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah and was completely safe. But watching the other Muslims persecuted while he sat untouched consumed him with guilt, and he asked for the protection to be revoked (Ch. 7). Soon after, when he corrected the poet Labīd in a Meccan gathering — affirming that everything besides Allah is naught, but denying that every pleasure must cease, for Paradise never ends — a man punched him and blackened his eye. Al-Walīd offered to reinstate the protection; ʿUthmān refused: 'No! Indeed, my other eye is in need of the blessings incurred by this eye' (Ch. 7). He emigrated again to Medina and witnessed Badr, dying shortly afterward in 2 AH — the first of the Muhājirūn to die in Medina, kissed on the forehead by the weeping Prophet ﷺ, and remembered in the books of sirah as one of the great ascetics of the Companions. His wife Khawlah bint Ḥakīm was herself an early believer.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia", "Ch. 7: renounced al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah's protection out of solidarity with the persecuted", "Ch. 7: struck in the eye over his exchange with the poet Labīd — 'my other eye is in need of these blessings'"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Badr"], "death": "d. 2 AH, Medina — the first of the Muhājirūn to die in Medina", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 413–415, 492", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Although he could do nothing to help them, he nonetheless felt a great deal of anxiety for being in a better situation than they were in; he said to himself, “It is a great defect in myself that I go about safely in the morning and afternoon, under the protection of a man from the people of polytheism, while my companions ؓ and the people of my religion are subjected to hardship and affliction for the cause of Allah.” ‘Uthmaan then went to Al-Waleed ibn Al-Mugheerah and said, “O Abu ‘Abd-Shams (i.e., Al-Waleed), you have fulfilled your obligation, but I now return to you your protection.”", "page": 413}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَزُهَّادِ مُهَاجِرَةِ الْحَبَشَةِ الْأُولَى، خَلَعَ جِوَارَ الْمُشْرِكِينَ لِيُشَارِكَ أَصْحَابَهُ مَا يَلْقَوْنَ مِنَ الْأَذَى."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-jahsh", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh", "arabic": "عبد الله بن جحش", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad; leader of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Cousin of the Prophet ﷺ, early convert and double emigrant; the first appointed commander of a Muslim expedition, martyred at Uḥud.", "story": "A cousin of the Prophet ﷺ through his mother Umaymah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh was among the first emigrants to Abyssinia, listed with the noblest early households of Quraysh (Ch. 7). After the Hijrah, the Prophet ﷺ nominated him to lead eight Muhājirūn on the secret reconnaissance mission of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah, with sealed orders to be opened after two days' travel and explicit instructions not to compel any man. Assuming certain death, ʿAbdullāh announced, 'Whoever of you is eager for martyrdom and wishes to meet Allah soon, let him come with me' — and every man followed (Ch. 14). The party's premature raid in the sacred month grieved the Prophet ﷺ until Allah revealed that expelling worshippers from the Sacred Mosque is a greater sin than fighting in the sacred months (Ch. 14). ʿAbdullāh fought at Badr and was martyred at Uḥud in 3 AH, buried, per the sirah tradition, with his uncle Ḥamzah. His sister Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh, wife of Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, bore the news of Uḥud's losses (Ch. 16); another sister, Zaynab, later married the Prophet ﷺ. His brother ʿUbaydullāh ibn Jaḥsh appears in the book only as one of the four pre-Islamic Ḥunafā' (Ch. 2); standard sirah records that ʿUbaydullāh emigrated to Abyssinia, converted to Christianity there, and died outside Islam — thus not counted among the Companions. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1138–1139) Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ's account of his duʿā' before Uḥud — that he asked Allah for a fierce enemy who would kill him and cut off his nose and ear, so that when asked on the Day of Resurrection he could answer, 'It was for You and for Your Messenger' — and Saʿd saw him at day's end with his nose and ear hanging on a thread.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia", "Ch. 14: appointed leader of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah with sealed orders — 'Whoever is eager for martyrdom, let him come with me'", "Ch. 16: his sister Ḥamnah, wife of Muṣʿab, receives the news of Uḥud"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 886–890, 1138–1139", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Not knowing that it was the first day of one of the four inviolable months, ‘Abdullah ibn Jahsh ؓ and his men attacked the Makkan caravan and killed a man. The Quraish tried to take advantage of the situation by launching a vicious slander campaign against the Muslims.", "page": 888}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمَّةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، سَبَقَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَهَاجَرَ الْهِجْرَتَيْنِ، وَهُوَ أَوَّلُ أَمِيرٍ عُقِدَ لَهُ عَلَى سَرِيَّةٍ فِي الْإِسْلَامِ، اسْتُشْهِدَ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "abu-salamah-ibn-abd-al-asad", "name": "Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad", "arabic": "أبو سلمة بن عبد الأسد", "kunya_titles": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Asad al-Makhzūmī; of 'The People of Two Emigrations'", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Early convert, husband of Umm Salamah; first Muslim to emigrate to Medina, and one of the 'People of Two Emigrations', dying of his Uḥud wounds.", "story": "Abū Salamah and his wife Umm Salamah were among the first to emigrate to Abyssinia, earning the title 'The People of Two Emigrations' (Ch. 7, Ch. 12). When permission came for the Hijrah to Medina, he was the very first to emigrate — and the Quraysh made his family pay for it. They seized Umm Salamah, his own former tribe seized their little son (dislocating the boy's arm in a literal tug-of-war), and for a year the family was torn three ways: Abū Salamah in Medina, Umm Salamah weeping daily at al-Abṭaḥ, and their child with a foreign tribe, until relatives finally interceded and she crossed the desert to rejoin him (Ch. 12). He fought at Badr, then suffered a serious injury at Uḥud, dying of his wounds six months later in Jumādā al-Ākhirah 4 AH (Ch. 16). He had once taught Umm Salamah the Prophet's ﷺ duʿā' for one afflicted by calamity — 'O Allah, reward me in my calamity and grant me better in its place' — and she wondered who could ever be better than Abū Salamah. Allah answered her: she was granted the Prophet ﷺ himself (Ch. 16).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia — 'The People of Two Emigrations'", "Ch. 12: first Muslim to emigrate to Medina; his wife and son seized and the family separated for a year", "Ch. 16: wounded at Uḥud, died of his wounds in 4 AH; his taught duʿā' answered with the Prophet's ﷺ marriage to Umm Salamah"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Badr", "Uḥud"], "death": "4 AH, Medina — died of wounds sustained at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 615–617, 1247"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When Abu Salamah ؓ resolved to go to Al-Madeenah, he put a saddle (and some provisions) on his camel for me, and carried me on top of it. And he put my son, Salamah ibn Abu Salamah ؓ, in my lap. Steering his camel, he then left with me.", "page": 615}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، وَزَوْجُ أُمِّ سَلَمَةَ، وَأَوَّلُ مُسْلِمٍ هَاجَرَ إِلَى الْمَدِينَةِ، وَأَحَدُ «أَصْحَابِ الْهِجْرَتَيْنِ»، مَاتَ مُتَأَثِّرًا بِجِرَاحِ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "asma-bint-abi-bakr", "name": "Asmā' bint Abī Bakr", "arabic": "أسماء بنت أبي بكر", "kunya_titles": "Umm ʿAbdillāh; Dhāt al-Niṭāqayn ('She of the Two Waistbands')", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "major", "who": "Early believer, daughter of Abū Bakr and elder sister of ʿĀ'ishah; quiet heroine of the Hijrah who provisioned the Prophet ﷺ and her father in Cave Thawr.", "story": "Asmā' embraced Islam in the earliest Meccan years as a daughter of the house of Abū Bakr. Her finest hour came at the Hijrah: while the Prophet ﷺ and her father hid in Cave Thawr, the household she helped run kept them supplied — tradition remembers her tearing her waistband in two to tie the provisions, earning her the title Dhāt al-Niṭāqayn. When her blind grandfather Abū Quḥāfah, still a hostile pagan, came to mock Abū Bakr for leaving his family penniless, Asmā' quietly filled a pouch with pebbles, tied it up, and placed it in his hands: 'No, my grandfather. He has left us this.' He felt its weight and recanted — though in truth her father had left them nothing but trust in Allah (Ch. 12). Years later, at the Conquest of Mecca, it was Asmā' who led that same aged grandfather by the hand to the Prophet ﷺ to pledge allegiance and accept Islam (Ch. 25). Wife of Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām and mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, she lived to nearly one hundred, dying in Mecca in 73 AH shortly after her son's death.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 12: the pouch of pebbles for Abū Quḥāfah — shielding her father's honour after he gave everything for the Hijrah", "Ch. 25: brought her grandfather Abū Quḥāfah by the hand to the Prophet ﷺ to accept Islam at the Conquest"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 73 AH, Mecca, aged nearly 100", "after_sirah": "Her last days produced one of the most celebrated counsels in Islamic history: when her son ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, besieged by al-Ḥajjāj, feared his body would be mutilated, she told him a slaughtered sheep feels no pain from the skinning — if he was upon truth, let him die upon it. Blind and bent with age, she died in Mecca within days of taking his body down for burial.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 675, 678", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 73 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Asmaa Dhaat-An-Nitaaqain ؓ: It was she who carried provisions from Makkah to the cave, having to do so in a stealthy and careful manner, in order to avoid being seen by those polytheists who were searching for the Prophet ﷺ in order to kill him.", "page": 675}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقَاتِ إِلَى الْإِيمَانِ، ابْنَةُ أَبِي بَكْرٍ وَأُخْتُ عَائِشَةَ الْكُبْرَى، بَطَلَةُ الْهِجْرَةِ الصَّامِتَةُ الَّتِي حَمَلَتِ الزَّادَ إِلَى النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَأَبِيهَا فِي غَارِ ثَوْرٍ."}, {"id": "abu-dharr-al-ghifari", "name": "Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī", "arabic": "أبو ذر الغفاري", "kunya_titles": "Jundub ibn Junādah; Abū Dharr", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Famed early convert from the tribe of Ghifār, renowned for blunt truthfulness and asceticism.", "story": "Abū Dharr, of the tribe of Ghifār, is counted in standard sirah among the very earliest converts — a seeker of tawḥīd who came to Mecca, embraced Islam, and was sent back to call his people until he rejoined the Prophet ﷺ in Medina. In this book he appears asking the Prophet ﷺ directly about the Ascension: 'O Messenger of Allah, did you see Allah?' — to which he ﷺ replied, 'There was light! How could I see Him?', a foundational narration in Islamic creed (Ch. 10). Famed for his uncompromising honesty and asceticism, he died in the desert at al-Rabadhah in 32 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 10: asked the Prophet ﷺ, 'Did you see Allah?' — 'There was light! How could I see Him?'"], "badges": ["Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 32 AH, al-Rabadhah (some say 31 AH)", "after_sirah": "His fearless preaching against hoarded wealth unsettled first Syria and then Medina, and he withdrew to the desert of al-Rabadhah, dying there with almost nothing — alone, as the Prophet ﷺ had foretold. A passing caravan carrying ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd arrived in time to pray over him and bury him. al-Ṣallābī records the prophecy from the road to Tabūk (p. 1834): 'May Allah have mercy on Abū Dharr: he walks alone, he will die alone, and he will be resurrected alone.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 374–381, 1834–1835", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Even before the advent of Islam, Abu Dharr ؓ refused to worship idols, and would reproach those who did. Three years before the Prophet ﷺ received revelation for the first time, Abu Dharr ؓ began to pray to Allah; however, not having a Shariah (set of laws) to follow, he didn’t pray in any particular direction.", "page": 374}, "who_ar": "مِنْ مَشَاهِيرِ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ مِنْ قَبِيلَةِ غِفَارٍ، اشْتُهِرَ بِصَرَاحَةِ الصِّدْقِ وَالزُّهْدِ."}, {"id": "amir-ibn-fuhayrah", "name": "ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah", "arabic": "عامر بن فهيرة", "kunya_titles": "Freed slave of Abū Bakr", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Abū Bakr's freed slave and shepherd; unsung logistician of the Hijrah, later martyred at Bi'r Maʿūnah.", "story": "An early convert who was tortured for his Islam before Abū Bakr freed him, ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah felt a deep loyalty to his liberator. During the three days the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr hid in Cave Thawr, his task was quietly vital: as a shepherd, he would trail his flock over the footprints of ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr each morning, erasing every trace of the supply runs without arousing suspicion (Ch. 12). Standard sirah records that he witnessed Badr and Uḥud and was martyred at the massacre of Bi'r Maʿūnah in 4 AH (the massacre is narrated in Ch. 17), where his killer, astonished at his death, later embraced Islam. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1242) that ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl, chieftain of the attackers, told how after ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah was slain he saw his body being raised up between the sky and the earth.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 12: erased the footprints of ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr with his flock during the days of Cave Thawr"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "4 AH — martyred at Bi'r Maʿūnah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 675, 1242", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Aamir ibn Fuhairah ؓ: He was a simple and trustworthy shepherd who would give meat and milk to the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr ؓ while they were in the cave; he also contributed to the Prophet’s mission by erasing his and Abu Bakr’s footprints, which would otherwise have been noticed by those who were searching for them.", "page": 675}, "who_ar": "مَوْلَى أَبِي بَكْرٍ وَرَاعِي غَنَمِهِ، وَصَاحِبُ الْيَدِ الْخَفِيَّةِ فِي تَدْبِيرِ الْهِجْرَةِ، اسْتُشْهِدَ بَعْدَ ذَلِكَ فِي بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ."}, {"id": "utbah-ibn-ghazwan", "name": "ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān", "arabic": "عتبة بن غزوان", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; later founder of Basra", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early Meccan convert of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah; later the founding governor of Basra.", "story": "An early convert and emigrant, ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān was one of the eight Muhājirūn of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah. When one of the party's camels escaped in the night, he and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ remained in the desert searching while the others went on; the Prophet ﷺ later refused to release the Quraysh prisoners until the two returned safely — such was his ﷺ care for them (Ch. 14). He fought at Badr and, in the caliphate of ʿUmar, founded and governed the garrison city of Basra, famously reminding its people that he had once been the seventh of seven with the Prophet ﷺ with nothing to eat but leaves.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: one of the eight of Sariyyah al-Nakhlah; the Prophet ﷺ withheld the prisoner release until he and Saʿd returned safely"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 17 AH, en route from Basra (some say 15 AH)", "after_sirah": "Under ʿUmar he marked out and founded the garrison city of Basra and served as its first governor — then travelled to ʿUmar begging to be relieved of office. Refused, he prayed not to be returned to it, and died on the road, aged fifty-seven.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 888–890", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 17 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Two men from the unit, Sa’d ibn Abee Waqqaas ؓ and ‘Utbah ibn Ghazwaan ؓ, did not return with the others, for they were busy searching for a stray camel. Then when the Quraish came seeking to ransom the freedom of the two captives, the Prophet ﷺ refused, saying, “I fear that you have attacked Sa’d ibn Maalik (i.e., Sa’d ibn Abee Waqqaas) and ‘Utbah ibn Ghazwaan.” The Prophet ﷺ refused to free the two prisoners, regardless of how much ransom money was being offered, until Sa’d and ‘Utbah returned safely to Al-Madeenah.", "page": 890}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ بِمَكَّةَ، شَهِدَ سَرِيَّةَ نَخْلَةَ، ثُمَّ كَانَ مُؤَسِّسَ الْبَصْرَةِ وَأَوَّلَ وُلَاتِهَا."}, {"id": "amir-ibn-rabiah", "name": "ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah", "arabic": "عامر بن ربيعة", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; ḥalīf (ally) of the family of al-Khaṭṭāb", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early convert and husband of Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah; among the first emigrants to Abyssinia.", "story": "ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, an ally of the family of al-Khaṭṭāb, embraced Islam in its first years and is named in the book among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia alongside his wife Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah (Ch. 7). It was to him that Laylā excitedly related ʿUmar's softened words as they packed for the second emigration — and he scoffed, 'By Allah, his father's donkey will accept Islam before he does!' (Ch. 8), a prediction Allah delightfully overturned. Standard sirah counts him among the few who made both emigrations to Abyssinia and the Hijrah to Medina, and a participant at Badr and the battles that followed. al-Ṣallābī records (p. 615) that in the Hijrah to Medina he was the very next Companion to arrive after Abū Salamah, his wife Laylā with him.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia with his wife Laylā", "Ch. 8: dismissed the possibility of ʿUmar's conversion — 'his father's donkey will accept Islam before he does!'"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Badr"], "death": "d. c. 35 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He held himself apart from the fitna that engulfed ʿUthmān's last days: it is related that he stood in prayer through those nights and died in Medina only days after ʿUthmān's assassination.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 492–493, 615", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 35 AH", "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، وَزَوْجُ لَيْلَى بِنْتِ أَبِي حَثْمَةَ، وَمِنْ أَوَائِلِ الْمُهَاجِرِينَ إِلَى الْحَبَشَةِ."}, {"id": "amr-ibn-abasa", "name": "ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasa", "arabic": "عمرو بن عبسة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Najīḥ al-Sulamī", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "The first non-Meccan convert to Islam — a Yemeni Arab who sought out the Prophet ﷺ before the daʿwah was even public.", "story": "ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasa always knew idolatry was wrong, and when news reached him of someone in Mecca preaching the same, he travelled all the way from Yemen to find him. Meeting the Prophet ﷺ before his mission was publicly disclosed, he asked, 'What are you?' and heard, 'I am a Messenger' — sent, the Prophet ﷺ explained, to uphold the ties of kinship and break the idols so that Allah alone is worshipped (Ch. 6). He believed, becoming the first non-Meccan convert, but the Prophet ﷺ sent him home for his own safety: 'Return home, and when news reaches you that I am victorious, come back.' Over fifteen years later, ʿAmr delivered on his promise and rejoined the Prophet ﷺ in Medina (Ch. 6). He later settled in Syria, a transmitter of precious hadith on the virtues of wuḍū' and prayer.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: travelled from Yemen to Mecca and became the first non-Meccan convert", "Ch. 6: told to return home until Islam prevailed — and rejoined the Prophet ﷺ in Medina over 15 years later"], "badges": [], "death": "d. in Ḥimṣ, Syria, in the era of the early caliphate (no precise year recorded)", "after_sirah": "He settled among the people of Syria at Ḥimṣ, where Companions and Successors sought him out for the precious ḥadīth he carried on wuḍūʾ, prayer, and the blessed hours of the night — including his famous long narration of his own conversion.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 369–372", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Here, as related in Saheeh Muslim, is ‘Amr ibn ‘Abasah’s story in his own words: “During the days of ignorance (i.e., pre-Islamic days), I would think that people were upon misguidance and that, since they worshipped idols, they were upon nothing (that is correct). Then I heard about a man in Makkah, who was informing (people) about much (significant) news. I sat on my mount and went to him.”", "page": 369}, "who_ar": "أَوَّلُ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ مِنْ غَيْرِ أَهْلِ مَكَّةَ، رَجُلٌ مِنْ عَرَبِ الْيَمَنِ قَصَدَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ قَبْلَ أَنْ يَجْهَرَ بِالدَّعْوَةِ."}, {"id": "nuaym-ibn-abdillah-al-nahham", "name": "Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Naḥḥām", "arabic": "نعيم بن عبد الله النحام", "kunya_titles": "al-Naḥḥām", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Secret early convert who intercepted ʿUmar's assassination march and redirected him — into Islam.", "story": "Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Naḥḥām had recently accepted Islam but not yet publicised it when he saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb striding through Mecca with an unsheathed sword, bound for the Prophet ﷺ to claim Abū Jahl's bounty. Thinking fast to protect the Prophet ﷺ, Nuʿaym challenged him — 'Do you really think the Banū ʿAbd Manāf will let you live after this? If you really want to do something, go and fix your own family!' — revealing that ʿUmar's own sister and brother-in-law had accepted Islam (Ch. 8). That redirection set in motion the events of ʿUmar's conversion, one of the great watershed moments of the sirah. Standard sirah adds that he was called al-Naḥḥām because the Prophet ﷺ heard his gentle cough (naḥmah) in Paradise, that he stayed in Mecca feeding the widows of Banū ʿAdī, and that he was martyred in the Syrian campaigns. al-Ṣallābī notes (p. 167) that in the phase of secret preaching he belonged to the same clandestine study circle as Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb and Saʿīd ibn Zayd, with Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt as their appointed teacher.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: intercepted ʿUmar's assassination march and diverted him to his sister's house — the road to ʿUmar's Islam"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "13 AH — martyred at Ajnādayn (some say al-Yarmūk)", "after_sirah": "His own clan of Banū ʿAdī held him back from hijrah because none but he fed their widows and orphans, and he emigrated only around the time of Ḥudaybiyyah — greeted by the Prophet ﷺ with the words that his people were better to him than ʿUmar's had been to ʿUmar. He fell in the Syrian campaigns.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 161, 167", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 13 AH", "who_ar": "أَسْلَمَ قَدِيمًا وَكَتَمَ إِسْلَامَهُ، وَاعْتَرَضَ عُمَرَ فِي مَسِيرِهِ لِقَتْلِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ فَحَوَّلَ وِجْهَتَهُ — إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "fatimah-bint-al-khattab", "name": "Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb", "arabic": "فاطمة بنت الخطاب", "kunya_titles": "Umm Jamīl; sister of ʿUmar al-Fārūq", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early convert whose courage and bleeding lip broke her brother ʿUmar's rage and opened his heart to Islam.", "story": "Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb and her husband Saʿīd ibn Zayd (see the Ten Promised Paradise) accepted Islam early and were being taught Qur'an in their home by Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt when her enraged brother ʿUmar banged on the door (Ch. 8). She hid the parchment under her garment, but when ʿUmar moved to strike Saʿīd, Fāṭimah stepped in and took the blow, blood gushing from her lip. She and her husband then declared defiantly, 'Do as you please! We have indeed accepted Islam.' Her courage — and her insistence that only the purified touch the pages, sending ʿUmar to wash before reading Sūrah Ṭā-Hā — turned his rage to reverence, and Islam wholly entered his heart (Ch. 8). Few Companions can claim so direct a hand in the conversion of ʿUmar al-Fārūq. al-Ṣallābī notes (pp. 166–167) that this home gathering was one of the organized secret study circles of the early daʿwah — she, Saʿīd, and Nuʿaym al-Naḥḥām, with Khabbāb as their appointed teacher — concerned not merely with reciting the Qur'an but with understanding and applying it.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: struck and bloodied defending her husband; declared her Islam defiantly to ʿUmar", "Ch. 8: made ʿUmar purify himself before reading Sūrah Ṭā-Hā — the turning point of his conversion"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 166–167"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Faatimah bint Al-Khattaab ؓ and her husband, Sa’eed ibn Zaid, were in the same group as Nu’aim ibn ‘Abdullah An-Nahhaam ibn ‘Adee. And their appointed teacher was Khabbaab ibn Al-Arat ؓ. When they met, their main concern was not simply to learn how to recite the Qur’an, but also to actually study the Qur’an, understand its Verses, learn its commands and prohibitions, and apply its teachings.", "page": 166}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقَاتِ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ، كَسَرَتْ شَجَاعَتُهَا وَشَفَتُهَا الدَّامِيَةُ غَضَبَ أَخِيهَا عُمَرَ وَفَتَحَتْ قَلْبَهُ لِلْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "khalid-ibn-said-ibn-al-as", "name": "Khālid ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "arabic": "خالد بن سعيد بن العاص", "kunya_titles": "Abū Saʿīd al-Umawī", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Umayyad nobleman among the very earliest converts; long-term emigrant in Abyssinia and later the Prophet's ﷺ mediator with Thaqīf.", "story": "Khālid ibn Saʿīd, son of a leading Umayyad chief, is counted in standard sirah among the first handful of converts — moved, it is related, by a dream of his father pushing him toward a fire from which the Prophet ﷺ pulled him back. He endured his own father's fury, emigrated to Abyssinia, and remained there until the return to Medina at Khaybar. In this book he appears in the Year of Delegations: having ties to both the Quraysh and the Thaqīf, he was chosen as mediator in the negotiations with Thaqīf's delegation, who trusted him so little was their own conscience clean that they insisted he taste every meal with them against poisoning (Ch. 28). He was martyred in the Syrian campaigns that Abū Bakr dispatched — at Marj al-Ṣuffar in 14 AH, or, as some report, at Ajnādayn. al-Ṣallābī adds that the treaty with Thaqīf was written down in Khālid's own hand (p. 1902), and that the Prophet ﷺ later appointed him governor over the region between Najrān, Zamaʿ, and Zabīd in Yemen (p. 1939).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: mediator between the Prophet ﷺ and the delegation of Thaqīf, eating with them at every meal"], "badges": ["Abyssinia", "Martyr"], "death": "14 AH — martyred at Marj al-Ṣuffar (some say Ajnādayn, 13 AH)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1901–1902, 1939", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 14 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Khaalid ibn Sa’eed ibn Al-‘Aas, yet another member of the delegation, was charged with the task of going back and forth as a negotiator between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Thaqeef delegation. He continued to carry out this task until the treaty was written down in its entirety.", "page": 1901}, "who_ar": "شَرِيفٌ مِنْ بَنِي أُمَيَّةَ، مِنْ أَوَائِلِ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ، طَالَتْ هِجْرَتُهُ بِالْحَبَشَةِ، ثُمَّ كَانَ وَسِيطَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مَعَ ثَقِيفٍ."}, {"id": "salamah-ibn-hisham", "name": "Salamah ibn Hishām", "arabic": "سلمة بن هشام", "kunya_titles": "al-Makhzūmī; brother of Abū Jahl", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early convert and brother of Abū Jahl, tortured and detained in Mecca; defended by the Prophet ﷺ after Mu'tah — 'not those who flee, but those who regroup'.", "story": "Salamah ibn Hishām al-Makhzūmī, a brother of Abū Jahl, embraced Islam early and paid dearly for it: standard sirah records that his own clan tortured and imprisoned him in Mecca, preventing his hijrah, and that the Prophet ﷺ would supplicate for his rescue in the qunūt alongside ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah and al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd. In this book he appears after the Battle of Mu'tah, when returning soldiers were mocked as deserters: noticing his absence from the mosque, the Prophet ﷺ asked his wife about him, learned he was hiding from the taunts of 'O you who fled!', and publicly declared, 'They are not those who flee (furrār), they are those who regroup to fight again (kurrār)' (Ch. 24). He was martyred fighting in Syria.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 24: shamed after Mu'tah until the Prophet ﷺ declared the army 'kurrār' — those who regroup to fight again"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "14 AH — martyred at Marj al-Ṣuffar (some say Ajnādayn)", "after_sirah": "Freed at last from his Meccan imprisonment — for which the Prophet ﷺ had supplicated in the qunūt by name — he joined the later campaigns and fell fighting in Syria at Marj al-Ṣuffar in 14 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 624, 1659", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 14 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After he migrated to Al-Madeenah, the Prophet ﷺ would make a special supplication during prayer for weak Muslims who were forced to remain in Makkah. Abu Hurairah ؓ reported that, when the Prophet ﷺ would raise his head from the bowing position of the final unit of prayer, he ﷺ would say, “O Allah, save ‘Ayyaash ibn Abee Rabee’ah ؓ; O Allah save Salamah ibn Hishaam ؓ; O Allah, save Al-Waleed ibn Al-Waleed; O Allah, save those believers who are weak.”", "page": 624}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَأَخُو أَبِي جَهْلٍ، عُذِّبَ وَحُبِسَ بِمَكَّةَ، وَدَافَعَ عَنْهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ بَعْدَ مُؤْتَةَ فَقَالَ: «لَيْسُوا بِالْفُرَّارِ، بَلْ هُمُ الْكُرَّارُ»."}, {"id": "sahlah-bint-suhayl", "name": "Sahlah bint Suhayl", "arabic": "سهلة بنت سهيل", "kunya_titles": "Wife of Abū Ḥudhayfah ibn ʿUtbah", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early believing woman of Quraysh, among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia.", "story": "Sahlah bint Suhayl, daughter of the Qurashī orator Suhayl ibn ʿAmr (then still a staunch pagan), embraced Islam early alongside her husband Abū Ḥudhayfah ibn ʿUtbah. The book names her among the first wave of emigrants to Abyssinia — twelve men and four women of noble Quraysh houses who gave up home and wealth overnight for the safety of their faith (Ch. 7). In Abyssinia she gave birth to her son Muhammad ibn Abī Ḥudhayfah, and she later made the second hijrah to Medina, a woman of the rare 'two emigrations'.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: named among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "Death date not recorded in the classical sources", "after_sirah": "Widowed when Abū Ḥudhayfah fell as a martyr at Yamāmah in the Apostasy Wars, she lived on in Medina as the narrator of the well-known ruling she herself had asked the Prophet ﷺ about, concerning Sālim, the freed client of her household.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 493", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Sahlah bint (daughter of) Suhail ibn ‘Amr ؓ, one of the children of ‘Aamir ibn Luai. Sahlah ؓ was travelling with her husband, Abu Hudhaifah ؓ; while in Abyssinia, she gave birth to his son, Muhammad ibn Abu Hudhaifah ؓ.", "page": 493}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقَاتِ إِلَى الْإِيمَانِ مِنْ نِسَاءِ قُرَيْشٍ، وَإِحْدَى الْمُهَاجِرِينَ السِّتَّةَ عَشَرَ الْأَوَّلِينَ إِلَى الْحَبَشَةِ."}, {"id": "layla-bint-abi-hathmah", "name": "Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah", "arabic": "ليلى بنت أبي حثمة", "kunya_titles": "Wife of ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early believing woman of the first Abyssinia emigration whose frank words softened ʿUmar's heart.", "story": "Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah was among the first sixteen emigrants to Abyssinia with her husband ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah (Ch. 7) — by some accounts the first woman to set out for that land. As she begrudgingly packed for the second emigration, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb asked why, and she answered with fearless frustration: 'It is because of you and your animosity that we are leaving our land and belongings!' Taken aback, ʿUmar said, 'Has it really reached this level?' and prayed, 'May Allah grant you protection' — one of the first cracks in his hard exterior before his conversion (Ch. 8). Her husband scoffed at the idea that ʿUmar could ever believe; Allah proved otherwise.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: among the first emigrants to Abyssinia", "Ch. 8: her rebuke drew ʿUmar's gentle duʿā' — an early sign of his softening toward Islam"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "Death date not recorded in the classical sources", "after_sirah": "She completed both emigrations with her husband, and some sources honour her further as the first woman of this Ummah to enter Medina as an emigrant. al-Ṣallābī states it plainly (p. 615): after Abū Salamah, the next to reach Medina was ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, and with him his wife Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah, 'the first woman-traveler to reach Al-Madeenah.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 493, 615", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "The first person from the Messenger of Allah’s Companions ؓ to reach Al-Madeenah was Abu Salamah ibn ‘Abdul-Asad ؓ; next to arrive after him was ‘Aamir ibn Rabee’ah ؓ, and with him was his wife, Laylaa bint Abee Hathmah ؓ, the first woman-traveler to reach Al-Madeenah. Thereafter, one group followed by another, the Messenger of Allah’s Companions ؓ arrived in Al-Madeenah, and they stayed with the Ansaar (the native inhabitants of Al-Madeenah from the Aus and Khazraj tribes) in their homes.", "page": 615}, "who_ar": "مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنَاتِ الْأَوَائِلِ وَمِنْ مُهَاجِرَاتِ الْحَبَشَةِ الْأُولَى، أَلَانَتْ كَلِمَتُهَا الصَّرِيحَةُ قَلْبَ عُمَرَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-hudhafah", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī", "arabic": "عبد الله بن حذافة السهمي", "kunya_titles": "Abū Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early convert and Abyssinia emigrant; the envoy who carried the Prophet's ﷺ letter to Khosrow of Persia.", "story": "An early Meccan convert of the Banū Sahm who, per standard sirah, emigrated in the second migration to Abyssinia, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah is remembered in this book for a singular honour: the Prophet ﷺ chose him to carry his letter calling Khosrow, Emperor of Persia, to Islam, sent via the ruler of Bahrain (Ch. 22). Khosrow arrogantly tore the letter to pieces, and when the news reached the Prophet ﷺ he declared, 'Allah will thoroughly tear up his kingdom' — a prophecy fulfilled as Khosrow was killed by his own son and the Sassanid realm unravelled (Ch. 22). Later sources celebrate ʿAbdullāh's boldness as a Roman captive whose defiance won the release of Muslim prisoners. He died in Egypt during ʿUthmān's caliphate. al-Ṣallābī dates the mission (p. 1617) to Muḥarram of 7 AH, citing Zād al-Maʿād.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 22: carried the Prophet's ﷺ letter to Khosrow, who tore it — 'Allah will thoroughly tear up his kingdom'"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "d. c. 33 AH, Egypt", "after_sirah": "Captured by the Romans in ʿUmar's era, he refused every inducement and threat — even the cauldron of boiling oil — and won the release of the Muslim captives by his boldness; ʿUmar declared it a duty upon every Muslim to kiss ʿAbdullāh's head, and rose to do so first. He died in Egypt during ʿUthmān's caliphate and was buried there.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1617", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 33 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ sent ‘Abdullah ibn Hudhaafah As-Sahmee ؓ with a letter to Kisra, the ruler of the Persian Empire. It is mentioned in Zaad Al-Ma’aad that this occurred in Muharram of the year 7 H. The Prophet ﷺ ordered ‘Abdullah ؓ to give the letter to the ruler of Bahrain, who in turn would pass it on to Kisra. Finally, when the letter reached Kisra, he tore it up.", "page": 1617}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَمُهَاجِرِي الْحَبَشَةِ، وَهُوَ الرَّسُولُ الَّذِي حَمَلَ كِتَابَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ إِلَى كِسْرَى مَلِكِ فَارِسَ."}, {"id": "umm-ruman", "name": "Umm Rūmān", "arabic": "أم رومان", "kunya_titles": "Wife of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq; mother of ʿĀ'ishah", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early believing woman of the household of Abū Bakr; mother of ʿĀ'ishah and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.", "story": "Umm Rūmān embraced Islam in the earliest days as the wife of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, making hers one of the first fully believing households in Mecca. The book shows her rejoicing with Abū Bakr when Khawlah bint Ḥakīm brought the Prophet's ﷺ proposal for their daughter ʿĀ'ishah (Ch. 9). She emigrated to Medina with the family of Abū Bakr and died there around 6 AH; a well-known (though debated) report has the Prophet ﷺ say that whoever wishes to look upon a maiden of Paradise should look at Umm Rūmān.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: with Abū Bakr, joyfully received the proposal of the Prophet ﷺ for ʿĀ'ishah"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 6 AH, Medina (though some evidence suggests she lived on into the caliphal era)", "beyond_sources": ["Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 6 AH", "who_ar": "مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنَاتِ الْأَوَائِلِ مِنْ أَهْلِ بَيْتِ أَبِي بَكْرٍ، وَأُمُّ عَائِشَةَ وَعَبْدِ الرَّحْمَنِ."}, {"id": "khawlah-bint-hakim", "name": "Khawlah bint Ḥakīm", "arabic": "خولة بنت حكيم", "kunya_titles": "Wife of ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early believing woman who, after Khadījah's death, proposed the marriages of Sawdah and ʿĀ'ishah to the Prophet ﷺ.", "story": "Khawlah bint Ḥakīm, an early convert and the wife of the ascetic ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, showed a tender perceptiveness after the death of Khadījah. Seeing the Prophet ﷺ withdrawn in grief while caring for three daughters, she gently asked whether she might make a proposal: 'If you want an unmarried lady, then ʿĀ'ishah is ideal, and if you want a widowed lady, then Sawdah is ideal' (Ch. 9). With his ﷺ permission she approached Sawdah bint Zamʿah and her family, and also Abū Bakr and Umm Rūmān for ʿĀ'ishah — quietly midwifing two of the most consequential marriages in Islamic history (Ch. 9).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: proposed both Sawdah and ʿĀ'ishah to the grieving Prophet ﷺ and carried the proposals to both families"], "badges": [], "death": "", "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقَاتِ إِلَى الْإِيمَانِ، أَشَارَتْ عَلَى النَّبِيِّ ﷺ بَعْدَ وَفَاةِ خَدِيجَةَ بِخِطْبَةِ سَوْدَةَ وَعَائِشَةَ."}, {"id": "asma-bint-umays", "name": "Asmā' bint ʿUmays", "arabic": "أسماء بنت عميس", "kunya_titles": "Umm ʿAbdillāh; of 'the people of two hijrahs'", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early convert and wife of Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib; emigrant to Abyssinia who defended the double-emigrants' honour before the Prophet ﷺ.", "story": "Asmā' bint ʿUmays embraced Islam before the Muslims entered Dār al-Arqam, and emigrated with her husband Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib to Abyssinia, where she remained until the return at Khaybar. When ʿUmar teased her that the Medinan emigrants had more right to the Prophet ﷺ, she shot back that they had enjoyed his ﷺ company for years while the Abyssinia party suffered in a strange land — and vowed not to eat or drink until she asked him ﷺ herself. He ruled in her favour: 'He made one hijrah and you made two!' — and the emigrants flocked to her to hear it repeated (Ch. 21). Widowed at Mu'tah, she later married Abū Bakr — giving birth to Muhammad ibn Abī Bakr on the road to the Farewell Hajj (Ch. 29) — and after him, ʿAlī.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: her retort to ʿUmar and the Prophet's ﷺ verdict — 'he made one hijrah and you made two!'", "Ch. 29: gave birth to Muhammad ibn Abī Bakr en route to the Farewell Hajj"], "badges": ["Abyssinia"], "death": "d. after 40 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "At Abū Bakr's death she washed his body herself, in fulfilment of his bequest, and in ʿAlī's household she lived to grieve her son Muhammad ibn Abī Bakr, killed in Egypt. The Companions knew her for her knowledge — and for her gift of interpreting dreams. She outlived ʿAlī, dying after 40 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 514, 1593, 1661–1662", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. 40 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When the Prophet ﷺ came, Asmaa ؓ said, ‘O Prophet of Allah, verily ‘Umar says such and such (she mentioned what he had said).’ The Prophet ﷺ asked, ‘And what did you say to him?’ She ؓ said, ‘I said such and such to him.’ The Prophet ﷺ said, ‘He has no greater right to me than all of you do. He and his Companions ؓ have a single migration (that they performed); but as for you, the people of the boat, you have two migrations (that you have performed).’", "page": 514}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقَاتِ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَزَوْجَةُ جَعْفَرِ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ، هَاجَرَتْ إِلَى الْحَبَشَةِ، وَذَبَّتْ عَنْ شَرَفِ أَصْحَابِ الْهِجْرَتَيْنِ عِنْدَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-umar", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar", "arabic": "عبد الله بن عمر", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; Ibn ʿUmar", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Son of ʿUmar al-Fārūq, a Muslim from boyhood in Mecca; great narrator of hadith and exemplar of scrupulous adherence to the Sunnah.", "story": "Ibn ʿUmar accepted Islam as a young boy in Mecca with his father and emigrated to Medina. He eagerly presented himself for Badr and again for Uḥud, but was sent back both times for being under fifteen (Ch. 14, Ch. 16), first fighting at the Trench. His narrations thread through the book: 'We never ate to our fill until after Khaybar' (Ch. 21); his testimony of Jaʿfar's fifty-plus frontal wounds at Mu'tah (Ch. 24); and his reflection that the Companions called it the Farewell Hajj without grasping the farewell (Ch. 29). At the Banū Jadhīmah incident he showed his father's moral steel, flatly refusing Khālid ibn al-Walīd's unlawful order to kill those who had surrendered (Ch. 25). He became one of the most prolific hadith narrators and the model of exacting fidelity to the Prophet's ﷺ example, dying in Mecca in 73 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: turned back from Badr for being under fifteen", "Ch. 16: turned back again at Uḥud among the adolescents", "Ch. 25: refused Khālid ibn al-Walīd's unlawful order at Banū Jadhīmah", "Ch. 29: 'We called it the Farewell Hajj... but we did not understand the implication'"], "badges": ["Khandaq", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 73 AH, Mecca (some say 74 AH)", "after_sirah": "For sixty years after the Prophet ﷺ he was the living pattern of the Sunnah, refusing every part in the civil wars and giving fatwa and hadith to the Ummah instead. He died in Mecca past the age of eighty, after an injury from a spearhead in the crowd — instigated, it is related, by al-Ḥajjāj, whom he had rebuked.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 949, 1111–1112, 1716", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 73 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "On these grounds, the Prophet ﷺ sent back Al-Baraa ibn ‘Aazib and ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar, who were both too young to fight. They had originally left with the Muslim contingent with the sincere intention of fighting alongside their Muslim brothers.", "page": 949}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عُمَرَ الْفَارُوقِ، أَسْلَمَ صَغِيرًا بِمَكَّةَ، وَهُوَ مِنْ كِبَارِ رُوَاةِ الْحَدِيثِ، وَالْقُدْوَةُ فِي شِدَّةِ التَّمَسُّكِ بِالسُّنَّةِ."}, {"id": "waraqah-ibn-nawfal", "name": "Waraqah ibn Nawfal", "arabic": "ورقة بن نوفل", "kunya_titles": "Cousin of Khadījah; the learned ḥanīf-turned-Christian scholar of Mecca", "group": "early-mecca", "tier": "minor", "who": "Khadījah's elderly cousin who affirmed the Prophet's ﷺ mission at the very first revelation.", "story": "An old, blind scholar who had abandoned idolatry and studied the earlier scriptures, Waraqah was the first person outside the Prophet's ﷺ home to be consulted after the encounter in Cave Ḥirā'. When Khadījah brought her trembling husband to him, Waraqah declared that what had come to him was the same Nāmūs (Jibrīl) who had come to Mūsā, and that his people would expel him — 'Never has a man brought what you have brought except that he was opposed.' He wished he could be young and strong to support the Prophet ﷺ when that day came (Ch. 5), but he died shortly afterwards, before the public call began. Because he believed in the Prophet ﷺ at the dawn of revelation, many scholars count him among the very first believers of this Ummah. al-Ṣallābī records (p. 145) the Prophet's ﷺ own testimony for him: 'Do not curse Waraqah, for I indeed saw that he had a garden or two gardens (in Paradise)' — and, in another narration, that he ﷺ saw him in white garments.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 5: affirmed the first revelation and foretold the expulsion from Mecca"], "badges": [], "death": "Died in Mecca shortly after the first revelation", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 131, 145–146"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "After the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished telling him what he had seen, Waraqah said, ‘He is An-Naamoos (i.e., Jibreel), whom Allah had sent down to Moosa. I wish that I were a strong, young man! I wish to be alive when your people expel you (from your land).’", "page": 131}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمِّ خَدِيجَةَ الشَّيْخُ الْكَبِيرُ، صَدَّقَ بِرِسَالَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ عِنْدَ أَوَّلِ الْوَحْيِ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "1 AH — died upon the road of Hijrah", "tier": "minor", "id": "junda-ibn-damrah", "name": "Jundaʿ ibn Ḍamrah", "arabic": "جندع بن ضمرة", "group": "early-mecca", "who": "The frail elder who set out for the Hijrah so as not to die having failed to emigrate.", "story": "When the command to emigrate came, Jundaʿ ibn Ḍamrah was an old, sick Muslim in Mecca with no strength to travel — yet he ordered his sons to carry him out on a bed, saying he would not die in the city of the oppressors. He passed away on the road before reaching Medina, and concerning him was revealed the verse that whoever leaves his home emigrating to Allah and His Messenger and is then overtaken by death — 'his reward has already been secured with Allah' (Ch. 12). al-Ṣallābī, who records his name as Ḍamrah ibn Jundub (p. 723), quotes his words to his sons: 'Carry me, for I am not one of the weak, and I am able to find my way (to Medina). And verily, I will not spend this night in Makkah.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 12: died on the road of Hijrah; the verse of the emigrant's secured reward"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 723"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 1 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Despite the fact that Damrah was old and weak, he ؓ said to his sons, “Carry me, for I am not one of the weak, and I am able to find my way (to Al-Madeenah). And verily, I will not spend this night in Makkah.” They carried him on a bier of sorts and headed with him in the direction of Al-Madeenah. In spite of his good intentions, Damrah ؓ was not to complete his journey.", "page": 723}, "who_ar": "الشَّيْخُ الْعَلِيلُ الَّذِي خَرَجَ مُهَاجِرًا لِئَلَّا يَمُوتَ وَقَدْ فَاتَتْهُ الْهِجْرَةُ."}, {"id": "saad-ibn-muadh", "name": "Saʿd ibn Muʿādh", "arabic": "سعد بن معاذ", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAmr; leader of the Aws; 'the man at whose death the Throne shook'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Chief of the Aws and the greatest leader of the Anṣār, at whose death the Throne of the Most Merciful shook.", "story": "Saʿd was one of the two up-and-coming leaders of Yathrib whose conversion changed history. When Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr began preaching in the house of Saʿd's cousin Asʿad ibn Zurārah, Saʿd first sent Usayd ibn Huḍayr to expel him, then marched in himself — only to be won over by Sūrah al-Zukhruf and embrace Islam on the spot, after which his entire tribe, the Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, followed (Ch. 11). Before Badr, when the Prophet ﷺ delicately probed the Anṣār's commitment, Saʿd stood and declared, 'Were you to take us to the ocean and charge, galloping into the sea, we would go right behind you' — words that lit the Prophet's ﷺ face like a shining moon; he then led the Anṣār on the right flank (Ch. 14). At the Trench he was struck by an arrow from Ḥibbān ibn al-ʿAriqah, and from his deathbed delivered the judgement on the treacherous Banū Qurayẓah, which the Prophet ﷺ declared to be 'the verdict of Allah from above the Seven Heavens'; the Prophet ﷺ had told the Aws, 'Stand up to greet your leader' (Ch. 19). At his death the Prophet ﷺ said, 'The Throne of the Most Gracious shook at the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh,' and later remarked that Saʿd's handkerchief in Paradise was more luxurious than a magnificent Roman robe (Ch. 19, 27). al-Ṣallābī, who devotes a virtues section to him (pp. 1408–1411), adds that the Prophet ﷺ hurried to his deathbed so fast that the Companions' robes slipped and their sandals broke, that seventy thousand angels who had never before descended attended his funeral, and that Saʿd was only thirty-seven years old when he died.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: converted by Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr; his tribe the Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal embraced Islam en masse", "Ch. 14: his ringing pledge of loyalty before Badr; led the Anṣār on the right flank", "Ch. 18: defended the Prophet ﷺ during the slander affair", "Ch. 19: wounded at the Trench; judged the Banū Qurayẓah; 'Stand up to greet your leader'", "Ch. 19: 'The Throne of the Most Gracious shook at the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh'", "Ch. 27: the Prophet ﷺ said Saʿd's handkerchief in Paradise surpasses a Roman robe"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq", "Martyr"], "death": "5 AH — died of his Khandaq wound after judging Qurayẓah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 587–590, 954–955, 1392, 1408–1411"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 5 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "One such subtribe was called Banu ‘Abdul-Ashhal, and it was headed by two chieftains: Sa’d ibn Mu’aadh and Usaid ibn Hudair. When Sa’d and Usaid, who were still polytheists, heard of Mus’ab ibn ‘Umair ؓ and his Da’wah activities, Sa’d said to Usaid, “May you have no father! Go to these two men (referring to Mus’ab and As’ad ibn Zuraarah) who have come to our homes to make fools of the weak ones among us.”", "page": 587}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ الْأَوْسِ وَأَعْظَمُ سَادَاتِ الْأَنْصَارِ، اهْتَزَّ لِمَوْتِهِ عَرْشُ الرَّحْمَنِ."}, {"id": "saad-ibn-ubadah", "name": "Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah", "arabic": "سعد بن عبادة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Thābit; chief of the Khazraj; famed for legendary generosity", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Chief of the Khazraj and, after Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's death, the most senior leader of all the Anṣār.", "story": "A young leader of the Khazraj who rose to become the foremost chieftain of the Anṣār. During the slander against ʿĀʾishah he immediately stood in the heated tribal exchange, and ʿĀʾishah herself excused him, saying, 'Saʿd was a righteous man' (Ch. 18). At the Trench he was among the trusted circle — with Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, and Khawwāt ibn Jubayr — sent to verify the Banū Qurayẓah's treachery, and he wisely held back the enraged Saʿd ibn Muʿādh from cursing them: 'Leave off cursing them' (Ch. 19). At the Conquest of Mecca he led the Helpers in the triumphant entry alongside Khālid ibn al-Walīd's column (Ch. 25). His finest hour came after Ḥunayn: when young Anṣār murmured over the spoils given to Quraysh, Saʿd politely carried their sentiment to the Prophet ﷺ, replying coyly when asked his own view, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am one of my people' — which prompted the Prophet's ﷺ immortal address, 'Are you not happy that these people go back with sheep and money, and you go back with the Prophet?', leaving the Anṣār weeping until their beards were soaked (Ch. 26). After the Prophet's ﷺ passing he was the Anṣār's obvious candidate for leadership at their gathering, before the community united behind Abū Bakr (Ch. 30). al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 740) that he would take as many as eighty of the poor of the Ṣuffah home at a time to feed them, and records (p. 1705) the famous check on his zeal at the Conquest: when Saʿd declared to Abū Sufyān, 'Today is a day of slaughter,' the Prophet ﷺ took the banner he carried and handed it to his son Qays ibn Saʿd, saying that this was rather a day on which Allah would honour the Kaʿbah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 18: stood up during the slander affair; praised by ʿĀʾishah as a righteous man", "Ch. 19: restrained Saʿd ibn Muʿādh at the Qurayẓah treachery; part of the verification party", "Ch. 25: led the Anṣār's column at the Conquest of Mecca", "Ch. 26: conveyed the young Anṣār's grievance at Ḥunayn, occasioning the Prophet's ﷺ speech to the Anṣār", "Ch. 30: the Anṣār's candidate for leadership at their gathering after the Prophet's ﷺ death"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 15 AH, Ḥawrān (Syria) (also reported 14 or 16 AH)", "after_sirah": "After Saqīfah he never gave bayʿah to Abū Bakr and quietly withdrew from public life, eventually leaving Medina altogether for Syria. He settled in the Ḥawrān, where he died around 15 AH — found dead in his bathing place, with the famous popular report attributing his death to the jinn.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 740, 1328, 1705", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 15 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "It is said that under normal circumstances, approximately seventy men lived in As-Suffah, but there were periods during which many more people lived there. It is related that Sa’d ibn Ubaadah ؓ used to invite eighty of them to his house as guests; and if there were still more of them, other members of the Ansaar invited them to their homes.", "page": 740}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ الْخَزْرَجِ، وَصَارَ بَعْدَ مَوْتِ سَعْدِ بْنِ مُعَاذٍ أَكْبَرَ سَادَةِ الْأَنْصَارِ جَمِيعًا."}, {"id": "usayd-ibn-hudayr", "name": "Usayd ibn Huḍayr", "arabic": "أسيد بن حضير", "kunya_titles": "Abū Yaḥyā", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Young leader of the Aws whose conversion at Muṣʿab's hands triggered the mass conversion of Yathrib.", "story": "Usayd was, with Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, one of the two up-and-coming leaders of the Aws and Khazraj after the Battle of Buʿāth wiped out the old leadership. Sent by Saʿd to expel Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr from the city, he stormed into Asʿad ibn Zurārah's house spear in hand — 'Why have you come to our lands? Have you come to brainwash those of us with low intellect?' Muṣʿab calmly replied, 'Why don't you sit and listen to what I have to say? If you find it agreeable then good, and if not then I will stop.' Usayd found the offer too reasonable to refuse, listened, and his heart opened: he came with his spear but left having performed ghusl and declared the shahādah, telling Muṣʿab, 'If you convince the person who sent me, then you will have no opposition left.' He then cleverly drew Saʿd himself into Muṣʿab's presence, and the two conversions brought the entire Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal into Islam (Ch. 11). At Uḥud he led the Aws contingent as the army marched out (Ch. 16). He remained a pillar of the Aws and of the Muslim community in Medina until he died in ʿUmar's caliphate, who helped carry his bier. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1056) that when chieftains met the returning Badr army at al-Rawḥāʾ, Usayd apologised for staying behind — he had assumed the Prophet ﷺ would only overtake a caravan, not meet an enemy in battle — and the Prophet ﷺ told him, 'You have spoken the truth'; he later names him among the four men who descended into Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's grave (p. 1410).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: went to expel Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, listened instead, and embraced Islam", "Ch. 11: engineered Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's meeting with Muṣʿab, leading to mass conversion", "Ch. 16: led the Aws at the Battle of Uḥud"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. Shaʿbān 20 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He remained one of the most trusted counsellors of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar — ʿUmar said of men like him that they were of those 'whose like the earth rarely carries.' When he died in Shaʿbān 20 AH, ʿUmar himself walked beneath his bier and he was buried in al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 587–589, 1056, 1410", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 20 AH", "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ الْأَوْسِ الشَّابُّ، أَسْلَمَ عَلَى يَدِ مُصْعَبٍ فَكَانَ إِسْلَامُهُ سَبَبَ إِسْلَامِ أَهْلِ يَثْرِبَ جَمِيعًا."}, {"id": "asad-ibn-zurarah", "name": "Asʿad ibn Zurārah", "arabic": "أسعد بن زرارة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Umāmah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "One of the earliest converts of Yathrib, who hosted Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr's daʿwah mission in his home.", "story": "Asʿad hosted Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr in his house, from which the daʿwah that transformed Yathrib was launched — it was there that both Usayd ibn Huḍayr and his own cousin Saʿd ibn Muʿādh came to expel Muṣʿab and left as believers (Ch. 11). At the second Covenant of ʿAqabah he dramatically pushed the Prophet's ﷺ blessed hand down before the pledge, warning his people to count the cost: 'If you are ready for your necks to meet swords, then give your oaths' (Ch. 11). One of the twelve nuqabāʾ (tribal representatives), he died of illness in the first year after the Hijrah, the first of the Anṣār to die after the Prophet's ﷺ arrival.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: hosted Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr's mission in his house", "Ch. 11: halted the second Pledge of ʿAqabah to warn his people of its consequences"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 1 AH, Medina — nine months after the Hijrah, before Badr", "after_sirah": "He died of a throat inflammation (al-dhubḥa) which the Prophet ﷺ himself treated by cauterisation, and the Prophet ﷺ followed his bier — the first of the Anṣār buried after the Hijrah. When the Banū Najjār asked for a new naqīb to replace him, the Prophet ﷺ told them, 'I am your naqīb.' al-Ṣallābī recounts the deathbed scene (p. 822): the Jews of Medina cast his sickness as a failing of the Prophet ﷺ, who answered that he had no power to benefit or harm even his own companion, treated him by cauterisation, and, when Asʿad died, said, 'What a bad deceased one is this for the Jews.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 586, 595, 822", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 1 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Usaid took his spear and began to search out for Mus’ab ؓ and As’ad ؓ. When the latter two saw him, As’ad ibn Zuraarah ؓ said, “He is the chief of his people, and he is coming to you. So be true to Allah when you deal with him.” Mus’ab ؓ replied, “If he sits down, I will speak to him.”", "page": 586}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَوَّلِ مَنْ أَسْلَمَ مِنْ أَهْلِ يَثْرِبَ، أَنْزَلَ مُصْعَبَ بْنَ عُمَيْرٍ فِي بَيْتِهِ لِيَقُومَ بِالدَّعْوَةِ مِنْهُ."}, {"id": "abu-al-haytham-ibn-tayyahan", "name": "Abū al-Haytham ibn Tayyahān", "arabic": "أبو الهيثم بن التيهان", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Haytham (kunya); name Mālik ibn al-Tayyahān", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Early Anṣārī of deep faith who questioned the Prophet ﷺ at ʿAqabah with remarkable foresight.", "story": "At the second Pledge of ʿAqabah, Abū al-Haytham stood and asked with striking īmān: since accepting the Prophet ﷺ meant breaking their treaties with the Jews of Yathrib, would he ﷺ abandon them and return to Mecca once Allah granted victory? — his foremost worry being the loss of the Prophet ﷺ, not worldly rule. The Prophet ﷺ replied, 'My blood is your blood, and my destruction is your destruction' (Ch. 11). Years later in Medina, finding the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar outside in the midday heat with nothing to eat at home, he insisted on hosting them and sacrificed his goat to feed them, occasioning the Prophet's ﷺ reminder of the verse on being questioned about worldly pleasures (Ch. 1).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: asked the Prophet ﷺ at ʿAqabah whether he would leave the Anṣār after victory", "Ch. 1: sacrificed his goat to feed the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar in their hunger"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. c. 21 AH, Medina (also reported killed at Ṣiffīn, 37 AH)", "after_sirah": "He lived quietly in Medina through the first caliphates; most classical authorities place his death around 20–21 AH in ʿUmar's era, though other well-known reports have him surviving to fight beside ʿAlī and fall at Ṣiffīn in 37 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 597", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 21 AH", "who_ar": "أَنْصَارِيٌّ مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ رَاسِخُ الْإِيمَانِ، سَأَلَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ لَيْلَةَ الْعَقَبَةِ سُؤَالَ ذِي بَصِيرَةٍ نَافِذَةٍ."}, {"id": "abu-ayyub-al-ansari", "name": "Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī", "arabic": "أبو أيوب الأنصاري", "kunya_titles": "Abū Ayyūb (kunya); name Khālid ibn Zayd; host of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "The Anṣārī of the Banū Najjār who hosted the Prophet ﷺ for six months upon his arrival in Medina.", "story": "When the Prophet's ﷺ camel stopped at the site of the future mosque, he ﷺ asked whose house of his relatives was nearest — and Khālid ibn Zayd, famously Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī of the Banū Najjār, ecstatically claimed the right to host him ﷺ (Ch. 13). For six months while the mosque was built, the Prophet ﷺ lived on the ground floor of Abū Ayyūb's two-storey house. The book paints an unforgettable portrait of his adab: when water spilled upstairs, he and his wife stayed up all night soaking it with their blankets lest a drop fall on the Prophet ﷺ; realising they were walking above his ﷺ head, they cramped themselves into a corner until morning, then begged him ﷺ to take the top floor — 'Never, O Messenger of Allah, can we be above a roof that is above you.' They would send him food and then eat from the exact spot his ﷺ fingers had touched (Ch. 13). During the slander affair, when his wife asked, 'Have you heard…?' he replied, 'How can we speak about such a thing! Glory be to You [O Lord]! This is a heinous slander!' — and Allah quoted his words in the Qur'an (Ch. 18). He spent his last years in jihad and died during the Muslim campaign against Constantinople, buried by its walls. al-Ṣallābī also relates (pp. 1600–1601) how at Khaybar Abū Ayyūb stood guard the whole night outside the Prophet's ﷺ tent on his wedding night with Ṣafiyyah, fearing for him from the newly widowed bride's kin — moving the Prophet ﷺ to pray, 'O Allah, protect Abū Ayyūb just as he spent the night guarding me.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: hosted the Prophet ﷺ for six months; refused to live above him; ate from where he ﷺ ate", "Ch. 18: his instant rejection of the slander was quoted by Allah in the Qur'an"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. 52 AH, buried by the walls of Constantinople (also reported 50 or 51 AH)", "after_sirah": "He never laid down the sword: he fought in the conquests of Syria and Egypt and, as a very old man, insisted on joining the campaign against Constantinople under Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah. Falling fatally ill on the way, he asked to be carried as deep as possible into enemy territory and buried there — and so he was laid to rest at the very walls of the city, where his grave remained honoured even by the Romans and, centuries later, gave its name to Istanbul's Eyüp district.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 632, 1338, 1600–1601", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 52 AH", "who_ar": "الْأَنْصَارِيُّ مِنْ بَنِي النَّجَّارِ الَّذِي نَزَلَ عِنْدَهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ سِتَّةَ أَشْهُرٍ حِينَ قَدِمَ الْمَدِينَةَ."}, {"id": "ubayy-ibn-kab", "name": "Ubayy ibn Kaʿb", "arabic": "أبي بن كعب", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Mundhir; Sayyid al-Qurrāʾ (master of the reciters)", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Anṣārī scribe of revelation and the greatest reciter of the Qur'an among the Companions.", "story": "Ubayy appears in the book at a moment of high trust: when al-ʿAbbās's secret messenger raced from Mecca to warn of the Quraysh army marching to Uḥud, it was Ubayy whom the Prophet ﷺ asked to read the letter, instructing him to keep its contents top-secret until further notice (Ch. 16). That single scene captures his standing — a man of the pen and of confidence in an age of swords. Beyond the book's narration, his rank is well established: an early Anṣārī convert who pledged at ʿAqabah, he fought at Badr, Uḥud, and the campaigns that followed, and served as one of the first and foremost scribes of revelation in Medina. The Prophet ﷺ told him that Allah had commanded that Sūrah al-Bayyinah be recited to him, at which Ubayy wept, and he ﷺ named him the finest of the Ummah in Qur'anic recitation. After the Prophet's ﷺ passing he remained the reference point for the Qur'an — 'the master of the reciters' — consulted by Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, who called him 'the master of the Muslims.' He died in Medina during the caliphate of ʿUmar or early in ʿUthmān's era, leaving a towering legacy in the transmission of the Book. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1137–1138) that it was Ubayy who volunteered after Uḥud to search the field for the dying Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ and carry the Prophet's ﷺ salām to him, returning with Saʿd's final testament to the Anṣār.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: read al-ʿAbbās's secret letter warning of the Quraysh march to Uḥud, sworn to secrecy by the Prophet ﷺ"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Scribe of Revelation", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. c. 22 AH, Medina (also reported 19 or 30 AH)", "after_sirah": "He remained Medina's supreme authority in recitation — ʿUmar called him 'sayyid al-Muslimīn' and kept him in the small circle whose fatwa he trusted — and his personal muṣḥaf and qirāʾah left a permanent mark on the transmission of the Qur'an. His death year is genuinely disputed: most place it under ʿUmar (19–22 AH), others as late as 30 AH under ʿUthmān.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1099, 1137–1138, 1439", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 22 AH", "who_ar": "كَاتِبُ الْوَحْيِ مِنَ الْأَنْصَارِ، وَأَقْرَأُ الصَّحَابَةِ لِكِتَابِ اللهِ."}, {"id": "muadh-ibn-jabal", "name": "Muʿādh ibn Jabal", "arabic": "معاذ بن جبل", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; 'the most knowledgeable of my Ummah in halal and haram'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Young Anṣārī scholar sent by the Prophet ﷺ as Governor of Yemen; the Ummah's authority on halal and haram.", "story": "Muʿādh embraced Islam as a youth of the Khazraj, pledged at ʿAqabah, and witnessed Badr and the battles after it. The book showcases both his fiqh and his chivalry at Tabūk: when a tribesman sneered that Kaʿb ibn Mālik's fine clothes had kept him back, Muʿādh shot back, 'Woe to you! By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I only know Kaʿb to be a good man' — defending his brother's honour in his absence. The author cites here the Prophet's ﷺ testimony, 'The most knowledgeable person in my Ummah of matters of halal and haram is Muʿādh ibn Jabal' (Ch. 27). Near the end of the Prophet's ﷺ life came the farewell that still moves hearts: appointing Muʿādh Governor of Yemen, the Prophet ﷺ walked beside him while insisting Muʿādh ride the donkey, and said, 'O Muʿādh, I certainly love you. Perhaps you shall not see me after this year, and perhaps when you return to Medina you will find my mosque and my grave' (Ch. 30). Muʿādh wept, and the premonition proved true. He later taught in Syria, where he died young in the plague of ʿAmwās — a scholar whom the Prophet ﷺ promised would be raised before the learned on the Day of Judgement. al-Ṣallābī explains (pp. 1935–1936) that in sending its most learned man to Yemen the Prophet ﷺ was deliberately setting a precedent: after his death his Companions would have to leave Medina — some of them forever — and carry Islam to far-off lands.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 27: defended Kaʿb ibn Mālik's honour at Tabūk; declared the most knowledgeable in halal and haram", "Ch. 30: appointed Governor of Yemen; the Prophet's ﷺ tender farewell foretelling his own death"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud"], "death": "d. 18 AH, in the plague of ʿAmwās, Syria", "after_sirah": "After Yemen he settled in Syria as its teacher of Qur'an and fiqh. When Abū ʿUbaydah died in the plague of ʿAmwās, Muʿādh briefly succeeded him over the Muslims of Syria — then the plague took him too, in 18 AH, still only in his thirties, buried in the Jordan Valley.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1873, 1935–1936", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 18 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "While he ﷺ was seated among the people at Tabook, he ﷺ said, ‘What has Ka’ab done?’ A man from the Banu Salamah clan said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, he was held back by his two robes and by the desire to look to his right and to his left (an expression that alludes to a person’s admiration of one’s self and one’s clothing).’ Mu’aadh ibn Jabal retorted, ‘Evil have you spoken! By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we know only good things about him.’", "page": 1873}, "who_ar": "عَالِمٌ أَنْصَارِيٌّ شَابٌّ، بَعَثَهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ وَالِيًا عَلَى الْيَمَنِ، وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ الْأُمَّةِ بِالْحَلَالِ وَالْحَرَامِ."}, {"id": "anas-ibn-malik", "name": "Anas ibn Mālik", "arabic": "أنس بن مالك", "kunya_titles": "Abū Ḥamzah; servant of the Prophet ﷺ for ten years", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "The boy gifted by his mother to serve the Prophet ﷺ, who became one of the greatest narrators of hadith.", "story": "Anas was introduced to the Prophet ﷺ at the age of seven, when his mother gifted him to serve the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — a gift that turned into ten blessed years in the Prophetic household. His testimony to the Prophet's ﷺ character is one of the most beloved in the sīrah: 'I served the Prophet ﷺ for ten years, and not once did he say as much as “uff” to me' (Ch. 1). His uncle Anas ibn al-Naḍr fell gloriously at Uḥud (Ch. 16), and Anas transmitted the family's devotion into decades of narration. He and ʿĀʾishah both described the Prophet's ﷺ perfect balance of gentleness in the home (Ch. 17), and he narrated the Prophet's ﷺ words at the death of baby Ibrāhīm (Ch. 28). In the Prophet's ﷺ final illness it was Anas who described the Companions' distraction in prayer when the Prophet ﷺ lifted the curtain and smiled at them one last time (Ch. 30), and his summary of the two defining days of Medina is immortal: 'The day the Prophet ﷺ entered Medina was the brightest day of our lives, and the day he left was the darkest day of our lives' (Ch. 30). Blessed with the Prophet's ﷺ duʿāʾ for long life, wealth, and offspring, he lived past ninety AH in Basra, among the last Companions to die. al-Ṣallābī's narrative leans on Anas's eyewitness reports throughout — from the women of the Anṣār tending the wounded on campaign (p. 1173) to the harshness of the Prophet's ﷺ daily fare (p. 1913) — and notes (p. 1443) that Anas, present at the wedding feast of Zaynab, could say, 'I was the first of people to learn these Verses,' the verses of ḥijāb whose revelation he witnessed.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 1: gifted to the Prophet ﷺ at age seven; 'not once did he say “uff” to me'", "Ch. 16: his uncle Anas ibn al-Naḍr martyred at Uḥud", "Ch. 28: narrated the Prophet's ﷺ grief at his son Ibrāhīm's death", "Ch. 30: described the Prophet's ﷺ last smile at the praying Companions, and the brightest and darkest days of Medina"], "badges": ["Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 93 AH, Basra (also reported 90–92 AH) — the last Companion to die in Basra", "after_sirah": "The Prophet's ﷺ duʿāʾ for him was fulfilled to the letter: abundant wealth, a garden that bore fruit twice a year, and around a hundred descendants buried before him. He settled in Basra, where he taught for decades and became the third most prolific narrator of hadith, dying in 93 AH aged past a hundred — the last of the Companions to die in Basra.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1173, 1443, 1913", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 93 AH", "who_ar": "الْغُلَامُ الَّذِي وَهَبَتْهُ أُمُّهُ لِخِدْمَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، فَصَارَ مِنْ أَعْظَمِ رُوَاةِ الْحَدِيثِ."}, {"id": "anas-ibn-al-nadr", "name": "Anas ibn al-Naḍr", "arabic": "أنس بن النضر", "kunya_titles": "Uncle of Anas ibn Mālik", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Uncle of Anas ibn Mālik who fell at Uḥud with over 80 wounds, and about whom a verse of the Qur'an was revealed.", "story": "Having missed Badr, Anas ibn al-Naḍr longed to prove his devotion. When rumours spread at Uḥud that the Prophet ﷺ had been killed and Muslims slumped in despair, he berated them: 'Even if the Prophet ﷺ has been killed, the Lord of the Prophet has not been killed… Let us go and die as he died.' Passing his friend Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, he said, 'O Saʿd, I can smell Paradise from behind that mountain,' then threw himself into the battle (Ch. 16). His body was found with over 80 wounds and could only be identified by his sister from his fingertips. Allah revealed about him, 'Among the believers are men who have proven true to what they pledged to Allah' (Ch. 16).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: 'I can smell Paradise from behind that mountain' — martyred at Uḥud with over 80 wounds", "Ch. 16: identified by his sister from his fingertips; verse of Sūrah al-Aḥzāb revealed about him"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1123–1124"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Anas ؓ asked them, “Why are you sitting down?” They answered, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has been killed!” He said, “O people, even if Muhammad ﷺ has been killed, the Lord of Muhammad has not been killed, so die for that which Muhammad ﷺ has died.” Anas ؓ then met Sa’d ibn Mu’aadh ؓ and said, “O Sa’d, I indeed smell the fragrance of Paradise beneath Uhud.”", "page": 1123}, "who_ar": "عَمُّ أَنَسِ بْنِ مَالِكٍ، اسْتُشْهِدَ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ وَبِهِ أَكْثَرُ مِنْ ثَمَانِينَ جِرَاحَةً، وَنَزَلَتْ فِيهِ آيَةٌ مِنَ الْقُرْآنِ."}, {"id": "al-rubayyi-bint-muawwidh", "name": "Al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh", "arabic": "الربيع بنت معوذ", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī female Companion, daughter of the Badr hero Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ, famed for her description of the Prophet ﷺ.", "story": "Al-Rubayyiʿ, daughter of Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ who fell at Badr, was asked in her old age about the appearance of the Prophet ﷺ, and all she could say was, 'My son, if you could have seen him, you would have thought that the Sun had risen up' (Ch. 1). She is also known in the wider hadith literature for narrating her wedding day, when the Prophet ﷺ sat on her bedding as young girls sang, and for tending the wounded in the Prophet's ﷺ campaigns.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 1: described the Prophet ﷺ as the risen Sun"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 45 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "She lived on in Medina as a respected muḥaddithah: the young Tābiʿūn came to her to learn the precise details of the Prophet's ﷺ wuḍūʾ, her wedding day, and the women's role in his campaigns — giving water, treating the wounded, and carrying the slain back to Medina, as she herself narrated in al-Bukhārī.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1174", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 45 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ar-Rubayya’ bint Mu’awwidh ؓ said, “We were with the Prophet ﷺ, giving drink to the (fighters) and serving them. Also, we would transport the wounded and the dead to Al-Madeenah.”", "page": 1174}, "who_ar": "صَحَابِيَّةٌ أَنْصَارِيَّةٌ، ابْنَةُ بَطَلِ بَدْرٍ مُعَوِّذِ بْنِ عَفْرَاءَ، اشْتُهِرَتْ بِوَصْفِهَا لِلنَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"id": "abu-dujanah", "name": "Abū Dujānah", "arabic": "أبو دجانة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Dujānah (kunya); name Simāk ibn Kharashah; wearer of the red 'Turban of Death'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "The Anṣārī warrior who took the Prophet's ﷺ sword at Uḥud 'with its due right'.", "story": "On the morning of Uḥud the Prophet ﷺ unsheathed his sword and asked, 'Who will take this sword from me and fight?' Everyone volunteered — but when he ﷺ added, 'with its due right?', silence fell. Abū Dujānah asked what its due right was, and hearing 'that you fight with it until it breaks or is no longer usable,' he answered, 'I will take it with its due right, O Messenger of Allah' (Ch. 16). A formidable force whose reputation preceded him, he donned his red 'Turban of Death,' reserved for the severest of battles, and strutted between the lines — prompting the Prophet's ﷺ remark, 'Allah despises this type of walking except in this time and this place.' Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, initially disappointed at being passed over, followed him and watched him obliterate every opponent in quick succession, conceding that Abū Dujānah gave the blessed sword its due right (Ch. 16). Alongside ʿAlī, he cut down the last of those who attacked the Prophet ﷺ in the battle's darkest hour (Ch. 16), and the book recalls his iconic red turban again in connection with the Trench (Ch. 19). He lived to fight the false prophet Musaylimah, falling as a martyr at Yamāmah. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1127) that in the crush around the Prophet ﷺ Abū Dujānah stood directly over him ﷺ and made his own body a shield, arrow after arrow lodging in his back while he did not move.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: took the Prophet's ﷺ sword 'with its due right' at Uḥud", "Ch. 16: donned the red 'Turban of Death' and strutted before the enemy — the walk Allah despises except then and there", "Ch. 16: with ʿAlī, killed off the attackers who reached the Prophet ﷺ"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "12 AH — martyred at Yamāmah", "after_sirah": "In the Riddah wars he was among the heroes who stormed the 'Garden of Death' at Yamāmah — reports have him vaulted over the wall to open the gate, fighting on with a broken leg — and he fell there in 12 AH, credited in some accounts with a share in bringing down Musaylimah himself.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1119–1120, 1127", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 12 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "A brave man, Abu Dujaanah ؓ was known to prance and strut during battle. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him prancing and strutting between the two rows (of the armies), he ﷺ said, “It is a way of walking that Allah hates (because it indicates arrogance and pride), except in a situation similar to this (because it raises the morale of Muslim army).” Imam Muslim related that Abu Dujaanah ؓ took the sword and began to split the heads of polytheists with it.", "page": 1119}, "who_ar": "الْفَارِسُ الْأَنْصَارِيُّ الَّذِي أَخَذَ سَيْفَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ «بِحَقِّهِ»."}, {"id": "hassan-ibn-thabit", "name": "Ḥassān ibn Thābit", "arabic": "حسان بن ثابت", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Walīd; the Poet of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "The official poet of the Prophet ﷺ, who defended Islam with verse as others did with swords.", "story": "Ḥassān was the tongue of the Anṣār and the Poet of the Prophet ﷺ, for whom a pulpit was set in the mosque and who was aided, as the Prophet ﷺ prayed, by the Rūḥ al-Qudus. The book shows him first as a witness of the Meccan years: he recalled seeing, on Hajj from Medina, the horrific torture of Bilāl and wondering how he remained alive (Ch. 6). His humanity, too, is recorded: he was one of the three believing Companions who slipped into gossip during the slander against ʿĀʾishah — not fabricating, but repeating — and he bore the 80 lashes of the Qur'anic punishment, which cleansed the sin in this world (Ch. 18). The Prophet ﷺ later gifted him Sīrīn, sister of Māriyah al-Qibṭiyyah, sent by the Muqawqis of Egypt; their son ʿAbd al-Raḥmān was thus a maternal cousin of the Prophet's ﷺ son Ibrāhīm (Ch. 28). When the Prophet ﷺ passed away, it was Ḥassān who composed the most eloquent of the elegies the Companions recited: 'What ails these eyes of mine? They fail to sleep… O Best of all who ever trod the Earth, don't go so far away from me, I beg' (Ch. 30). He lived to a great age, honoured in Medina as the poet who had defended the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. al-Ṣallābī analyses his verses after Badr as psychological warfare — 'bullets sprayed at the Quraysh' (pp. 1214–1215) — and, defending his honour, dismisses as baseless the well-known tale that Ḥassān cowered in the fortress with Ṣafiyyah at the Trench, noting that it has no isnād at all and is belied by the fact that no enemy poet ever taunted him with cowardice (pp. 1402–1403).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: recalled witnessing Bilāl's torture while on Hajj from Medina", "Ch. 18: lapsed into gossip during the slander affair and bore the prescribed punishment", "Ch. 28: gifted Sīrīn, sister of Māriyah al-Qibṭiyyah, by the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 30: composed the most eloquent elegy at the Prophet's ﷺ passing"], "badges": ["Poet of the Prophet"], "death": "d. c. 54 AH, Medina (also reported 40 or 50 AH)", "after_sirah": "He was granted an extraordinary span — the sources say he lived a hundred and twenty years, sixty in Jāhiliyyah and sixty in Islam — losing his sight in his final years and dying in Medina in Muʿāwiyah's reign, most commonly dated 54 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1214–1215, 1338–1343, 1402–1403", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 54 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "On a psychological level, Hassaan’s poems were like bullets being sprayed at the Quraish. Quraish’s victory, Hassaan ؓ explained, was no victory at all. The Muslims, with a wonderful show of bravery, managed to kill the flag-bearer of Quraish’s army.", "page": 1214}, "who_ar": "شَاعِرُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الْمُعْتَمَدُ، ذَبَّ عَنِ الْإِسْلَامِ بِشِعْرِهِ كَمَا ذَبَّ غَيْرُهُ بِالسُّيُوفِ."}, {"id": "kab-ibn-malik", "name": "Kaʿb ibn Mālik", "arabic": "كعب بن مالك", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; poet of the Anṣār; one of the three whose repentance was accepted", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Senior Anṣārī of the ʿAqabah pledge whose repentance after missing Tabūk became a Qur'anic masterclass in tawbah.", "story": "Kaʿb of the Banū Salamah witnessed the second Covenant of ʿAqabah — which he treasured above even Badr — and narrated its secret nighttime pledge (Ch. 11, 27). He described the Prophet's ﷺ joyful face as the full Moon (Ch. 1), and at Uḥud he was the first to recognise the Prophet ﷺ alive amid the rumours of his death, screaming, 'O Muslims! Rejoice! The Prophet ﷺ is alive!' (Ch. 16). His immortal legacy, however, is his own unsparing confession of failure at Tabūk: healthy and wealthy, he procrastinated day after day until the army was gone. Refusing to lie, he told the Prophet ﷺ, 'I swear by Allah that I have no excuse' — and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'This man has spoken the truth' (Ch. 27). For fifty days the Muslims were forbidden from speaking to him, Murārah ibn al-Rabīʿ, and Hilāl ibn Umayyah; the Earth, 'despite its vastness, seemed to close in on them.' He burned the Ghassanid king's letter of temptation, obeyed the command to separate from his wife, and on the sixtieth morning heard a voice from Mount Selaʿ: 'O Kaʿb ibn Mālik! Rejoice!' Allah had revealed his forgiveness in Sūrah al-Tawbah. He gifted the shirt off his back to the first man who congratulated him, and the Prophet ﷺ told him it was the best day since his mother gave birth to him (Ch. 27). al-Ṣallābī records (p. 602) that when Kaʿb first came to Mecca with al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr before the pledge, al-ʿAbbās identified him to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked, '(Kaʿb) the poet?'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: narrated the secret second Pledge of ʿAqabah, which he prized above Badr", "Ch. 16: first to announce that the Prophet ﷺ was alive at Uḥud", "Ch. 27: missed Tabūk without excuse, told the truth, endured the 50-day boycott", "Ch. 27: his repentance accepted by revelation in Sūrah al-Tawbah — announced from Mount Selaʿ"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Poet of the Prophet"], "death": "d. c. 50 AH, Medina (also reported 51 or 53 AH)", "after_sirah": "He lost his sight in his old age and died in Medina around 50 AH, in Muʿāwiyah's reign; it was through his sons — his son ʿAbdullāh leading the blind Kaʿb by the hand — that his magnificent tawbah narrative was transmitted into the Ṣaḥīḥayn.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 602, 1871–1877", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 50 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Seeing them approach, the Prophet ﷺ asked Al-’Abbaas, “Do you know these two men, O Abul-Fadl?” He said, “Yes, he is Al-Baraa ibn Ma’roor, chief of his people; and the other is Ka’ab ibn Maalik.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “(Ka’ab) the poet?” Al-‘Abbaas ؓ said, “Yes.”", "page": 602}, "who_ar": "مِنْ كِبَارِ الْأَنْصَارِ شُهُودِ بَيْعَةِ الْعَقَبَةِ، صَارَتْ تَوْبَتُهُ بَعْدَ تَخَلُّفِهِ عَنْ تَبُوكَ دَرْسًا قُرْآنِيًّا بَلِيغًا فِي التَّوْبَةِ."}, {"id": "jabir-ibn-abdillah", "name": "Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh", "arabic": "جابر بن عبد الله", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "One of the most famous Companions of the Anṣār and among the most prolific narrators of the sīrah.", "story": "The book calls Jābir 'one of the most famous Companions from the Anṣār,' and his narrations thread through it — from the story of how Allah guided the people of Yathrib to invite the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 11) to the most comprehensive account of the Farewell Hajj (Ch. 29). His father, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām, was martyred at Uḥud leaving seven daughters and a massive debt that Jābir inherited. Riding the oldest, weakest camel on the return from Dhāt al-Riqāʿ, drowning in worry, he heard the Prophet's ﷺ soothing call from behind. In the celebrated 'Hadith of Jābir' — which Imam al-Nawawī says is replete with benefits — the Prophet ﷺ teased him about marrying an older widow (chosen so she could care for his sisters), struck his camel so it became the fastest, and then bought it from him for an ever-rising price, returning both camel and money in Medina so Jābir would not feel he was given charity (Ch. 17). At the Trench, Jābir saw the rock tied to the Prophet's ﷺ stomach from hunger and prepared a small meal for him — which the Prophet ﷺ, by his blessing, stretched to feed around 1,000 Companions (Ch. 19). Blind in old age, he remained a fountain of knowledge in Medina until his death, among the last Companions there.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: narrated how Allah guided the Anṣār to invite the Prophet ﷺ to Yathrib", "Ch. 17: the Hadith of Jābir — his father's debt, his marriage, and the camel the Prophet ﷺ bought and returned", "Ch. 19: his one-goat meal fed 1,000 Companions at the Trench", "Ch. 29: narrated the most comprehensive account of the Farewell Hajj"], "badges": ["Khandaq", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. c. 78 AH, Medina (also reported 73–77 AH) — among the last Companions to die in Medina", "after_sirah": "He held a teaching circle in the Prophet's ﷺ mosque and famously travelled a full month to Syria to hear a single hadith from ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays. Blind in his last years, he died in Medina around 78 AH aged about ninety-four — by most accounts the last of the Companions to die there. al-Ṣallābī also records that journey to ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays for a single hadith on the Day of Judgement (p. 917), and devotes a section of his own to 'the Prophet's ﷺ dealings with Jābir' (pp. 1295–1297).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 917, 1141–1143, 1295–1297", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 78 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Another good example is found in a Hadeeth that is related by Jaabir ibn ‘Abdullah ؓ from ‘Abdullah ibn Unais - the Hadeeth which prompted the former, when he heard about it, to travel to the latter in order to learn it. Unais said, “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, ‘Allah will resurrect the slaves (i.e., His slaves) - or he ﷺ said, ‘the people’ - naked, uncircumcised, Buhman.’", "page": 917}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَشْهَرِ صَحَابَةِ الْأَنْصَارِ، وَمِنْ أَكْثَرِ الرُّوَاةِ لِأَخْبَارِ السِّيرَةِ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-amr-ibn-haram", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām", "arabic": "عبد الله بن عمرو بن حرام", "kunya_titles": "Abū Jābir; father of Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Father of Jābir, who tried to stop the Hypocrites' desertion before Uḥud and was martyred in the battle.", "story": "When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and his 300 followers slunk away from the army before Uḥud, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām rode after them, reminding them to fear Allah and not abandon their Prophet ﷺ and people with the enemy at hand — appealing to both their religious duty and tribal honour, but to no avail (Ch. 16). He then fought at Uḥud and was martyred, leaving behind seven daughters and a massive debt that his son Jābir inherited, a burden the Prophet ﷺ later helped lift with characteristic tenderness (Ch. 17). It is well established that the Prophet ﷺ consoled Jābir that Allah spoke to his father directly and that he was among those alive with their Lord, well provided for.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: pursued the deserting Hypocrites and pleaded with them to return", "Ch. 17: his martyrdom at Uḥud left Jābir with seven sisters and a massive debt"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1141–1143", "Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "In spite of the pressing needs of his family, ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Amr ibn Haraam ؓ was adamant about participating in the Battle of Uhud. Before leaving, he said to his son Jaabir ؓ, “O Jaabir, you will do no wrong by staying within Al-Madeenah until you find out what happens to us. For by Allah, had it not been for the daughters I am leaving behind (and who need someone to take care of them), I would have loved for you to be killed (as a martyr) in my presence.”", "page": 1141}, "who_ar": "وَالِدُ جَابِرٍ، حَاوَلَ رَدَّ الْمُنَافِقِينَ عَنِ الِانْخِذَالِ قَبْلَ أُحُدٍ، ثُمَّ اسْتُشْهِدَ فِي الْمَعْرَكَةِ."}, {"id": "al-bara-ibn-azib", "name": "Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib", "arabic": "البراء بن عازب", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿUmārah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Young Anṣārī sent back from Badr for his age, who grew into a warrior and major narrator.", "story": "Al-Barāʾ was one of the two boys the Prophet ﷺ sent back from the march to Badr for being too young to participate (Ch. 14). He came of age quickly: he narrated the rout of the Quraysh at the start of Uḥud — 'They turned and fled until I saw with my own eyes the ankle-bracelets of their women' (Ch. 16) — and at the Trench described the Prophet ﷺ carrying dirt for so long that dust covered his skin (Ch. 19). He later fought in the great conquests and settled in Kufa, becoming a prolific narrator of the sīrah's battles.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: sent back from Badr as too young", "Ch. 16: narrated the initial Muslim rout of the Quraysh at Uḥud", "Ch. 19: described the Prophet ﷺ digging and carrying dirt at the Trench"], "badges": ["Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 72 AH, Kufa (also reported 71 AH)", "after_sirah": "He took part in the eastern conquests — the sources credit him with the conquest of Rayy — and stood with ʿAlī at the Jamal, Ṣiffīn, and Nahrawān before settling permanently in Kufa, where the people took his hadith of the battles until his death around 72 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 949–950, 1112", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 72 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "On these grounds, the Prophet ﷺ sent back Al-Baraa ibn ‘Aazib ؓ and ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar ؓ, who were both too young to fight. They had originally left with the Muslim contingent with the sincere intention of fighting alongside their Muslim brothers.", "page": 949}, "who_ar": "أَنْصَارِيٌّ صَغِيرٌ رُدَّ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ لِصِغَرِ سِنِّهِ، ثُمَّ صَارَ مُقَاتِلًا وَرَاوِيًا مُكْثِرًا."}, {"id": "ubadah-ibn-al-samit", "name": "ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit", "arabic": "عبادة بن الصامت", "kunya_titles": "Abū al-Walīd", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Senior Khazrajī of both Pledges of ʿAqabah who publicly renounced his alliance with the Banū Qaynuqāʿ for Allah and His Messenger ﷺ.", "story": "ʿUbādah was among the elite of the Khazraj — a participant in both Covenants of ʿAqabah and one of the twelve nuqabāʾ appointed over the tribes, and it is through his narration that the terms of the first pledge are famously transmitted. The book records his defining moment of walāʾ: historically, the Jews of Yathrib had two main allies, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, and when the treacherous Banū Qaynuqāʿ appealed to both, the contrast could not have been starker. While Ibn Ubayy blustered and demanded their release, ʿUbādah went straight to the Prophet ﷺ and relinquished his ties: 'O Messenger of Allah, I want to inform you that I am no longer their representative. My ties are with Allah and His Messenger' (Ch. 15) — a declaration scholars connect to the revelation that Allah, His Messenger ﷺ, and the believers are the true allies of the believer. A Badr veteran who witnessed the great battles, he was later sent to teach Qur'an to the people of Syria, served as the first judge appointed in Palestine, and died in Ramla around 34 AH, revered as a scholar of the Anṣār who feared no blame in the cause of Allah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: publicly renounced his alliance with the Banū Qaynuqāʿ — 'My ties are with Allah and His Messenger'"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. 34 AH, Ramla (Palestine) (also reported 45 AH)", "after_sirah": "In Syria his fearlessness became proverbial — he confronted Muʿāwiyah openly over what he saw as worldly compromise, and when Muʿāwiyah complained, ʿUmar replied that the likes of ʿUbādah could not be spared from any land. He died in Ramla in 34 AH aged seventy-two, some also placing his grave in Jerusalem.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 585, 596, 1070–1073", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 34 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Ubaadah ibn As-Saamit, a member of the Khazraj tribe, later said, “I was among those who were present at the First Al-‘Aqabah (pledge). We were twelve men, and we gave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the pledge of women (i.e., a pledge that does not involve a promise to fight); and that was before fighting was made obligatory upon us.”", "page": 585}, "who_ar": "مِنْ كِبَارِ الْخَزْرَجِ، شَهِدَ بَيْعَتَيِ الْعَقَبَةِ، وَتَبَرَّأَ عَلَانِيَةً مِنْ حِلْفِ بَنِي قَيْنُقَاعَ لِلَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِ ﷺ."}, {"id": "muhammad-ibn-maslamah", "name": "Muhammad ibn Maslamah", "arabic": "محمد بن مسلمة", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; 'the knight of the Prophet ﷺ'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Trusted Awsī operative of the Prophet ﷺ, who led the mission against Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf and guarded Medina during Tabūk.", "story": "A senior member of the Aws, Muhammad ibn Maslamah was the man the Prophet ﷺ turned to for the most delicate missions. When Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf's treachery and vile poetry crossed every line, the Prophet ﷺ asked, 'Who will take care of Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf?' — and Muhammad ibn Maslamah volunteered, obtaining permission to speak ill of the Prophet ﷺ as a ruse, ingeniously using weapons as loan collateral to justify arriving armed, and carrying out the operation on the 14th of Rabīʿ al-Awwal (Ch. 15). He served as the Prophet's ﷺ messenger to the Banū Naḍīr, who could not bargain with their former Jāhiliyyah ally (Ch. 17). During the Qurayẓah siege he seized the fleeing ʿAmr ibn Saʿdā — one of the few who had opposed the betrayal — and let him go, saying, 'O Allah, overlook my overlooking of him,' an act the Prophet ﷺ endorsed (Ch. 19). Shortly after the Trench he captured Thumāmah ibn Uthāl, chieftain of the Banū Ḥanīfah, whose ensuing conversion is one of the sweetest stories of the sīrah (Ch. 19). Before Tabūk, the Prophet ﷺ appointed him over Medina in his absence (Ch. 27). True to the sword-of-wood counsel attributed to him, he withdrew from the civil strife after ʿUthmān's death and died in Medina.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: led the operation against Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf", "Ch. 17: the Prophet's ﷺ messenger to the Banū Naḍīr", "Ch. 19: released ʿAmr ibn Saʿdā of Qurayẓah; captured Thumāmah ibn Uthāl", "Ch. 27: appointed over Medina during the Expedition of Tabūk"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 43 AH, Medina (also reported 46 or 47 AH)", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar made him his trusted inspector, sending him to audit and discipline governors across the provinces. When the fitna erupted he kept a sword of wood — acting on counsel attributed to the Prophet ﷺ — and refused to shed Muslim blood, dying in Medina around 43 AH; some reports say he was killed there by a raider from Syria.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1076–1083, 1262–1264, 1829–1830", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 43 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Jaabir ibn ‘Abdullah ؓ related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Who will take care of Ka’ab ibn Al-Ashraf, the man who has wronged Allah and His Messenger?” Muhammad ibn Maslamah ؓ stood up and said, “O Messenger of Allah, would you love for me to kill him?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Yes.” Muhammad ibn Maslamah ؓ said, “Then give me permission to say something,” to which the Prophet ﷺ responded, “Speak.”", "page": 1076}, "who_ar": "مِنْ ثِقَاتِ رِجَالِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مِنَ الْأَوْسِ، تَوَلَّى قِيَادَةَ السَّرِيَّةِ إِلَى كَعْبِ بْنِ الْأَشْرَفِ، وَحَرَسَ الْمَدِينَةَ زَمَنَ تَبُوكَ."}, {"id": "mahmud-ibn-maslamah", "name": "Maḥmūd ibn Maslamah", "arabic": "محمود بن مسلمة", "kunya_titles": "Brother of Muhammad ibn Maslamah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī warrior of the Aws, brother of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, martyred at Khaybar.", "story": "Maḥmūd, brother of the famous Muhammad ibn Maslamah, fought in the campaign of Khaybar, where he was martyred after a large millstone-like log was thrown over the fortress wall onto him (Ch. 21). His death was among the griefs of that hard-won victory, and his brother's line continued in service of the Ummah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: martyred at Khaybar when a large log was thrown over the wall"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "7 AH — martyred at Khaybar", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1585"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 7 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Muslims faced strong resistance while conquering some of the fortresses; for example, while they were attacking the Naa’im fortress, Mahmood ibn Maslamah Al-Ansaaree ؓ was martyred underneath its walls. His killer was a man named Muraahib, who threw a stone hand-mill onto him from on top of the fortress. The Naa’im fortress by itself took ten days to conquer.", "page": 1585}, "who_ar": "فَارِسٌ أَنْصَارِيٌّ مِنَ الْأَوْسِ، أَخُو مُحَمَّدِ بْنِ مَسْلَمَةَ، اسْتُشْهِدَ يَوْمَ خَيْبَرَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-rawahah", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah", "arabic": "عبد الله بن رواحة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad; poet of the Prophet ﷺ; third commander at Muʾtah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Anṣārī poet and commander, third of the three appointed at Muʾtah, who leaped into martyrdom.", "story": "One of the Prophet's ﷺ poets and a nobleman of the Khazraj, Ibn Rawāḥah appears at Badr among the three Anṣār who rose for the opening duel before the Quraysh demanded their 'equals' (Ch. 14), and at the Trench among the trusted group sent to verify the Banū Qurayẓah's betrayal (Ch. 19). His destiny was Muʾtah. The Prophet ﷺ appointed the commanders in sequence: 'Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and if he is killed, then Jaʿfar, and if Jaʿfar is killed, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah' — an unprecedented arrangement hinting at what was to come. Given leave for Friday prayer, he chose instead to march out with the army immediately. As the Companions bade farewell, he wept — not from fear of death, but from the verse, 'There is none of you but will pass over it (Hellfire)': 'How will I fare in that crossing?' When the Muslims saw the enemy's overwhelming numbers, Zayd sought his counsel, and his fiery eloquence carried the day: 'That which you fear — martyrdom — is the very thing you came out seeking!' After Zayd and Jaʿfar fell, he took the flag, steadied his hesitating soul with verses of poetry, and plunged in to meet his desire (Ch. 24). The Prophet ﷺ, witnessing by revelation, described each commander taking the flag and falling in turn. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1598) that after Khaybar the Prophet ﷺ sent him every harvest to assess the Jews' crop; when they tried to bribe him he flared, 'O enemies of Allah, will you feed me the vile earnings of bribery? My hatred for you and my love for the Prophet ﷺ will not make me act unjustly towards you' — and they answered, 'It is upon this that the heavens and the earth have been raised.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: among the three Anṣār who rose for the opening duel at Badr", "Ch. 19: part of the party sent to verify Qurayẓah's treachery", "Ch. 24: third commander at Muʾtah — his speech, his tears over the crossing of Hellfire, his martyrdom"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr", "Poet of the Prophet"], "death": "8 AH — martyred at Muʾtah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1598, 1652–1656, 1663"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After an agreement was arrived at between the Muslims and the Jews of Khaibar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued to send ‘Abdullah ibn Rawaahah ؓ to them every year during harvest time, in order to estimate the total quantity of the harvest and then to guarantee to them their half. They complained bitterly about the estimation process, and they even tried to bribe ‘Abdullah ibn Rawaahah ؓ. ‘Abdullah ؓ became very angry and said to them, “O enemies of Allah, will you try to feed me As-Suht,” referring thus to the vile proceeds of bribery.", "page": 1598}, "who_ar": "شَاعِرُ الْأَنْصَارِ وَأَمِيرُهُمْ، ثَالِثُ الْأُمَرَاءِ الثَّلَاثَةِ يَوْمَ مُؤْتَةَ، وَثَبَ إِلَى الشَّهَادَةِ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-zayd", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd", "arabic": "عبد الله بن زيد", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad; 'the man of the adhān dream'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Anṣārī whose dream brought the words of the adhān.", "story": "After the Companions' meeting on how to call the people to prayer ended without a decision — bells and shofars both rejected — ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd dreamt of a man who taught him the words of the adhān. He rushed to the Prophet ﷺ, who confirmed it and said, 'Stand up, O Bilāl! You have the loudest voice,' with ʿAbdullāh relaying each phrase from the roof; ʿUmar then ran out saying he had seen the same dream (Ch. 13). The adhān thus became the only act of Islam legislated via the dream of a Companion, ratified by the Prophet's ﷺ approval. The book notes that no other narrations survive of him — yet, as it observes, he must have had a blessed relationship with Allah and His Messenger ﷺ to be bestowed such an honour.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: saw the dream of the adhān, confirmed by the Prophet ﷺ and echoed by ʿUmar's identical dream"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 32 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He lived on quietly in Medina, remembered by the Ummah every single day through the words of the adhān his dream had carried; he died in 32 AH aged sixty-four, and ʿUthmān prayed over him.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 732–733", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Abdullah ibn Zaid ؓ, one of the callers to prayer, saw a dream while he was in between the states of wakefulness and sleep. In the dream, a person appeared before him and said, “Shall I not teach you words that you should say when you make the call for prayer?” Abdullah answered, “Yes.”", "page": 732}, "who_ar": "الْأَنْصَارِيُّ الَّذِي جَاءَتْ فِي رُؤْيَاهُ كَلِمَاتُ الْأَذَانِ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-jubayr", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Jubayr", "arabic": "عبد الله بن جبير", "kunya_titles": "Commander of the archers at Uḥud", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Commander of the 50 archers on the hill at Uḥud, who stood firm when 40 abandoned their post.", "story": "The Prophet ﷺ placed 50 of the best archers on Jabal ʿAynayn at Uḥud under ʿAbdullāh ibn Jubayr, with the strict command not to move even if birds ate their corpses. When victory seemed certain and the spoils beckoned, ʿAbdullāh rebuked the impatient: 'Have you forgotten what the Messenger of Allah instructed?!' Forty left; he remained with ten steadfast Companions (Ch. 16). When Khālid ibn al-Walīd's cavalry swept around the hill, ʿAbdullāh and his loyal band fought to the death at their post — faithful to the command to the very end.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: commanded the archers at Uḥud and rebuked those who abandoned the hill"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud, holding his post", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1114"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Fearing that eventuality, the Prophet ﷺ ordered the archers, under the leadership of ‘Abdullah ibn Jubair ؓ, to remain on top of Mount ‘Ainain and deter horsemen from attempting to go around it. The Prophet ﷺ ordered them to ignore what was happening on the battlefield and to remain where they were regardless of whether the Muslims were winning or losing. He ﷺ said to them, “Even if you see that we are being snatched away by birds, do not leave this position of yours until I send for you.”", "page": 1114}, "who_ar": "أَمِيرُ الرُّمَاةِ الْخَمْسِينَ عَلَى الْجَبَلِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ، ثَبَتَ حِينَ تَرَكَ أَرْبَعُونَ مِنْهُمْ مَوَاقِعَهُمْ."}, {"id": "saad-ibn-al-rabi", "name": "Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ", "arabic": "سعد بن الربيع", "kunya_titles": "Naqīb of the Khazraj", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Anṣārī of the famous muʾākhāh with ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who offered him half of everything he owned.", "story": "When the Prophet ﷺ instituted the muʾākhāh pact of brotherhood, the most famous pairing was Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ with the Muhājir ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf. Saʿd, a successful businessman, said: 'I am the richest of the Anṣār, and I will give you half of all of my money; I have a two-storey house, and one of them is yours; I have two gardens, and one of them is yours; and I have two wives, and one of them is yours.' ʿAbdurraḥmān humbly replied, 'May Allah bless your wealth and family — just direct me to the marketplace' (Ch. 13). The book calls it magnanimity matched only by dignity, each acting out of īmān and brotherhood. Saʿd, a naqīb of ʿAqabah and veteran of Badr, was later found dying at Uḥud, sending his final salām to the Prophet ﷺ and urging the Anṣār to protect him ﷺ. al-Ṣallābī notes that the Prophet ﷺ had entrusted Saʿd with the secret intelligence of al-ʿAbbās's letter before Uḥud (p. 1099), and names Ubayy ibn Kaʿb as the man sent after the battle to search for him — the Prophet ﷺ had seen spears aimed at Saʿd — finding him with barely a breath left (pp. 1137–1138).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: offered ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf half of all he owned under the muʾākhāh brotherhood"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 771, 1099, 1137–1138", "Sīrat Ibn Hishām", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "who_ar": "الْأَنْصَارِيُّ صَاحِبُ الْمُؤَاخَاةِ الْمَشْهُورَةِ مَعَ عَبْدِ الرَّحْمَنِ بْنِ عَوْفٍ، إِذْ عَرَضَ عَلَيْهِ نِصْفَ كُلِّ مَا يَمْلِكُ."}, {"id": "al-hubab-ibn-al-mundhir", "name": "Al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir", "arabic": "الحباب بن المنذر", "kunya_titles": "Known for his sound counsel in war", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Experienced Anṣārī scout whose strategic advice at Badr the Prophet ﷺ adopted.", "story": "Before camp was set at Badr, al-Ḥubāb, an experienced scout of the Anṣār, asked the Prophet ﷺ whether the campsite was divinely ordained or his own tactical opinion. Told it was strategy, he suggested camping beyond the mid-point of the plains so the wells of Badr lay behind the Muslims, cutting the Quraysh off from water. The Prophet ﷺ followed his advice — 'You have directed us to the better opinion' — and the book highlights this as a lesson distinguishing the Prophet ﷺ acting as Prophet from acting as a man (Ch. 14). He also led the Khazraj contingent as the army marched to Uḥud (Ch. 16). al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1099) that before Uḥud the Prophet ﷺ sent him ahead to scout the Quraysh camp and estimate their strength, instructing him to report back discreetly.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: his water-strategy advice at Badr was adopted by the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 16: led the Khazraj as the army marched to Uḥud"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud"], "death": "d. during ʿUmar's caliphate, Medina", "after_sirah": "He lived into ʿUmar's caliphate and died in Medina; the books remember him by the byname his Badr counsel earned — dhū al-raʾy, the man of sound judgement.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 958–959, 1099", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Here, Al-Hubaab ibn Al-Mundhir ؓ stood up and said, “O Messenger of Allah, concerning this particular spot (where we are making camp), is it a spot concerning which Allah sent down revelation to you, so that we may not advance from it or go behind it? Or is it (i.e., the choosing of this spot to make camp) based on opinion, warfare, and strategy?” The Prophet ﷺ answered, “Rather, it is (based on) opinion, warfare, and strategy (and not on revelation).”", "page": 958}, "who_ar": "رَائِدٌ أَنْصَارِيٌّ مُحَنَّكٌ، أَخَذَ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ بِمَشُورَتِهِ الْحَرْبِيَّةِ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "abu-lubabah", "name": "Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir", "arabic": "أبو لبابة بن عبد المنذر", "kunya_titles": "Abū Lubābah (kunya); name Bashīr (or Rifāʿah) ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Awsī Companion who betrayed a secret by a gesture during the Qurayẓah siege, then tied himself to a pillar until Allah forgave him.", "story": "Abū Lubābah shared a camel with the Prophet ﷺ and ʿAlī on the march to Badr (Ch. 14). His defining trial came at the siege of the Banū Qurayẓah, who requested him as a trusted old ally: asked whether they should surrender, he said yes — but, moved by the wailing women and children, gestured to his throat, signalling slaughter. 'As soon as I said this, I immediately realised that I had been treacherous to Allah and His Messenger.' He sneaked out, marched to the mosque, and tied himself to a pillar, vowing to remain until Allah accepted his repentance. The Prophet ﷺ said that had Abū Lubābah come to him he would have sought forgiveness for him, but as he had gone to Allah directly, the matter was Allah's. Revelation eventually came, and the Prophet ﷺ announced, 'Allah has accepted the repentance of Abū Lubābah' (Ch. 19).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: shared the Prophet's ﷺ camel on the march to Badr", "Ch. 19: his gesture to the Banū Qurayẓah, and his repentance tied to the pillar of the mosque"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. after 35 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He lived long in Medina, surviving into the era after ʿUthmān's martyrdom, and the pillar to which he had bound himself — usṭuwānat Abī Lubābah, 'the pillar of repentance' — still stands named for him in the Prophet's ﷺ mosque. al-Ṣallābī records (pp. 1404–1406) that he remained bound to the pillar for six days before the news of Allah's pardon reached him, and that Qur'anic verses of repentance were revealed about him.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 948–950, 1404–1406", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 35 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When he ﷺ set out for Badr, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ charged ‘Abdullah ibn Umm Maktoom with the duty of leading Muslims in prayer in Al-Madeenah during his absence. Later on during the expedition, when he ﷺ had reached Ar-Rauhaa, the Prophet ﷺ sent Abu Lubaabah ؓ back to Al-Madeenah, appointing him as its temporary governor.", "page": 948}, "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ أَوْسِيٌّ أَفْشَى سِرًّا بِإِشَارَةٍ فِي حِصَارِ بَنِي قُرَيْظَةَ، ثُمَّ رَبَطَ نَفْسَهُ بِسَارِيَةٍ حَتَّى تَابَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ."}, {"id": "thabit-ibn-qays", "name": "Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās", "arabic": "ثابت بن قيس بن شماس", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad; the Orator (Khaṭīb) of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ orator among the Anṣār, remembered in the book for his loyalty in repaying an old debt of honour.", "story": "Thābit, the eloquent orator of the Anṣār, had once been saved in the Battle of Buʿāth by Zubayr ibn Bāṭā of the Banū Qurayẓah. After Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's judgement, Thābit repaid the favour: he secured from the Prophet ﷺ Zubayr's freedom, then his family's, then his possessions — but Zubayr, learning his companions had been executed, asked, 'What is the purpose of life without companions?' and resigned himself to death (Ch. 19). Thābit, promised Paradise by the Prophet ﷺ despite his fear that his loud voice had doomed his deeds, later fell at Yamāmah wrapped in his burial shrouds, fighting the apostates.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: sought to repay Zubayr ibn Bāṭā of Qurayẓah for saving his life at Buʿāth"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "12 AH — martyred at Yamāmah", "after_sirah": "At Yamāmah he shamed the faltering ranks — 'this is not how we fought with the Messenger of Allah!' — perfumed himself with ḥanūṭ, wrapped himself in his shrouds, and fought until he was killed. After his death he appeared in a dream directing a Muslim to recover his stolen armour and settle his bequests, and Abū Bakr executed the will — remembered as the only bequest carried out on the strength of a dream.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1416–1417", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 12 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Thaabit ibn Qais ibn Shimaas approached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, “Donate Az-Zubair the Jew to me (grant him his freedom for me), so that I can reward him for the help he gave me on the Day of Bu’aath (a war that took place prior to the advent of Islam).” The Prophet ﷺ granted Thaabit’s wish. Thaabit ؓ went to Az-Zubair ibn Baataa and said, “Do you know me?”", "page": 1416}, "who_ar": "خَطِيبُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مِنَ الْأَنْصَارِ، ذُكِرَ فِي الْكِتَابِ لِوَفَائِهِ بِرَدِّ مَعْرُوفٍ قَدِيمٍ أُسْدِيَ إِلَيْهِ."}, {"id": "zayd-ibn-arqam", "name": "Zayd ibn Arqam", "arabic": "زيد بن أرقم", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAmr", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The young Anṣārī whose truthful report of Ibn Salūl's treason was vindicated by Sūrah al-Munāfiqūn.", "story": "Sent back from Uḥud for his youth (Ch. 16), Zayd's great moment came on the Muṣṭaliq expedition: he overheard ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl's brazen kufr — 'if we return to Medina, the honourable will expel the inferior' — and reported it to the Prophet ﷺ. When Ibn Ubayy swore his innocence and was outwardly believed, Zayd was deeply disheartened, his honour bruised. The next morning Allah revealed the entirety of Sūrah al-Munāfiqūn, quoting Ibn Ubayy directly, and the Prophet ﷺ held Zayd by his ear and said, 'Allah has confirmed that you have spoken the truth!' (Ch. 17). He later settled in Kufa and narrated many hadith.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: sent back from Uḥud as too young", "Ch. 17: reported Ibn Salūl's treachery and was vindicated by Sūrah al-Munāfiqūn"], "badges": ["Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 66 AH, Kufa (also reported 68 AH)", "after_sirah": "He settled in Kufa and stood with ʿAlī at Ṣiffīn, and in his last years his old courage flared once more: he publicly rebuked ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād over the degrading treatment of al-Ḥusayn's severed head, weeping for what the Ummah had come to.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1313–1316", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 66 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ summoned me, and I told him what happened. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then summoned ‘Abdullah ibn Ubai and his companions, and they all swore that they never said those words. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ didn’t believe me, and instead believed them. I was overcome with grief of the kind I had never before been afflicted with.", "page": 1313}, "who_ar": "الْغُلَامُ الْأَنْصَارِيُّ الَّذِي صَدَقَ فِي نَقْلِ خِيَانَةِ ابْنِ سَلُولَ، فَصَدَّقَتْهُ سُورَةُ الْمُنَافِقِينَ."}, {"id": "rafi-ibn-khadij", "name": "Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj", "arabic": "رافع بن خديج", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The 14-year-old expert archer allowed to fight at Uḥud despite his age.", "story": "When the Prophet ﷺ sent back the Companions under fifteen before Uḥud, 14-year-old Rāfiʿ pleaded his case, his relatives assuring the Prophet ﷺ that he was an expert archer despite his age; the Prophet ﷺ accepted — prompting Samurah ibn Jundub to argue, and even wrestle Rāfiʿ, to win the same permission (Ch. 16). The book holds up these zealous youths against the seasoned Hypocrites who turned back in cowardice. Rāfiʿ fought Uḥud and the battles after it, carried an arrowhead in his body from the Prophet's ﷺ campaigns, and lived long in Medina as a jurist of agricultural rulings.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: pleaded his way into the army at Uḥud as a 14-year-old expert archer"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 73 AH, Medina (also reported 74 AH)", "after_sirah": "The arrowhead he carried from the Prophet's ﷺ campaigns — left in his body on the Prophet's ﷺ word that he would testify for him as a martyr on the Day of Judgement — reopened in his old age and caused his death around 73 AH, aged about eighty-six, the elder of the Anṣār in Medina and their jurist on tenancy and farming.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1112", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 73 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "At first, Raafai’ ibn Khadeej ؓ was among this group, but then the Prophet ﷺ gave him permission to fight when he ﷺ was told that Raafai’ ؓ was a skilled spear-thrower. When Samurah ibn Jundub ؓ - one of those who was ordered sent back - heard about Raafai’ ؓ being given permission to fight, he ؓ went to his mother’s husband, crying and complaining about what had just happened.", "page": 1112}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ أَرْبَعَ عَشْرَةَ سَنَةً، الرَّامِي الْمَاهِرُ الَّذِي أُذِنَ لَهُ فِي الْقِتَالِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ عَلَى صِغَرِ سِنِّهِ."}, {"id": "abu-said-al-khudri", "name": "Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī", "arabic": "أبو سعيد الخدري", "kunya_titles": "Abū Saʿīd (kunya); name Saʿd ibn Mālik ibn Sinān", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Young Anṣārī turned back from Uḥud who became one of the most prolific narrators of hadith.", "story": "Abū Saʿīd was among the adolescents the Prophet ﷺ sent back from Uḥud for being under fifteen (Ch. 16). He narrated the vivid scene of the flooded mosque in 8 AH: 'For by Allah I saw the Prophet ﷺ prostrate into the mud, and the mud was on his forehead and nose' (Ch. 13). His father Mālik ibn Sinān was martyred at Uḥud, and Abū Saʿīd grew into one of the seven most prolific narrators among all the Companions, a scholar of the Anṣār who taught in Medina for decades.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: sent back from Uḥud as too young", "Ch. 13: narrated the Prophet ﷺ prostrating in the mud of the flooded mosque"], "badges": ["Khandaq", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 74 AH, Medina (also reported 63–65 AH)", "after_sirah": "He became one of the seven most prolific narrators of the Companions — over a thousand hadith — and a fountain of fatwa in Medina for half a century, living through the tragedy of al-Ḥarrah in his old age and dying in Medina in 74 AH, buried in al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1112", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 74 AH", "who_ar": "فَتًى أَنْصَارِيٌّ رُدَّ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ، ثُمَّ صَارَ مِنْ أَكْثَرِ الصَّحَابَةِ رِوَايَةً لِلْحَدِيثِ."}, {"id": "umayr-ibn-al-humam", "name": "ʿUmayr ibn al-Ḥumām", "arabic": "عمير بن الحمام", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Badr martyr who threw away his dates to race to Paradise.", "story": "As the Quraysh charged at Badr, the Prophet ﷺ called, 'Stand up and charge towards a Paradise whose width is like the Heavens and the Earth.' ʿUmayr made a sound of wonder, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked why, he said that if one level of Paradise truly equalled the Heavens and Earth, 'what use is it to remain living on this Earth? I want to be of those people!' The Prophet ﷺ replied, 'You are of them.' ʿUmayr threw away the dates in his hand — 'If I live long enough to finish these dates, I would have lived too long of a life!' — charged the enemy, and met his martyrdom (Ch. 14). He is remembered as among the first of the Anṣār to fall in battle for Islam.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: threw away his dates and charged to martyrdom at Badr"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "2 AH — martyred at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 975"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ said, “Then you are indeed one of its people.” ‘Umair ؓ then removed some dates from his quiver and began to eat them; he paused and then said, “If I stay alive until I finish eating these dates, then it is truly a long life.” Having said that, he cast away the dates he had with him and rushed to face the enemy and continued to fight them until he achieved martyrdom.", "page": 975}, "who_ar": "شَهِيدُ بَدْرٍ الَّذِي أَلْقَى تَمَرَاتِهِ مِنْ يَدِهِ لِيُسَابِقَ إِلَى الْجَنَّةِ."}, {"id": "haram-ibn-milhan", "name": "Ḥarām ibn Milḥān", "arabic": "حرام بن ملحان", "kunya_titles": "Maternal uncle of Anas ibn Mālik", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The envoy of Biʾr Maʿūnah who died proclaiming, 'I won, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!'", "story": "Ḥarām was chosen by the seventy learned Companions at Biʾr Maʿūnah to deliver their letter to the chieftain ʿĀmir ibn Ṭufayl — who, in violation of every tribal law of amnesty for messengers, signalled his guard to kill him. Speared through the back, the blade exiting his chest, Ḥarām exclaimed in his last waking moment, 'I won, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!' and died instantly (Ch. 17). One of the guards was so astonished by his delight at martyrdom that he began enquiring about Islam and later became a Muslim. A brother of Umm Sulaym and maternal uncle of Anas ibn Mālik, his words became the emblem of the seventy martyrs of Biʾr Maʿūnah. al-Ṣallābī names the man — Jabbār ibn Salmā, the very spearman who killed him — who was so shaken by Ḥarām's dying words that he sought out Islam and became a Muslim (p. 1238).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: martyred as envoy at Biʾr Maʿūnah — 'I won, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah!'"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Martyr", "Ahl al-Ṣuffah"], "death": "4 AH — martyred at Biʾr Maʿūnah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1236, 1238"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "During the slaughter, one of the attackers approached Anas’s uncle, Haraam ؓ, from behind and ran his spear through him; as this was happening to him, Haraam said, “By the Lord of the Ka’bah, I have indeed achieved success!” Allah answered the supplication of those Muslims, for back in Al-Madeenah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions ؓ, “Verily, your brothers have been killed.”", "page": 1236}, "who_ar": "الرَّسُولُ الَّذِي قُتِلَ فِي بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ وَهُوَ يُنَادِي: «فُزْتُ وَرَبِّ الْكَعْبَةِ!»."}, {"id": "qays-ibn-abi-sasaah", "name": "Qays ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah", "arabic": "قيس بن أبي صعصعة", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī entrusted with commanding the rear-guard at Badr.", "story": "At Badr, when the Prophet ﷺ divided the troops — ʿAlī leading the Muhājirūn on the left and Saʿd ibn Muʿādh the Anṣār on the right — he designated a back-up group under Qays ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah (Ch. 14). This quiet trust in a rear-guard commander speaks to his reliability among the Khazraj, of whom he was a man of standing.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: commanded the designated back-up group at Badr"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 955"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "After witnessing the sincerity and high level of obedience of his Companions ؓ, the Prophet ﷺ gave the white banner of his army to Mus’ab ibn ‘Umair ؓ and the two black banners to Sa’d ibn Mu’aadh ؓ and ‘Alee ibn Abee Taalib ؓ respectively. And he ﷺ placed Qais ibn Abee Sa’sa’ah ؓ at the back of his army.", "page": 955}, "who_ar": "أَنْصَارِيٌّ وُكِلَتْ إِلَيْهِ قِيَادَةُ السَّاقَةِ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "abu-khaythamah", "name": "Abū Khaythamah", "arabic": "أبو خيثمة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Khaythamah (kunya); name ʿAbdullāh ibn Khaythamah al-Sālimī", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Tabūk latecomer who left the shade of his garden to race after the Prophet ﷺ.", "story": "Abū Khaythamah procrastinated until the army left for Tabūk without him. Entering his garden, he found his two wives had prepared food in the shade of a tree — and the sight jolted him: 'What am I doing here? I am here in this pleasure while the Prophet ﷺ is in the heat of the desert? By Allah I will not enter any of your gardens until I join the Messenger of Allah.' He rushed out alone across the desert, and when the Prophet ﷺ saw a lone rider in the distance he said, 'May it be Abū Khaythamah!' It was, and the Prophet ﷺ received his confession endearingly: 'Woe to you, O Abū Khaythamah!' — he had saved himself at the last moment from the fate of the three who stayed behind (Ch. 27). al-Ṣallābī, who devotes a section to his story (pp. 1836–1838), notes that he crossed some seven hundred miles of desert to catch the army, overtaking ʿUmayr ibn Wahb al-Jumaḥī on the way, and holds him up as the model of the believer who slips and then swiftly rights himself.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 27: abandoned the comfort of his garden to catch up with the army at Tabūk"], "badges": ["Uḥud"], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1836–1838"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "الْمُتَأَخِّرُ عَنْ غَزْوَةِ تَبُوكَ، تَرَكَ ظِلَّ بُسْتَانِهِ مُسْرِعًا يَلْحَقُ بِالنَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"id": "bishr-ibn-al-barra", "name": "Bishr ibn al-Barrāʾ", "arabic": "بشر بن البراء", "kunya_titles": "Son of al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī veteran who died of the poisoned meat served to the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar.", "story": "Bishr, son of the ʿAqabah spokesman al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr and a veteran of Badr and Uḥud, was eating beside the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar when a Jewish woman served them a poisoned lamb. The Prophet ﷺ tasted a piece and announced, 'The shoulder of the lamb has told me that it has been poisoned' — but Bishr had already swallowed his bite. He fell severely ill and eventually died from the poison (Ch. 21). The book notes, via Imam Ibn al-Qayyim, that although the Prophet ﷺ forgave the woman for what she did to him, she was punished for Bishr's murder under the divine mandate of qiṣāṣ.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: swallowed the poisoned meat at Khaybar and died of it"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "7 AH — died of the Khaybar poison", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1605"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 7 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "First she asked what part of a sheep the Prophet ﷺ liked best, and when she was told that he ﷺ liked the arm best, she placed extra poison on it. When the shoulder piece of the sheep was presented to him, the Prophet ﷺ chewed a piece of it but did not swallow. Bishr ibn Al-Baraa ؓ did swallow a piece, however, and he died as a result.", "page": 1605}, "who_ar": "مِنْ قُدَمَاءِ صَحَابَةِ الْأَنْصَارِ، مَاتَ مِنْ أَثَرِ اللَّحْمِ الْمَسْمُومِ الَّذِي قُدِّمَ لِلنَّبِيِّ ﷺ بِخَيْبَرَ."}, {"id": "nusaybah-bint-kab", "name": "Nusaybah bint Kaʿb", "arabic": "نسيبة بنت كعب", "kunya_titles": "Umm ʿUmārah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "Heroine of the Anṣār who pledged at the second ʿAqabah and shielded the Prophet ﷺ with her body at Uḥud.", "story": "Nusaybah bint Kaʿb — Umm ʿUmārah — was one of only two women, alongside Asmāʾ bint ʿAmr, among the 75 who gave the second Pledge of ʿAqabah, 'The Covenant of War,' pledging in equal standing to the men by raising her hand towards the Prophet ﷺ, who never touched a non-maḥram woman (Ch. 11). The book says of her, simply and stunningly, that she 'lived the rest of her life on the battlefield.' At Uḥud she suffered twelve wounds — she had come to give water to the wounded, and when the lines broke she took up sword and shield to defend the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself, who is well-known to have said that wherever he turned that day, right or left, he saw Umm ʿUmārah fighting before him. She went on serving in campaign after campaign for decades: she was present at Ḥudaybiyyah and at the wars after the Prophet's ﷺ passing, and at the Battle of Yarmūk against the Romans her hand was amputated (Ch. 11). She also bore the loss of her son Ḥabīb, tortured to death by the false prophet Musaylimah, and fought at Yamāmah to avenge him. Few Companions, male or female, so completely embodied the ʿAqabah oath of war.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: one of two women who gave the second Pledge of ʿAqabah, 'The Covenant of War'", "Ch. 11: suffered 12 wounds at Uḥud; fought for decades until her hand was amputated at Yarmūk"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "d. c. 13 AH, Medina — after recovering from her Yamāmah wounds", "after_sirah": "At Yamāmah (12 AH) she fought through eleven or twelve wounds seeking her son's killer, and it was in that battle — in the standard account of Ibn Saʿd and al-Iṣāba, rather than at Yarmūk — that her hand was severed. Abū Bakr and ʿUmar visited and honoured her in her final years, and she died around 13 AH. al-Ṣallābī confirms this account (p. 606): at Yamāmah she fought until she bore twelve wounds, and it was there that she lost her hand. (For Uḥud he relates her grandson Ḍamrah's count of thirteen wounds, still traceable on her body when she was washed at death, pp. 1174–1175.)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 596, 605–606, 1174–1175", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 13 AH", "who_ar": "بَطَلَةُ الْأَنْصَارِ، بَايَعَتْ فِي الْعَقَبَةِ الثَّانِيَةِ، وَوَقَتِ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ بِنَفْسِهَا يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "hanzalah", "name": "Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī ʿĀmir", "arabic": "حنظلة بن أبي عامر", "kunya_titles": "Ghasīl al-Malāʾikah — 'the one washed by the Angels'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The newlywed martyr of Uḥud whose body the Angels washed.", "story": "Ḥanẓalah was martyred at Uḥud and is famously described as the one washed by the Angels. A newlywed, he was intimate with his wife on the morning of the battle and had no time to bathe before rushing out to fight. The Prophet ﷺ described seeing Ḥanẓalah's body being raised and washed by the Angels — though martyrs are not ordinarily washed — as they carried him to the Heavens (Ch. 16). By poignant contrast, his father was Abū ʿĀmir 'the Monk,' the embittered Awsī leader who fought on the side of the Quraysh at that very battle; the son ascended washed by Angels while the father earned the title al-Fāsiq. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1139) that just before his death Ḥanẓalah had struck down the horse of Abū Sufyān himself and was on the point of finishing him when a polytheist ran him through with a spear.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: martyred at Uḥud; the Prophet ﷺ saw the Angels washing his body"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "3 AH — martyred at Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1139–1141"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "What happened to Hanzalah ؓ was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said, “Verily, between the heavens and the earth I saw the angels washing him with the water of Al-Muzn in sheets of silver.” He ﷺ then said to his Companions ؓ, “Ask his family about his situation.”", "page": 1139}, "who_ar": "الْعَرُوسُ الشَّهِيدُ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ الَّذِي غَسَّلَتْهُ الْمَلَائِكَةُ."}, {"id": "asim-ibn-thabit", "name": "ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit", "arabic": "عاصم بن ثابت", "kunya_titles": "Protected by the swarm of wasps", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Leader of the ten Companions ambushed at al-Rajīʿ, whose body Allah protected by wasps and a flood.", "story": "ʿĀṣim led the ten learned Companions treacherously lured out and ambushed by a hundred men at the well of al-Rajīʿ. At Uḥud he had killed the husband of a woman named Sulāfah, who offered a hundred camels for his skull to drink wine from it. Knowing the fate awaiting a captive, he vowed to fight to the death, praying, 'O Allah, inform our Prophet that we were sincere,' and, 'O Allah, as I protected your religion in the day, protect my body at night.' He fought until arrows and sword were spent and was killed — but when the bounty-hunters came for his head, a giant swarm of wasps stung away all who approached, and at nightfall a sudden flood, despite no rain, swept his body away. 'Allah certainly answered his duʿāʾ' (Ch. 17).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: led the al-Rajīʿ party; his body protected by wasps and swept away by a flood"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "4 AH — martyred at al-Rajīʿ", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1227–1228"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The leader of the Muslim delegates, ‘Aasim ibn Thaabit, refused to accept their offer; true, the Muslims were outnumbered about twenty to one and that death was more likely than not, but ‘Aasim did not want to surrender. He said, “Verily, I have indeed vowed never to accept the protection of a polytheist.” ‘Aasim ؓ then began to fight them, firing arrows at them until he ran out of them, and then fighting against them with his spear until it broke.", "page": 1227}, "who_ar": "أَمِيرُ الْعَشَرَةِ الَّذِينَ غُدِرَ بِهِمْ بِالرَّجِيعِ، حَمَى اللَّهُ جُثْمَانَهُ بِالدَّبْرِ وَالسَّيْلِ."}, {"id": "fudalah-ibn-ubayd", "name": "Fuḍālah ibn ʿUbayd", "arabic": "فضالة بن عبيد", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muhammad", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Companion whose would-be dagger attack at the Conquest of Mecca melted into love at the Prophet's ﷺ touch.", "story": "The book narrates that at the Conquest of Mecca, Fuḍālah — described there as a young man who could not accept the new order — concealed a dagger and approached the Prophet ﷺ during ṭawāf intending to kill him. The Prophet ﷺ turned, asked, 'Are you Fuḍālah?… What were you about to do, O Fuḍālah?', smiled at his flustered denial, and placed his blessed hand on Fuḍālah's chest. Fuḍālah later said, 'As soon as he removed his hand, there was no-one on Earth more beloved to me in the whole world than him,' and he immediately accepted Islam (Ch. 25). He went on to a distinguished career of jihad and scholarship, settling in Syria, where Muʿāwiyah appointed him judge of Damascus. (Note: al-Ṣallābī, following Ibn Hishām, gives the would-be assassin of the ṭawāf story as Fuḍālah ibn ʿUmayr ibn al-Mulawwiḥ al-Laythī, a Meccan, p. 1737 — a distinct man from the Anṣārī jurist Fuḍālah ibn ʿUbayd to whom the Syrian career and judgeship recounted here belong.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: his assassination attempt during ṭawāf transformed into love and Islam by the Prophet's ﷺ touch"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 53 AH, Damascus", "after_sirah": "In Syria he combined jihad and judgeship: he led campaigns by sea and land, deputised for Muʿāwiyah over Damascus, and when he died there around 53 AH Muʿāwiyah himself shouldered his bier, telling those with him to help carry a man whose like they would not carry again.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1737", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 53 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ laughed and said, “Ask Allah to forgive you,” after which he placed his hand over his chest. Fudaalah’s heart suddenly became calm, and he later said, “By Allah, no sooner did he raise his hand from my chest than he became the most beloved of Allah’s created beings to me.”", "page": 1737}, "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ هَمَّ بِطَعْنِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ بِخِنْجَرٍ يَوْمَ فَتْحِ مَكَّةَ، فَاسْتَحَالَ ذَلِكَ حُبًّا حِينَ مَسَّهُ ﷺ بِيَدِهِ."}, {"id": "thabit-ibn-arqam", "name": "Thābit ibn Arqam", "arabic": "ثابت بن أرقم", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Anṣārī who rescued the flag at Muʾtah after the three commanders fell.", "story": "When Zayd, Jaʿfar, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah had all been martyred at Muʾtah, Thābit ibn Arqam grabbed the flag and ran clear of the melee so that the army would not scatter without a standard, then turned to the Muslims to choose a commander — declining it himself and passing the leadership to Khālid ibn al-Walīd, whose genius saved the army (Ch. 24). A veteran of Badr, he was later killed fighting the apostates in the Riddah campaigns. al-Ṣallābī — who writes his name Thābit ibn Aqram — draws from him a lesson in leadership (p. 1663): a Badr veteran whom the army would gladly have followed, he insisted that the best-qualified man take command, though Khālid had been a Muslim barely three months.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 24: seized the flag at Muʾtah after the three commanders fell and passed command to Khālid"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud", "Martyr"], "death": "11 AH — killed in the Riddah campaign of Buzākhah", "after_sirah": "In the Riddah wars he rode out with ʿUkkāshah ibn Miḥṣan scouting ahead of Khālid ibn al-Walīd's army, and the two were intercepted and killed by the false prophet Ṭulayḥah al-Asadī and his brother before the Battle of Buzākhah in 11 AH; Khālid avenged them when Ṭulayḥah's force was crushed.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1656, 1663", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 11 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "It is related in Imtaa’ Al-Asmaa’ that, once he picked up the banner, Thaabit ibn Aqram ؓ turned to Khaalid ibn Al-Waleed ؓ and said, “Take the banner, O Abu Sulaimaan.” Khaalid ؓ said, “I will not take it. You are more deserving of it: You are older and more experienced, and you took part in Badr.” Thaabit ؓ replied, “Take it, man, for by Allah, I picked it up only for you.”", "page": 1656}, "who_ar": "الْأَنْصَارِيُّ الَّذِي أَنْقَذَ الرَّايَةَ يَوْمَ مُؤْتَةَ بَعْدَ مَصْرَعِ الْأُمَرَاءِ الثَّلَاثَةِ."}, {"id": "samurah-ibn-jundub", "name": "Samurah ibn Jundub", "arabic": "سمرة بن جندب", "kunya_titles": "Raised among the Anṣār of Medina", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The boy who wrestled his way into the army at Uḥud.", "story": "When 14-year-old Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj was allowed to fight at Uḥud for his archery, Samurah argued his own case, claiming to be stronger than Rāfiʿ — some narrations note he wrestled Rāfiʿ to prove his superiority — and the Prophet ﷺ duly accepted his plea (Ch. 16). The book holds these zealous youths up against the veterans who fled. Raised in Medina among the Anṣār after his mother remarried there, Samurah later settled in Basra and became a narrator of hadith and, at times, its governor.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: wrestled Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj to earn his place in the army at Uḥud"], "badges": ["Uḥud"], "death": "d. c. 59 AH, Basra (also reported 58 or 60 AH)", "after_sirah": "He settled in Basra, where he served Ziyād ibn Abīhi as deputy — governing Basra (and for a period Kufa) in his absence — and transmitted a large body of hadith to the Basrans, dying there around 59 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1112", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 59 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ turned to Raafai’ ؓ and Samurah ؓ and said, “Wrestle (against one another).” The two of them then wrestled, and Samurah defeated Raafai’ ؓ, after which the Prophet ﷺ declared that both of them were allowed to fight in the upcoming battle.", "page": 1112}, "who_ar": "الْغُلَامُ الَّذِي صَرَعَ قِرْنَهُ فَأُجِيزَ فِي الْجَيْشِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "hudhayfah-ibn-al-yaman", "name": "Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān", "arabic": "حذيفة بن اليمان", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; Ṣāḥib al-Sirr — keeper of the Prophet's ﷺ secret regarding the Hypocrites", "group": "ansar", "tier": "major", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ confidant on the identities of the Hypocrites; the daring scout of the Trench.", "story": "Ḥudhayfah's story carries both tragedy and glory. At Uḥud, his aged father Ḥusayl ibn Jābir al-Yamān — exempted from fighting — came out seeking martyrdom, and in the chaos of Khālid's counter-attack the Muslims, not recognising him, killed him by mistake. Ḥudhayfah screamed, 'Stop! That is my father!' but could not be heard; afterwards he forgave them with the words of Prophet Yūsuf, 'May Allah forgive you, He is the Most Merciful of the merciful,' and distributed the blood money the Prophet ﷺ paid him among the poor of Medina (Ch. 16). At the Trench, on the freezing night of the storm, the Prophet ﷺ chose him by name — 'O Ḥudhayfah! You go and bring me news of the people' — to infiltrate the Quraysh camp. Shivering, he crept in, was nearly exposed by Abū Sufyān's identity-check, quick-wittedly grabbed the hand of the man next to him and demanded his name first, and returned with news of the enemy's collapse, warmed under the Prophet's ﷺ own blanket as he prayed (Ch. 19). Trusted uniquely with the names of the Hypocrites, he was consulted even by ʿUmar. He died in Madāʾin in 36 AH, just after ʿUthmān's martyrdom. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1055) that Ḥudhayfah and his father missed Badr only because the Quraysh intercepted them on the road and released them on a pledge not to fight alongside the Prophet ﷺ — who honoured it, saying, 'We will fulfil their pledge and seek Allah's help against them'; he also notes (p. 1108) that ʿUmar would watch whether Ḥudhayfah attended a funeral before praying over the deceased, so unique was his knowledge of the Hypocrites' names.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: his father Ḥusayl al-Yamān killed by mistake at Uḥud; he forgave and gave away the blood money", "Ch. 19: the night infiltration of the Quraysh camp at the Trench", "Ch. 19: chosen by name by the Prophet ﷺ; warmed in his ﷺ blanket on return"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. 36 AH, al-Madāʾin — forty days after ʿUthmān's death", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar appointed him governor of al-Madāʾin, where he lived with famous austerity, and it was his alarm at the recitation disputes he witnessed on the Armenia–Azerbaijan campaigns that moved ʿUthmān to commission the unified muṣḥaf — 'rescue this Ummah before they differ over the Book as the Jews and Christians differed.' He died in al-Madāʾin in 36 AH, about forty days after ʿUthmān's murder, grieving the fitna he had long foretold.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1055, 1108, 1149, 1383–1386", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 36 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Both Hudhaifah ibn Al-Yamaan ؓ and his father ؓ sincerely wanted to participate in the Battle of Badr but were prevented from doing so because of a vow they were forced to make. Hudhaifah said, “The only reason why we did not participate in Badr has to do with what happened to us when we traveled (from Makkah to Al-Madeenah) with the intention of going to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. On the way, we were captured by the Quraish.”", "page": 1055}, "who_ar": "صَاحِبُ سِرِّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ فِي أَسْمَاءِ الْمُنَافِقِينَ، وَعَيْنُهُ الْجَرِيءُ يَوْمَ الْخَنْدَقِ."}, {"id": "salit-ibn-al-numan", "name": "Salīṭ ibn al-Nuʿmān", "arabic": "سليط بن النعمان", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Muslim informant in Mecca whose tip led to the Qaradah caravan raid.", "story": "When a drunken Qurashī tribesman boasted of the Quraysh's secret plan to reroute their Syria trade, a Muslim in the gathering — Salīṭ ibn al-Nuʿmān, still residing in Mecca — immediately sent word to the Prophet ﷺ. Acting on this intelligence, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah intercepted the caravan at Qaradah, seizing over 50,000 dirhams without casualties, a tremendous blow that hastened the road to Uḥud (Ch. 15). Salīṭ is counted among the Companions of the early Medinan period whose quiet service mattered as much as swords.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: his secret message from Mecca enabled the Qaradah caravan raid"], "badges": [], "death": "", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "الْمُسْلِمُ الَّذِي كَانَ يَنْقُلُ الْأَخْبَارَ مِنْ مَكَّةَ، فَكَانَ خَبَرُهُ سَبَبَ سَرِيَّةِ الْقَرَدَةِ."}, {"id": "khubayb-ibn-adiyy", "name": "Khubayb ibn ʿAdiyy", "arabic": "خبيب بن عدي", "kunya_titles": "First to establish the Sunnah of two rakʿahs before execution", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Badr veteran captured at al-Rajīʿ and executed in Mecca, whose final two rakʿahs became a Sunnah.", "story": "Khubayb was one of three survivors of the al-Rajīʿ ambush, captured after a false promise of safety and sold to the Banū Ḥārith, whose tribesman he had killed at Badr. Awaiting execution, he asked for a blade to purify himself to meet Allah; when a baby crawled to him and its mother panicked, he said, 'Are you scared that I would kill this child? By Allah I would never do such a thing' — and she later testified, 'I have never seen a prisoner nobler or more well-mannered than him.' Before his execution he prayed two units, saying, 'Were it not for the fact that you'd think I was being cowardly, I would have lengthened my prayer.' His own ijtihād, approved by the Prophet ﷺ, became the Sunnah of praying two rakʿahs before execution (Ch. 17). al-Ṣallābī records (p. 1229) his final imprecation — 'O Allah, count their numbers, kill them one by one, and leave not one of them' — and his answer to Abū Sufyān that he would not wish himself safe with his family at the price of the Prophet ﷺ being pricked by a single thorn.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: captured at al-Rajīʿ, honoured even by his captors, initiated the Sunnah of two rakʿahs before execution"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "4 AH — executed near Mecca after al-Rajīʿ", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1228–1230"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "who_ar": "شَهِدَ بَدْرًا، ثُمَّ أُسِرَ يَوْمَ الرَّجِيعِ وَقُتِلَ بِمَكَّةَ، فَصَارَتْ رَكْعَتَاهُ الْأَخِيرَتَانِ سُنَّةً."}, {"id": "al-bara-ibn-marur", "name": "Al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr", "arabic": "البراء بن معرور", "kunya_titles": "Abū Bishr; first to clasp the Prophet's ﷺ hand at the second ʿAqabah", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Khazrajī elder who stepped forward first at the second Pledge of ʿAqabah.", "story": "When the Prophet ﷺ asked the 75 of Yathrib to pledge protection 'as one of your own,' al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr stood first and declared, 'We are a people experienced in the art of war; we have inherited it from our forefathers. Stretch forth your hand and we will give you allegiance' (Ch. 11). Father of Bishr ibn al-Barrāʾ, he died shortly before the Prophet ﷺ reached Medina, bequeathing that his grave face the Kaʿbah; the Prophet ﷺ is well-known to have prayed at his grave upon arrival. al-Ṣallābī relates his famous ijtihād (p. 602): on the road to Mecca he refused to turn his back on the Kaʿbah and prayed facing it while all his companions faced Jerusalem, until the Prophet ﷺ told him, 'You were upon a qiblah; would that you had remained patient upon it' — and he returned to facing Jerusalem.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: first to rise and demand the Prophet's ﷺ hand at the second Pledge of ʿAqabah"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 1 AH, Medina — about a month before the Prophet's ﷺ arrival", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 597, 601–602", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Sīrat Ibn Hishām"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 1 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Baraa ibn Ma’roor ؓ took him by the hand and said, ‘Yes, and by the One Who has sent you with the truth, we will protect you from that which we protect our Uzur (Uzur literally means clothing; what is intended here is women or souls, so that the meaning of his words is: We will protect you from that which we protect our women and our own selves). So take our pledge, O Messenger of Allah. By Allah, we are the people of war, and the people of weapons.’", "page": 597}, "who_ar": "شَيْخُ الْخَزْرَجِ الَّذِي كَانَ أَوَّلَ مَنْ تَقَدَّمَ لِلْبَيْعَةِ لَيْلَةَ الْعَقَبَةِ الثَّانِيَةِ."}, {"id": "khawwat-ibn-jubayr", "name": "Khawwāt ibn Jubayr", "arabic": "خوات بن جبير", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī trusted in the party sent to verify the Banū Qurayẓah's treachery at the Trench.", "story": "At the crisis point of the Trench, when word came that the Banū Qurayẓah had broken their treaty, the Prophet ﷺ sent a small trusted party — Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah, and Khawwāt ibn Jubayr — to verify the report discreetly (Ch. 19). Counted among the people of Badr because of the share and reward the Prophet ﷺ assigned him when injury halted his march, Khawwāt lived on into the caliphates and died in Medina.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: among the four sent to verify Qurayẓah's betrayal of the treaty"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 40 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He lived on in Medina through the caliphates, a companion of good humour whom ʿUmar teased about his youthful past, and died there around 40 AH aged seventy-four.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1012, 1367", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 40 AH", "who_ar": "أَنْصَارِيٌّ مَوْثُوقٌ بِهِ، كَانَ فِي النَّفَرِ الَّذِينَ بُعِثُوا لِيَسْتَيْقِنُوا غَدْرَ بَنِي قُرَيْظَةَ يَوْمَ الْخَنْدَقِ."}, {"id": "muadh-ibn-amr-ibn-al-jamuh", "name": "Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Jamūḥ", "arabic": "معاذ بن عمرو بن الجموح", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "One of the two Anṣārī teenagers who cut down Abū Jahl at Badr.", "story": "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf, hoping for strong soldiers beside him at Badr, was disappointed to find two small-framed adolescents — until each whispered his oath to kill Abū Jahl for disrespecting the Prophet ﷺ. When Abū Jahl came in sight, Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr leaped sword-first and sliced off his leg; ʿIkrimah's counter-blow left Muʿādh's arm dangling by a strip of skin, which he ripped off under his foot to keep fighting (Ch. 14). He and Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ both claimed the kill, and the Prophet ﷺ ruled, 'You both killed Abū Jahl.' Muʿādh survived his wound and lived into the era of the caliphs. (al-Ṣallābī, following the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, names the co-claimant Muʿādh ibn ʿAfrāʾ, and notes the spoils went to Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr as the one who delivered the decisive blow, p. 992.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: struck down Abū Jahl at Badr and tore off his own dangling arm to keep fighting"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. during ʿUthmān's caliphate, Medina", "after_sirah": "He survived the terrible wound of Badr and carried its mark through a long life in Medina, dying in ʿUthmān's caliphate.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 992", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "He ﷺ then said, ‘Both of you killed him, and his things (his armour, his sword, etc.) go to Mu’aadh ibn ‘Amr ibn Al-Jamooh.’ The two young men were Mu’aadh ibn ‘Afraa ؓ and Mu’aadh ibn ‘Amr ibn Al-Jamooh ؓ.", "page": 992}, "who_ar": "أَحَدُ الْغُلَامَيْنِ الْأَنْصَارِيَّيْنِ اللَّذَيْنِ أَجْهَزَا عَلَى أَبِي جَهْلٍ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "muawwidh-ibn-afra", "name": "Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ", "arabic": "معوذ بن عفراء", "kunya_titles": "Son of ʿAfrāʾ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The other teenage slayer of Abū Jahl, martyred at Badr.", "story": "The second of the two adolescents flanking ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf at Badr, competing with his friend Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr for the honour of killing Abū Jahl to avenge the Prophet ﷺ. As ʿIkrimah turned on Muʿādh, Muʿawwidh struck another blow to Abū Jahl, finishing the tyrant's fighting power; the Prophet ﷺ credited both youths with the kill (Ch. 14). He had also been among the three Anṣār who first rose for the opening duel (Ch. 14). Muʿawwidh fought on and was martyred at Badr itself, and his daughter al-Rubayyiʿ became a narrator of the Prophet's ﷺ description. (In al-Ṣallābī's account of the Ṣaḥīḥ narration the second youth is named Muʿādh ibn ʿAfrāʾ, his brother, p. 992; the sources famously interchange the two sons of ʿAfrāʾ in this scene.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: rose for the opening duel; struck Abū Jahl alongside Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "2 AH — martyred at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 992"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ said, “Both of you killed him,” in order to appease and console Mu’aadh ibn ‘Afraa, who, after all, did take part in the killing of Abu Jahl. In the end, it made no difference that Mu’aadh ibn ‘Afraa was not awarded the said spoils, for he was soon martyred during the very same battle.", "page": 992}, "who_ar": "الْغُلَامُ الْآخَرُ مِنْ قَاتِلَيْ أَبِي جَهْلٍ، اسْتُشْهِدَ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "awf-ibn-afra", "name": "ʿAwf ibn ʿAfrāʾ", "arabic": "عوف بن عفراء", "kunya_titles": "Son of ʿAfrāʾ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī who rose for the opening duel at Badr and was martyred there.", "story": "When ʿUtbah, Shaybah, and al-Walīd strode out demanding single combat at Badr, three of the Anṣār immediately rose: ʿAwf ibn ʿAfrāʾ, his brother Muʿawwidh, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah — but the Quraysh, thinking tribally, refused to fight the Anṣār and demanded their Meccan 'equals' (Ch. 14). ʿAwf plunged into the general battle that followed and was martyred at Badr, one of the celebrated sons of ʿAfrāʾ. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1002) that ʿAwf — 'Ibn ʿAfrāʾ' — asked at Badr, 'O Messenger of Allah, what makes the Lord laugh at His slave?' and, hearing 'That he plunges his hand into the enemy without armour,' cast off his coat of mail, took his sword, and fought until he was killed.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: among the three Anṣār who rose first for the duel at Badr"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "2 AH — martyred at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1002"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "According to a narration that is related by Ibn Ishaaq, ‘Auf ibn Al-Haarith - who was also known as Ibn ‘Afraa - said on the Day of Badr, “O Messenger of Allah, what can a slave (of Allah) do to make the Lord (Allah) laugh?” The Prophet ﷺ said, “For him to plunge his hand into the enemy (i.e., for him to attack the enemy), while he is not wearing armour.” ‘Auf removed the armour he was wearing, cast it off, took his sword, and fought hard against the enemy, continuing to do so until he was killed.", "page": 1002}, "who_ar": "أَنْصَارِيٌّ بَرَزَ لِلْمُبَارَزَةِ الْأُولَى يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ فَاسْتُشْهِدَ فِيهَا."}, {"id": "qatadah-ibn-al-numan", "name": "Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān", "arabic": "قتادة بن النعمان", "kunya_titles": "al-Awsī; 'the man whose eye was returned'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Awsī archer who shielded the Prophet ﷺ with his face at Uḥud and whose injured eye was miraculously healed.", "story": "At the desperate defence of the Prophet ﷺ at Uḥud, Qatādah used the bow the Prophet ﷺ handed him until it lost its flexibility, then shielded him ﷺ with his own body. 'I saw an arrow that I could not stop except with my face, so I blocked the Prophet ﷺ with my face and the arrow hit my eye.' The Prophet ﷺ wept and prayed, 'O Allah, Qatādah has protected your Prophet with his face, so let his eye become the most precise eye,' then removed the arrow — and Qatādah swore the eye healed instantly, becoming more precise than the other (Ch. 16). A Badr veteran of the Aws, he died in Medina in ʿUmar's caliphate, who descended into his grave.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: took an arrow in the eye shielding the Prophet ﷺ; the eye miraculously restored"], "badges": ["Badr", "Uḥud"], "death": "d. 23 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He died in Medina in 23 AH aged sixty-five, and ʿUmar descended into his grave; his memories of the Prophet's ﷺ battles reached the sīrah literature through his grandson ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, one of the great early authorities on the maghāzī.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1154", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 23 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Whether it was with an arrow, a spear, or a sword, Qataadah was struck by the enemy in the eye. His eye actually fell out of its socket and onto his cheeks. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the eye in his hand and returned it into Qataadah’s eye socket. And by the Grace and Mercy of Allah, a miracle occurred at the hands of the Prophet ﷺ: Qataadah’s eye returned to normal, as if it had never been dislodged from its socket; in fact, it became the stronger of his two eyes.", "page": 1154}, "who_ar": "رَامٍ أَوْسِيٌّ وَقَى النَّبِيَّ ﷺ بِوَجْهِهِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ، فَشُفِيَتْ عَيْنُهُ الْمُصَابَةُ بِمُعْجِزَةٍ."}, {"id": "abu-qatadah", "name": "Abū Qatādah", "arabic": "أبو قتادة الأنصاري", "kunya_titles": "Abū Qatādah (kunya); name al-Ḥārith ibn Ribʿī; 'the horseman of the Prophet ﷺ'", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Anṣārī warrior, cousin and best friend of Kaʿb ibn Mālik, famed as the Prophet's ﷺ cavalier.", "story": "The book shows Abū Qatādah at the hardest test of loyalty: during the boycott of the three who missed Tabūk, his cousin and best friend Kaʿb ibn Mālik jumped over his garden wall and begged him three times, 'Don't you know me to be a Muslim who loves Allah and His Messenger?' Abū Qatādah, in strict obedience to the Prophet's ﷺ command, would only say, 'Allah and His Messenger know best' — and Kaʿb burst into tears (Ch. 27). Beyond the book, he is well known as the horseman of the Prophet ﷺ, hero of the Dhū Qarad rescue, for whom the Prophet ﷺ prayed that his youth be preserved.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 27: refused to break the boycott even for his beloved cousin Kaʿb ibn Mālik"], "badges": ["Uḥud", "Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 54 AH, Medina (also reported 38–40 AH, Kufa)", "after_sirah": "He stood with ʿAlī through the fitna, and the sources genuinely split over his end: the majority have him dying in Medina around 54 AH aged about seventy, while others report he died in Kufa during ʿAlī's caliphate with ʿAlī himself praying over him.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1483, 1789–1790, 1875", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 54 AH", "who_ar": "فَارِسٌ أَنْصَارِيٌّ، وَابْنُ عَمِّ كَعْبِ بْنِ مَالِكٍ وَخَاصَّةُ أَصْحَابِهِ، اشْتُهِرَ بِأَنَّهُ فَارِسُ رَسُولِ اللهِ ﷺ."}, {"id": "kab-ibn-zayd", "name": "Kaʿb ibn Zayd", "arabic": "كعب بن زيد", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The sole survivor pulled from beneath the bodies at Biʾr Maʿūnah.", "story": "Of the seventy learned Companions slaughtered at Biʾr Maʿūnah, Kaʿb ibn Zayd was injured and trapped under the pile of bodies; assumed dead, he eventually escaped to carry his wounds home (Ch. 17). Allah decreed that his martyrdom be only delayed: he fell fighting at the Battle of the Trench the following year.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: survived the Biʾr Maʿūnah massacre from beneath the bodies"], "badges": ["Khandaq", "Martyr"], "death": "5 AH — martyred at the Battle of the Trench", "beyond_sources": ["Sīrat Ibn Hishām", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 5 AH", "who_ar": "النَّاجِي الْوَحِيدُ الَّذِي اسْتُخْرِجَ مِنْ تَحْتِ الْقَتْلَى فِي بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ."}, {"id": "al-mundhir-ibn-muhammad", "name": "Al-Mundhir ibn Muhammad", "arabic": "المنذر بن محمد", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Biʾr Maʿūnah Companion who chose to march back into the massacre rather than live to tell of it.", "story": "Al-Mundhir and ʿAmr ibn Umayyah had left the Biʾr Maʿūnah camp on an errand and returned to see vultures circling over their slaughtered companions. When ʿAmr suggested returning to inform the Prophet ﷺ, al-Mundhir answered, 'As for me, I will not be reduced to a messenger that lives to tell their story.' The two marched back in; al-Mundhir was killed, joining the martyrs he refused to outlive (Ch. 17). al-Ṣallābī records (p. 628) that in the housing of the Muhājirūn al-Mundhir's home received al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām and his wife Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr as guests.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: marched back into the Biʾr Maʿūnah massacre to die with his companions"], "badges": ["Badr", "Martyr"], "death": "4 AH — martyred at Biʾr Maʿūnah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 628"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ مِنْ أَصْحَابِ بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ، آثَرَ أَنْ يَرْجِعَ إِلَى الْمَقْتَلَةِ عَلَى أَنْ يَبْقَى حَيًّا يُخْبِرُ عَنْهَا."}, {"id": "al-mundhir-ibn-qudamah", "name": "Al-Mundhir ibn Qudāmah", "arabic": "المنذر بن قدامة", "kunya_titles": "", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Companion in charge of the Banū Qaynuqāʿ captives who stood up to Ibn Salūl.", "story": "When ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl marched up to the Banū Qaynuqāʿ captives demanding their release — 'Either do it or I will!' — the Companion in charge, al-Mundhir ibn Qudāmah, flatly responded, 'If you dare, I will kill you' (Ch. 15). A small moment, but a telling one: the discipline of the believers would not bend to the future chief of the Hypocrites.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: refused Ibn Salūl's demand to release the Qaynuqāʿ captives"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1067"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "When ‘Abdullah ibn Ubai passed by them and saw how their hands were tied up behind their backs, he said, “Untie them,” perhaps being under the delusion that, as in the pre-Islamic days of ignorance (when he was a venerated chief), his commands held weight. But Al-Mundhir ؓ, who was in charge of the prisoners, was not intimidated in the least by ‘Abdullah ibn Ubai; to the contrary, he answered with a threat, saying, “Do you want to untie a people who were tied up by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? By Allah, if any man unties them, I will strike his neck (with my sword).”", "page": 1067}, "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الَّذِي وُكِّلَ بِأَسْرَى بَنِي قَيْنُقَاعَ فَتَصَدَّى لِابْنِ سَلُولَ."}, {"id": "zayd-ibn-thabit", "name": "Zayd ibn Thābit", "arabic": "زيد بن ثابت", "kunya_titles": "Abū Saʿīd; chief scribe of revelation and compiler of the muṣḥaf", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "Young Anṣārī genius who became the Prophet's ﷺ foremost scribe and later compiled the Qur'an.", "story": "The book records Zayd among the adolescents the Prophet ﷺ sent back from Uḥud for being too young (Ch. 16) — but this boy of the Khazraj was already memorising revelation. It is well established that he became the Prophet's ﷺ principal scribe of revelation in Medina, learned the script of the Jews in a matter of days at his ﷺ command, fought at the Trench and after, and under Abū Bakr and again under ʿUthmān was entrusted with the momentous task of compiling the Qur'an into the muṣḥaf. He remained Medina's authority in Qur'an, farāʾiḍ, and fatwā until his death. al-Ṣallābī specifies (p. 1254) that he mastered the Jews' script in just fifteen days, and that on the Prophet's ﷺ arrival in Medina the boy of Banū al-Najjār was presented to him already carrying over a dozen sūrahs by heart.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: sent back from Uḥud as too young alongside Ibn ʿUmar, Usāmah, Abū Saʿīd, and Zayd ibn Arqam"], "badges": ["Khandaq", "Scribe of Revelation", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. c. 45 AH, Medina (also reported 42, 51 or 55 AH)", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar and ʿUthmān repeatedly left him as their deputy over Medina and entrusted him with the judiciary, the treasury, and the division of inheritances — the Companions deferred to him in farāʾiḍ above all others. At his death around 45 AH, Abū Hurayrah said, 'Today the scholar of this Ummah has died.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1112, 1254", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 45 AH", "who_ar": "نَابِغَةُ الْأَنْصَارِ الشَّابُّ، صَارَ أَكْبَرَ كُتَّابِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، ثُمَّ تَوَلَّى جَمْعَ الْقُرْآنِ بَعْدَهُ."}, {"id": "asma-bint-amr", "name": "Asmāʾ bint ʿAmr", "arabic": "أسماء بنت عمرو", "kunya_titles": "Umm Manīʿ", "group": "ansar", "tier": "minor", "who": "One of the two women who gave the second Pledge of ʿAqabah.", "story": "Asmāʾ bint ʿAmr was, with Nusaybah bint Kaʿb, one of only two women among the 75 who gave the second Covenant of ʿAqabah — 'The Covenant of War' — pledging in equal standing to the men by raising her hand towards the Prophet ﷺ, who never physically touched a non-maḥram woman (Ch. 11). Known in the wider literature by her kunya Umm Manīʿ, her presence at that midnight oath places her among the founding mothers of the Anṣār. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 606) that she was of the Banū Salamah, and that she is said to have been either the mother of Muʿādh ibn Jabal or his cousin.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: one of the two women of the second Pledge of ʿAqabah"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 596, 606"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "We left stealthily, concealing ourselves and moving just as quietly as doves move. When we finally gathered in the mountain-pass at Al-’Aqabah, we were a total of 73 men; and accompanying us were two of our women: Nusaibah bint Ka’ab ؓ and Asmaa bint ‘Amr ؓ. We waited in the mountain-pass until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us.", "page": 596}, "who_ar": "إِحْدَى الْمَرْأَتَيْنِ اللَّتَيْنِ شَهِدَتَا بَيْعَةَ الْعَقَبَةِ الثَّانِيَةَ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "Killed at the Battle of Buʿāth before the Hijrah — 'we are certain he died a Muslim'", "tier": "minor", "id": "suwayd-ibn-al-samit", "name": "Suwayd ibn al-Ṣāmit", "arabic": "سويد بن الصامت", "group": "ansar", "who": "The leading Khazraj poet who met the Prophet ﷺ at Hajj — his people said he died a Muslim.", "story": "The leading poet of the Khazraj came to Mecca for Hajj and sought out the Prophet ﷺ, offering his most eloquent verses: 'I have heard that you have eloquent speech. I have something similar.' The Prophet ﷺ replied, 'What you have is good, but what I have is better — Revelation from the Lord of the worlds,' and recited the Qur'an, leaving Suwayd dumbfounded by its divine eloquence (Ch. 11). He returned to Yathrib and was killed later that year in the Battle of Buʿāth; his people said, 'We are certain that he died a Muslim.' He stands at the very threshold of the Anṣār's story — the first Yathribite heart the Qur'an touched. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 581) that what Suwayd recited to the Prophet ﷺ was 'the Scroll of Luqmān'; the Prophet ﷺ heard it out, called it good, and offered what is better — the Qur'an — and that when Suwayd fell at Buʿāth his people were convinced he had died a Muslim.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: traded eloquence with the Prophet ﷺ at Hajj and was overcome by the Qur'an"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 581"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "شَاعِرُ الْخَزْرَجِ الْكَبِيرُ، لَقِيَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ فِي الْحَجِّ، وَقَالَ قَوْمُهُ إِنَّهُ مَاتَ مُسْلِمًا."}, {"id": "khalid-ibn-al-walid", "name": "Khālid ibn al-Walīd", "arabic": "خالد بن الوليد", "kunya_titles": "Abū Sulaymān; Sayf Allāh ('a sword from amongst the swords of Allah')", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "major", "who": "Son of al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah of Banū Makhzūm; the Quraysh's master tactician who became Islam's most celebrated general.", "story": "For years, Khālid's genius was aimed against the Prophet ﷺ. At Uḥud he commanded the Quraysh cavalry, and it was his flanking manoeuvre after the archers left their hill that turned the battle (Ch. 16). At the Trench he was among the few who crossed over, and before Ḥudaybiyyah he was sent to intercept the Muslims at Ghumaym (Ch. 19, 20). Yet he later confessed that every time he engaged, he sensed he was being turned away and that the Prophet ﷺ would be victorious — the final straw being Ḥudaybiyyah, when the Prophet ﷺ remained protected even while praying. During ʿUmrah al-Qaḍā', the Prophet ﷺ asked after him, saying it was not befitting for a man of his intelligence to neglect Islam. His brother al-Walīd wrote to him, and 'Islam entered my heart'. He travelled to Medina with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah and embraced Islam before the Conquest (Ch. 23). Less than a month later at Muʾtah, the Companions handed him the flag after the three commanders fell; he broke nine swords in his hand and saved the army, and the Prophet ﷺ named him 'a sword from amongst the swords of Allah' (Ch. 24). He led the Emigrants' contingent into Mecca at the Conquest (Ch. 25) and was later sent to invite tribes to Islam (Ch. 25, 28). He went on to lead the great conquests against Persia and Rome, dying on his bed in Ḥimṣ — famously lamenting that he had sought martyrdom in every battle. al-Ṣallābī adds (pp. 1643–1645) that before setting out Khālid saw a dream of leaving a narrow, barren land for one green and spacious — which Abū Bakr later interpreted as his passage from shirk to Islam — and that he first invited Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah and ʿIkrimah to join him, both refusing vehemently, before finding a willing companion in ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah; the party reached Medina in Ṣafar 8 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: commanded the Quraysh cavalry at Uḥud and exploited the archers' abandoned hill", "Ch. 19: one of the few horsemen to cross the Trench, duelling before crossing back", "Ch. 20: sent to intercept the Muslims before Ḥudaybiyyah", "Ch. 23: converted after his brother's letter relaying the Prophet's ﷺ words, travelling with ʿAmr and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah", "Ch. 24: took the flag at Muʾtah and rescued the army; named the Sword of Allah", "Ch. 25: led the Emigrants into Mecca and quelled the only skirmish at the Conquest"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 21 AH, Ḥimṣ (Syria) (some say 22 AH)", "after_sirah": "After the Prophet ﷺ he broke the apostate armies at Yamāmah, conquered Iraq, made the legendary desert march to Syria, and commanded at Yarmūk — never once defeated. ʿUmar removed him at the height of his fame, lest people put their trust in Khālid rather than Allah, and he obeyed like a soldier. He died on his bed in Ḥimṣ, with not a hand-span of his body free of scars, grieving that the Sword of Allah should die like a camel dies.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1641–1645 (conversion), p. 1656 (Muʾtah)", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 21 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ changed course towards the right and went around Khaalid and his men, making his way towards Al-Hudaibiyyah, where the famous treaty between him and the Quraish was signed. It was when the treaty of Al-Hudaibiyyah was signed that Khaalid had truly reached a crossroads in his life. He began to question himself and his faith.", "page": 1641}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ الْوَلِيدِ بْنِ الْمُغِيرَةِ مِنْ بَنِي مَخْزُومٍ، دَاهِيَةُ قُرَيْشٍ فِي الْحَرْبِ، صَارَ أَشْهَرَ قَادَةِ الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "amr-ibn-al-as", "name": "ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "arabic": "عمرو بن العاص", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; the shrewd statesman of the Quraysh; later conqueror and governor of Egypt", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "major", "who": "Son of the chieftain al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Wāʾil; the Quraysh's senior diplomat who became one of Islam's great commanders and statesmen.", "story": "ʿAmr represented the political elite of the Quraysh. When the Muslims found refuge in Abyssinia, it was ʿAmr — a skilled envoy with ties to the Negus — whom the Quraysh sent to lobby for their extradition; the Negus refused, telling him to be gone and take his gifts with him (Ch. 7). Years later, ʿAmr left Mecca to escape the Prophet's ﷺ message only to encounter it again in Abyssinia: Allah willed that the Negus himself convinced him of the truth, and ʿAmr accepted Islam at his hand. Returning to Arabia, he met Khālid ibn al-Walīd and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah on the road, heading to Medina for the same purpose — 'For how long will we deny it?' When the Prophet ﷺ stretched out his hand, ʿAmr pulled his back, stipulating that his past sins be forgiven; the Prophet ﷺ gave him the glad tiding, 'Don't you know that Islam erases everything before it?' (Ch. 23). Mere weeks after converting he was appointed commander of the Dhāt al-Salāsil expedition of 300 men, where he showed both discipline and cunning; when he protested that he had not accepted Islam for wealth, the Prophet ﷺ replied, 'How beautiful is pure money for a righteous man?' (Ch. 24). Under ʿUmar he conquered Egypt and governed it, dying there in 43 AH with famous words of hope and fear on his lips. al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1639–1640) that on a later embassy to Abyssinia ʿAmr had even asked the Negus to surrender the Prophet's ﷺ envoy, ʿAmr ibn Umayyah, so that he could kill him — and it was the king's furious refusal, and his manifest sincerity, that set ʿAmr's heart on the road to Islam.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: sent by the Quraysh to the Negus to demand the return of the Muslim refugees — and failed", "Ch. 23: accepted Islam at the hand of the Negus, then before the Prophet ﷺ, on the condition that Islam erases all that came before", "Ch. 24: appointed commander of the Dhāt al-Salāsil expedition despite being the newest Muslim"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 43 AH, Egypt", "after_sirah": "Under ʿUmar he conquered Palestine and then Egypt, founding al-Fusṭāṭ and the mosque that still bears his name — the first in Africa. He governed Egypt again in his old age for Muʿāwiyah, and died there on the night of ʿĪd al-Fiṭr, 43 AH, close to ninety.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1639–1641", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 43 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "A sudden change came over ‘Amr ؓ, the very change that comes over all people who suddenly become aware of the truth. ‘Amr ؓ said, “Will you take my pledge of Islam on his behalf,” to which An-Najaashee (may Allah have mercy on him) replied, “Yes.” An-Najaashee extended his hand, and ’Amr ؓ made the pledge of Islam, bearing witness that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.", "page": 1639}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ السَّيِّدِ الْعَاصِ بْنِ وَائِلٍ، وَكَبِيرُ سُفَرَاءِ قُرَيْشٍ، صَارَ مِنْ أَعْظَمِ قَادَةِ الْإِسْلَامِ وَسَاسَتِهِ."}, {"id": "uthman-ibn-talhah", "name": "ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah", "arabic": "عثمان بن طلحة", "kunya_titles": "Keeper of the keys of the Kaʿbah (Banū ʿAbd al-Dār)", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Hereditary custodian of the Kaʿbah's keys who embraced Islam shortly before the Conquest.", "story": "While still a pagan, ʿUthmān found Umm Salamah stranded with her child at Tanʿīm and, out of sheer chivalry, walked the entire road from Mecca to Medina escorting her, guarding her honour at every stop; she said she never knew a more honourable gentleman (Ch. 12). Allah repaid that nobility: he embraced Islam and emigrated alongside Khālid ibn al-Walīd and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, the last converts before the Conquest (Ch. 23). At the Conquest, the verse 'Allah commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners' was revealed concerning him, and the keys of the Kaʿbah were handed back to him — his descendants hold them to this day (Ch. 12, 25). al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1708) that when ʿUthmān murmured that Quraysh had been 'destroyed and humiliated', the Prophet ﷺ replied that today Quraysh had rather become 'prosperous and strong' — words ʿUthmān said filled him with hope as the key was placed back in his hand.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 12: escorted Umm Salamah on foot from Mecca to Medina while not yet Muslim", "Ch. 23: emigrated and embraced Islam together with Khālid and ʿAmr", "Ch. 25: the keys of the Kaʿbah were returned to him after the Conquest"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 42 AH, Mecca (a minority report has him fall at Ajnādayn, 13 AH)", "after_sirah": "He settled back in Mecca after the Prophet's ﷺ passing, holding the keys of the Kaʿbah as the Prophet ﷺ had restored them — 'forever, to be taken from you only by a tyrant.' From him and his cousin Shaybah the custodianship passed down the Banū Shaybah, who hold it to this day.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 617–619, 1644, 1707–1708", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 42 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "By Allah, I never accompanied any Arab man whom I think to be more honourable than him. If we reached a stopping place, he would make the camel kneel, and then he would move back, away from me (allowing me to descend in such a way that he could not see me). When I descended, he would then move away with my camel, remove from it its load, and tie it to a tree.", "page": 617}, "who_ar": "سَادِنُ الْكَعْبَةِ وَحَامِلُ مَفَاتِيحِهَا بِالْوِرَاثَةِ، أَسْلَمَ قُبَيْلَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "abu-sufyan-ibn-harb", "name": "Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb", "arabic": "أبو سفيان بن حرب", "kunya_titles": "Ṣakhr ibn Ḥarb; Abū Ḥanẓalah; paramount chieftain of the Quraysh", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "major", "who": "Leader of the Quraysh and commander of the campaigns against the Prophet ﷺ, who accepted Islam on the eve of the Conquest of Mecca.", "story": "Abū Sufyān led the enmity of Mecca for two decades — yet even in the early years he crept out at night with Abū Jahl and al-Akhnas to secretly listen to the Qurʾan (Ch. 6). It was his caravan that triggered Badr, and after Badr he swore not to bathe until he avenged it (Ch. 14, 15). He commanded the Quraysh at Uḥud and assembled the ten-thousand-strong Confederate coalition at the Trench (Ch. 16, 19). On the night before the Conquest, al-ʿAbbās brought him on the Prophet's ﷺ mule into the Muslim camp. Face to face with the Prophet ﷺ, he could only marvel — 'How gentle are you? How merciful are you?' — conceding, 'Had there been any other gods besides Allah, they would have helped me by now,' before finally uttering the shahādah. Honouring his pride, the Prophet ﷺ declared, 'Whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe,' and Abū Sufyān raced into Mecca urging his people to yield (Ch. 25). His faith was young — when Bilāl gave the adhān atop the Kaʿbah he stayed silent, 'for even the rocks would notify the Prophet ﷺ of what I would say' — but it settled and grew; he received a hundred camels from the spoils of Ḥunayn as his heart was reconciled (Ch. 25, 26). He lived to see his son Muʿāwiyah serve the Muslim state, and is reported to have lost his eyes in the path of Allah before dying in Medina in the caliphate of ʿUthmān. al-Ṣallābī adds (pp. 1696–1697) that at ʿAbbās's suggestion the Prophet ﷺ had Abū Sufyān held at the neck of the valley to watch the ten-thousand-strong army file past tribe by tribe, so that he would carry back to Mecca the certainty that resistance was hopeless.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: secretly listened to the Qurʾan at night with Abū Jahl and al-Akhnas", "Ch. 14–15: leader of the Badr caravan; swore vengeance after the defeat", "Ch. 16: commanded the Quraysh at Uḥud", "Ch. 19: led the Confederate armies at the Battle of the Trench", "Ch. 25: accepted Islam at Marr al-Ẓahrān; 'whoever enters the house of Abū Sufyān is safe'", "Ch. 26: given 100 camels from the spoils of Ḥunayn to strengthen his heart"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 32 AH, Medina (also reported 31, 33 or 34 AH)", "after_sirah": "He gave what remained of himself to the religion he had fought: one eye was lost at the siege of Ṭāʾif and, by the reports, the other at Yarmūk, where the old commander of the Confederates stood urging the Muslim lines on. He died in Medina in ʿUthmān's caliphate, close to ninety, and ʿUthmān prayed over him.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1693–1697, 1767", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 32 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Upon seeing Abu Sufyaan sitting on the back of the mule, ‘Umar exclaimed, “It is Abu Sufyaan, the enemy of Allah. All praise is for Allah, who has given me power over you when you have no pact or covenant (that guarantees your safety).” Al-‘Abbaas explained the purpose of his coming, but ‘Umar was not satisfied and wanted to kill Abu Sufyaan right then and there.", "page": 1693}, "who_ar": "زَعِيمُ قُرَيْشٍ وَقَائِدُ غَزَوَاتِهَا عَلَى النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، أَسْلَمَ لَيْلَةَ فَتْحِ مَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "hind-bint-utbah", "name": "Hind bint ʿUtbah", "arabic": "هند بنت عتبة", "kunya_titles": "Umm Muʿāwiyah; wife of Abū Sufyān", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Daughter of ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah and wife of Abū Sufyān; a fierce enemy of Islam who embraced it after the Conquest.", "story": "Her history must be told honestly. Enraged that Ḥamzah had killed her father ʿUtbah at Badr, Hind promised Waḥshī gold and jewellery to kill him, and after Uḥud she brutally mutilated Ḥamzah's body — cutting off his nose, making a necklace of his fingers, and by some reports biting his liver (Ch. 16). At the Conquest she smacked her own husband and demanded he be killed for urging surrender (Ch. 25). Yet days later she came veiled among the women pledging allegiance at Mount Safa, sparring wittily with the Prophet ﷺ over each clause of the pledge — 'Would a free lady fornicate?!' — until he accepted her Islam, though the memory of Ḥamzah remained raw and he did not prolong the conversation (Ch. 25). She lived on as a Muslim, and is remembered urging the fighters on at Yarmūk — this time for Islam. al-Ṣallābī relates (p. 1120) that at Uḥud Abū Dujānah stood over her with the Prophet's ﷺ sword raised — then turned it aside, refusing to strike a woman with the Messenger's ﷺ sword.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: put a bounty on Ḥamzah and mutilated his body after Uḥud", "Ch. 25: struck Abū Sufyān for preaching surrender, then took the women's pledge of allegiance and embraced Islam"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 14 AH, in the caliphate of ʿUmar", "after_sirah": "She died in ʿUmar's caliphate — on the very day, it is reported, that Abū Quḥāfah died. Between her pledge and her death she had stood at Yarmūk with the Muslim women, beating the drum and shaming any man who gave ground, as fierce for Islam as she had once been against it.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1120, 1711, 1714–1715", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنها", "dyr": "d. c. 14 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ibn Ishaaq related that Abu Dujaanah ؓ said, “I saw a person who was fighting fiercely against us, and I went towards him (in order to fight him). When I raised my sword over him, he made a howling noise, and I realized that it was a woman (i.e., it was Hind bint ‘Utbah), and I honoured the sword of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by not striking a woman with it (which is why I abstained from killing her).”", "page": 1120}, "who_ar": "ابْنَةُ عُتْبَةَ بْنِ رَبِيعَةَ وَزَوْجُ أَبِي سُفْيَانَ، كَانَتْ مِنْ أَشَدِّ أَعْدَاءِ الْإِسْلَامِ ثُمَّ أَسْلَمَتْ بَعْدَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "muawiyah-ibn-abi-sufyan", "name": "Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān", "arabic": "معاوية بن أبي سفيان", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān; founder of the Umayyad caliphate", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Son of Abū Sufyān and Hind who embraced Islam around the Conquest and later ruled the Muslim world.", "story": "Muʿāwiyah appears in this book among the recent converts of the Quraysh whose hearts the Prophet ﷺ chose to soften: after Ḥunayn, he was given generously from the surplus spoils alongside al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, for 'while they did not have the strongest faith, they had the most to gain from the Prophet's magnanimity' (Ch. 26). It is well established that he went on to serve as one of the scribes who wrote down revelation, governed Syria for twenty years under ʿUmar and ʿUthmān, and after the era of the Rightly-Guided Caliphs became the first Umayyad caliph, ruling from Damascus until his death in 60 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 26: gifted from the surplus spoils of Ḥunayn among the noblemen whose hearts were being reconciled"], "badges": ["Scribe of Revelation"], "death": "d. 60 AH, Damascus", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar set him over Damascus after his brother Yazīd ibn Abī Sufyān died in the plague of ʿAmwās, and Syria never left his hand: twenty years a governor, then — after the Year of Unity in 41 AH — twenty as caliph. He died in Damascus in Rajab 60 AH, in his late seventies.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1767", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 60 AH", "who_ar": "ابْنُ أَبِي سُفْيَانَ وَهِنْدٍ، أَسْلَمَ زَمَنَ الْفَتْحِ، ثُمَّ صَارَ إِلَيْهِ مُلْكُ الْمُسْلِمِينَ."}, {"id": "hakim-ibn-hizam", "name": "Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām", "arabic": "حكيم بن حزام", "kunya_titles": "Abū Khālid; nephew of Khadījah", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Nobleman of the Quraysh and Khadījah's nephew, a lifelong sympathiser who accepted Islam at the Conquest.", "story": "Long before Islam, it was Ḥakīm who bought young Zayd ibn Ḥārithah at the Souk of ʿUkkāẓ on behalf of his aunt Khadījah (Ch. 3). Though he remained on the other side for two decades, his decency kept surfacing: he secretly sent food to the besieged Banū Hāshim during the boycott and helped engineer its annulment (Ch. 8), and at Badr he went between the camps with ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah trying to prevent the war (Ch. 14). On the night before the Conquest he stood with Abū Sufyān at Marr al-Ẓahrān and entered Islam in its wake (Ch. 25). After Ḥunayn, when he kept asking the Prophet ﷺ for more of the spoils, the Prophet's ﷺ words about sweet and luscious wealth shook him — he vowed never to ask anyone for anything again, and kept that vow through a very long life (Ch. 26).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 3: purchased Zayd ibn Ḥārithah at ʿUkkāẓ for his aunt Khadījah", "Ch. 8: smuggled food to the Banū Hāshim during the boycott and helped end it", "Ch. 14: tried with ʿUtbah to avert the Battle of Badr", "Ch. 25: met the Muslim army at Marr al-Ẓahrān on the eve of the Conquest", "Ch. 26: moved by the Prophet's ﷺ counsel on greed, vowed never to ask of anyone again"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 54 AH, Medina, aged 120 (some say 58 or 60 AH)", "after_sirah": "He lived sixty years in Jāhiliyyah and sixty in Islam — born, uniquely among men, inside the Kaʿbah itself — and kept his vow so completely that he refused even his lawful stipend from ʿUmar, who called the Muslims to witness that he had offered it. He spent his last decades freeing slaves and giving in charity in Medina.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 963, 1693, 1767", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 54 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Hakeem went to Abu Jahl, in whose company a number of people were gathered, among whom was ‘Aamir ibn Al-Hadramee, brother of the said ‘Amr ibn Al-Hadramee. Hakeem tried to convince ‘Aamir and Abu Jahl to accept the blood money and to return to Makkah, but to no avail. Abu Jahl outright rejected the offer, saying in a derogatory manner, “Was not he (i.e., ‘Utbah) able to find a messenger other than you?”", "page": 963}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَشْرَافِ قُرَيْشٍ وَابْنُ أَخِي خَدِيجَةَ، لَمْ يَزَلْ مَائِلًا إِلَى الْمُسْلِمِينَ حَتَّى أَسْلَمَ عَامَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "suhayl-ibn-amr", "name": "Suhayl ibn ʿAmr", "arabic": "سهيل بن عمرو", "kunya_titles": "Abū Yazīd; the orator of the Quraysh", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "major", "who": "The Quraysh's most eloquent statesman and negotiator of the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, who became a devoted Muslim after the Conquest.", "story": "Suhayl fought the Prophet ﷺ at every turn. He offered only excuses when the Prophet ﷺ sought protection after Ṭāʾif (Ch. 9), and was the most senior captive taken at Badr (Ch. 14). At Ḥudaybiyyah the Quraysh sent him as their negotiator: he struck 'al-Raḥmān' and 'Messenger of Allah' from the treaty's text, and when his own son Abū Jandal staggered in chains begging refuge, Suhayl insisted he be returned as the first test of the very clause he had dictated (Ch. 20). At the Conquest he even joined the brief resistance, then hid in his house, telling his son, 'I do not know anyone still alive that has done more wrong to this man than me.' The Prophet ﷺ not only granted him protection 'by the protection of Allah' but instructed the Companions to show him respect, 'for he is a man of intelligence and honour... if he sees benefit, he will embrace Islam' (Ch. 25). He did, after Ḥunayn — and gave himself to Islam wholly. It is famously related that when the Prophet ﷺ passed away and Mecca wavered, Suhayl — the orator whose speech ʿUmar had once wished to see silenced — stood and delivered a speech echoing Abū Bakr's, holding Mecca firm upon Islam. He spent his final years in worship and jihad in Syria, dying in the plague of ʿAmwās.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: met the Prophet's ﷺ request for protection after Ṭāʾif with apologies", "Ch. 14: the most senior captive of Badr", "Ch. 20: negotiated the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, refusing even his own son Abū Jandal", "Ch. 25: forgiven at the Conquest and honoured by the Prophet's ﷺ instruction; accepted Islam after Ḥunayn"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 18 AH, Syria — plague of ʿAmwās (some reports have him fall earlier in the Syrian campaigns)", "after_sirah": "True to the Prophet's ﷺ word about his intelligence and honour, his late devotion was total: much prayer, much fasting, much weeping over the years he had lost, and a vow — once he set foot on the Syrian frontier — never to leave it. He died there in the plague of ʿAmwās. al-Ṣallābī cites al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār (p. 1727): he fasted and prayed until his face turned pale, wept much when the Qurʾān was recited, and at Yarmūk led one of the army's great battalions.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1523–1527, 1726–1727, 1767", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 18 AH", "who_ar": "خَطِيبُ قُرَيْشٍ الْأَبْلَغُ وَمُفَاوِضُهَا فِي صُلْحِ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، حَسُنَ إِسْلَامُهُ بَعْدَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "safwan-ibn-umayyah", "name": "Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah", "arabic": "صفوان بن أمية", "kunya_titles": "Abū Wahb; son of Umayyah ibn Khalaf", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Son of Umayyah ibn Khalaf; a bitter enemy after Badr who was won over by the Prophet's ﷺ clemency at the Conquest.", "story": "Ṣafwān lost his father and brother at Badr, and his grief hardened into plots: he financed ʿUmayr ibn Wahb's attempt to assassinate the Prophet ﷺ, vowing to care for his family (Ch. 15), helped drive the Quraysh to Uḥud (Ch. 16), and rejected Khālid's invitation to Islam in the sternest terms — 'If I were the last person on Earth, I would still not follow him' (Ch. 23). At the Conquest he fought, fled, and vowed to kill himself rather than live humiliated; but ʿUmayr, his old friend, begged the Prophet ﷺ for his amnesty and tracked him to Jeddah carrying the Prophet's ﷺ own turban as a pledge of safety. Granted four months when he asked for two, Ṣafwān returned, witnessed Ḥunayn, received a hundred camels from its spoils, and embraced Islam soon after; the book records that he later died as a martyr (Ch. 25, 26). al-Ṣallābī records his own words (p. 1731): 'The Messenger of Allah gave me what he gave me when he was the most hated of people to me, and he continued giving until he became the most beloved of people to me' — the shahādah following the Prophet's ﷺ gift of an entire mountain pass of livestock at al-Jiʿrānah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: sponsored ʿUmayr ibn Wahb's assassination attempt on the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 23: rebuffed Khālid ibn al-Walīd's invitation to Islam", "Ch. 25: fled at the Conquest, then returned under the Prophet's ﷺ guarantee — given four months instead of two", "Ch. 26: gifted 100 camels after Ḥunayn; accepted Islam soon after"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 41 AH, Mecca (some say 42 AH)", "after_sirah": "He stayed in his beloved Mecca — for the Prophet ﷺ had said there is no hijrah after the Conquest — and was counted among its most generous nobles, of those, he said, in whose valley wealth never slept. He died there around 41 AH, at the dawn of Muʿāwiyah's reign.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1728–1731, 1756", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 41 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Like Suhail ibn ‘Amr ؓ, Safwaan ibn Umayyah feared that he would be killed upon sight; but unlike Suhail, Safwaan was completely bereft of hope. He ran away from Makkah until he reached Ash-Shu’aibah, a port that was situated near Jeddah and that was often used by the inhabitants of Makkah. His plan seemed to be that he wanted to kill himself rather than be killed at the hands of the Muslims, and perhaps that explains why he took no one with him except for his servant, Yasaar.", "page": 1728}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ أُمَيَّةَ بْنِ خَلَفٍ، اشْتَدَّتْ عَدَاوَتُهُ بَعْدَ بَدْرٍ، ثُمَّ اسْتَمَالَهُ حِلْمُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يَوْمَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "ikrimah-ibn-abi-jahl", "name": "ʿIkrimah ibn Abī Jahl", "arabic": "عكرمة بن أبي جهل", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿUthmān; son of Abū Jahl", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Son of the Prophet's ﷺ arch-enemy who fought Islam to the very gates of Mecca, then died striving for it.", "story": "ʿIkrimah inherited his father's war: at Badr he severed Muʿādh's arm as the young man cut down Abū Jahl (Ch. 14), he commanded the right flank at Uḥud (Ch. 16), attempted the crossing at the Trench (Ch. 19), and led the only armed resistance at the Conquest of Mecca — earning a place on the kill-list (Ch. 25). Fleeing by ship to Abyssinia, a deadly storm broke his defiance; when even the captain admitted their idols were useless, ʿIkrimah vowed that if Allah saved him he would place his hand in the Prophet's ﷺ hand 'and I will find him to be forgiving and merciful.' He returned, declared the shahādah before the Prophet ﷺ, and lived the rest of his life striving for Islam, dying a martyr fighting the Romans (Ch. 25). al-Ṣallābī relates (pp. 1732–1734) that it was his wife, Umm Ḥakīm, who obtained his amnesty from the Prophet ﷺ and then pursued him to the coast of Tihāmah, pleading with him until he turned back with her.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: fought at Badr beside his father, maiming Muʿādh ibn ʿAmr", "Ch. 16: commanded the Quraysh right flank at Uḥud", "Ch. 25: led the resistance at the Conquest, fled by sea, and converted after surviving the storm"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "13 AH — martyred at Ajnādayn (or 15 AH at Yarmūk; the classical sources genuinely differ)", "after_sirah": "He redeemed the years spectacularly: in the Apostasy Wars and the Syrian campaigns he sought out the fiercest fighting, saying that he who had fought so long against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not now hold back from fighting for Him. He fell covered with wounds, fighting the Romans — at Ajnādayn by one report, at Yarmūk by the other — and with him fell others of his house.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1731–1735", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 13 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After the death of his father, Abu Jahl, ‘Ikrimah continued where his father had left off and assumed a leadership role among the Quraish in their war against the Prophet ﷺ; in fact, ‘Ikrimah became consumed with one purpose and one purpose only: to avenge the death of his father by destroying the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions ؓ. It is no wonder, therefore, that the universal pardon bestowed after the Makkah conquest did not apply to ‘Ikrimah; no, he was a wanted man and was to be killed upon sight, even if he were to be found hanging on to the curtain of the Ka’bah.", "page": 1731}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ أَلَدِّ أَعْدَاءِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، قَاتَلَ الْإِسْلَامَ حَتَّى أَبْوَابِ مَكَّةَ، ثُمَّ مَاتَ مُجَاهِدًا فِي سَبِيلِهِ."}, {"id": "umayr-ibn-wahb", "name": "ʿUmayr ibn Wahb al-Jumaḥī", "arabic": "عمير بن وهب الجمحي", "kunya_titles": "The would-be assassin whose heart turned", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Qurashī nobleman who set out to assassinate the Prophet ﷺ and returned as a caller to Islam.", "story": "ʿUmayr scouted the Muslim lines for the Quraysh at Badr (Ch. 14), and after the defeat he struck a secret pact with Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah: Ṣafwān would pay his debts and keep his family, and ʿUmayr would ride to Medina with a poisoned sword to kill the Prophet ﷺ. ʿUmar recognised him at once, but the Prophet ﷺ calmly recounted to ʿUmayr the private conversation in the Ḥijr word for word — a secret no soul knew but the two plotters — and ʿUmayr immediately bore witness that he was the Messenger of Allah (Ch. 15). The man who had tortured believers asked leave to return to Mecca and call them to Islam instead, converting a number before emigrating. Years later, at the Conquest, his old love for his cousin moved him to secure Ṣafwān's amnesty and coax him back to Mecca and, eventually, to Islam (Ch. 25).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: scouted the Muslim army before Badr", "Ch. 15: came to assassinate the Prophet ﷺ and left a believer when his secret plot was exposed by revelation", "Ch. 25: won the Prophet's ﷺ amnesty for Ṣafwān and brought him home"], "badges": [], "death": "Died in the era of the early caliphate — no precise year recorded in the classical sources", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1034–1037, 1728–1729", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Then ‘Umair said, “By Allah, had it not been for the facts that I am in debt, that I cannot pay off my debt, and that I have dependents whom I feel will waste away after I die, I would have traveled to Muhammad and not stopped until I killed him. For indeed, I have reason to show them enmity: My son is a prisoner in their hands.”", "page": 1034}, "who_ar": "شَرِيفٌ مِنْ قُرَيْشٍ، خَرَجَ لِيَغْتَالَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ فَرَجَعَ دَاعِيًا إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "al-harith-ibn-hisham", "name": "al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām", "arabic": "الحارث بن هشام", "kunya_titles": "Brother of Abū Jahl", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Abū Jahl's brother, who embraced Islam at the Conquest and grew into a man of piety.", "story": "When Bilāl gave the adhān atop the Kaʿbah at the Conquest, al-Ḥārith muttered with the old aristocratic disdain, 'By Allah, if I thought this man was upon the truth, I would have followed him.' The Prophet ﷺ later approached him and disclosed the private conversation word for word, and al-Ḥārith declared on the spot, 'You must be a Prophet, because no-one was listening' — and accepted Islam (Ch. 25). He was among the noblemen given generously after Ḥunayn to strengthen his new faith (Ch. 26), yet he outgrew the need: the book notes he grew in piety, sought knowledge from the Prophet ﷺ, and — as this book records — died a martyr at the Battle of Yarmūk. He is also the Companion who famously asked the Prophet ﷺ how revelation came to him (Ch. 5).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 5: asked the Prophet ﷺ how revelation descended upon him", "Ch. 25: converted when the Prophet ﷺ revealed his secret words at the Conquest", "Ch. 26: gifted from the spoils of Ḥunayn among the reconciled hearts"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "18 AH — died in the plague of ʿAmwās, Syria (martyrdom at Yarmūk also reported, as this book records)", "after_sirah": "When the jihad opened in Syria he left Mecca for good with his whole household, and the city wept at the departure of so noble a man. He fought through the Syrian campaigns and died in the plague of ʿAmwās in 18 AH — though the report of his martyrdom at Yarmūk is also preserved.", "beyond_sources": ["Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 18 AH", "who_ar": "أَخُو أَبِي جَهْلٍ، أَسْلَمَ يَوْمَ الْفَتْحِ وَصَارَ رَجُلًا تَقِيًّا."}, {"id": "abu-quhafah", "name": "Abū Quḥāfah", "arabic": "أبو قحافة (عثمان بن عامر)", "kunya_titles": "ʿUthmān ibn ʿĀmir; father of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Father of Abū Bakr; the oldest man in Mecca, who accepted Islam at the Conquest.", "story": "For twenty years Abū Quḥāfah mocked both the Prophet ﷺ and his own son for believing in him, even taunting Abū Bakr for freeing weak slaves like Bilāl (Ch. 6, 12). At the Conquest, old, blind, and unable to walk, he softened at last; his granddaughter Asmā' led him by the hand, and the Prophet ﷺ protested, 'Why didn't you leave the old gentleman for us to come to his house?' As his father's hand rested in the Prophet's ﷺ to pledge, Abū Bakr wept — wishing that this hand had been Abū Ṭālib's, so beloved to the Prophet ﷺ, rather than his own father's (Ch. 25). He outlived his son, dying in the caliphate of ʿUmar at a great age. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1736) that seeing the old man's head white as the thaghāmah plant, the Prophet ﷺ instructed that his hair be dyed — though not with black.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: brought by Asmā' to the Prophet ﷺ at the Conquest, where he pledged allegiance and accepted Islam"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 14 AH, Mecca, aged 97", "after_sirah": "He outlived the son who had believed before him, inheriting a share from the caliph Abū Bakr — which he returned to Abū Bakr's children — and died in Mecca in 14 AH at ninety-seven, having seen his household become the first family of Islam.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1736", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 14 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Abu Bakr’s father, Abu Quhaafah, was seated before the Prophet ﷺ, who passed his hand over his chest and said, “Embrace Islam.” Abu Quhaafah ؓ embraced Islam, and it is related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ congratulated Abu Bakr for his father’s entry into the fold of Islam. It is also related that, when the Prophet ﷺ saw that Abu Quhaafah’s head was extremely white (so much so that it was likened to a plant that was known for its whiteness), he ﷺ ordered for his hair to be dyed to another colour, but not to black.", "page": 1736}, "who_ar": "وَالِدُ أَبِي بَكْرٍ، وَأَسَنُّ رَجُلٍ بِمَكَّةَ، أَسْلَمَ يَوْمَ الْفَتْحِ."}, {"id": "wahshi-ibn-harb", "name": "Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb", "arabic": "وحشي بن حرب", "kunya_titles": "Abū Dasmah; the killer of Ḥamzah — and of Musaylamah", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Abyssinian slave whose javelin killed Ḥamzah at Uḥud; later a Muslim who killed the false prophet Musaylamah.", "story": "Waḥshī cared nothing for tribal politics or religious wars; he killed for his freedom. Promised manumission by his master Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, and gold by Hind bint ʿUtbah, he stalked Ḥamzah at Uḥud and felled him with a single javelin throw — he was the best thrower in Arabia (Ch. 16). Named on the kill-list at the Conquest, he fled to Ṭāʾif and accepted Islam when its people converted; the Prophet ﷺ heard his account of Ḥamzah's death, then asked him to keep his face from him thereafter, and Waḥshī complied for the rest of the Prophet's ﷺ life (Ch. 16, 25). He never mitigated what he had done, and in the Apostasy Wars under Abū Bakr he made duʿā' to redeem it — killing Musaylamah the Liar with the same javelin arm (Ch. 16). He struggled with alcoholism in later life, and the book cites the sobering ḥadīth that Allah's punishment may be hastened for some and deferred for others; he died in Ḥimṣ. al-Ṣallābī preserves his summing-up of his own life (p. 1135): 'I killed the best of people — Ḥamzah — and I killed the worst of people, Musaylamah the Liar.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: killed Ḥamzah at Uḥud with a javelin, in exchange for his freedom", "Ch. 25: on the kill-list at the Conquest; fled to Ṭāʾif and later accepted Islam", "Ch. 16 (noted): killed Musaylamah the Liar in the Apostasy Wars"], "badges": [], "death": "d. in Ḥimṣ, in the era of the early caliphate (no precise year recorded)", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1130–1131, 1134–1135", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "I waited in ambush for Hamzah, placing myself underneath a stone. When he approached me, I threw my spear at him, aiming at and hitting his private area, so that it came out on the other side (of his body) from his hips; he died as a result. When the people returned (to Makkah), I returned with them and stayed in Makkah until Islam spread there.", "page": 1130}, "who_ar": "عَبْدٌ حَبَشِيٌّ قَتَلَ حَمْزَةَ بِحَرْبَتِهِ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ، ثُمَّ أَسْلَمَ فَقَتَلَ مُسَيْلِمَةَ الْكَذَّابَ."}, {"id": "habbar-ibn-al-aswad", "name": "Habbār ibn al-Aswad", "arabic": "هبار بن الأسود", "kunya_titles": "Assailant of Zaynab bint Muhammad; later forgiven and Muslim", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Qurashī who attacked the Prophet's ﷺ daughter Zaynab during her emigration, and was later forgiven and accepted Islam.", "story": "His crime must be stated plainly: as Zaynab, the Prophet's ﷺ daughter, attempted to emigrate to Medina, Habbār attacked her camel, causing her to fall and miscarry the Prophet's ﷺ grandchild (Ch. 25). For this he was named on the kill-list at the Conquest of Mecca — one of only nine people excluded from the general amnesty (al-Ṣallābī, compiling the reports through Ibn Ḥajar, counts between thirteen and nineteen names, p. 1711). Yet while his fellow offender al-Ḥuwayrith was executed, Habbār managed to negotiate his forgiveness (Ch. 25). It is well established that he thereafter came to the Prophet ﷺ, declared his Islam sincerely, and bore with patience the rebukes of those who remembered his past — dying as a Muslim.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: named on the kill-list for causing Zaynab's fall and miscarriage, but negotiated his forgiveness"], "badges": [], "death": "Died a Muslim; no death date recorded in the classical sources", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1711", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "قُرَشِيٌّ اعْتَدَى عَلَى زَيْنَبَ بِنْتِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ فِي أَثْنَاءِ هِجْرَتِهَا، ثُمَّ عُفِيَ عَنْهُ فَأَسْلَمَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-sad-ibn-abi-sarh", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ", "arabic": "عبد الله بن سعد بن أبي سرح", "kunya_titles": "Former scribe of the Prophet ﷺ; later governor of Egypt", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "A scribe of the Prophet ﷺ who apostatized, then re-embraced Islam at the Conquest through ʿUthmān's intercession.", "story": "His story is told without softening: he accepted Islam, emigrated, and served as a scribe for the Prophet ﷺ — then apostatized, returned to Mecca, and spread slanders against Islam, earning a place on the kill-list at the Conquest (Ch. 25). He sought refuge with his foster-brother ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, who brought him before the Prophet ﷺ pleading for his forgiveness. The Prophet ﷺ kept a long, deafening silence — hoping, as he later explained, that someone would carry out the standing sentence, for a Prophet does not order a killing with a gesture of his eyes — before finally accepting his plea (Ch. 25). Allah willed his survival and his sincerity: he lived righteously, and the book notes his status grew until he became governor of Egypt, leading campaigns in North Africa. He died around 36–37 AH, reportedly passing away immediately after praying Fajr.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: on the kill-list for apostasy and slander; forgiven through ʿUthmān's intercession after the Prophet's ﷺ heavy silence"], "badges": ["Scribe of Revelation"], "death": "d. c. 36–37 AH, ʿAsqalān (some say al-Ramlah)", "after_sirah": "Under his foster-brother ʿUthmān he governed Egypt, led the conquest of Ifrīqiyah, and commanded the fleet at the Battle of the Masts — the Muslims' first great naval victory. When the fitna came he withdrew from it entirely, pledging to neither side, and prayed, 'O Allah, make Fajr the last of my deeds' — dying at ʿAsqalān moments after the dawn prayer.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1711", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 37 AH", "who_ar": "كَاتِبٌ مِنْ كُتَّابِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، ارْتَدَّ ثُمَّ عَادَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ يَوْمَ الْفَتْحِ بِشَفَاعَةِ عُثْمَانَ."}, {"id": "jubayr-ibn-mutim", "name": "Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim", "arabic": "جبير بن مطعم", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muḥammad; son of Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy, the Prophet's ﷺ protector", "group": "quraysh-late", "tier": "minor", "who": "Son of the chieftain who granted the Prophet ﷺ protection after Ṭāʾif; a late convert who became a noted narrator and genealogist.", "story": "Jubayr's father, Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy, sheltered the Prophet ﷺ upon his return from Ṭāʾif, and the Prophet ﷺ never forgot it — declaring after Badr, 'If Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy were alive, and he uttered a word about these captives, I would have released them all for him' (Ch. 14). Jubayr himself came to Medina regarding those captives while still a pagan, and it was there, hearing the Prophet ﷺ recite Sūrah al-Ṭūr at Maghrib, that faith first touched his heart — as he later narrated. Grief clouded that path for a time: aggrieved by his uncle Ṭuʿaymah's death at Badr, he promised his slave Waḥshī freedom for killing Ḥamzah at Uḥud (Ch. 16). He embraced Islam in the period between Ḥudaybiyyah and the Conquest, and lived on in Medina as a respected Companion, an authority in Qurashī genealogy, and a narrator of ḥadīth.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: the Prophet ﷺ honoured his father Muṭʿim's protection when speaking of the Badr captives", "Ch. 16: while still a pagan, offered Waḥshī his freedom to kill Ḥamzah in revenge for his uncle"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 58–59 AH, Medina (some say 57 AH)", "after_sirah": "He became the acknowledged authority of Quraysh in genealogy — a science he took from Abū Bakr himself — and a respected narrator whose Sūrah al-Ṭūr conversion account entered Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī through his sons. He died in Medina in the reign of Muʿāwiyah.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1016–1017, 1095", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 59 AH", "who_ar": "ابْنُ السَّيِّدِ الَّذِي أَجَارَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ بَعْدَ الطَّائِفِ، تَأَخَّرَ إِسْلَامُهُ ثُمَّ صَارَ رَاوِيَةً مَشْهُورًا وَنَسَّابَةً."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "8 AH — martyred at the siege of Ṭāʾif", "tier": "minor", "id": "abdullah-ibn-abi-umayyah", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah", "arabic": "عبد الله بن أبي أمية", "group": "quraysh-late", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ cousin and fiercest mocker — who met him on the road to Mecca and believed.", "story": "A cousin of the Prophet ﷺ and half-brother of Umm Salamah, ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah stood with Abū Jahl in the cruellest years, demanding the Prophet ﷺ ascend to heaven with a ladder and bring back an open book before he would believe (Ch. 8). Yet on the road to Mecca in 8 AH, he intercepted the army together with Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith — the two old mockers seeking forgiveness. Through Umm Salamah's intercession the Prophet ﷺ received them, and both embraced Islam before the Conquest (Ch. 25). He fought at Ḥunayn and was martyred at the siege of Ṭāʾif — from mocker to martyr within a single year.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: demanded impossible miracles before he would believe", "Ch. 25: accepted Islam on the road to the Conquest, alongside Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1691–1692", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "And ‘Abdullah ibn Umayyah was little better, for he once said to the Prophet ﷺ, “By Allah, I will not believe in you until you take to a ladder to the sky and then climb up to it, while I am looking at you; and until you return and bring with you a document along with four angels who bear witness to what you say. By Allah, even if you did that, I think that I still would not believe you.” Despite the seriousness of their crimes, the Prophet ﷺ forgave them, and both men embraced Islam.", "page": 1691}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمَّةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَأَشَدُّ النَّاسِ اسْتِهْزَاءً بِهِ، لَقِيَهُ فِي طَرِيقِ مَكَّةَ فَآمَنَ بِهِ."}, {"id": "salman-al-farisi", "name": "Salmān al-Fārisī", "arabic": "سلمان الفارسي", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh; 'The Persian'", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The Persian seeker whose long journey to the truth ended at the Prophet's ﷺ feet, and whose idea of the trench saved Medina.", "story": "Salmān's life before Medina is one of the great seeker-stories of Islam: born into a Zoroastrian family in Persia, he passed through Christian monasteries in Syria, was told by his last teacher to await a Prophet in a land of date palms, was betrayed and sold into slavery, and finally found the Prophet ﷺ in Medina — recognising the signs he had been taught and embracing Islam. The book presents him at his most consequential moment: when word arrived that some 10,000 Confederates were marching on Medina, the Prophet ﷺ convened his Companions in the mosque to seek counsel, as was his Sunnah. The Companions were hard pressed for ideas until Salmān al-Fārisī suggested that they dig a trench, as was common in Persia (Ch. 19). The plan — a trench estimated at 2 km long and 13 ft wide, with the Muslims' backs to Mount Selaʿ and Medina's lava plains and date farms guarding the flanks — neutralised the largest army Arabia had ever assembled against the Muslims. Every Companion, rich and poor, dug alongside the Prophet ﷺ for less than ten days to complete it. Salmān later governed al-Madāʾin in the heart of his native Persia, living with legendary simplicity. al-Ṣallābī also devotes a section (p. 1400) to the scene at the Trench when the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār each claimed 'Salmān is from us', and the Prophet ﷺ settled the matter: 'Salmān is from us, the People of (my) House.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: suggested digging a trench, 'as was common in Persia', when the Companions were hard pressed for ideas against the Confederates"], "badges": ["Khandaq"], "death": "d. c. 33 AH (also reported 32, 35, and 36 AH — all within ʿUthmān's caliphate), al-Madāʾin (Persia)", "after_sirah": "Under ʿUmar he governed al-Madāʾin, the very Persian capital region he had once left as a seeker — refusing to touch his stipend, weaving palm-fronds for his bread, and giving his allowance away in charity. He remained there teaching until he died during the caliphate of ʿUthmān; Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ was among those who sat weeping at his deathbed as he asked only for a traveller's provision.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 121–122, 1359, 1400", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 33 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Eventually, Salmaan ؓ made it to Al-Madeenah, though in the process he was wrongfully taken captive and turned into a slave. Shortly after Salmaan ؓ arrived there, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ migrated to Al-Madeenah. Wanting to put the monk’s words to the test, Salmaan ؓ went to the Prophet ﷺ and gave him food, saying that he was giving it as charity. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the food to his Companions, but did not eat any of it himself.", "page": 121}, "who_ar": "الْفَارِسِيُّ الَّذِي طَالَتْ رِحْلَتُهُ فِي طَلَبِ الْحَقِّ حَتَّى انْتَهَتْ بَيْنَ يَدَيِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَبِرَأْيِهِ فِي حَفْرِ الْخَنْدَقِ نَجَتِ الْمَدِينَةُ."}, {"id": "abu-hurayrah", "name": "Abū Hurayrah", "arabic": "أبو هريرة", "kunya_titles": "ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣakhr al-Dawsī; Abū Hurayrah ('The Father of Kittens'); 'The Preserver of the Sunnah'", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The Yemeni Companion of the Ṣuffah who, in barely two years with the Prophet ﷺ, became the greatest narrator of Hadith.", "story": "His name was ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣakhr, but he was famously known as Abū Hurayrah — 'The Father of Kittens' — for the small kitten he would carry in his sleeve (Ch. 13). From the Daws tribe of Yemen, he came from a middle-class family yet sacrificed every luxury to live among Ahl al-Ṣuffah in the Prophet's ﷺ Mosque, enduring extended bouts of hunger; he would prolong questions to Companions walking home in the hope of being invited in to eat (Ch. 13). He arrived in Medina only after the seventh year of Hijrah, emigrating alongside his chieftain Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī — at whose hand he had accepted Islam, so that every Hadith he narrated is credited to Ṭufayl too (Ch. 28). At Muʾtah, when his eyes widened at the mammoth Roman army, a Companion chided him, 'But you were not with us at Badr. We did not win due to the size of our army!' (Ch. 24). Despite only accompanying the Prophet ﷺ for around two years, he narrated the most Hadiths of all the Companions — 1,236 individual Hadiths in 5,374 iterations — explaining that while the Muhājirūn traded and the Anṣār farmed, 'I would stick to the Prophet ﷺ with an empty stomach, and I would memorise what they would not' (Ch. 13).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: the most famous member of the Ṣuffah; endured hunger for closeness to the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 24: chided at Muʾtah — 'you were not with us at Badr; we did not win due to the size of our army'", "Ch. 28: emigrated to Medina alongside Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr, at whose hand he had accepted Islam"], "badges": ["Ahl al-Ṣuffah", "Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. 59 AH (also reported 57 and 58 AH), Medina", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar appointed him over Bahrain, but his true post was the Prophet's ﷺ Mosque, where for decades he poured out the Hadith he had hoarded in those two hungry years, teaching the great generation of Successors. In his last years he deputised as governor of Medina for Marwān, and when he died he was buried in al-Baqīʿ. al-Ṣallābī records his arrival among the people of al-Ṣuffah in 7 AH and his own answer to those who marvelled at his abundant narration — his Muhājirūn brothers were occupied with the markets and the Anṣār with their properties, while he clung to the Prophet's ﷺ company for the mere filling of his stomach, 'and I memorized when they forgot' (pp. 738–739).", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 735, 738–739", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 59 AH", "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الْيَمَنِيُّ مِنْ أَهْلِ الصُّفَّةِ، صَارَ فِي أَقَلَّ مِنْ سَنَتَيْنِ مَعَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ أَعْظَمَ رُوَاةِ الْحَدِيثِ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-salam", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām", "arabic": "عبد الله بن سلام", "kunya_titles": "Abū Yūsuf; formerly al-Ḥuṣayn, renamed ʿAbdullāh by the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "One of the most senior rabbis of Medina, who looked at the Prophet's ﷺ face and knew it was not the face of a liar.", "story": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām was one of the most senior rabbis in Medina, a scholar of the Banū Qaynuqāʿ steeped in the scriptures that foretold a final Prophet. When the Prophet ﷺ entered Medina and the people rushed out to see him, ʿAbdullāh was among the first to arrive. He narrated, 'As soon as I saw his face clearly, I knew that this was not the face of a liar' (Ch. 13). The first words he heard the Prophet ﷺ say became the first Hadith reported in Medina: 'Spread peace, feed the people, be good to your relatives, and pray at night when the people are sleeping — you will enter Paradise with peace.' It was famously known as the 'Hadith of Salām (Peace) by ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām' (Ch. 13). His conversion — a man who knew the descriptions of the awaited Prophet better than almost anyone — was a living testimony from the People of the Book, and he is widely understood by the commentators to be the 'witness from the Children of Israel' alluded to in the Qur'an. He lived on in Medina as a revered scholar into the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: rushed out at the Prophet's ﷺ arrival — 'this was not the face of a liar'", "Ch. 13: narrated the first Hadith reported in Medina, the 'Hadith of Salām'"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 43 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "He remained Medina's living witness from the People of the Book, honoured by ʿUmar and the Companions; during the siege of ʿUthmān he stood before the rebels pleading that they not spill the Caliph's blood, then kept himself entirely clear of the fitna that followed. He died in Medina early in Muʿāwiyah's caliphate.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 819–821", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 43 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "“I bear witness,” ‘Abdullah ibn Salaam ؓ said, “that you are indeed the Messenger of Allah,” after which he ؓ said, “O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a people of great lies. If they know that I have embraced Islam before you ask them (about my status among them), they will speak vulgar lies about me to you.” The Jews (of Al-Madeenah) then came, and ‘Abdullah ؓ entered the house so as to avoid being seen by them.", "page": 819}, "who_ar": "مِنْ كِبَارِ أَحْبَارِ الْمَدِينَةِ، نَظَرَ إِلَى وَجْهِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ فَعَلِمَ أَنَّهُ لَيْسَ بِوَجْهِ كَذَّابٍ."}, {"id": "tufayl-ibn-amr-al-dawsi", "name": "Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī", "arabic": "الطفيل بن عمرو الدوسي", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Daws; called Dhū al-Nūr ('possessor of light') in the classical sources", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The chieftain of Daws who tried to shield himself from the Prophet ﷺ, was captivated by the Qur'an, and brought Abū Hurayrah to Islam.", "story": "Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr, the chieftain of the Yemeni tribe of Daws, accepted Islam in the Meccan era when he came to perform ʿUmrah. The likes of Abū Jahl were warning visitors of a 'magician' leading people astray, and Ṭufayl tried his best to avoid the Prophet ﷺ — the classical accounts describe him going so far as to stuff cotton in his ears. But one day he found himself beside the Prophet ﷺ as he prayed, and upon hearing the Qur'an he was captivated and realised his fears were irrational. He spoke to the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam once the Truth became clear, even inviting the Prophet ﷺ to emigrate to Yemen — but Allah had willed otherwise (Ch. 28). He returned to give daʿwah to his people for years. In 8 AH he headed to Medina not merely as a delegate but as an emigrant, preferring the companionship of the Prophet ﷺ over his stature and prestige in Yemen — in medieval society, the pinnacle of sacrifice. Alongside him came a young tribesman who would become 'The Preserver of the Sunnah': Abū Hurayrah. Since Abū Hurayrah accepted Islam at Ṭufayl's hand, every Hadith he narrated — and the deeds of everyone who acted upon them — is credited to Ṭufayl (Ch. 28). He was later martyred fighting the false prophet Musaylamah at Yamāmah. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1800) that during the Ṭāʾif campaign the Prophet ﷺ sent Ṭufayl at the head of a unit against Dhū al-Kaffayn, the idol of ʿAmr ibn Ḥumamah al-Dawsī — he broke and burned the idol, then rejoined the army at Ṭāʾif with four hundred of his tribesmen and a catapult.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: converted in Mecca after hearing the Qur'an, despite Abū Jahl's warnings to visitors", "Ch. 28: invited the Prophet ﷺ to emigrate to Yemen", "Ch. 28: emigrated to Medina in 8 AH with Abū Hurayrah, his convert, beside him"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "12 AH — martyred at Yamāmah", "after_sirah": "In the riddah wars he marched against Musaylamah, and on the road dreamt that his head was shaved and a bird flew from his mouth — he read it as his own martyrdom, and at Yamāmah it came true. His son ʿAmr, maimed in that same battle, fell a martyr at Yarmūk soon after.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 372, 1800", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 12 AH", "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ دَوْسٍ الَّذِي حَاوَلَ أَنْ يَتَّقِيَ سَمَاعَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، فَسَبَاهُ الْقُرْآنُ، وَكَانَ سَبَبَ إِسْلَامِ أَبِي هُرَيْرَةَ."}, {"id": "dimam-ibn-thalabah", "name": "Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah", "arabic": "ضمام بن ثعلبة", "kunya_titles": "Envoy of the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The blunt Bedouin envoy whose abrasive cross-examination of the Prophet ﷺ ended in a promise of Paradise.", "story": "In 9 AH, the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr — a subtribe of the Hawāzin — sent one of their elders, Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah, to negotiate with the Prophet ﷺ. As Bedouins they were abrasive and uncouth, and Ḍimām epitomised that image: he stomped in, demanded to know which man was Muhammad, and announced, 'I will ask you, and I will be harsh' (Ch. 28). He then interrogated the Prophet ﷺ under oath: Who created the Heavens? The Earth? The mountains? Who placed everything around us? To each the Prophet ﷺ replied, 'Allah.' Ḍimām pressed on through the pillars — the five daily prayers, and the rest — asking each time whether Allah truly commanded it, and receiving the affirmative. Then he declared, 'I swear by the One Who sent you, I will not add to them nor detract a bit!' and stormed out. The Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions, 'If he is truthful, he will enter Paradise' (Ch. 28). The book draws out the lessons: the pillars of Islam as the religion's foundation, and the Prophet's ﷺ patience — he was not offended by the roughness but accepted the questioning as sincere. When Ḍimām returned to his tribe, he began calling them to what he had verified with his own ears, and his people followed him into Islam.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: sent by the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr; interrogated the Prophet ﷺ bluntly about the Creator and the pillars", "Ch. 28: 'I will not add to them nor detract a bit!' — and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'If he is truthful, he will enter Paradise'"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1927–1929"], "after_sirah": "al-Ṣallābī carries the story home (pp. 1928–1929): back among his people, Ḍimām opened with 'Evil indeed are al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā!', waved aside their warnings of leprosy and madness, and before that day ended every man and woman of them had become Muslim — Ibn ʿAbbās declaring that no people ever had a better envoy than Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah.", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "As Dimaam ؓ turned around and walked towards his riding camel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “If the possessor of the two braids (i.e., Dimaam ؓ) is truthful (in his promise to do no more and no less than what I have commanded him to do), he will enter Paradise.” When Dimaam ؓ returned to his people, they all gathered around him, and the first thing he ؓ said to them was, “Evil indeed are both Al-Laat and Al-Uzzah (the idols they worshipped)!”", "page": 1927}, "who_ar": "رَسُولُ قَوْمِهِ الْأَعْرَابِيُّ الْجَافِي، سَاءَلَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ مُسَاءَلَةً فِيهَا غِلْظَةٌ، فَانْتَهَى أَمْرُهُ إِلَى وَعْدٍ بِالْجَنَّةِ."}, {"id": "thumamah-ibn-uthal", "name": "Thumāmah ibn Uthāl", "arabic": "ثمامة بن أثال", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of the Banū Ḥanīfah of Yamāmah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The chieftain of Yamāmah who entered the mosque as a prisoner tied to a pillar and left as a believer wielding Mecca's grain supply.", "story": "Shortly after the Battle of the Trench, Muhammad ibn Maslamah's patrol captured a small, visibly prestigious entourage. The Prophet ﷺ asked, 'Don't you know who your captive is? You have captured Thumāmah ibn Uthāl, the Chieftain of the Banū Ḥanīfah' — leader of one of the largest tribes in Arabia, rivalling Abū Ṭālib in prestige, a supporter of the Quraysh who had once publicly threatened to kill the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 19). Yet the Prophet ﷺ ordered that he be treated well and held in the mosque itself, fed the same meal as the Prophet ﷺ — a calculated decision that let Thumāmah observe the Muslim lifestyle firsthand. Each morning the Prophet ﷺ asked, 'What do you have to say, O Thumāmah?' and the proud chieftain, refusing to beg, answered: if you kill me, expect war; if you want money, name your price. After days without ransom demands, the Prophet ﷺ simply set him free — and Thumāmah's heart flipped, and Islam entered it entirely (Ch. 19). He became the first Muslim to perform ʿUmrah with the talbiyah ringing through Mecca. When the Quraysh raged at him, he fulfilled a stunning threat: he cut off all grain shipments from the Najd, starving Mecca until the Prophet ﷺ himself, out of compassion, wrote instructing him to lift the boycott (Ch. 19). He later stood firm for Islam when Yamāmah apostatised behind Musaylamah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: captured by Muhammad ibn Maslamah's patrol and held in the Prophet's ﷺ Mosque", "Ch. 19: freed without ransom — 'Thumāmah's heart flipped, and Islam entered it entirely'", "Ch. 19: first Muslim to perform ʿUmrah; imposed a grain boycott on Mecca until the Prophet ﷺ wrote to lift it", "Ch. 28: cited among the chieftains of the Banū Ḥanīfah who accepted Islam"], "badges": [], "death": "", "after_sirah": "When Musaylamah swept Yamāmah into apostasy, Thumāmah stood against his own tribe, publicly denouncing the impostor and separating with those who stayed firm; he then joined al-ʿAlāʾ al-Ḥaḍramī's campaign against the apostates of Bahrain. The classical sources record no firm death date — one report has him killed unjustly by tribesmen who mistook spoils he wore for stolen goods.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1448–1451", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Having gained his freedom, Thumaamah went to a garden of date-palm trees that was situated near the Masjid; he took a bath, after which he returned, entered the Masjid, and declared, “I bear witness that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. O Muhammad, by Allah, I hated no face on the entire earth as much as I hated your face, but now your face has become the most beloved of faces to me.”", "page": 1448}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ أَهْلِ الْيَمَامَةِ، دَخَلَ الْمَسْجِدَ أَسِيرًا مَرْبُوطًا بِسَارِيَةٍ، وَخَرَجَ مِنْهُ مُؤْمِنًا مُتَحَكِّمًا فِي مِيرَةِ مَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "suraqah-ibn-malik", "name": "Surāqah ibn Mālik", "arabic": "سراقة بن مالك", "kunya_titles": "Abū Sufyān al-Mudlijī; leader of a Juʿshum clan", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The bounty hunter who chased the Hijrah party, left with a promise of Khosrow's bracelets — and lived to wear them.", "story": "When the Quraysh offered 100 camels for the Prophet ﷺ, dead or alive, Surāqah ibn Mālik — leader of a Juʿshum clan — deflected other trackers with a lie so he could claim the full bounty himself, then rode out alone after the Hijrah party (Ch. 12). Each time he closed in, his horse sank into the sand until, as he narrated, 'I knew that this was a force beyond me.' The hunter called out for peace, and almost instantaneously went from hunting the Prophet ﷺ to requesting a guarantee of his protection. As he turned to leave, the Prophet ﷺ made one of the sīrah's most astonishing predictions: 'O Surāqah, how will you be on the day that you put on the bracelets of Kisrā?' Surāqah, stunned, could barely fathom it — there was only one Khosrow (Ch. 12). He formally embraced Islam years later, approaching the Prophet ﷺ after Ḥunayn with his guarantee in hand. At the Farewell Pilgrimage it was Surāqah who stood at Marwah and asked whether joining ʿUmrah with Hajj applied 'for this year only, or forever?' — and the Prophet ﷺ intertwined his fingers and said, 'Rather, forever' (Ch. 29). Under ʿUmar, when Persia fell, the Caliph summoned Surāqah, sat him on Kisrā's throne, and placed Kisrā's bracelets on his wrists as the Muslims chanted 'Allāhu Akbar!' (Ch. 12).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 12: chased the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr for the bounty; his horse sank and he begged for peace", "Ch. 12: promised the bracelets of Kisrā — fulfilled when ʿUmar placed them on his wrists", "Ch. 29: asked at the Farewell Pilgrimage whether ʿUmrah-in-Hajj applied forever"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 24 AH", "after_sirah": "He returned to his home country around Qudayd and lived just long enough to see the Prophet's ﷺ prediction fulfilled on his own wrists; he died at the very start of ʿUthmān's caliphate, about a year after ʿUmar. al-Ṣallābī also relates the fulfilment (p. 667): ʿUmar sat him on a mount and paraded him before the people, praising Allah who had stripped the bracelets from Khosrow, the self-styled 'lord of mankind', to place them on Surāqah ibn Juʿshum, a Bedouin of Banū Mudlij.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 665–668", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 24 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "As soon as he heard about the said reward, Suraaqah ibn Maalik ibn Jo’shum decided to do whatever he could to find and apprehend - and kill, if necessary - the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was, however, not the only one that was looking to gain the reward; many others also joined in the hunt, which is why it was of course wise for the Prophet ﷺ to remain in the cave of Thaur for a number of days. Little did Suraaqah know that, although he went out seeking the blood of the Prophet ﷺ, he would return as a follower of the Prophet ﷺ, doing all that was in his power to protect him from the Quraish.", "page": 665}, "who_ar": "طَالِبُ الْجَائِزَةِ الَّذِي تَعَقَّبَ رَكْبَ الْهِجْرَةِ، فَانْصَرَفَ بِوَعْدٍ بِسِوَارَيْ كِسْرَى، وَعَاشَ حَتَّى لَبِسَهُمَا."}, {"id": "nuaym-ibn-masud", "name": "Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd", "arabic": "نعيم بن مسعود", "kunya_titles": "al-Ashjaʿī, of the Ghaṭafān", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The Ghaṭafānī who secretly converted during the siege of Medina and single-handedly dissolved the Confederate alliance.", "story": "At the darkest hour of the Battle of the Trench, Allah used a man from the Ghaṭafān — one of the very tribes besieging Medina — to turn the tides. Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd sneaked into Muslim territory and crept to the Prophet ﷺ, later remarking, 'Allah threw Islam into my heart that day' (Ch. 19). Since one extra fighter would change little, the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to return as a spy. Nuʿaym asked if he was allowed to lie, and the Prophet ﷺ gave the famous reply, 'War is deception' — the book noting that while wartime trickery (khadʿah) is permitted, treachery (khiyānah) is forbidden at all times (Ch. 19). Nuʿaym executed a meticulous plan: to the Banū Qurayẓah, with whom he had close ties, he suggested the Quraysh were wavering and that they should demand hostages as security; to the Quraysh he disclosed that the Qurayẓah would ask for hostages — in order to hand them to Muhammad ﷺ for execution; to his own Ghaṭafān he whispered the same doubts. When the Qurayẓah's emissary duly asked Abū Sufyān for 70 nobles, alarm bells rang, and the three factions collapsed into a deadlock of mutual distrust (Ch. 19). The coalition of 10,000 unravelled from within — undone by one sincere convert and a freezing wind sent by Allah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: crept to the Prophet ﷺ mid-siege — 'Allah threw Islam into my heart that day'", "Ch. 19: 'War is deception' — sent back as a spy", "Ch. 19: turned Qurayẓah, Quraysh, and Ghaṭafān against each other over the hostage demand"], "badges": ["Khandaq"], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1377–1381"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Then the Prophet ﷺ was presented with another opportunity to weaken the confederate army when an intelligent man from the Ghatfaan, Nu’aim ibn Mas‘ood ؓ, came to him, announcing that he had become a Muslim. Nu’aim ؓ said, “O Messenger of Allah, my people do not know that I have become a Muslim, so command me howsoever you want (and I will execute your command).” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Among us, you are only one man (i.e., if you join us, our situation will not greatly improve), so, on our behalf, cause betrayal among them, if you are able to do so. For indeed war is stratagem.”", "page": 1377}, "who_ar": "الْغَطَفَانِيُّ الَّذِي أَسْلَمَ سِرًّا فِي حِصَارِ الْمَدِينَةِ، فَفَرَّقَ وَحْدَهُ جَمْعَ الْأَحْزَابِ."}, {"id": "abu-basir", "name": "Abū Baṣīr", "arabic": "أبو بصير عتبة بن أسيد الثقفي", "kunya_titles": "ʿUtbah ibn Usayd al-Thaqafī; Abū Baṣīr", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The first Muslim returned under the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, who built a coastal guerrilla camp that brought the Quraysh to their knees.", "story": "Abū Baṣīr was the first Muslim to emigrate to Medina after the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, and under its terms two Qurashī emissaries came to take him back. The Prophet ﷺ told him, 'These two men have come to take you... return to your people,' and Abū Baṣīr protested as Abū Jandal had: 'Will you return me to the pagans?' The Prophet ﷺ counselled patience and the rewards of the Hereafter (Ch. 20). On the road, Abū Baṣīr built rapport with his escorts, seized one's sword and killed him; the other fled to Medina crying for protection. When Abū Baṣīr returned too, the Prophet ﷺ — bound by treaty — remarked pointedly, 'If only he had someone to help him.' Abū Baṣīr clearly understood (Ch. 20). He set up camp in a remote area near the coast on the outskirts of Mecca; Abū Jandal fled to join him, and converts kept coming until 80 Muslims formed a settlement, relentlessly raiding Quraysh caravans for 18 months — without violating the treaty, since the camp had no formal allegiance with Medina. Finally Abū Sufyān himself wrote to the Prophet ﷺ, invoking kinship and imploring him to absorb the 80 men into Medina. The Prophet ﷺ sent a letter inviting them, but Abū Baṣīr was gravely ill; Allah decreed that he pass away with the Prophet's ﷺ letter in his hand (Ch. 20).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: returned to the Quraysh under the treaty; escaped his escorts", "Ch. 20: 'If only he had someone to help him' — founded the coastal camp", "Ch. 20: 80 men raided the Quraysh for 18 months until Abū Sufyān begged for their integration into Medina", "Ch. 20: died with the Prophet's ﷺ letter of invitation in his hand"], "badges": [], "death": "c. 7 AH — died at his coastal camp, the Prophet's ﷺ letter in his hand", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1550–1553"], "after_sirah": "al-Ṣallābī follows the band of Sayf al-Baḥr to its end (pp. 1552–1553): some sixty or seventy escapees gathered around Abū Baṣīr until the Quraysh themselves pleaded with the Prophet ﷺ to take the raiders into Medina and gave up the extradition clause — but by the time the Prophet's ﷺ letter of recall reached the coast, Abū Baṣīr lay on his deathbed.", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 7 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Shortly after the signing of the Treaty of Al-Hudaibiyyah, and after the Prophet ﷺ returned to Al-Madeenah, Abu Baseer ’Utbah ibn Usaid ؓ succeeded in escaping from his prison in Makkah. Free at last, Abu Baseer ؓ made the long and arduous journey to Al-Madeenah, but he was not the only one to make the journey; two men from the Quraish also went in order to seek the extradition of Abu Baseer ؓ, as per the terms of the Al-Hudaibiyyah Treaty.", "page": 1550}, "who_ar": "أَوَّلُ مُسْلِمٍ رُدَّ إِلَى قُرَيْشٍ بِمُقْتَضَى صُلْحِ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، أَقَامَ عَلَى سَاحِلِ الْبَحْرِ مُعَسْكَرًا يُغِيرُ عَلَى قُرَيْشٍ حَتَّى أَذَلَّهَا."}, {"id": "abu-jandal-ibn-suhayl", "name": "Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl", "arabic": "أبو جندل بن سهيل بن عمرو", "kunya_titles": "Son of Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, the Quraysh's negotiator at Ḥudaybiyyah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The shackled convert dragged back by his own father at Ḥudaybiyyah, who endured until he marched into Medina at the head of 79 men.", "story": "As the ink of the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah was drying, a figure appeared from afar stumbling in shackles: Abū Jandal, the son of Suhayl ibn ʿAmr — the very man negotiating for the Quraysh. He had escaped his imprisonment for accepting Islam and began screaming, 'Help, O Muslims!' Unbeknownst to him, he was a live test case of the treaty's cruellest clause (Ch. 20). The Prophet ﷺ pleaded that he be excluded, but Suhayl would not acquiesce; Mikraz's compromise merely spared him formal return to a dungeon. Abū Jandal cried in despair, 'O Muslims! Will you return me to the Pagans after I have come as a Muslim?' The Prophet ﷺ told him, 'Be patient, O Abū Jandal,' promising that Allah would grant him and the oppressed with him a way out; ʿUmar walked beside him gesturing at his sword's hilt, but Abū Jandal did not take the hint (Ch. 20). He counted the days, escaped once more, and joined Abū Baṣīr's coastal camp of raiders. When the Quraysh themselves begged for the camp's dissolution and Abū Baṣīr died with the Prophet's ﷺ letter in hand, it was Abū Jandal who marched the 79-man settlement into Medina — dignified and vindicated, the book calls it, a divine turn of events that began with his failed escape in chains (Ch. 20).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: appeared in shackles as the treaty was signed — 'Will you return me to the Pagans?'", "Ch. 20: told 'Be patient, O Abū Jandal' and returned to his father", "Ch. 20: escaped to Abū Baṣīr's camp and finally led its 79 men into Medina"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 18 AH — plague of ʿAmwās, Syria", "after_sirah": "He fought in the riddah wars and then in the conquest of Syria, and died there in the plague of ʿAmwās — the same scourge that took his father Suhayl, both of them by then long reconciled in Islam.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1530–1533", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 18 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After the Prophet ﷺ agreed in principle with Suhail to the terms of the treaty, Suhail’s son, Abu Jandal, came hobbling towards them in chains. He was in chains because of his faith; he wanted to migrate but had been overcome by the Quraish, and was kept in chains in order to prevent him from migrating to Al-Madeenah. When Abu Jandal ؓ heard about the coming of the Prophet ﷺ to Al-Hudaibiyyah, he was infused with enough hope and enthusiasm to try and escape from his captors. And escape he did, making it all the way to the Muslim encampment at Al-Hudaibiyyah.", "page": 1530}, "who_ar": "الْمُسْلِمُ الْمُقَيَّدُ الَّذِي جَرَّهُ أَبُوهُ رَاجِعًا يَوْمَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، فَصَبَرَ حَتَّى دَخَلَ الْمَدِينَةَ عَلَى رَأْسِ تِسْعَةٍ وَسَبْعِينَ رَجُلًا."}, {"id": "urwah-ibn-masud-al-thaqafi", "name": "ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī", "arabic": "عروة بن مسعود الثقفي", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Thaqīf in Ṭāʾif; likened by the Prophet ﷺ to the 'companion of Yā-Sīn'", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The chief of Ṭāʾif who mediated at Ḥudaybiyyah, embraced Islam after the siege of his own city, and was martyred calling the adhān from his rooftop.", "story": "ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd was one of the two 'great men of the two cities' the Quraysh had in mind when they protested, 'If only this Qur'an was revealed to a great man' (Ch. 28). At Ḥudaybiyyah he volunteered to mediate, lecturing the Prophet ﷺ through a tribal lens and dismissing the Companions as bandits who would flee — provoking Abū Bakr's uncharacteristic curse. Whenever he reached for the Prophet's ﷺ beard, a masked guard smacked his hand with a sword pommel: his own nephew, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah (Ch. 20). He returned transformed, telling the Quraysh he had entered the courts of Caesar, Khosrow, and the Negus, 'but I have never seen any king shown more respect than the Companions of Muhammad show Muhammad' (Ch. 20). Before the siege of Ṭāʾif he and Ghaylān ibn Salamah were away at Jurash learning catapults, testudo formations, and battering rams (Ch. 26). As the army withdrew from Ṭāʾif, ʿUrwah caught up with the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam, insisting — against the Prophet's ﷺ warning 'I fear that they may kill you' — on returning to call his people. At Fajr he climbed his rooftop and called the adhān over Ṭāʾif; at the shahādah an arrow struck him dead. Dying, he called it 'a gift from Allah' and asked to be buried with the martyrs. The Prophet ﷺ said, 'This man was to his people as the companion of Yā-Sīn was to his' (Ch. 28).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: mediated at Ḥudaybiyyah; awed testimony about the Companions' love for the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 26: sent with Ghaylān ibn Salamah to Jurash to learn siege weaponry before Ṭāʾif", "Ch. 28: accepted Islam after the siege of Ṭāʾif; martyred calling the adhān from his rooftop", "Ch. 28: eulogised by the Prophet ﷺ as 'the companion of Yā-Sīn' to his people"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "9 AH — martyred in Ṭāʾif while calling the adhān", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1501–1502, 1778"], "after_sirah": "al-Ṣallābī confirms the whole arc from his own pages: the Ḥudaybiyyah embassy and his awed report on the Companions' reverence (pp. 1501–1502), and his conversion on the road after Ṭāʾif, the fatal arrows, and his dying request to lie with the Muslim martyrs of the siege (p. 1778).", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 9 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Wanting to lend wise counsel to his allies from the Quraish, ‘Urwah ibn Mas’ood Ath-Thaqafee (from the Thaqeef tribe) offered to meet the Prophet ﷺ, to speak to him on their behalf, and to return to them with sure news about his intentions. He knew that the Quraish trusted him implicitly, and why shouldn’t they have, for without the aid of his fellow tribesmen, he came to Makkah to support the Quraish in their war against the Prophet ﷺ.", "page": 1501}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ الطَّائِفِ، تَوَسَّطَ يَوْمَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، وَأَسْلَمَ بَعْدَ حِصَارِ بَلَدِهِ، وَاسْتُشْهِدَ وَهُوَ يُؤَذِّنُ عَلَى سَطْحِ دَارِهِ."}, {"id": "hatib-ibn-abi-baltaah", "name": "Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah", "arabic": "حاطب بن أبي بلتعة", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muḥammad; veteran of Badr", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "The Badr veteran whose intercepted letter to the Quraysh became the sīrah's great lesson on forgiveness and the rank of the Badriyyūn.", "story": "As the Prophet ﷺ secretly prepared to march on Mecca, Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah made a grave error in judgement: he paid a woman courier to carry a letter warning the Quraysh of the coming army (Ch. 25). Revelation exposed the plot; the courier was intercepted and the letter recovered. ʿUmar immediately asked leave to strike off the head of 'this hypocrite,' but the Prophet ﷺ instead asked Ḥāṭib why he did it. His answer was disarmingly honest: he had not abandoned his faith or preferred kufr — but unlike the other Muhājirūn, he was an outsider with no clan in Mecca to shield his family, and he hoped to establish a favour with the Quraysh to protect them (Ch. 25). The Prophet ﷺ declared, 'Ḥāṭib has spoken the truth, so do not speak of him except with goodness,' and when ʿUmar persisted, gave the immortal reply: 'Perhaps Allah has looked upon all the attendees of Badr and said: Do as you please, for I have forgiven you.' ʿUmar wept (Ch. 25). Allah then revealed the opening of Sūrah al-Mumtaḥanah, censuring the taking of His enemies as confidants — yet, the book stresses, the verse says he 'strayed from the Right Way' while he remained a Muslim, and scholars differ only over whether he was forgiven for his intention or for Badr itself. No one spoke ill of him thereafter.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: sent a secret warning letter to the Quraysh via a woman courier; exposed by Revelation", "Ch. 25: 'Perhaps Allah has looked upon the attendees of Badr... Do as you please, for I have forgiven you'", "Ch. 25: Sūrah al-Mumtaḥanah revealed about his lapse — yet he remained a forgiven Muslim"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 30 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "The same daring that once carried his letter had also carried the Prophet's ﷺ letter to al-Muqawqas of Egypt — the mission he is best remembered for outside the Ifk of his lapse. He lived out his years in Medina as a prosperous merchant, and when he died in 30 AH, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān led his funeral prayer.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1619, 1683–1688", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 30 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "According to Ibn Sa’d’s account, the Prophet ﷺ sent that letter in the hands of Haatib ibn Abu Balta’ah Al-Lakhmee ؓ. It is furthermore related that Al-Muqawqis gave an eloquent and positive reply; yet in spite of his pleasant words, he did not embrace Islam. He nonetheless sent a number of gifts to the Prophet ﷺ; one of those gifts was Maariyyah Al-Qabtiyyah ؓ.", "page": 1619}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَهْلِ بَدْرٍ، اعْتُرِضَ كِتَابُهُ إِلَى قُرَيْشٍ، فَكَانَ فِي قِصَّتِهِ أَعْظَمُ دَرْسٍ فِي السِّيرَةِ فِي الْعَفْوِ وَمَنْزِلَةِ الْبَدْرِيِّينَ."}, {"id": "al-miqdad-ibn-amr", "name": "al-Miqdād ibn ʿAmr", "arabic": "المقداد بن عمرو", "kunya_titles": "Also known as al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad; among the first seven to publicise their Islam", "group": "beyond", "tier": "major", "who": "An early Muslim and Badr cavalryman whose defiant pledge before Badr lit up the Prophet's ﷺ face.", "story": "Al-Miqdād ibn ʿAmr belonged to the tiny band who bore the full brunt of early Mecca: the book counts him among the first seven to publicise their Islam — alongside the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿAmmār, Sumayyah, Ṣuhayb, and Bilāl (Ch. 6). His immortal moment came on the road to Badr. When the Prophet ﷺ asked the Companions, 'What do you think we should do?', Abū Bakr and ʿUmar offered words of support — but al-Miqdād, a Muhājir, stood and answered as though nothing less than a creed would do: 'O Messenger of Allah, do as Allah commanded you, and we are right behind you. We will not say to you as the Children of Israel said to Mūsā, \"Go — both you and your Lord — and fight; we are staying right here!\" Rather we say, \"Go — both you and your Lord — and fight; we are right behind you!\" Take us to all corners of the world, and we will follow you until we meet Allah's decree!' (Ch. 14). The Prophet ﷺ thanked him — and then, tellingly, asked again, drawing out the Anṣār's own pledge through Saʿd ibn Muʿādh. Al-Miqdād fought at Badr — traditionally remembered as its lone cavalryman — and at the battles that followed, and died in the caliphate of ʿUthmān, near Medina.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: counted among the first seven Muslims to publicise their Islam in Mecca", "Ch. 14: his stirring pledge before Badr — 'we are right behind you!'"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 33 AH, al-Jurf near Medina", "after_sirah": "He rode in the conquests after the Prophet's ﷺ passing, including the campaign for Egypt, then retired to his estate at al-Jurf a few miles outside Medina. He died there in ʿUthmān's caliphate; his body was carried into the city, ʿUthmān prayed over him, and he was buried in al-Baqīʿ.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 953–954, 970", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 33 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Abdullah ibn Mas’ood ؓ said, “I witnessed something from Al-Miqdaad ibn Al-Aswad that made me more desirous of being his companion than of having anything else in comparison (here, exaggeration is used to emphasize the significance of Al-Miqdaad’s positive attitude and contribution to the tense situation). He went to the Prophet ﷺ as he was supplicating against the polytheists, and he said, ‘We do not say as the people of Moosa said: Go, you and your Lord, and both of you fight. Rather, we will fight from your right, from your left, from before you and behind you.’", "page": 953}, "who_ar": "مِنَ السَّابِقِينَ إِلَى الْإِسْلَامِ وَفُرْسَانِ بَدْرٍ، أَشْرَقَ وَجْهُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ لِمَقَالَتِهِ الْجَرِيئَةِ قَبْلَ الْمَعْرَكَةِ."}, {"id": "dimad-al-azdi", "name": "Ḍimād al-Azdī", "arabic": "ضماد الأزدي", "kunya_titles": "A chief and healer of the Azd of Yemen", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Yemeni tribal leader and 'doctor' who came to cure the Prophet ﷺ of madness and was cured of disbelief instead.", "story": "Frightened by Abū Jahl and Abū Lahab's warnings about a 'magician' at Mecca's gates, Ḍimād al-Azdī, a leader of a Yemeni tribe, literally put cotton in his ears — until, as an intelligent man, he questioned his own actions (Ch. 6). A practitioner of healing, he approached the Prophet ﷺ saying, 'I heard you are a madman, and I am here to cure you.' The Prophet ﷺ replied, 'I am not a madman, but listen to my Message,' and began the Khuṭbah al-Ḥājjah. Before a single verse of Qur'an was recited, Ḍimād stopped him: 'By Allah, I have not heard anything more beautiful than this' — and accepted Islam (Ch. 6). The book later notes that the Khuṭbah al-Ḥājjah alone led the likes of Ḍimād to convert (Ch. 13).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: stuffed cotton in his ears against the 'magician', then came to 'cure' the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 6: converted upon hearing the Khuṭbah al-Ḥājjah alone"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 366–367"], "after_sirah": "al-Ṣallābī completes the story (p. 367): when Ḍimād gave his pledge, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether it extended to his people, and he answered, 'And upon my people.' Long afterwards, a Muslim expedition passing through his lands ordered even a seized water-vessel returned, its commander saying, 'These are the people of Ḍimād.'", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Dimaad Al-Azdee, who had come to Makkah as a delegate, heard the claim that the Prophet ﷺ was mad. Since it was reputable chieftains of the Quraish that made the claim, and not unknown people whose veracity could not be trusted, Dimaad Al-Azdee was convinced that the Prophet ﷺ was afflicted with some form of insanity. Dimaad was particularly interested in the Prophet’s case because he himself was a medical practitioner who treated mentally sick patients.", "page": 366}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدٌ يَمَنِيٌّ وَ«طَبِيبٌ» جَاءَ يَرْقِي النَّبِيَّ ﷺ مِنَ الْجُنُونِ، فَشُفِيَ هُوَ مِنَ الْكُفْرِ."}, {"id": "buraydah-ibn-al-hasib", "name": "Buraydah ibn al-Ḥaṣīb", "arabic": "بريدة بن الحصيب الأسلمي", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Aslamī", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "An Aslamī Companion who served as the Prophet's ﷺ scout and narrated the count of his battles.", "story": "Buraydah ibn al-Ḥaṣīb of the Aslam tribe — who in the classical accounts first met the Prophet ﷺ during the very journey of the Hijrah — appears in the book as a trusted narrator and operative. It is Buraydah who transmits the famous tally: 'The Prophet ﷺ attended 19 ghazawāt, and physically fought in eight of them' (Ch. 13). Before the campaign of al-Muraysīʿ, the Prophet ﷺ — never one to act on rumours — sent Buraydah to the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq posing as a Bedouin; he verified al-Ḥārith's war preparations firsthand and surreptitiously returned to inform the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 17). He later settled in the eastern lands and died in Merv, in Khurāsān.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: narrated that the Prophet ﷺ attended 19 ghazawāt and fought in eight", "Ch. 17: sent undercover to verify the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq's war preparations"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 63 AH (also reported 62 AH), Merv (Khurāsān)", "after_sirah": "He settled first in Basra, then carried his jihad to the far northeast, joining the campaigns into Khurāsān. He died at Merv in the reign of Yazīd, one of the furthest-flung Companion graves in the East, and his resting place there was venerated for centuries.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 686, 1309, 1798", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 63 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When the Prophet ﷺ heard about suspicious military activities that were taking place among the Banu Al-Mustaliq tribe, he ﷺ didn’t immediately go out with his army; instead, he ﷺ first sent Buraidah ibn Al-Haseeb Al-Aslamee ؓ to find out whether they really had intentions of attacking Al-Madeenah. Buraidah ؓ went to them and pretended that he had come to help them. Thus having gained their trust, Buraidah ؓ found out that they were truly bent on waging war against the Prophet ﷺ. He promptly returned to Al-Madeenah and informed the Messenger ﷺ about what he had found out.", "page": 1309}, "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ أَسْلَمِيٌّ كَانَ عَيْنًا لِلنَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَرَوَى عَدَدَ غَزَوَاتِهِ."}, {"id": "uqbah-ibn-amir-al-juhani", "name": "ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī", "arabic": "عقبة بن عامر الجهني", "kunya_titles": "Abū Ḥammād; later governor of Egypt", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Juhaynah Companion, reciter and narrator, who preserved the Prophet's ﷺ sermon at Tabūk.", "story": "ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir of the tribe of Juhaynah was one of the young Companions devoted to learning directly from the Prophet's ﷺ lips. The book preserves through him the Prophet's ﷺ great sermon at Tabūk: 'The most truthful speech is the Book of Allah, the firmest handhold is the statement of righteousness, the best way is the way of Ibrāhīm... the worst matters are those which are innovated' (Ch. 27). He became a renowned reciter of the Qur'an and narrator of Hadith, and served as governor of Egypt under Muʿāwiyah, where he died.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 27: narrated the Prophet's ﷺ sermon upon settling at Tabūk"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 58 AH, Egypt (buried at the foot of al-Muqaṭṭam)", "after_sirah": "He fought in the Syrian campaigns and settled in Egypt, whose governorship Muʿāwiyah gave him in 44 AH. A master reciter who wrote out his own muṣḥaf, he became Egypt's great teacher of Qur'an and Hadith — including the famous narrations on archery — and died there in 58 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 58 AH", "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الْجُهَنِيُّ الْمُقْرِئُ الرَّاوِيَةُ، الَّذِي حَفِظَ خُطْبَةَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ بِتَبُوكَ."}, {"id": "imran-ibn-husayn", "name": "ʿImrān ibn Ḥusayn", "arabic": "عمران بن حصين", "kunya_titles": "Abū Nujayd al-Khuzāʿī", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Khuzāʿī Companion and narrator who forever regretted chasing his camel mid-Hadith.", "story": "ʿImrān ibn Ḥusayn, who accepted Islam in the year of Khaybar, appears in the book at one of the delegation-era gatherings of knowledge. When the tribe of Ḥimyar came from Yemen asking how creation began, the Prophet ﷺ answered, 'There was Allah, and there was nothing before Him. He then created the Heavens and the Earth while His Throne was on the Water.' ʿImrān, the Hadith's narrator, recalled that at that moment someone yelled that his camel had fled; he chased it into the desert, caught neither camel nor the rest of the Hadith, and lamented, 'O how I wish I let the camel go and finished the Hadith' (Ch. 28). He became one of Basra's great teachers, famed for his patience in long illness, and died there in 52 AH. al-Ṣallābī also relates (pp. 372–373) the conversion of his father: sent by the Quraysh's leaders to reason with the Prophet ﷺ, al-Ḥuṣayn was won over by a single argument — the One in the heavens alone answers you when you are afflicted, 'yet you make them His partners' — and when he rose a Muslim, ʿImrān kissed his father's head, hands, and feet, moving the Prophet ﷺ to tears.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: narrated the Hadith of creation to the Ḥimyar delegation — and lost its ending chasing his camel"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 52 AH, Basra", "after_sirah": "ʿUmar sent him to Basra to teach its people their religion, and al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī counted no visitor to the city better than him; he also served for a time as its judge. Bedridden for roughly thirty years with a painful illness, he bore it with such contentment that he spoke of the angels' greetings — which ceased, he said, when he finally accepted cautery.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 372–373", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 52 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Husain’s son, ‘Umraan stood up, went to his father and kissed his head, his hands, and his feet. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him doing that, he ﷺ began to cry. He ﷺ said, “I cried because of what ‘Umraan did: When Al-Husain entered here, he was a disbeliever, and ‘Umraan neither stood up for him nor turned in his direction; then when Al-Husain embraced Islam, ‘Umraan ؓ fulfilled his (father’s) right (over him). A feeling of warmness and mercy entered me because of that.”", "page": 372}, "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الْخُزَاعِيُّ الرَّاوِيَةُ، الَّذِي لَمْ يَزَلْ نَادِمًا عَلَى انْصِرَافِهِ فِي طَلَبِ نَاقَتِهِ وَالنَّبِيُّ ﷺ يُحَدِّثُ."}, {"id": "al-mughirah-ibn-shubah", "name": "al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah", "arabic": "المغيرة بن شعبة الثقفي", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿĪsā al-Thaqafī; famed as one of the shrewdest of the Arabs", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Thaqafī Companion who guarded the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥudaybiyyah and sprinted with joy at his own tribe's delegation.", "story": "At Ḥudaybiyyah, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah stood masked and armed at the Prophet's ﷺ side. Whenever the Qurashī-aligned mediator reached to grab the Prophet's ﷺ beard in the customary chieftain's gesture, al-Mughīrah smacked his hand with the pommel of his sword, harder each time — 'Get your hand away from the Prophet ﷺ before it is cut off from its owner.' The envoy demanded to know who he was, and it turned out to be his own uncle, ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī (Ch. 20). Years later, when the Thaqīf chieftains finally came to Medina to submit, it was al-Mughīrah who met them first; overjoyed, he sprinted to give the Prophet ﷺ the glad tidings — only for Abū Bakr to beg the honour of delivering the news himself, and al-Mughīrah yielded out of respect for his seniority (Ch. 28). He was present at the Pledge of Riḍwān, and later governed Kufa under ʿUmar and Muʿāwiyah. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 1904) that when Ṭāʾif finally submitted, it was al-Mughīrah who demolished its revered idol al-Rabbah before the assembled tribe — heavily guarded by his own clansmen lest Thaqīf strike him down as they had his uncle ʿUrwah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: guarded the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥudaybiyyah, rapping his own uncle's hand with his sword pommel", "Ch. 28: sprinted to announce the Thaqīf delegation, yielding the glad tidings to Abū Bakr"], "badges": ["Bayʿat al-Riḍwān"], "death": "d. 50 AH, Kufa", "after_sirah": "Under ʿUmar he governed Basra and then Kufa, his shrewdness proverbial in both; and when Muʿāwiyah came to power he returned him to Kufa, where he remained governor until his death in the plague of 50 AH — by most accounts the first man in Islam to be greeted as 'amīr' while another caliph's reign still ran through him.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 352, 1503–1504, 1904", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 50 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Mugheerah ؓ, wearing a helmet on his head and carrying a sword in his hand, stood guard directly over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. And so no sooner did his uncle grab the Prophet’s beard than he ؓ poked his uncle’s hand with the butt of his sword and said, “Draw back your hand from touching the beard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and do it quickly before your hand will no longer be able to draw back to your person (i.e., before I cut it off).”", "page": 1504}, "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الثَّقَفِيُّ الَّذِي حَرَسَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ يَوْمَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، وَعَدَا فَرَحًا بِقُدُومِ وَفْدِ قَوْمِهِ."}, {"id": "ghaylan-ibn-salamah", "name": "Ghaylān ibn Salamah", "arabic": "غيلان بن سلمة الثقفي", "kunya_titles": "A noble of Thaqīf", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Thaqafī noble sent with ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd to Jurash to master the machines of siege warfare.", "story": "The book records Ghaylān ibn Salamah alongside ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd on a special expedition to the Roman-aligned tribe of Jurash, where they learned to build and use the catapult, the testudo formation, and the battering ram — instruments completely foreign to the Arabs — as the Muslims prepared for the campaign against Ṭāʾif (Ch. 26). He later entered Islam when Thaqīf submitted, and is remembered in the books of fiqh as the convert whom the Prophet ﷺ instructed to retain only four of his ten wives. He died late in the caliphate of ʿUmar.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 26: sent with ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd to Jurash to learn catapults, testudo formations, and battering rams"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 23 AH — at the end of ʿUmar's caliphate", "beyond_sources": ["al-Iṣāba", "Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 23 AH", "who_ar": "شَرِيفٌ ثَقَفِيٌّ أُرْسِلَ مَعَ عُرْوَةَ بْنِ مَسْعُودٍ إِلَى جُرَشَ لِيُتْقِنَ صَنْعَةَ آلَاتِ الْحِصَارِ."}, {"id": "amr-ibn-salim-al-khuzai", "name": "ʿAmr ibn Sālim al-Khuzāʿī", "arabic": "عمرو بن سالم الخزاعي", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of the Banū Khuzāʿah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Khuzāʿī chieftain whose anguished poem after the massacre of his tribe set the Conquest of Mecca in motion.", "story": "After the Banū Bakr — armed by the Quraysh themselves — slaughtered over 20 of the Khuzāʿah, killing even within the Ḥaram, ʿAmr ibn Sālim arrived in Medina at the head of 40 delegates. He recited a page-long monologue of the most eloquent poetry, invoking his common ancestry with the Prophet ﷺ, the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, and the ancient pact ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib had codified with Khuzāʿah: '...they slaughtered us while we were bowing and in prostration!' The Prophet ﷺ, moved, replied: 'You shall be helped, O ʿAmr ibn Sālim' (Ch. 25). The raid was a direct violation of the treaty, and most scholars of Islamic history trace the Prophet's ﷺ march on Mecca directly to this appeal.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: his poem of appeal after the massacre of Khuzāʿah — 'You shall be helped, O ʿAmr ibn Sālim'"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1677–1678"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Standing before the Prophet ﷺ, who was surrounded by his Companions ؓ, ‘Amr ibn Saalim began reciting Verses of poetry through which he pleaded for the Prophet’s help. As soon as ‘Amr read the last verse of his poem, the Prophet ﷺ reassured him, saying, “You have gained help, O ‘Amr ibn Saalim. May Allah not help me if I do not help Banu Ka’ab.”", "page": 1677}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ خُزَاعَةَ الَّذِي أَنْشَدَ شِعْرَهُ الْبَاكِيَ بَعْدَ مَقْتَلَةِ قَوْمِهِ، فَكَانَ ذَلِكَ مُبْتَدَأَ فَتْحِ مَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "budayl-ibn-warqa", "name": "Budayl ibn Warqāʾ", "arabic": "بديل بن ورقاء الخزاعي", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of the Banū Khuzāʿah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Khuzāʿī chieftain who mediated at Ḥudaybiyyah and welcomed the conquering army at Marr al-Ẓahrān.", "story": "Budayl ibn Warqāʾ, chieftain of the Banū Khuzāʿah, was the first mediator to shuttle between the Prophet ﷺ and the Quraysh at Ḥudaybiyyah; when the Quraysh initially refused to hear him, wisdom prevailed and he relayed the Prophet's ﷺ message of peace (Ch. 20). On the eve of the Conquest, he was riding with Abū Sufyān and Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām when they found the plain of Marr al-Ẓahrān ablaze with the campfires of 10,000 men; Budayl rejoiced at the sight (Ch. 25). He embraced Islam in that final chapter of the Quraysh's resistance and his clan's cause — the massacre that triggered the Conquest — was vindicated by the Prophet ﷺ himself.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: first mediator at Ḥudaybiyyah, relaying the Prophet's ﷺ offer of peace to the Quraysh", "Ch. 25: rode out with Abū Sufyān and beheld the 10,000 campfires at Marr al-Ẓahrān"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1500–1502, 1693"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Along with a number of men from the Khuzaa’ah, Budail ibn Warqaa went to see the Prophet ﷺ. The Khuzaa’ah tribe, it must be remembered, was allied to the Muslims, and the Prophet ﷺ would entrust its leaders with some important secrets. The Khuzaa’ah delegates explained to the Prophet ﷺ that the Quraish were determined to prevent the Muslims from entering Makkah.", "page": 1500}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ خُزَاعَةَ، تَوَسَّطَ يَوْمَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، وَاسْتَقْبَلَ جَيْشَ الْفَتْحِ بِمَرِّ الظَّهْرَانِ."}, {"id": "dihyah-al-kalbi", "name": "Diḥyah al-Kalbī", "arabic": "دحية بن خليفة الكلبي", "kunya_titles": "Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah of the Banū Kalb; famed for his beauty", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The strikingly handsome Companion in whose likeness Jibrīl would appear, and the Prophet's ﷺ envoy toward Caesar.", "story": "Diḥyah al-Kalbī was a Companion known for his beauty — so much so that in Medina, when Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ in human form, he would often take the form of Diḥyah (Ch. 5). After Khaybar, Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy was initially allocated to Diḥyah before the Companions pointed out that the daughter of a chieftain befitted only the Prophet ﷺ, and he was compensated (Ch. 21). His greatest commission came when the Prophet ﷺ wrote to the emperors: Diḥyah carried the letter to the governor of Bosra in Syria, from where it reached Caesar — the audience that produced Heraclius's famous interrogation of Abū Sufyān (Ch. 22). He lived on in the Damascus countryside until the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah. al-Ṣallābī also records (p. 1648) that Diḥyah, returning from one such mission, was robbed of everything at Ḥismā by raiders of Judhām and Lakhm — the outrage that prompted Zayd ibn Ḥārithah's punitive expedition there in 6 AH.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 5: Jibrīl would often appear in the form of Diḥyah al-Kalbī", "Ch. 21: Ṣafiyyah initially allocated to him after Khaybar", "Ch. 22: carried the Prophet's ﷺ letter toward Caesar via the governor of Bosra"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 45 AH, al-Mizzah near Damascus", "after_sirah": "He fought in the Syrian conquests — present at Yarmūk — and then settled at al-Mizzah in the orchards of the Ghouta outside Damascus, living there quietly into the caliphate of Muʿāwiyah.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1599, 1616, 1648", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn ʿAsākir, Tārīkh Dimashq"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 45 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "And when it came time to distributing the war booty, the Prophet ﷺ gave her to Daihyah Al-Kalbee ؓ. A man went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, you have given Safiyyah bint Huyai, the most important woman among her people, to Daihyah; it is not befitting that she be given to anyone other than you.” The Prophet ﷺ approved of the man’s advice; he ﷺ said to Daihyah ؓ, “Take a girl other than this one from the captives,” after which the Prophet ﷺ took her for himself.", "page": 1599}, "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الْبَارِعُ الْجَمَالِ الَّذِي كَانَ جِبْرِيلُ يَأْتِي عَلَى صُورَتِهِ، وَرَسُولُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ إِلَى قَيْصَرَ."}, {"id": "khirash-ibn-umayyah", "name": "Khirāsh ibn Umayyah", "arabic": "خراش بن أمية الخزاعي", "kunya_titles": "Of the tribe of Khuzāʿah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Khuzāʿī envoy sent into Mecca at Ḥudaybiyyah, who barely escaped with his life.", "story": "When mediation at Ḥudaybiyyah stalled, the Prophet ﷺ sent Khirāsh ibn Umayyah of Khuzāʿah into Mecca to negotiate, mounting him on al-Thaʿlab, one of the Prophet's ﷺ own camels. The Quraysh took badly to the embassy: they hamstrung the camel and nearly killed Khirāsh, who was saved only by the intervention of those wary of inflaming tribal tensions with the Banū Khuzāʿah (Ch. 20). He rushed back to the Muslim camp — and his rough reception paved the way for ʿUthmān's more protected mission into the city.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: sent as envoy into Mecca on the Prophet's ﷺ camel al-Thaʿlab; attacked and nearly killed"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1509"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Kharraash ibn Umayyah Al-Khuzaa’ee ؓ was chosen to be that ambassador. Having mounted a camel named ‘Ath-Tha’ab,’ Kharraash ؓ rode towards Makkah to convey the Prophet’s message to the Quraish. Once he entered Makkah, however, the Quraish did not even give him the opportunity to speak. They killed the camel and showed every intention of killing Kharraash ؓ himself, but they were prevented from doing so by men from the Ahaabeesh.", "page": 1509}, "who_ar": "الرَّسُولُ الْخُزَاعِيُّ الَّذِي بُعِثَ إِلَى مَكَّةَ زَمَنَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، فَكَادَ يَهْلِكُ وَنَجَا بِنَفْسِهِ."}, {"id": "sawad-ibn-ghaziyyah", "name": "Sawād ibn Ghaziyyah", "arabic": "سواد بن غزية", "kunya_titles": "An Anṣārī ally of the Banū ʿAdiyy ibn al-Najjār", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Badr soldier who demanded qiṣāṣ from the Prophet ﷺ for a poke — so he could hug him before battle.", "story": "As the Prophet ﷺ straightened the rows before Badr, he poked Sawād ibn Ghaziyyah in the stomach to bring him back in line. Sawād said, 'O Messenger of Allah, you have poked me and hurt me... I demand qiṣāṣ (equal retaliation).' The Prophet ﷺ bared his own stomach — and Sawād hugged and kissed it. Asked, 'What is this, O Sawād?', he explained that with battle looming, he wanted his last act to be his skin touching the Prophet's ﷺ (Ch. 14). The book highlights the moment as proof of the Prophet's ﷺ absolute justice even as a commander, and of Sawād's overwhelming love for him on the edge of death. al-Ṣallābī adds (p. 974) that the Prophet ﷺ then supplicated for Sawād — a sign, he notes, that he was pleased with him.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: demanded qiṣāṣ for a poke while the rows were straightened at Badr — then kissed the Prophet's ﷺ stomach"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 973–974"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laid bare his stomach and said, “Get even (with me).” But instead of poking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the stomach, Sawwaad ؓ embraced him and kissed his stomach. The Prophet ﷺ asked, “What made you do this, O Sawwaad?” He replied, “O Messenger of Allah, you see what is about to happen. During my last meeting with you, I wanted my skin to touch your skin.”", "page": 973}, "who_ar": "جُنْدِيُّ بَدْرٍ الَّذِي طَلَبَ الْقِصَاصَ مِنَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ مِنْ طَعْنَةٍ خَفِيفَةٍ، لِيَعْتَنِقَهُ قَبْلَ الْقِتَالِ."}, {"id": "mistah-ibn-uthathah", "name": "Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah", "arabic": "مسطح بن أثاثة", "kunya_titles": "A poor Muhājir, relative of Abū Bakr", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Badr veteran and dependant of Abū Bakr who slipped into the slander of the Ifk — and became the occasion for a verse on forgiveness.", "story": "Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah, a poor Muhājir and Badr veteran whose living expenses Abū Bakr regularly paid, was among the Muslims who fell into repeating the Hypocrites' slander against ʿĀʾishah during the Ifk episode — the book handles the affair with the same restraint as the Hadith narrators, who never record the slander's specifics (Ch. 18). It was his own mother's curse — 'May Misṭaḥ be cursed!' — that first alerted ʿĀʾishah to what was being said (Ch. 18). After ʿĀʾishah's vindication by Revelation, a wounded Abū Bakr vowed never to spend on Misṭaḥ again; Allah then revealed the verse urging forgiveness — 'Do you not love that Allah should forgive you?' — and Abū Bakr resumed his support and vowed never to stop (Ch. 18). Misṭaḥ received the prescribed punishment, repented, and remained, as the book's framing insists, a Companion whose lapse was met by divine standards of justice and mercy that are simply unparalleled.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 18: named among those who repeated the slander in the Ifk episode", "Ch. 18: Abū Bakr's vow to cut him off was overturned by Revelation urging forgiveness"], "badges": ["Badr"], "death": "d. 34 AH (some report he lived to witness Ṣiffīn and died 37 AH)", "after_sirah": "He lived quietly in Medina among the Badriyyūn whose rank the Prophet ﷺ had defended even in his lapse; one report has him surviving to stand with ʿAlī at Ṣiffīn before dying that same year, though most place his death in 34 AH.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1326, 1332", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 34 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Umm Mistah then tripped on (the bottom of) her robe; she exclaimed, ‘May Mistah (her son) be destroyed (or, may evil befall him).’ I said to her, ‘Poorly have you spoken! Are you cursing a man who participated in Badr?’ She said, ‘O the naiveté! Did you not hear what he said (for Mistah was among those Muslims who believed in the lie that the hypocrites had concocted against ‘Aishah ؓ)?’", "page": 1326}, "who_ar": "شَهِدَ بَدْرًا وَكَانَ فِي عِيَالِ أَبِي بَكْرٍ، فَزَلَّ فِي حَدِيثِ الْإِفْكِ، فَكَانَ سَبَبَ نُزُولِ آيَةِ الْعَفْوِ."}, {"id": "safwan-ibn-muattal", "name": "Ṣafwān ibn Muʿaṭṭal al-Sulamī", "arabic": "صفوان بن المعطل السلمي", "kunya_titles": "Abū ʿAmr al-Sulamī", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The chaste rear-guard soldier slandered in the Ifk, personally vindicated by the Prophet ﷺ, and later blessed with martyrdom.", "story": "Returning from the Muraysīʿ campaign, Ṣafwān ibn Muʿaṭṭal — who had overslept and was catching up behind the army — found ʿĀʾishah asleep and alone where the caravan had left her. He announced himself only with 'Innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn', lowered his camel, turned his back as she mounted, and led it on foot to Medina without exchanging a single word (Ch. 18). For this act of pure chivalry the Hypocrites made him the second target of the Ifk slander. The Prophet ﷺ declared before the assembled Companions, 'I know nothing but good from this man they mention' — the honour of being personally praised by the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 18). A chaste bachelor, he later swore, 'I have never even lifted the veil of a woman.' The book records that Allah blessed him with martyrdom during the reign of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (Ch. 18).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 18: escorted ʿĀʾishah back to Medina in silence and perfect propriety", "Ch. 18: publicly vindicated by the Prophet ﷺ — 'I know nothing but good from this man'"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "c. 19 AH — martyred at Shimshāṭ, on the Armenian frontier, during the caliphate of ʿUmar (some sources report d. c. 58–60 AH at Sumaysāṭ)", "after_sirah": "He joined the northern frontier campaigns after the Prophet's ﷺ passing; Ibn Saʿd and al-Wāqidī record him falling martyr at Shimshāṭ in the Armenian campaign under ʿUmar — the martyrdom the book alludes to — though some Damascene sources stretch his life decades later to Muʿāwiyah's era.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1325–1326", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn ʿAsākir, Tārīkh Dimashq"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 19 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Safwaan ibn Al-Muattil As-Sullamee ؓ, and then Adh-Dhakwaanee, was in the rear of the army (it was his job to remain behind the army, and pick up things anyone might have inadvertently dropped during the journey). It was the last part of the night, and when it was morning, he came across the place where I was. He saw a blackness (from a distance), and realized that it was a person who was sleeping. He came to me and recognized me when he saw me, for he had seen me before the legislation of the Hijaab (was revealed).", "page": 1325}, "who_ar": "صَاحِبُ السَّاقَةِ الْعَفِيفُ الَّذِي رُمِيَ فِي حَدِيثِ الْإِفْكِ، فَبَرَّأَهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ بِنَفْسِهِ، ثُمَّ رُزِقَ الشَّهَادَةَ بَعْدُ."}, {"id": "amir-ibn-al-akwa", "name": "ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ", "arabic": "عامر بن الأكوع", "kunya_titles": "Uncle of Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ; poet of the Khaybar march", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Companion who answered Mirḥab's challenge at Khaybar and fell a martyr.", "story": "At Khaybar, when the Jewish warrior Mirḥab strode out and challenged the Muslims to a duel, it was ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ who accepted — and was martyred in the exchange, before ʿAlī stepped forward and cut Mirḥab down, breaking the defenders' morale (Ch. 21). In the fuller classical accounts, ʿĀmir was the poet who had chanted for the army on the night march to Khaybar, earning the Prophet's ﷺ duʿāʾ; his own sword rebounded fatally upon him in the duel, and when some feared his deeds were void, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed he would have a double reward. His nephew Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ carried the family's fame as one of the great skirmishers of the era. (al-Ṣallābī records his chant on the night march and the Prophet's ﷺ 'May Allah have mercy on him', p. 1582; he attributes ʿĀmir's martyrdom to Marḥab's fighting in the duel, p. 1586.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 21: accepted Mirḥab's duel at Khaybar and was martyred"], "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "7 AH — martyred at Khaybar", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1582, 1586", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 7 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Salamah ibn Al-Akwa’ ؓ related that ‘Aamir ibn Al-Akwa’ ؓ encouraged the people to proceed onwards by reciting Verses of poetry, beautiful Verses in which he ؓ praised Allah, asked for forgiveness, supplicated for help from Allah against the enemy, and invoked Him to send tranquility and calmness upon the Muslims. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked to be told who it was that was reading those Verses and encouraging the people onwards. “’Aamir ibn Al-Akwa’,” the Prophet ﷺ was informed. The Prophet ﷺ said, “May Allah have mercy on him.”", "page": 1582}, "who_ar": "الصَّحَابِيُّ الَّذِي أَجَابَ دَعْوَةَ مَرْحَبٍ إِلَى الْمُبَارَزَةِ يَوْمَ خَيْبَرَ فَقُتِلَ شَهِيدًا."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-amr-ibn-al-as", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "arabic": "عبد الله بن عمرو بن العاص", "kunya_titles": "Abū Muḥammad; the Companion permitted to write Hadith", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The devout son of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, an early convert before his father, and eyewitness-narrator of Mecca's worst persecution.", "story": "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ appears in the book as the narrator preserving one of the darkest scenes of the Meccan period. When ʿUrwah ibn Zubayr asked him the worst thing he saw done to the Prophet ﷺ, he described ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ throwing his garment around the Prophet's ﷺ neck as he prayed at the Kaʿbah and choking him, until Abū Bakr ran over, pushed ʿUqbah off, and shouted, 'Are you going to kill a man just because he says: My Lord is Allah?' (Ch. 7). ʿAbdullāh converted before his own famous father, was known for relentless worship, and — uniquely — received the Prophet's ﷺ permission to write down Hadith, compiling the ṣaḥīfah he called al-Ṣādiqah. It is likewise ʿAbdullāh whom al-Ṣallābī quotes (p. 120) swearing that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ 'is described in the Torah with the same description that he is given in the Qur'an' — 'My slave and Messenger... I have named you al-Mutawakkil; he is neither rude nor harsh, and he is not one who raises his voice in the marketplace.'", "book_moments": ["Ch. 7: narrated the choking of the Prophet ﷺ at the Kaʿbah and Abū Bakr's intervention"], "badges": ["Ḥadīth Scholar"], "death": "d. c. 63 AH (also reported 65 and 68 AH; place variously Egypt, Ṭāʾif, or Syria)", "after_sirah": "He stood — reluctantly, at his father's insistence — on the Umayyad side at Ṣiffīn, and spent the rest of his life lamenting it. He settled on his estate in Egypt, devoted to worship and to his ṣaḥīfah al-Ṣādiqah, most of which survives through the family chain of his great-grandson ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 120", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 63 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Abdullah ibn ‘Amr ؓ said, “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is described in the Torah with the same description that he ﷺ is given in the Qur‘an: ‘O Prophet, We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of glad tidings, a warner, and protector of the illiterate ones (i.e., Arabs). You are My slave and Messenger. I have named you Al-Mutawakkil.’”", "page": 120}, "who_ar": "ابْنُ عَمْرِو بْنِ الْعَاصِ الْعَابِدُ، أَسْلَمَ قَبْلَ أَبِيهِ، وَرَوَى مَا شَهِدَهُ بِعَيْنِهِ مِنْ أَشَدِّ مَا لَقِيَ الْمُسْلِمُونَ بِمَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "ukaydir-of-dumah", "name": "Ukaydir of Dūmah", "arabic": "أكيدر بن عبد الملك", "kunya_titles": "King of Dūmat al-Jandal, of the tribe of Kindah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Christian king captured by Khālid during the Tabūk campaign — per this book, a treaty vassal who did not convert, not a Companion.", "story": "During the 20 days at Tabūk, the Prophet ﷺ sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd with a small contingent to the tribe of Kindah, and Khālid swiftly captured their king, Ukaydir, bringing him before the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 27). The book is explicit in its framing — and this entry follows it: Ukaydir did not convert, but agreed to break off ties with the Roman Empire and serve as a vassal, part of the buffer of northern treaties that proved crucial in the expeditions after the sīrah. He sent the Prophet ﷺ a series of gifts, including the opulent gold-threaded garment at which the Companions marvelled — prompting the Prophet's ﷺ words, 'By Allah, the handkerchief of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise is more luxurious than this!' (Ch. 27). Some later sources debate whether he ever accepted Islam; per this book, he is included here as a figure of the sīrah, not as a Companion. (al-Ṣallābī's account, p. 1840, has Ukaydir captured by Khālid's horsemen while out hunting, agreeing to the jizyah only after his capture — and the Companions marvelling at the expensive robe Ukaydir himself wore in the Muslim camp, which occasioned the Prophet's ﷺ remark about Saʿd's handkerchiefs.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 27: captured by Khālid ibn al-Walīd near Tabūk; made a vassal treaty without converting", "Ch. 27: his gold-threaded gift occasioned the Hadith on Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's handkerchief in Paradise"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 12 AH — killed at Dūmat al-Jandal after repudiating the treaty (per classical accounts)", "after_sirah": "After the Prophet's ﷺ passing he broke off the vassal treaty; when Khālid ibn al-Walīd swept back through the north during the riddah-era campaigns, Dūmat al-Jandal fell and Ukaydir was killed. The sources continue to debate whether he ever inwardly accepted Islam — the book's cautious framing is the sound one.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1840", "Tārīkh al-Ṭabarī", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 12 AH", "who_ar": "الْمَلِكُ النَّصْرَانِيُّ الَّذِي أَسَرَهُ خَالِدٌ فِي غَزْوَةِ تَبُوكَ، وَهُوَ عَلَى مَا فِي هَذَا الْكِتَابِ مُعَاهَدٌ لَمْ يُسْلِمْ، وَلَيْسَ بِصَحَابِيٍّ."}, {"id": "zayd-ibn-amr-ibn-nufayl", "name": "Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl", "arabic": "زيد بن عمرو بن نفيل", "kunya_titles": "One of the Ḥunafāʾ; father of Saʿīd ibn Zayd, cousin of ʿUmar", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The ḥanīf who rejected idolatry and sought the religion of Ibrāhīm — but died before prophethood, and so is not technically a Companion.", "story": "The book names Zayd ibn ʿAmr among the four prominent Ḥunafāʾ — with Waraqah ibn Nawfal, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Jaḥsh, and ʿUthmān ibn al-Ḥuwayrith — who made a pact to search for the true religion of Ibrāhīm, turning away from shirk toward tawḥīd; Ḥanīfiyyah, it notes, was the closest thing to Islam in those times (Ch. 2). Zayd famously refused to eat what was sacrificed to idols and rescued infant girls from burial alive, and the wider tradition records the Prophet ﷺ affirming that he would be raised as a nation unto himself. An honest note: Zayd died before the prophethood began and never met the Prophet ﷺ as a believer, so he is not technically a Companion — he stands in this directory as a righteous forerunner. His son Saʿīd ibn Zayd became one of the Ten Promised Paradise.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 2: named among the four Ḥunafāʾ who pledged to seek the religion of Ibrāhīm"], "badges": [], "death": "Died shortly before the prophethood began (c. five years before revelation), Mecca", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 38, 48, 96–97", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "الْحَنِيفُ الَّذِي رَفَضَ عِبَادَةَ الْأَوْثَانِ وَطَلَبَ دِينَ إِبْرَاهِيمَ، لَكِنَّهُ مَاتَ قَبْلَ الْبِعْثَةِ، فَلَيْسَ صَحَابِيًّا فِي الِاصْطِلَاحِ."}, {"id": "quss-ibn-saidah", "name": "Quss ibn Sāʿidah al-Iyādī", "arabic": "قس بن ساعدة الإيادي", "kunya_titles": "Orator of the Arabs at ʿUkāẓ", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The pre-Islamic monotheist orator who preached against idolatry — not a Companion, but a voice of Ḥanīfiyyah before the dawn.", "story": "The book gives Quss ibn Sāʿidah al-Iyādī a single but telling mention: he was 'another significant Ḥanīf', who used to preach against idolatry in the age when Ḥanīfiyyah was the closest thing to Islam (Ch. 2). The wider tradition remembers him as the legendary orator of the fair of ʿUkāẓ, whose sermons on death and the One God the Prophet ﷺ later recalled hearing in his youth. He died before the prophethood and is therefore not a Companion; he is included here, per the book's framing, as one of the scattered monotheist lights of the Jāhiliyyah. al-Ṣallābī cites (pp. 38–39) Abū Nuʿaym's narration from Ibn ʿAbbās of that very preaching at ʿUkāẓ: 'The truth shall become known from this direction' — pointing toward Mecca — a man of the children of Luʾayy ibn Ghālib who would call to the word of sincerity, whom Quss vowed he would be the first to follow if he lived to see him sent.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 2: cited as a significant Ḥanīf who preached against idolatry"], "badges": [], "death": "Died before the prophethood", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 38–39", "al-Iṣāba", "Ibn Kathīr, al-Bidāya wa-l-Nihāya"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Another example of a Haneef - a pure Monotheist who followed the religion of Ibraaheem and Ismaa’eel - was Qiss ibn Saa’idah Al-Iyaadee. Qiss worshipped Allah alone, without associating any partner with Him in worship; and he was known for his intelligence, wisdom, insight, and noble character. He believed in resurrection after death, and would, prior to the advent of Islam, give glad tidings about the coming of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.", "page": 38}, "who_ar": "خَطِيبٌ مُوَحِّدٌ فِي الْجَاهِلِيَّةِ، أَنْكَرَ عِبَادَةَ الْأَوْثَانِ، وَلَيْسَ بِصَحَابِيٍّ، لَكِنَّهُ صَوْتُ الْحَنِيفِيَّةِ قَبْلَ بُزُوغِ الْفَجْرِ."}, {"id": "amr-ibn-umayyah-al-damri", "name": "ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī", "arabic": "عمرو بن أمية الضمري", "kunya_titles": "Of the Banū Ḍamrah; a trusted courier of the Prophet ﷺ", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The hardy Ḍamrī scout who survived the massacre of Biʾr Maʿūnah and served the Prophet ﷺ on distant errands.", "story": "ʿAmr ibn Umayyah was one of only three survivors when 400–500 tribesmen of ʿAṣiyyah, Riʿl, and Dhakwān slaughtered the seventy Qurʾan-teachers at Biʾr Maʿūnah. He and al-Mundhir ibn Muhammad had left to run an errand; returning, they saw the vultures circling and knew. ʿAmr counselled reporting back to the Prophet ﷺ, but al-Mundhir refused to be 'reduced to a messenger that lives to tell their story' — they marched back in, al-Mundhir was killed, and ʿAmr was taken hostage and eventually released to carry the terrible news to Medina (Ch. 17). On the road home, his killing of two men who unbeknownst to him held the Prophet's ﷺ protection created the blood-money obligation that set off the Banū Naḍīr crisis. In the wider sīrah literature he served as the Prophet's ﷺ courier to the Negus of Abyssinia.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: survivor of Biʾr Maʿūnah; brought the news of the massacre back to the Prophet ﷺ"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 55 AH, Medina", "after_sirah": "The Negus mission was only the most famous of his courier errands; he remained a trusted man of state through the early caliphates and then settled back in Medina, where he died during the reign of Muʿāwiyah.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1592, 1639", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. c. 55 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Such was the degree to which the Prophet ﷺ loved Ja’far ؓ. They came because the Prophet ﷺ sent ‘Amr ibn Umayyah Ad-Damree ؓ to An-Najaashee, the king of Abyssinia, asking for their return; An-Najaashee then generously provided two ships for their sea voyage to Arabia.", "page": 1592}, "who_ar": "الضَّمْرِيُّ الْجَلْدُ الَّذِي كَانَ عَيْنًا لِلْمُسْلِمِينَ، نَجَا مِنْ مَقْتَلَةِ بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ، وَكَانَ يَبْعَثُهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ فِي الْمَهَامِّ الْبَعِيدَةِ."}, {"id": "addas", "name": "ʿAddās", "arabic": "عداس", "kunya_titles": "The Christian servant boy of Nineveh, in Ṭāʾif", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Christian servant from Nineveh who recognised the Prophet ﷺ in the orchard outside Ṭāʾif and kissed his hands and feet.", "story": "At the Prophet's ﷺ lowest worldly moment — bleeding and rejected outside Ṭāʾif — the orchard's owners sent their Christian servant ʿAddās with a plate of grapes. When the Prophet ﷺ said 'Bismillāh' and then named 'Yūnus ibn Mattā, my brother — he was a Prophet and I am a Prophet', ʿAddās, a lone Christian from Nineveh surrounded by pagans, was stunned that anyone in these lands knew Jonah, and began kissing the Prophet's ﷺ head, hands, and feet, telling his scandalised masters, 'There is no-one on Earth better than him, for he told me things only a Prophet could know' (Ch. 9). Years later, ordered to fight at Badr, he refused: 'By Allah, the mountains could not harm him' — while his masters ʿUtbah and Shaybah both died in that battle (Ch. 9). The book leaves his ultimate status untold; he stands here as the one soul in Ṭāʾif who saw clearly that day.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 9: brought grapes to the wounded Prophet ﷺ; recognised him through the mention of Yūnus ibn Mattā", "Ch. 9: refused to fight the Prophet ﷺ at Badr"], "badges": [], "death": "", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 521, 535–536"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "After being on the receiving end of such harsh treatment, the Prophet ﷺ did not rest for even a short while; in fact, he even performed Da’wah on his way back from Taaif. For on his way back, the Prophet ﷺ met ‘Addaas, who was a Christian. The Prophet ﷺ invited ‘Addaas ؓ unto Allah, and the latter embraced Islam.", "page": 521}, "who_ar": "الْغُلَامُ النَّصْرَانِيُّ مِنْ أَهْلِ نِينَوَى، عَرَفَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ فِي الْبُسْتَانِ خَارِجَ الطَّائِفِ فَقَبَّلَ يَدَيْهِ وَقَدَمَيْهِ."}, {"id": "al-hakam-ibn-hazn", "name": "al-Ḥakam ibn Ḥazn", "arabic": "الحكم بن حزن الكلفي", "kunya_titles": "Delegate of the Banū Kulfah", "group": "beyond", "tier": "minor", "who": "The delegation-era Companion who preserved a jewel from the Prophet's ﷺ Friday sermon.", "story": "Al-Ḥakam ibn Ḥazn led a party of around nine delegates from the Banū Kulfah to Medina in the Year of Delegations. He narrated how the Prophet ﷺ made duʿāʾ for them, fed them, and hosted them for days, during which they attended the Friday Prayer; one phrase of the sermon stayed with him for life: 'O people! Do what you can, and know that you will never be able to do everything you are commanded, but come as close as you can and fill in the gap' (Ch. 28). A small entry in the sīrah — and a perfect specimen of how the delegations carried the Prophet's ﷺ words back across Arabia.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: led the Banū Kulfah delegation and preserved a line of the Prophet's ﷺ Friday sermon"], "badges": [], "death": "", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ مِنْ زَمَنِ الْوُفُودِ، حَفِظَ دُرَّةً مِنْ خُطْبَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يَوْمَ الْجُمُعَةِ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "d. 74 AH (also reported 66 AH), Kufa", "tier": "minor", "id": "jabir-ibn-samurah", "name": "Jābir ibn Samurah", "arabic": "جابر بن سمرة", "group": "beyond", "who": "A young Companion whose descriptions of the Prophet's ﷺ radiant appearance open the book.", "story": "Jābir ibn Samurah is one of the Companions the book quotes in its opening portrait of the Prophet ﷺ: he saw the Prophet ﷺ on a moonlit night and kept looking between him and the moon — 'and he was more beautiful to me than the full moon' (Ch. 1). A young Companion of Banū Suwāʾah who transmitted many descriptions of the Prophet's ﷺ appearance and gatherings, he settled in Kufa and lived long into the era of the Successors.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 1: compared the Prophet's ﷺ face to the full moon — 'more beautiful to me than the moon'"], "after_sirah": "He settled in Kufa and became one of its longest-surviving links to the Prophet's ﷺ own gatherings, transmitting his descriptions and the famous Hadith of the twelve leaders to the Successors there until his death in the mid-70s AH. al-Ṣallābī likewise cites him (p. 124) transmitting the Prophet's ﷺ words about the stone in Mecca that would greet him with salām before his prophethood — 'indeed, I know it now.'", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 124", "Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 74 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "The coming mission of Prophethood was being hinted at in many ways to the Prophet ﷺ. For instance, Jaabir ibn Samurah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once said, “Verily, I know a stone in Makkah that would extend greetings of peace to me before I was sent (as a Prophet); indeed, I (still) know it now.”", "page": 124}, "who_ar": "صَحَابِيٌّ فَتِيٌّ، بِأَوْصَافِهِ لِإِشْرَاقِ طَلْعَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ يُفْتَتَحُ الْكِتَابُ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "id": "abdurrahman-ibn-aqil", "name": "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAqīl", "arabic": "عبد الرحمن بن عقيل", "group": "beyond", "who": "A delegate of the Year of Delegations whose testimony captures the Prophet's ﷺ transforming presence.", "story": "The book preserves his unforgettable testimony from the Year of Delegations: 'When we arrived, there was no-one on Earth more despised to us than the one we were sent to meet, but when we left, there was no-one on Earth more beloved to us than the one from whom we departed' (Ch. 28). In that same sitting a youngster asked why the Prophet ﷺ did not request a kingdom like Sulaymān's — and the Prophet ﷺ laughed and explained that he had saved his one guaranteed request as intercession for his Ummah on the Day of Judgement.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: 'no-one more despised when we arrived... no-one more beloved when we left'"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "وَافِدٌ مِنْ وُفُودِ عَامِ الْوُفُودِ، صَوَّرَتْ شَهَادَتُهُ أَثَرَ حَضْرَةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ فِي تَغْيِيرِ النُّفُوسِ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "id": "busr-ibn-sufyan", "name": "Busr ibn Sufyān al-Khuzāʿī", "arabic": "بسر بن سفيان الخزاعي", "group": "beyond", "who": "The Khuzāʿī scout sent ahead of the Ḥudaybiyyah march to observe the Quraysh.", "story": "When the Prophet ﷺ set out for ʿUmrah in 6 AH, he sent Busr ibn Sufyān al-Khuzāʿī ahead to Mecca to observe the Quraysh. Busr returned with the intelligence that shaped the whole encounter: the Quraysh had gathered their allies and sworn to block the Muslims from the Kaʿbah, with Khālid ibn al-Walīd's cavalry already dispatched (Ch. 20). His tribe, Khuzāʿah, were the Prophet's ﷺ trusted confidants in the region, Muslim and non-Muslim alike. (al-Ṣallābī, who gives his name as Bishr ibn Sufyān al-Kaʿbī al-Khuzāʿī, records him delivering this report to the Prophet ﷺ at ʿUsfān, p. 1492.)", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: scouted Mecca before Ḥudaybiyyah and reported the Quraysh war preparations"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1492"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached ’Asfaan, he ﷺ was met by Bishr ibn Sufyaan Al-Ka’bee Al-Khuzaa’ee ؓ. Bishr said, “O Messenger of Allah, the Quraish has heard about your journey. And they have come out with their women and children (i.e., they are prepared for a long confrontation; also, the women and children will try to dissuade them from fleeing from battle). They have attired themselves in leopard skins, and they have vowed to Allah that you will never be able to enter upon them by force.”", "page": 1492}, "who_ar": "الْخُزَاعِيُّ الَّذِي بَعَثَهُ النَّبِيُّ ﷺ عَيْنًا أَمَامَ مَسِيرِهِ إِلَى الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ لِيَأْتِيَهُ بِخَبَرِ قُرَيْشٍ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "4 AH — martyred at Biʾr Maʿūnah (per classical sources)", "tier": "minor", "id": "al-hakam-ibn-kaysan", "name": "al-Ḥakam ibn Kaysān", "arabic": "الحكم بن كيسان", "group": "beyond", "who": "A captive of the Nakhlah expedition who chose Islam freely after his ransom was paid.", "story": "Captured in the sariyyah of ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh at Nakhlah, al-Ḥakam was held until the ransom arrived — and then, a free man with every right to return to Mecca, he accepted Islam and went back to the Prophet ﷺ of his own accord (Ch. 13). Classical sources record that he later joined the seventy reciters sent to Najd and was martyred at Biʾr Maʿūnah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 13: accepted Islam of his own accord after being ransomed following Nakhlah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 880, 890", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 4 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Seeing the opportunity to overcome a trading caravan that belonged to the Quraish, they did so, killing its leader, ‘Amr ibn Al-Hadramee, seizing its merchandise as war booty, and taking two of its men as captives: ‘Uthmaan ibn ‘Abdullah and Al-Hakam ibn Kaisaan. They then returned with their two captives to Al-Madeenah; the problem was that neither ‘Abdullah ibn Jahsh nor the men who were with him had realized that they attacked the caravan on the first day of one of the four inviolable months, months during which fighting was prohibited.", "page": 880}, "who_ar": "أَسِيرٌ مِنْ أَسْرَى سَرِيَّةِ نَخْلَةَ، اخْتَارَ الْإِسْلَامَ طَوْعًا بَعْدَ أَنْ أُدِّيَ فِدَاؤُهُ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "id": "malik-ibn-awf", "name": "Mālik ibn ʿAwf al-Naṣrī", "arabic": "مالك بن عوف النصري", "group": "beyond", "who": "The young Hawāzin commander at Ḥunayn who later embraced Islam.", "story": "At just thirty years old, Mālik ibn ʿAwf marched the whole of Hawāzin — with their women, children and herds behind the fighters to make retreat unthinkable — against the Muslims at Ḥunayn, and his archers' ambush in the ravine caused the initial rout (Ch. 26). The book leaves him at the battle; classical sources complete the story: when the Prophet ﷺ promised him his family, his wealth, and a hundred camels if he came as a Muslim, Mālik embraced Islam, praised the Prophet ﷺ in verse as the noblest of all mankind, and became a loyal commander thereafter.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 26: commanded Hawāzin at Ḥunayn with the all-or-nothing strategy"], "after_sirah": "The Prophet ﷺ made good on every promise and then set him in command over the Muslims of his own people — a command he used to harry Thaqīf until Ṭāʾif itself came in to Islam. He is later reported in the ranks of the Persian conquests; the classical sources record no firm death date for him. al-Ṣallābī confirms the sequence (pp. 1777–1779): the promise of his family, his wealth, and a hundred camels relayed to him inside Ṭāʾif; his coming out to the Prophet ﷺ to accept Islam; and his appointment over his people and the neighbouring tribes — upon which Mālik, overwhelmed by such generosity, praised the Prophet ﷺ in verse.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1751–1752, 1777–1779", "Ibn Hishām, Sīrah", "al-Iṣāba", "Usd al-Ghāba"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Maalik ibn ‘Auf decided to ride out with their women, children, and wealth following the rear of the army. Duraid asked him why he would do such a thing, and Maalik responded, “I wanted to place behind every man his family and his wealth, so that he will fight to defend them.”", "page": 1751}, "who_ar": "قَائِدُ هَوَازِنَ الشَّابُّ يَوْمَ حُنَيْنٍ، ثُمَّ أَسْلَمَ بَعْدَ ذَلِكَ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "d. 41 AH (also reported 40 or 42 AH), Kufa, at a great age", "tier": "minor", "id": "labid-ibn-rabiah", "name": "Labīd ibn Rabīʿah", "arabic": "لبيد بن ربيعة", "group": "beyond", "who": "One of Arabia's greatest poets — author of 'the truest word a poet ever spoke'.", "story": "A poet of the legendary Muʿallaqāt class. The book records his line 'Indeed everything besides Allah is falsehood', which the Prophet ﷺ described as the truest word a poet ever spoke (Ch. 6) — though when Labīd continued 'and every pleasure will undoubtedly cease', ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn objected that the pleasure of Paradise never ends. Labīd embraced Islam in the delegations era, and it is famously related that he thereafter all but abandoned poetry, saying the Qur'an had replaced it for him.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: his verse praised by the Prophet ﷺ as the truest a poet ever spoke"], "after_sirah": "He settled in Kufa and, by his own word, traded poetry for the Qur'an — the sources say he completed barely a line after his Islam, answering ʿUmar's request for his verse by writing out Sūrat al-Baqarah instead. He died there at a legendary age, well past a hundred by every account, at the very start of Muʿāwiyah's reign — the year of the community's reunification.", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 414", "al-Iṣāba", "al-Istīʿāb", "Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 41 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Among those present was Labeed ibn Rabee’ah, the famous poet. Labeed was reciting some of his poetry. “Indeed, everything other than Allah is false,” Labeed said. ‘Uthmaan ؓ said, “You have spoken the truth.”", "page": 414}, "who_ar": "مِنْ أَعْظَمِ شُعَرَاءِ الْعَرَبِ، قَائِلُ «أَصْدَقِ كَلِمَةٍ قَالَهَا شَاعِرٌ»."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": ["Martyr"], "death": "8 AH — martyred as an envoy near Muʾtah", "tier": "minor", "id": "al-harith-ibn-umayr", "name": "al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUmayr al-Azdī", "arabic": "الحارث بن عمير الأزدي", "group": "beyond", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ envoy whose murder set the Battle of Muʾtah in motion.", "story": "The Prophet's ﷺ messenger sent north with a letter to the ruler of Buṣrā. He was intercepted by the Ghassanid governor Shuraḥbīl ibn ʿAmr, tied up and executed — the only envoy of the Prophet ﷺ ever killed, a crime against every norm of Arabia (Ch. 24). His murder was the direct cause of the expedition to Muʾtah, where three thousand Muslims faced the massed armies of Rome and its Arab clients.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 24: martyred as an envoy — the murder that triggered Muʾtah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1648–1649"], "after_sirah": "al-Ṣallābī frames his killing (pp. 1648–1649) as the opening of the northern tribes' escalation after Ḥudaybiyyah: Shuraḥbīl al-Ghassānī's act violated the universally accepted rule that ambassadors were guaranteed safe passage, and the Muslim army later encamped at the very place where al-Ḥārith had been killed.", "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "d. 8 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After the Treaty of Al-Hudaibiyyah, the Arabs of the north took to a more intense military campaign against the Muslims, a campaign that began with the death of Al-Haarith ibn ‘Umair Al-Azdee ؓ. Al-Haarith was the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to the king of Basrah, who was the viceroy of the emperor of Rome. Shurahbeel ibn ‘Amr Al-Ghassaanee killed Al-Haarith ؓ, in what was a blatant violation of an unwritten though universally accepted rule which stated that ambassadors and messengers were guaranteed safe passage and should never be harmed.", "page": 1648}, "who_ar": "رَسُولُ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الَّذِي كَانَ قَتْلُهُ سَبَبَ غَزْوَةِ مُؤْتَةَ."}, {"kunya_titles": "", "badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "id": "rabiah-ibn-abbad", "name": "Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbbād", "arabic": "ربيعة بن عباد", "group": "beyond", "who": "An eyewitness of the Prophet ﷺ preaching at the tribal fairs — trailed by Abū Lahab.", "story": "Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbbād saw with his own boyhood eyes a scene the book paints from the final Meccan years: the Prophet ﷺ going tent to tent at the fairs and pilgrim camps saying 'O people, say lā ilāha illa Allāh and you will succeed' — while behind him a bright-faced man with two locks of hair cried 'Do not believe him!' It was his own uncle, Abū Lahab (Ch. 11). Rabīʿah later became a Muslim and transmitted this vivid memory.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 11: witnessed the Prophet ﷺ preaching at the fairs, shadowed by Abū Lahab"], "after_sirah": "He accepted Islam and lived to a great age, one of the last eyewitnesses of the Prophet ﷺ preaching at the fairs still narrating in Medina in the Successors' time; no firm death date is recorded.", "beyond_sources": ["Ibn Saʿd, Ṭabaqāt", "al-Iṣāba", "Musnad Aḥmad"], "ra": "رضي الله عنه", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "شَاهِدُ عِيَانٍ رَأَى النَّبِيَّ ﷺ يَدْعُو النَّاسَ فِي أَسْوَاقِ الْعَرَبِ وَأَبُو لَهَبٍ يَتْبَعُهُ."}, {"id": "abu-talib", "name": "Abū Ṭālib", "arabic": "أبو طالب", "kunya_titles": "Uncle and protector of the Prophet ﷺ; chieftain of Banū Hāshim", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ uncle who raised him and shielded the daʿwah for a decade without ever accepting Islam.", "story": "Abū Ṭālib raised the Prophet ﷺ from the age of eight and, as chieftain of Banū Hāshim, stood immovably against every Qurashī demand to hand him over — 'Say whatever you please, for by Allah they will never reach you' (Ch. 6) — enduring the boycott in the valley alongside the believers (Ch. 8). On his deathbed the Prophet ﷺ pleaded with him to utter the shahādah, but with Abū Jahl at his side he declared he would die upon the religion of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib (Ch. 9). His death in the Year of Sorrow removed the Prophet's ﷺ earthly shield, and the book treats his fate as a sobering lesson that guidance belongs to Allah alone.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: refused every Qurashī demand to surrender his nephew", "Ch. 8: led Banū Hāshim through the boycott", "Ch. 9: died without accepting Islam, opening the Year of Sorrow"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 10th year of prophethood, Mecca", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 360–362, 518–519"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "The magnanimous way in which Abu Taalib treated the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is truly amazing; he selflessly linked his fate with the fate of his nephew, Muhammad ﷺ; and he was in an ideal position to do so, for he was the leader and most influential member of the Banu Haashim clan, and was thus able to unite the children of Haashim and Al-Muttalib. He united them together - both the Muslims and non-Muslims among them - to support the Messenger of Allah. He protected his nephew without doubt or limitation.", "page": 360}, "who_ar": "عَمُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ الَّذِي كَفَلَهُ وَحَمَى دَعْوَتَهُ عَشْرَ سِنِينَ، وَمَاتَ وَلَمْ يُسْلِمْ."}, {"id": "abu-lahab", "name": "Abū Lahab", "arabic": "أبو لهب (عبد العزى بن عبد المطلب)", "kunya_titles": "Uncle and enemy of the Prophet ﷺ; condemned in Sūrah al-Masad", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ own uncle and one of his vilest enemies, cursed by name in the Qurʾan.", "story": "When the Prophet ﷺ gathered his clan at Mount Safa, Abū Lahab answered, 'May you perish! Is this why you gathered us?' — and Allah revealed Sūrah al-Masad condemning him and his wife by name (Ch. 6). He alone of Banū Hāshim abandoned his clan during the boycott, and after Abū Ṭālib's death his brief flicker of protectiveness was extinguished by Abū Jahl's goading (Ch. 8, 9). He avoided Badr by sending a debtor in his place, and died of a repulsive illness shortly after news of the defeat reached Mecca (Ch. 14, 15).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: rejected the call at Mount Safa; Sūrah al-Masad revealed about him", "Ch. 15: died of a crippling illness shortly after Badr"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 2 AH, Mecca, shortly after Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 385–386, 1032–1033"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Abu Lahab dedicated his life to fight against Islam; he would follow the Messenger of Allah ﷺ around in the marketplace, in gatherings, and during Hajj season in order to interrupt and harass him when he ﷺ was inviting people to embrace Islam. These are just a few examples of how the polytheists tried to inflict harm upon the Prophet ﷺ.", "page": 385}, "who_ar": "عَمُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ نَفْسُهُ، وَمِنْ أَخْبَثِ أَعْدَائِهِ، لُعِنَ بِاسْمِهِ فِي الْقُرْآنِ."}, {"id": "abd-al-muttalib", "name": "ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "arabic": "عبد المطلب (شيبة الحمد)", "kunya_titles": "Grandfather of the Prophet ﷺ; chieftain of Banū Hāshim; rediscoverer of Zamzam", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ grandfather and guardian, the most venerated man of the Quraysh in his day.", "story": "Shaybah al-Ḥamd, known as ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, rediscovered and re-dug the well of Zamzam after a recurring dream, and it was he who chose the unheard-of name 'Muhammad' — 'so that he would be praised in the Heavens and the Earth' (Ch. 3). He took custody of his orphaned grandson with a special affinity, seating him beside himself where none of his own sons would sit. He died when the Prophet ﷺ was eight, before the revelation, entrusting the boy to Abū Ṭālib (Ch. 3).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 3: rediscovered Zamzam; named and cherished his grandson Muhammad ﷺ", "Ch. 3: died when the Prophet ﷺ was eight years old"], "badges": [], "death": "d. before the revelation, Mecca; the Prophet ﷺ was eight", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 64–65, 91"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "جَدُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَكَافِلُهُ، وَأَعْظَمُ قُرَيْشٍ قَدْرًا فِي زَمَانِهِ."}, {"id": "aminah-bint-wahb", "name": "Āminah bint Wahb", "arabic": "آمنة بنت وهب", "kunya_titles": "Mother of the Prophet ﷺ; daughter of the chieftain of Banū Zuhrah", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Prophet's ﷺ mother, widowed before his birth and taken from him in his childhood.", "story": "Daughter of the chieftain of Banū Zuhrah, Āminah was widowed while pregnant with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she died when he was only six years old, on the road near al-Abwāʾ (Ch. 3). Some fifty years later the Prophet ﷺ detoured to her grave and wept until his beard was soaked — one of the few times his Companions ever saw him cry — and the permission to visit graves was granted thereafter (Ch. 3).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 3: died when the Prophet ﷺ was six", "Ch. 3: the Prophet ﷺ wept at her grave five decades later"], "badges": [], "death": "d. when the Prophet ﷺ was six, at al-Abwāʾ", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 90"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Aaminah died when the Prophet ﷺ was six years old, and like her husband before her, she died during a journey. She had traveled to see her uncles from the Banu ‘Adee ibn Najjaar tribe, and the purpose of her visit was to show them her son. She died on the way back in a place called Al-Abwaa, which is situated somewhere between Makkah and Al-Madeenah, and she was buried there as well.", "page": 90}, "who_ar": "أُمُّ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، تَرَمَّلَتْ قَبْلَ مَوْلِدِهِ وَمَاتَتْ عَنْهُ وَهُوَ صَغِيرٌ."}, {"id": "abu-jahl", "name": "Abū Jahl", "arabic": "أبو جهل (عمرو بن هشام)", "kunya_titles": "ʿAmr ibn Hishām; 'the Pharaoh of this Ummah'", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Chieftain of Banū Makhzūm and the fiercest enemy of the Prophet ﷺ in Mecca.", "story": "ʿAmr ibn Hishām earned the name 'Father of Ignorance' by leading Mecca's persecution: he tormented the weak believers, goaded others into abuse, and knew the Qurʾan's truth yet rejected it out of pride (Ch. 6). The Prophet ﷺ called him 'the Pharaoh of my Ummah.' He dragged the Quraysh to Badr even after the caravan was safe, and there two teenage Anṣārī brothers cut him down; ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, once his victim, ended his life as he spat defiance to his last breath (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: chief persecutor of the early Muslims", "Ch. 14: forced the march to Badr and was killed there"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — killed at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 992–994"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Abdullah ibn Mas’ood recounted what happened when he found Abu Jahl on the battlefield. He related that, when he saw Abu Jahl lying down on the ground, he said to him, “O enemy of Allah, Allah has indeed humiliated you!” Abu Jahl replied, “And how has He humiliated me? Is there anyone that is better than the one who is killed by his own people?”", "page": 992}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ بَنِي مَخْزُومٍ، وَأَشَدُّ أَعْدَاءِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ بِمَكَّةَ."}, {"id": "umayyah-ibn-khalaf", "name": "Umayyah ibn Khalaf", "arabic": "أمية بن خلف", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Banū Jumaḥ; torturer of Bilāl", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Meccan chieftain who tortured Bilāl, killed at Badr before Bilāl's eyes.", "story": "Umayyah dragged his slave Bilāl into the desert at noon and pressed burning boulders onto his chest for saying 'One; One!' (Ch. 6). He tried desperately to avoid marching to Badr, and when the battle turned he tried to buy his life as a captive of his old business partner ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf — but Bilāl saw him, cried 'The leader of kufr? Over my dead body!', and rallied the Anṣār who killed him and his son (Ch. 14). His body could not be lifted for burial, his flesh cleaving to the hot ground he once used against Bilāl.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: tortured Bilāl with burning rocks", "Ch. 14: killed at Badr after Bilāl identified him"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — killed at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 996–997"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Seeing Umayyah at Badr had a tremendous impact on Bilaal and surely rekindled many bad memories, so much so that Bilaal exclaimed, “I will not be saved if he is saved (from death today)!” His reaction should not surprise us, considering the brutal torture that Umayyah subjected him to in Makkah, a topic that we have covered in a previous chapter.", "page": 996}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدٌ مِنْ سَادَةِ مَكَّةَ عَذَّبَ بِلَالًا، ثُمَّ قُتِلَ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ عَلَى مَرْأًى مِنْ بِلَالٍ."}, {"id": "utbah-ibn-rabiah", "name": "ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah", "arabic": "عتبة بن ربيعة", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Banū ʿAbd Shams; father of Hind bint ʿUtbah", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "A senior Qurashī statesman, more measured than his peers, who nonetheless died fighting Islam at Badr.", "story": "ʿUtbah was among the Quraysh's elder statesmen; it was his vineyard in Ṭāʾif from which the servant ʿAddās brought grapes to the wounded Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 9). Before Badr he hesitated and argued there was no need to fight once the caravan was safe, working with Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām to avert war (Ch. 14). Shamed into battle, he stepped forward with his brother Shaybah and son al-Walīd in the three-on-three duel that opened Badr, and all three were killed by Ḥamzah, ʿAlī, and ʿUbaydah (Ch. 14). His daughter Hind and grandson Muʿāwiyah later embraced Islam.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: argued against fighting, then fell in the opening duel of Badr"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — killed in the duel at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 432–433, 962–963"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Someone said, “We do not know of anyone (who is better suited to this task) than ‘Utbah ibn Rabee’ah.” They all agreed, saying, “It is you then, O Abu Al-Waleed (i.e., ‘Utbah ibn Rabee’ah) [who will go to him (i.e., to the Prophet ﷺ)].” ‘Utbah went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Muhammad, who is better: you or ‘Abdul-Muttalib?”", "page": 432}, "who_ar": "مِنْ كِبَارِ سَادَةِ قُرَيْشٍ، كَانَ أَرْجَحَ حِلْمًا مِنْ أَقْرَانِهِ، وَمَعَ ذَلِكَ قُتِلَ يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ مُحَارِبًا لِلْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "shaybah-ibn-rabiah", "name": "Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah", "arabic": "شيبة بن ربيعة", "kunya_titles": "Brother of ʿUtbah; nobleman of Banū ʿAbd Shams", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "ʿUtbah's brother, who fell beside him in the opening duel of Badr.", "story": "Shaybah shared his brother's standing and his fate. He co-owned the Ṭāʾif vineyard from which grapes were sent to the Prophet ﷺ after his stoning (Ch. 9), and when ʿUtbah wavered before Badr, it was Shaybah who reminded him that they had come for a purpose (Ch. 14). He stepped out with ʿUtbah and al-Walīd ibn ʿUtbah in the three-on-three duel that opened the battle, and was killed with them by the Muslim champions (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: killed in the opening duel of Badr alongside his brother and nephew"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — killed in the duel at Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 535–536"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "At the time, both ‘Utbah and Shaibah were present in their garden, and though they were situated in a position from which they could see the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ did not see them (at least that is what is apparent from the narration). Upon seeing the Prophet’s condition, ‘Utbah and Shaibah’s hearts softened towards him; they called for ‘Addaas, a Christian slave of theirs, and said to him, “Take a bunch of these dates and put them in this tray; then go with the tray to that man and tell him to eat from it.”", "page": 535}, "who_ar": "أَخُو عُتْبَةَ، صُرِعَ إِلَى جَنْبِهِ فِي مُبَارَزَةِ بَدْرٍ الْأُولَى."}, {"id": "ubayy-ibn-khalaf", "name": "Ubayy ibn Khalaf", "arabic": "أبي بن خلف", "kunya_titles": "Brother of Umayyah ibn Khalaf; the only man the Prophet ﷺ personally killed", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Brother of Umayyah, who vowed to kill the Prophet ﷺ and instead was killed by him at Uḥud.", "story": "In Mecca, Ubayy taunted the Prophet ﷺ that he was fattening a special horse on the choicest grain to one day kill him from its back; the Prophet ﷺ replied, 'No; rather, I will kill you, by the will of Allah' (Ch. 16). At Uḥud he charged the Prophet ﷺ on that very horse. Waving his Companions aside — 'No, he is mine' — the Prophet ﷺ thrust a spear into his neck. The wound seemed minor, but Ubayy knew the Prophet's ﷺ vow was true, and he died in panic on the road back to Mecca — the only pagan the Prophet ﷺ ever personally killed (Ch. 16).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: charged the Prophet ﷺ at Uḥud and died of the spear-wound he inflicted"], "badges": [], "death": "3 AH — died of his wound returning from Uḥud", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 336, 1154"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Mujaahid and others related that Ubai ibn Khalaf once went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a decayed bone in his hand. As he broke the bone into small pieces and scattered those pieces into the wind, he said, “O Muhammad, do you claim that Allah will resurrect this?” The Prophet ﷺ answered, “Yes, Allah will cause you to die; then He will resurrect you; and then He will thrust you into the Hellfire.”", "page": 336}, "who_ar": "أَخُو أُمَيَّةَ، نَذَرَ أَنْ يَقْتُلَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ، فَكَانَ هُوَ الْمَقْتُولَ بِيَدِهِ ﷺ يَوْمَ أُحُدٍ."}, {"id": "al-walid-ibn-al-mughirah", "name": "al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah", "arabic": "الوليد بن المغيرة", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Banū Makhzūm; father of Khālid ibn al-Walīd; Mecca's greatest poet", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Mecca's foremost literary authority, who privately conceded the Qurʾan's inimitability yet publicly branded it magic.", "story": "Hearing the Prophet ﷺ recite at the Kaʿbah, al-Walīd — the greatest poet of the Quraysh — declared that this speech 'supersedes and nothing supersedes it' (Ch. 6). Under Abū Jahl's pressure he dismantled every proposed slander (madman, fortune-teller, poet) as demonstrably false, then settled on calling the Qurʾan magic; Allah exposed his deliberations with striking precision in the verses of Sūrah al-Muddaththir (Ch. 6). Too arrogant even to bow — he claimed his prostration was beneath him (Ch. 7) — he died in Mecca an unbeliever, in the period before Badr, leaving behind the son who would become the Sword of Allah.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: praised the Qurʾan, then branded it magic under pressure; exposed in Sūrah al-Muddaththir"], "badges": [], "death": "d. in Mecca before Badr, an unbeliever", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 363–364"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "The leader of that campaign was Al-Waleed ibn Al-Mugheerah, who by that time was an aged chieftain of the Quraish, and leader of the Banu Makhzoom clan. Having gathered together with a number of other chieftains and nobles, Al-Waleed said, “O people of the Quraish, the season (for Hajj) has arrived, and Arab delegates are coming to you. They have indeed heard about this companion of yours (i.e., Prophet Muhammad ﷺ), so let us agree to say the same thing about him; otherwise, if you say different things (about him), you will end up contradicting one another.”", "page": 363}, "who_ar": "أَعْلَمُ أَهْلِ مَكَّةَ بِالْكَلَامِ، أَقَرَّ سِرًّا بِإِعْجَازِ الْقُرْآنِ، ثُمَّ رَمَاهُ عَلَانِيَةً بِأَنَّهُ سِحْرٌ."}, {"id": "uqbah-ibn-abi-muayt", "name": "ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ", "arabic": "عقبة بن أبي معيط", "kunya_titles": "The most shameless of the Prophet's ﷺ Meccan persecutors", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The lowest of the Quraysh in his abuse of the Prophet ﷺ; executed after Badr.", "story": "The book calls ʿUqbah the worst of them all, knowing no bounds of disgrace: he strangled the Prophet ﷺ with his own garment as he prayed until Abū Bakr threw him off — 'Will you kill a man for saying, My Lord is Allah?' — he spat in the Prophet's ﷺ blessed face, and he dumped camel entrails on his back in prostration until young Fāṭimah removed them (Ch. 6, 8). Captured at Badr, he was one of only two prisoners the Prophet ﷺ ever ordered executed, his crimes too severe to ransom (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: strangled the Prophet ﷺ at the Kaʿbah; threw entrails on him in prayer", "Ch. 14: executed after being captured at Badr"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — executed after Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1017–1018"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When he heard about his impending execution, ‘Uqbah ibn Abee Mu’ait said, “Woe upon me! O people of Quraish, why am I chosen to be killed out of all prisoners who are here?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ responded, “Because of your enmity towards Allah and His Messenger.” He said, “O Muhammad, for you to show kindness to me is better. Treat me like any other man from my people.”", "page": 1017}, "who_ar": "أَخَسُّ قُرَيْشٍ فِي أَذَى النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، قُتِلَ صَبْرًا بَعْدَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "al-as-ibn-wail", "name": "al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil", "arabic": "العاص بن وائل السهمي", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Banū Sahm; father of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Influential Qurashī chieftain and politician, father of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who died an unbeliever.", "story": "It was al-ʿĀṣ's brazen cheating of a Yemeni merchant that provoked the pre-Islamic pact of justice, Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl, which the Prophet ﷺ proudly witnessed as a young man (Ch. 4). In the Islamic era he remained an opponent, deflecting blame when the Quraysh's persecution drew censure (Ch. 8), and the book records him being sent to Badr in Abū Lahab's stead over a debt (Ch. 14). He is remembered in the tafsīr tradition as the mocker who called the Prophet ﷺ 'cut off' after his sons died — answered by Sūrah al-Kawthar — and he died in Mecca around the time of Badr, never having believed. His son ʿAmr became one of Islam's great commanders.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 4: his injustice sparked the Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl pact", "Ch. 14: sent towards Badr in Abū Lahab's place over a debt"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 2 AH, Mecca, an unbeliever", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 408"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. c. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-’Aas ibn Waail owed me money, and when I went to him in order to collect what he owed me, he said, ‘I will not pay you back until you disbelieve in Muhammad.’ I said, ‘You will die and be resurrected before I disbelieve!’ He said, ‘And will I be resurrected after death? If that is the case, then I will pay you back when I will return (after death) to my wealth and children.’", "page": 408}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدٌ قُرَشِيٌّ ذُو نُفُوذٍ وَسِيَاسَةٍ، وَالِدُ عَمْرِو بْنِ الْعَاصِ، مَاتَ كَافِرًا."}, {"id": "al-nadr-ibn-al-harith", "name": "al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith", "arabic": "النضر بن الحارث", "kunya_titles": "The storyteller who mocked the revelation", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Literate Qurashī propagandist against the Qurʾan; executed after Badr.", "story": "One of the few literate men of the Quraysh, al-Naḍr made it his mission to derail the daʿwah: he travelled with ʿUqbah to the Jews of Medina to gather trick questions for the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 6), and whenever the Qurʾan was recited he was first with sarcastic, disparaging remarks, countering it with imported Persian tales. Ibn ʿAbbās called him 'a Shayṭān from the Shayṭāns of the Quraysh' and noted that over eight Qurʾanic verses were revealed about him (Ch. 14). Captured at Badr, he was one of the only two prisoners the Prophet ﷺ ordered executed (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: sought questions from the Jews of Medina to trap the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 14: executed after Badr — one of only two captives ever executed"], "badges": [], "death": "2 AH — executed after Badr", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 459–460, 1017–1018"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 2 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Frustrated by his people’s situation, An-Nadr ibn Al-Haarith expressed Quraish’s inability to put down the message of Islam by saying to the other leaders of the Quraish, “O people of Quraish, by Allah, a matter has descended upon you for which you have not yet been able to make a counterattack through the use of trickery. So consider your situation, for by Allah, a great matter indeed has befallen you.” The leaders of the Quraish thereafter agreed to send An-Nadr ibn Al-Haarith and ‘Uqbah ibn Abee Mu’ait to Jewish scholars in Al-Madeenah.", "page": 459}, "who_ar": "قُرَشِيٌّ يَقْرَأُ وَيَكْتُبُ، تَصَدَّى لِلْقُرْآنِ بِالطَّعْنِ وَالدِّعَايَةِ، وَقُتِلَ صَبْرًا بَعْدَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"id": "kab-ibn-al-ashraf", "name": "Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf", "arabic": "كعب بن الأشرف", "kunya_titles": "Poet-chieftain allied to Banū Naḍīr; inciter against the Muslims", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Wealthy poet of Arab-Jewish parentage in Medina who weaponized his verse against Islam.", "story": "Born to an Arab father and a Jewish mother of Banū Naḍīr, Kaʿb was rich, handsome, and eloquent — and set against Islam throughout. He discouraged the Anṣār from paying zakāh, declared after Badr that the grave was better than a world where Muhammad ﷺ had won, forged a treacherous alliance with Abū Sufyān, and wrote erotic poetry naming Muslim women (Ch. 15). That final transgression sealed his fate: the Prophet ﷺ asked, 'Who will take care of Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf?' and Muhammad ibn Maslamah of the Aws led the party that killed him (Ch. 15).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 15: incited the Quraysh after Badr and defamed Muslim women in verse; killed by Muhammad ibn Maslamah"], "badges": [], "death": "3 AH — killed in Medina", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1074–1079"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 3 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Ka’ab was a poet, and with the advent of Islam, he dedicated his poetry to the fight against Islam and the Prophet ﷺ. He got especially irate when the Muslims defeated the Quraish in the Battle of Badr. Resolving to do something about the situation, Ka’ab travelled to Makkah, where he composed many poems in which he vilified and satirized the Prophet ﷺ, and in which he exhorted the Quraish to exact revenge for their fighters who died on the Day of Badr.", "page": 1074}, "who_ar": "شَاعِرٌ ثَرِيٌّ بِالْمَدِينَةِ، أَبُوهُ عَرَبِيٌّ وَأُمُّهُ يَهُودِيَّةٌ، جَعَلَ شِعْرَهُ سِلَاحًا عَلَى الْإِسْلَامِ."}, {"id": "huyayy-ibn-akhtab", "name": "Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab", "arabic": "حيي بن أخطب", "kunya_titles": "Chieftain of Banū Naḍīr; architect of the Confederate siege", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Leader of the Banū Naḍīr who engineered the Confederacy and Qurayẓah's treachery; father of Ṣafiyyah.", "story": "Ḥuyayy hardened his tribe against departure during the Banū Naḍīr crisis (Ch. 17), then, after their exile to Khaybar, became the chief architect of the Battle of the Trench, rallying the Confederates and personally pressuring Kaʿb ibn Asad of Banū Qurayẓah into betraying their treaty with the Prophet ﷺ (Ch. 19). When the coalition collapsed, he was trapped inside Qurayẓah's fortress and shared their sentence, saying as he was led out, 'By Allah, I do not regret my animosity towards you, but whomever Allah humiliates is truly humiliated' (Ch. 19). His daughter Ṣafiyyah later became a Mother of the Believers (Ch. 21).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: masterminded the Confederate siege and Qurayẓah's betrayal; executed with the Banū Qurayẓah"], "badges": [], "death": "5 AH — executed with the Banū Qurayẓah", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1412–1414"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 5 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When Huyai reached Ar-Rauhaa, he remembered the pledge he made to the people of Banu Quraizah. And so he returned to Al-Madeenah and entered one of the fortresses of Banu Quraizah, deciding to stay with them until the end. Later on, when Banu Quraizah surrendered, Huyai, who was tied up, was taken to be killed. He said to the Prophet ﷺ, “Lo, by Allah, I do not blame myself for showing enmity towards you; nonetheless, the reality is that when one abandons Allah, one is abandoned himself (as I have become).”", "page": 1412}, "who_ar": "زَعِيمُ بَنِي النَّضِيرِ الَّذِي دَبَّرَ تَأْلِيبَ الْأَحْزَابِ وَغَدْرَ بَنِي قُرَيْظَةَ، وَهُوَ أَبُو صَفِيَّةَ."}, {"id": "abdullah-ibn-ubayy", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl", "arabic": "عبد الله بن أبي بن سلول", "kunya_titles": "Leader of the Hypocrites of Medina", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Khazrajī noble who lost a crown to the Hijrah and led the Hypocrites until his death in 9 AH.", "story": "About to be crowned king of Medina when the Hijrah made him irrelevant, Ibn Ubayy professed Islam outwardly while working against it: he withdrew 300 men on the way to Uḥud (Ch. 16), fanned the heinous slander against ʿĀʾishah (Ch. 18), promised the Banū Naḍīr support he never gave (Ch. 17), and vowed at al-Muraysīʿ that 'the honourable will expel the lowly' from Medina — while his own believing son remained fiercely loyal to the Prophet ﷺ. He stayed behind at Tabūk (Ch. 27) and died within a month of the army's return; the Prophet ﷺ, ever gentle, gave his own shirt for the shroud and prayed over him until Allah revealed the prohibition of praying over the Hypocrites (Ch. 27).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: deserted with 300 men before Uḥud", "Ch. 18: fuelled the slander against ʿĀʾishah", "Ch. 27: died in 9 AH; the Prophet ﷺ was thereafter forbidden to pray over the Hypocrites"], "badges": [], "death": "d. 9 AH, Medina", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1108–1109"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 9 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "When the Muslims reached Ash-Shaut - a garden that is situated between Al-Madeenah and Uhud - Ibn Salool withdrew from the Muslim army, taking along with him three-hundred fellow hypocrites. He justified his withdrawal with a claim and a complaint: he claimed that no fighting would take place against the polytheists and he complained that the Prophet ﷺ did not listen to him when he suggested that the Muslims should stay in Al-Madeenah instead of going out to fight the polytheists.", "page": 1108}, "who_ar": "شَرِيفُ الْخَزْرَجِ الَّذِي فَاتَهُ الْمُلْكُ بِالْهِجْرَةِ، فَقَادَ الْمُنَافِقِينَ حَتَّى مَاتَ سَنَةَ تِسْعٍ مِنَ الْهِجْرَةِ."}, {"id": "musaylamah", "name": "Musaylamah the Liar", "arabic": "مسيلمة الكذاب (مسيلمة بن حبيب)", "kunya_titles": "False prophet of the Banū Ḥanīfah in Yamāmah", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Leader of the Banū Ḥanīfah who claimed prophethood in the Prophet's ﷺ own lifetime.", "story": "Musaylamah ibn al-Ḥabīb came with his tribe's delegation but was far too arrogant to submit, later writing to the Prophet ﷺ as 'Musaylamah, the Messenger of Allah' and proposing to split the earth between them; the Prophet ﷺ replied that the earth belongs to Allah, addressing him as 'Musaylamah the Liar', and told his two envoys that were emissaries not immune, he would have executed them (Ch. 28). He concocted imitation 'revelations' and amassed a huge following in Yamāmah. In the Apostasy Wars under Abū Bakr, his movement was crushed and Allah decreed that he be killed by none other than Waḥshī — the former killer of Ḥamzah (Ch. 28).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 28: claimed prophethood and wrote his insolent letter to the Prophet ﷺ", "Ch. 28: killed by Waḥshī in the Apostasy Wars"], "badges": [], "death": "12 AH — killed at Yamāmah in the Apostasy Wars", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1135"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. 12 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "After all, assassinations were what Wahshee specialized in, for the precise spear-thrower of yesterday was the precursor of the skilled marksman of today who can fire at a target from hundreds of meters away. Wahshee ؓ achieved his goal by killing Moosailamah, and he is reported to have later said, “I killed the best of people (i.e., Hamzah ibn ‘Abdul-Muttalib), and I killed the worst of people, Moosailamah the Liar.”", "page": 1135}, "who_ar": "زَعِيمُ بَنِي حَنِيفَةَ الَّذِي ادَّعَى النُّبُوَّةَ فِي حَيَاةِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"id": "abu-amir-al-fasiq", "name": "Abū ʿĀmir al-Fāsiq", "arabic": "أبو عامر الفاسق (عبد عمرو بن صيفي)", "kunya_titles": "ʿAbd ʿAmr ibn Ṣayfī; 'al-Rāhib (the Monk)', renamed 'al-Fāsiq (the Dissolute)'", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "The Awsī ascetic who expected prophethood for himself and fought the Prophet ﷺ to his dying breath.", "story": "A foremost monotheist ascetic of the Aws known as 'the Monk', Abū ʿĀmir could not tolerate the Prophet's ﷺ arrival eclipsing his own standing. He defected to Mecca and at Uḥud stepped out to sway his tribesmen — who answered, 'You are not Abū ʿĀmir the Monk, you are Abū ʿĀmir al-Fāsiq (the Dissolute)!' (Ch. 16). After that humiliation he fled to the Roman Empire, gained the Caesar's ear, and by some reports masterminded Masjid al-Ḍirār, the Hypocrites' rival mosque that Allah ordered exposed and destroyed during Tabūk (Ch. 27). He died in exile in Roman lands, estranged and unrepentant, while his own son Ḥanẓalah — washed by the angels — had died a martyr for Islam at Uḥud.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 16: renounced by his own tribe at Uḥud", "Ch. 27: reported mastermind of Masjid al-Ḍirār from Roman exile"], "badges": [], "death": "d. c. 10 AH, in exile in Roman lands", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1117, 1862–1863"], "ra": "", "dyr": "d. c. 10 AH", "sallabi": {"text": "Abu ‘Aamir went to certain members of the Aus tribe and said, “I am Abu ‘Aamir,” expecting that his return to Al-Madeenah would be welcome news to the Aus, but instead this is the response he received: “Then may Allah not make you pleased, O wicked-doer!” Upon hearing their response, Abu ‘Aamir said, “Since I have parted from here, my people have become afflicted with evil.” Later on that day, he fought fiercely alongside the polytheists and against the Muslims.", "page": 1117}, "who_ar": "رَاهِبُ الْأَوْسِ الَّذِي كَانَ يَرْجُو النُّبُوَّةَ لِنَفْسِهِ، فَحَارَبَ النَّبِيَّ ﷺ حَتَّى آخِرِ نَفَسٍ."}, {"id": "umm-jamil", "name": "Umm Jamīl bint Ḥarb", "arabic": "أم جميل بنت حرب (العوراء)", "kunya_titles": "Wife of Abū Lahab; sister of Abū Sufyān; 'the carrier of firewood'", "group": "context", "tier": "minor", "who": "Abū Lahab's wife, condemned with him in Sūrah al-Masad as the carrier of firewood.", "story": "This book names her only as the wife of Abū Lahab, but her role is unmistakable: she partnered in her husband's campaign of abuse, and Sūrah al-Masad condemned them together — 'and his wife, the carrier of firewood, around her neck will be a rope of palm-fibre' (Ch. 6). The book records her swearing by al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā to spend her prized necklace on enmity to the Prophet ﷺ, for which the exegetes said Allah would replace it with the rope of Hellfire (Ch. 6). Tradition adds that she once came seeking the Prophet ﷺ with a stone-pestle in hand, but Allah veiled him from her eyes. She died in Mecca upon her hostility, before its conquest.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: condemned alongside Abū Lahab in Sūrah al-Masad; vowed her necklace to the Prophet's ﷺ enmity"], "badges": [], "death": "d. in Mecca, an unbeliever", "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 385–386"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "When Umm Jameel heard these Verses, she went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who at the time was sitting down beside the Ka’bah, in the company of Abu Bakr As-Siddeeq. With a stone in her hand, Umm Jameel stood over them and said, “O Abu Bakr, where is your companion? It has been conveyed to me that he satirizes me. By Allah, had I found him (now), I would have struck his mouth with this stone!” She then left.", "page": 385}, "who_ar": "امْرَأَةُ أَبِي لَهَبٍ، ذُمَّتْ مَعَهُ فِي سُورَةِ الْمَسَدِ بِأَنَّهَا حَمَّالَةُ الْحَطَبِ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "amr-ibn-luhayy", "name": "ʿAmr ibn Luḥayy al-Khuzāʿī", "arabic": "عمرو بن لحي الخزاعي", "kunya_titles": "The man who brought idolatry to Arabia", "who": "The Khuzāʿī chief who first introduced idols into the pure religion of Ibrāhīm.", "story": "Centuries before the Prophet ﷺ, this chief of Khuzāʿah brought the idol Hubal to Mecca and changed the religion of Ibrāhīm — the book presents him as the origin-point of Arabian idolatry (Ch. 2). The Prophet ﷺ reported seeing him dragging his intestines in the Fire.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 2: introduced idol-worship into Mecca"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1720"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "As we discussed in the first chapter of this work, the first person who introduced idols such as Manaat into Arabia was ‘Amr ibn Luhai Al-Khuzaa’ee, who was alive many generations before the advent of Islam. After the passing of many years, when Makkah was conquered, the Prophet ﷺ sent a man to go to Manaat.", "page": 1720}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدُ خُزَاعَةَ الَّذِي أَدْخَلَ الْأَصْنَامَ أَوَّلَ مَرَّةٍ فِي دِينِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ الْحَنِيفِ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "abu-al-bukhtari", "name": "Abū al-Bukhtarī", "arabic": "أبو البختري", "kunya_titles": "The fair-minded pagan", "who": "A Qurashī who defended the Prophet ﷺ and helped end the boycott — yet died a pagan at Badr.", "story": "One of the fairer pagans of Quraysh: he stood against the boycott of Banū Hāshim and once struck Abū Jahl with a camel jawbone in defence of the Prophet ﷺ. Because of this the Prophet ﷺ ordered that he not be killed at Badr — but he refused to abandon his comrade and fell fighting (Ch. 14).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: helped tear up the boycott", "Ch. 14: spared by decree, died at Badr by his own choice"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 467, 1031"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "It was time for the next phase of the plan: Zum’ah ibn Al-Aswad said to Abu Jahl, “By Allah, you are the greater liar. We were not pleased with the writing of the declaration when it was being written.” It was now Abu Al-Bukhtaree’s turn to speak: “Zum’ah has spoken the truth. We were not pleased with what was written in it, and we do not accept it.”", "page": 467}, "who_ar": "قُرَشِيٌّ دَافَعَ عَنِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ وَسَعَى فِي نَقْضِ الصَّحِيفَةِ، وَمَعَ ذَلِكَ مَاتَ مُشْرِكًا يَوْمَ بَدْرٍ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "al-akhnas-ibn-shurayq", "name": "al-Akhnas ibn Shurayq", "arabic": "الأخنس بن شريق", "kunya_titles": "The secret listener", "who": "One of the three pagans caught secretly listening to the Qur'an at night, three nights running.", "story": "With Abū Jahl and Abū Sufyān, al-Akhnas crept out three nights in a row to listen to the Prophet ﷺ reciting Qur'an in his home — each pagan hiding from the others, each unable to stay away (Ch. 6). He withdrew Banū Zuhrah from the Badr campaign, so his clan never fought the Prophet ﷺ at Badr.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 6: the three-night secret listening episode"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 530"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "After the people of At-Taaif refused to believe in and help the Prophet ﷺ, and after he left At-Taaif, the Prophet ﷺ went to Hira. From there, he ﷺ sent for Al-Akhnas ibn Shareeq, asking that he grant him protection. Al-Akhnas replied, ‘I am an ally (of the Quraish, and not one of its core members), and an ally cannot grant protection.’", "page": 530}, "who_ar": "أَحَدُ الْمُشْرِكِينَ الثَّلَاثَةِ الَّذِينَ ضُبِطُوا يَسْتَمِعُونَ إِلَى الْقُرْآنِ خُفْيَةً بِاللَّيْلِ ثَلَاثَ لَيَالٍ مُتَتَابِعَةٍ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "damdam-ibn-amr", "name": "Ḍamḍam ibn ʿAmr al-Ghifārī", "arabic": "ضمضم بن عمرو الغفاري", "kunya_titles": "The hired crier", "who": "The messenger Abū Sufyān hired to rouse Quraysh to march to Badr.", "story": "Hired by Abū Sufyān when he feared for his caravan, Ḍamḍam rode into Mecca on a mutilated camel, tore his shirt, and screamed for Quraysh to save their wealth — igniting the mobilization for Badr (Ch. 14). ʿĀtikah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib had already dreamt of his cry days before he arrived.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 14: his theatrical cry launched the march to Badr"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 951–952"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Before entering Makkah, Damdam cut off the nose of his camel, ripped his shirt from both the front and the back and smeared blood over himself. He entered Makkah and called out as loudly as he was able to, “O people of the Quraish, the caravan, the caravan! Your wealth that is with Abu Sufyaan! Muhammad and his Companions are trying to overtake it, and I do not think that you can reach there (on time). Help! Help!”", "page": 951}, "who_ar": "الرَّسُولُ الَّذِي اسْتَأْجَرَهُ أَبُو سُفْيَانَ لِيَسْتَنْفِرَ قُرَيْشًا لِلْخُرُوجِ إِلَى بَدْرٍ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "abdullah-ibn-khatal", "name": "ʿAbdullāh ibn Khaṭal", "arabic": "عبد الله بن خطل", "kunya_titles": "Apostate excluded from the Conquest amnesty", "who": "An apostate who murdered his servant and hired singers to mock the Prophet ﷺ.", "story": "Once nominally Muslim, he murdered a servant, apostatized, fled to Mecca, and commissioned poetry and song mocking the Prophet ﷺ. He was among the handful excluded from the Conquest amnesty and was executed even as he clung to the coverings of the Kaʿbah (Ch. 25).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: executed at the Kaʿbah despite the general amnesty"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1711"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "مُرْتَدٌّ قَتَلَ خَادِمَهُ وَاسْتَأْجَرَ قَيْنَاتٍ يُغَنِّينَ بِهِجَاءِ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "miqyas-ibn-subabah", "name": "Miqyas ibn Ṣubābah", "arabic": "مقيس بن ضبابة", "kunya_titles": "The treacherous avenger", "who": "Feigned Islam to take blood-vengeance, then apostatized.", "story": "After his brother was killed by mistake, Miqyas came to Medina professing Islam, received the blood money — then murdered his brother's killer and fled to Mecca as an apostate. He was excluded from the Conquest amnesty and executed (Ch. 25).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: excluded from the amnesty for murder and apostasy"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1711"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "أَظْهَرَ الْإِسْلَامَ لِيَأْخُذَ بِالثَّأْرِ، ثُمَّ ارْتَدَّ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "al-huwayrith", "name": "al-Ḥuwayrith ibn al-Nuqaydh", "arabic": "الحويرث بن نقيذ", "kunya_titles": "Excluded from the amnesty", "who": "A persecutor who had abused the Prophet's ﷺ daughters during their emigration.", "story": "Among those excluded from the Conquest amnesty for his crimes — the book links him to the assaults on the Prophet's ﷺ family, including goading the camel of his daughters as they emigrated. He was executed at the Conquest (Ch. 25).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 25: executed at the Conquest"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1711"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "مِنَ الْمُؤْذِينَ لِلنَّبِيِّ ﷺ، آذَى بَنَاتِهِ فِي أَثْنَاءِ هِجْرَتِهِنَّ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "shurahbil-ibn-amr", "name": "Shuraḥbīl ibn ʿAmr al-Ghassānī", "arabic": "شرحبيل بن عمرو الغساني", "kunya_titles": "The Ghassanid governor", "who": "Rome's Arab client who executed the Prophet's ﷺ envoy — the crime behind Muʾtah.", "story": "As Ghassanid governor under Rome, he seized the Prophet's ﷺ envoy al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUmayr, bound him and executed him — the only envoy of the Prophet ﷺ ever killed. The Battle of Muʾtah was the direct response (Ch. 24).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 24: murdered the envoy, triggering Muʾtah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1649"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Al-Haarith was the messenger of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to the king of Basrah, who was the viceroy of the emperor of Rome. Shurahbeel ibn ‘Amr Al-Ghassaanee killed Al-Haarith ؓ, in what was a blatant violation of an unwritten though universally accepted rule which stated that ambassadors and messengers were guaranteed safe passage and should never be harmed.", "page": 1649}, "who_ar": "عَامِلُ الرُّومِ مِنَ الْعَرَبِ، قَتَلَ رَسُولَ النَّبِيِّ ﷺ، وَتِلْكَ هِيَ الْجِنَايَةُ الَّتِي كَانَتْ وَرَاءَ مُؤْتَةَ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "durayd-ibn-al-simmah", "name": "Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah", "arabic": "دريد بن الصمة", "kunya_titles": "The aged strategist of Hawāzin", "who": "The near-blind veteran poet-warrior brought to Ḥunayn for his counsel — and killed there.", "story": "Carried to Ḥunayn in a litter at a legendary age, Durayd heard the camels and children and immediately condemned Mālik ibn ʿAwf's strategy of bringing the families to the battle — 'a shepherd of sheep, what do you know of war?' He was killed in the rout, a relic of Jāhiliyyah chivalry (Ch. 26).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 26: overruled at Ḥunayn; killed in the aftermath"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1751, 1758"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "الشَّاعِرُ الْفَارِسُ الْمُسِنُّ الَّذِي كَادَ يَعْمَى، حُمِلَ إِلَى حُنَيْنٍ لِيُشِيرَ بِرَأْيِهِ فَقُتِلَ هُنَاكَ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "abu-al-bara", "name": "Abū al-Barāʾ ʿĀmir ibn Mālik", "arabic": "أبو البراء عامر بن مالك", "kunya_titles": "'The Player with Spears'", "who": "The ʿĀmirī chief whose protection of the seventy reciters was betrayed by his own nephew.", "story": "The aged chief of Banū ʿĀmir asked the Prophet ﷺ for teachers for Najd and guaranteed their protection. His nephew ʿĀmir ibn Ṭufayl violated it, orchestrating the massacre of the seventy at Biʾr Maʿūnah — a betrayal that broke the old man; he died soon after, grieving the dishonour (Ch. 17).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: his guarantee of protection betrayed at Biʾr Maʿūnah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, p. 1234"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "Mulaa’ib Al-Asinnah, whose real name was Abu Baraa, said, “I will grant them protection, so send to the people of Najd whomsoever you desire.” Having obtained a guarantee of protection from the leader of Banu ‘Aamir - who was sincere in his offer and had no intention of betraying the Prophet ﷺ - the Prophet ﷺ sent a group of men, among whom was Al-Mundhir ibn ‘Amr ؓ, to invite the people of Najd to Islam.", "page": 1234}, "who_ar": "سَيِّدٌ عَامِرِيٌّ أَجَارَ السَّبْعِينَ مِنَ الْقُرَّاءِ، فَغَدَرَ بِجِوَارِهِ ابْنُ أَخِيهِ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "amir-ibn-tufayl", "name": "ʿĀmir ibn Ṭufayl", "arabic": "عامر بن الطفيل", "kunya_titles": "Chief of Banū ʿĀmir", "who": "The arrogant chieftain behind the Biʾr Maʿūnah massacre.", "story": "He demanded half the harvest of Medina and successorship as the price of his Islam, and when refused, threatened the Prophet ﷺ with cavalry. It was he who engineered the massacre of the seventy reciters at Biʾr Maʿūnah despite his uncle's protection (Ch. 17). He died soon after of a plague-boil, crying that he was dying 'like a camel, in the house of a woman of Banū Salūl'.", "book_moments": ["Ch. 17: demanded power as the price of faith; orchestrated Biʾr Maʿūnah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1234–1235, 1241"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "‘Aamir ibn At-Tufail was a leader, though not the head chieftain, of the Banu ‘Aamir tribe. He was a haughty and arrogant man who had ambitions of becoming king not just of his tribe but of all of Arabia. Notwithstanding his blinding arrogance, he was realistic in one regard: he recognized that, sooner or later, the Prophet ﷺ was going to have authority over the entire Arabian Peninsula.", "page": 1234}, "who_ar": "السَّيِّدُ الْمُتَكَبِّرُ الَّذِي كَانَ مِنْ وَرَاءِ مَقْتَلَةِ بِئْرِ مَعُونَةَ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "hulays-ibn-alqamah", "name": "Ḥulays ibn ʿAlqamah", "arabic": "حليس بن علقمة", "kunya_titles": "Chief of the Aḥābīsh", "who": "The Ḥudaybiyyah envoy moved by the sight of the garlanded sacrificial animals.", "story": "Sent by Quraysh to confront the Muslims at Ḥudaybiyyah, this lord of the Aḥābīsh — a people of deep reverence for the rites — saw the seventy garlanded camels and turned back without even speaking, telling Quraysh it was not lawful to block people who had come honouring the House (Ch. 20).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 20: turned back by the sight of the sacrificial animals"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1507–1508"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "As the Prophet ﷺ had hoped, Al-Hulais was greatly moved when he saw the Hadyee being steered towards him from the valley; he saw the Qalaaid (that which is hung around the neck of animals that are to be sacrificed during the pilgrimage to Makkah). Al-Hulais could not stand what he was seeing, and so he went back to the Quraish before he even reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.", "page": 1507}, "who_ar": "رَسُولُ قُرَيْشٍ يَوْمَ الْحُدَيْبِيَةِ، الَّذِي هَزَّهُ مَنْظَرُ الْهَدْيِ مُقَلَّدًا."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "zubayr-ibn-batta", "name": "Zubayr ibn Baṭṭā", "arabic": "الزبير بن باطا", "kunya_titles": "Elder of Banū Qurayẓah", "who": "The Qurayẓah elder who had once spared Thābit ibn Qays — and was offered life in return.", "story": "A leader of Banū Qurayẓah who had spared Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās at the Battle of Buʿāth years earlier. When Qurayẓah's treachery during the Trench brought judgement upon them, Thābit secured his life, family and wealth in repayment — but the old man, his loved ones gone, chose to share his people's fate (Ch. 19).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 19: the debt of Buʿāth repaid after the judgement on Qurayẓah"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 1416–1417"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "شَيْخُ بَنِي قُرَيْظَةَ الَّذِي مَنَّ يَوْمًا عَلَى ثَابِتِ بْنِ قَيْسٍ، فَعُرِضَتْ عَلَيْهِ الْحَيَاةُ جَزَاءً."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "zuhayr-ibn-abi-umayyah", "name": "Zuhayr ibn Abī Umayyah", "arabic": "زهير بن أبي أمية", "kunya_titles": "Grandson of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "who": "A leader of the movement that tore up the boycott document.", "story": "A son of ʿĀtikah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Zuhayr could not bear his kin starving in the ravine. Spurred by Hishām ibn ʿAmr, he stood in the Kaʿbah's precincts and publicly demanded the boycott's annulment, and with four others forced its end — whereupon the document was found eaten by termites, all but the words 'In Your Name, O Allah' (Ch. 8).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: led the public annulment of the boycott"], "beyond_sources": ["al-Ṣallābī, Noble Life, pp. 466–467"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "sallabi": {"text": "As for Zuhair, he was wearing a robe, and he began the morning by making seven circuits around the Ka’bah. He then stood before the people and said, “Shall we eat food and wear clothing, while the children of Haashim are being destroyed. They cannot buy, nor can anyone buy from them. By Allah, I will not sit down until this false declaration, this divisive declaration, is torn up.”", "page": 466}, "who_ar": "مِنْ زُعَمَاءِ الْحَرَكَةِ الَّتِي مَزَّقَتْ صَحِيفَةَ الْمُقَاطَعَةِ."}, {"badges": [], "death": "", "tier": "minor", "group": "context", "id": "buayd-ibn-amir", "name": "Buʿayd ibn ʿĀmir", "arabic": "بعيض بن عامر", "kunya_titles": "Scribe of the boycott treaty", "who": "Said to have written the boycott document — and to have lost the use of his hand.", "story": "The book records that Buʿayd ibn ʿĀmir was said to have penned the treaty of the boycott against Banū Hāshim, which was hung inside the Kaʿbah; the Prophet ﷺ made duʿāʾ against him and his hand became paralysed until he died (Ch. 8).", "book_moments": ["Ch. 8: wrote the boycott treaty; his hand withered"], "ra": "", "dyr": "", "who_ar": "يُقَالُ إِنَّهُ هُوَ الَّذِي كَتَبَ صَحِيفَةَ الْمُقَاطَعَةِ، وَإِنَّ يَدَهُ شُلَّتْ بَعْدَ ذَلِكَ."}], "timeline": {"eras": [{"id": "pre-prophethood", "title": "Before Prophethood (570–610 CE)", "events": [{"year": "570 CE", "ah": "53 BH", "title": "Birth of the Prophet ﷺ — the Year of the Elephant", "chapter": "Ch. 3", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ was born on a Monday in Rabīʿ al-Awwal in the Year of the Elephant (ʿĀm al-Fīl), the year of Abraha's failed assault on the Kaʿbah; the book stresses that the exact day is disputed, with over ten early opinions (the 12th being popular but unproven). His father ʿAbdullāh had died while Āminah was pregnant, and his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib named him 'Muhammad' so that he would be praised in the heavens and on earth.", "people": ["ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Āminah bint Wahb", "ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib"]}, {"year": "c. 570–574 CE", "ah": "c. 53–49 BH", "title": "Nursed by Ḥalīmah among the Banū Saʿd", "chapter": "Ch. 3", "desc": "As was the custom of Qurashī nobility, the infant Prophet ﷺ was sent to the desert to be nursed. No wet-nurse wanted an orphan, so Ḥalīmah took him last of all — and her milk, her she-camel and her donkey all overflowed with blessings, so much so that she begged Āminah to extend the two-year term.", "people": ["Ḥalīmah bint Saʿdiyyah", "Āminah bint Wahb"]}, {"year": "c. 574 CE", "ah": "c. 49 BH", "title": "The splitting of the chest", "chapter": "Ch. 3", "desc": "When the Prophet ﷺ was four years old, Jibrīl came to him, opened his chest, removed 'Shayṭān's portion' from his heart and washed it in a golden cup of Zamzam before sealing it. The frightened Ḥalīmah returned him to his mother; Anas ibn Mālik later said he could still see the mark on his chest.", "people": ["Jibrīl", "Ḥalīmah bint Saʿdiyyah", "Anas ibn Mālik"]}, {"year": "c. 576 CE", "ah": "c. 47 BH", "title": "Death of his mother Āminah", "chapter": "Ch. 3", "desc": "Āminah passed away when the Prophet ﷺ was six years old, leaving him an orphan of both parents. Some fifty years later he ﷺ wept at her grave until his beard was wet, and the visiting of graves — once forbidden — was permitted for the Ummah. He was then taken into the loving custody of his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.", "people": ["Āminah bint Wahb", "ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib"]}, {"year": "c. 578 CE", "ah": "c. 45 BH", "title": "Death of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; custody passes to Abū Ṭālib", "chapter": "Ch. 3", "desc": "At the tender age of eight the Prophet ﷺ was orphaned once more when his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib died. He was entrusted to his uncle Abū Ṭālib, who raised him and would remain his protector until the Prophet ﷺ was over fifty years old.", "people": ["ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Abū Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "c. 585 CE", "ah": "c. 38 BH", "title": "The Fijār Wars", "chapter": "Ch. 4", "desc": "When the Prophet ﷺ was around fifteen, a series of battles broke out among the tribes. He ﷺ later recalled collecting and returning arrows for his uncles and said, 'I do not regret participating in that war' — a precedent, the book notes, for taking part in just causes even in a non-Muslim society.", "people": ["Abū Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "c. 585–590 CE", "ah": "c. 38–33 BH", "title": "Ḥilf al-Fuḍūl — the pact of justice", "chapter": "Ch. 4", "desc": "After a merchant was cheated in Mecca, al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib convened the Quraysh in the house of ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān, where they vowed before the Kaʿbah to support the oppressed over the oppressor regardless of tribe. The Prophet ﷺ witnessed it and said that were he asked to uphold it even in Islam, he would do so.", "people": ["al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Judʿān", "al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Wā'il"]}, {"year": "c. 595 CE", "ah": "c. 28 BH", "title": "Trade for Khadījah and their marriage", "chapter": "Ch. 4", "desc": "Impressed by reports of his honesty and bashfulness, the noblewoman Khadījah bint Khuwaylid offered the Prophet ﷺ a generous business partnership, and developments led to marriage; Abū Ṭālib gave the sermon and a dowry of 12 ūqiyyah of silver was offered. The book prefers the view that Khadījah was 28 (not 40) when she married him ﷺ, and they remained married for 25 years.", "people": ["Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "Abū Ṭālib", "ʿAmr ibn Asad"]}, {"year": "c. 595–610 CE", "ah": "c. 28–13 BH", "title": "The Prophet's ﷺ children", "chapter": "Ch. 4", "desc": "Khadījah bore the Prophet ﷺ al-Qāsim — from whom came his title Abū al-Qāsim — then Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthūm, Fāṭimah and ʿAbdullāh; al-Qāsim died at six or seven and ʿAbdullāh in infancy, after which Abū Jahl gloated that his progeny was 'cut off'. The Prophet ﷺ would bury six of his seven children in his own lifetime; only Fāṭimah outlived him.", "people": ["Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "al-Qāsim", "Zaynab bint Muhammad", "Ruqayyah bint Muhammad", "Umm Kulthūm bint Muhammad", "Fāṭimah bint Muhammad"]}, {"year": "c. 605 CE", "ah": "c. 18 BH", "title": "Rebuilding of the Kaʿbah and the Black Stone arbitration", "chapter": "Ch. 4", "desc": "After a fire and a flood damaged the Kaʿbah, the Quraysh tore it down and rebuilt it, with al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah striking the first blow. When the clans quarrelled over who would set the Black Stone in place, the Prophet ﷺ — trusted by all — spread his cloak, had every tribe lift a corner, and placed the Stone with his own hands, averting bloodshed.", "people": ["al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah"]}, {"year": "c. 609–610 CE", "ah": "c. 14–13 BH", "title": "Seclusion in Cave Hira and the true dreams", "chapter": "Ch. 5", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ withdrew for ever-longer periods to Cave Hira overlooking the Kaʿbah. Stones would give him salām, and for six months — beginning in Rabīʿ al-Awwal — he received premonitions in dreams that came true the following day; Khadījah reassured him that these visions were glad tidings.", "people": ["Khadījah bint Khuwaylid"]}]}, {"id": "meccan-dawah", "title": "The Meccan Era — Quiet & Public Daʿwah (610–619 CE)", "events": [{"year": "610 CE (Ramadan)", "ah": "13 BH", "title": "The first revelation — 'Iqra'!'", "chapter": "Ch. 5", "desc": "On a Monday in Ramadan, Jibrīl appeared in Cave Hira, squeezed the Prophet ﷺ three times and revealed the first verses of Sūrah al-ʿAlaq: 'Read in the name of your Lord Who created.' He ﷺ raced home crying 'Cover me up!', and Khadījah comforted him: 'Allah will never cause you harm.' Her cousin Waraqah ibn Nawfal recognised 'the Keeper of Secrets who came down to Mūsā' and foretold that his own people would expel him.", "people": ["Jibrīl", "Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "Waraqah ibn Nawfal"]}, {"year": "610 CE", "ah": "13 BH", "title": "The pause in revelation and Sūrah al-Muddaththir", "chapter": "Ch. 5", "desc": "After some months without revelation, the Prophet ﷺ saw Jibrīl in his true form seated on a throne spanning the heavens and the earth, and again cried 'Cover me, cover me.' Then came 'O you covered up, arise and warn' — with 'Read' he became a Prophet, the scholars say, and with 'Arise and warn' he became a Messenger.", "people": ["Jibrīl", "Khadījah bint Khuwaylid"]}, {"year": "610–613 CE", "ah": "13–10 BH", "title": "The first converts and three years of quiet daʿwah", "chapter": "Ch. 6", "desc": "Khadījah was the first woman to accept Islam, Abū Bakr the first free man, ʿAlī the first child and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah the first freed slave; at Abū Bakr's hand came Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, Zubayr and ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf, followed by converts of the slave class such as Bilāl, Khabbāb and the family of Yāsir. For three years the daʿwah remained private and caused no major confrontation with the Quraysh.", "people": ["Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ", "Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām", "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ"]}, {"year": "613 CE", "ah": "10 BH", "title": "The daʿwah goes public — the feast and Mount Ṣafā", "chapter": "Ch. 6", "desc": "Three years after 'Read', Allah commanded the Prophet ﷺ to proclaim openly. He first hosted around forty of the Banū Hāshim at a miraculously blessed meal, then climbed Mount Ṣafā and called the Quraysh tribe by tribe — 'save yourselves from the fire of Hell' — while Abū Lahab hurled sand and the famous retort. From then on he ﷺ preached at the Kaʿbah, the marketplaces and to the Hajj pilgrims.", "people": ["Abū Lahab", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Fāṭimah bint Muhammad"]}, {"year": "c. 613–614 CE", "ah": "c. 10–9 BH", "title": "Dār al-Arqam becomes the first base", "chapter": "Ch. 6", "desc": "As the believers grew beyond a dozen and could not meet safely in public, the Prophet ﷺ chose the house of al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam as the secret headquarters of the fledgling community, where the Companions gathered, learned and prayed.", "people": ["al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam"]}, {"year": "c. 614–616 CE", "ah": "c. 9–7 BH", "title": "Persecution — Bilāl, Khabbāb, and the first martyrs", "chapter": "Ch. 6", "desc": "The Quraysh tortured the weak among the believers: Umayyah ibn Khalaf dragged Bilāl through the streets and pinned him under rocks while he cried 'One! One!', and Umm Anmār seared Khabbāb's back with burning iron. Yāsir was brutally killed as the first martyr of Islam, then Abū Jahl murdered Sumayyah; their son ʿAmmār, forced to utter words of disbelief, was excused by revelation, and the Prophet ﷺ promised: 'Be patient, O family of Yāsir, indeed your place is in Paradise.'", "people": ["Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ", "Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt", "Yāsir ibn ʿĀmir", "Sumayyah bint Khayyāṭ", "ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir", "Abū Jahl", "Umayyah ibn Khalaf", "Ṣuhayb al-Rūmī"]}, {"year": "c. 615 CE", "ah": "c. 8 BH", "title": "First emigration to Abyssinia", "chapter": "Ch. 7", "desc": "In the fifth year of the daʿwah the Prophet ﷺ counselled the persecuted to seek refuge with the just Christian king of Abyssinia. Twelve men and four women slipped out of Mecca, among them ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and his wife Ruqayyah, the Prophet's ﷺ daughter, along with Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib, ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf and Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr.", "people": ["ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "Ruqayyah bint Muhammad", "Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib", "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf", "Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr", "Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām", "Umm Salamah", "Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad"]}, {"year": "c. 615 CE", "ah": "c. 8 BH", "title": "The prostration at Sūrah al-Najm and the emigrants' brief return", "chapter": "Ch. 7", "desc": "When the Prophet ﷺ recited Sūrah al-Najm publicly and reached 'Prostrate to Allah and worship Him', the captivated Pagans fell into prostration alongside the Muslims — all except al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah. Rumours that the Quraysh had accepted Islam reached Abyssinia after three months and the emigrants hurried home, only to find the report false; the book firmly rejects the 'Satanic Verses' (Gharānīq) embellishments of this event as inauthentic.", "people": ["al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah", "ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn"]}, {"year": "c. 616 CE", "ah": "c. 7 BH", "title": "Second emigration to Abyssinia and Jaʿfar before the Negus", "chapter": "Ch. 7", "desc": "More than 80 Muslims — around half the community — emigrated a second time. The Quraysh sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ with gifts to lobby for their extradition, but the Negus insisted on hearing the refugees: Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib described Jāhiliyyah and Islam and recited from Sūrah Maryam, and the king wept and refused to surrender them, saying this and the message of Mūsā 'come from the same well'.", "people": ["Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib", "ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "the Negus"]}, {"year": "c. 616 CE", "ah": "c. 7 BH", "title": "Conversion of Ḥamzah", "chapter": "Ch. 8", "desc": "Enraged at Abū Jahl's public abuse of his nephew, Ḥamzah struck him with his hunting bow before the Quraysh and blurted out that he too followed the Prophet's ﷺ religion. After a sleepless night and a heartfelt duʿā' for guidance, he spent the morning with the Prophet ﷺ and testified sincerely — becoming, as son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, the most senior convert to Islam.", "people": ["Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Abū Jahl"]}, {"year": "c. 616 CE", "ah": "c. 7 BH", "title": "Conversion of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "chapter": "Ch. 8", "desc": "Days after Ḥamzah's conversion, ʿUmar set out sword in hand to kill the Prophet ﷺ, was diverted to his sister Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb's house, and was conquered by the verses of Sūrah Ṭā-Hā. He declared his Islam at Dār al-Arqam to the Prophet's ﷺ resounding takbīr, and the Muslims then marched to the Kaʿbah in two rows led by Ḥamzah and ʿUmar and prayed publicly for the first time — earning him the title al-Fārūq.", "people": ["ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb", "Saʿīd ibn Zayd", "Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt", "Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Naḥḥām", "Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib"]}, {"year": "c. 617–619 CE", "ah": "c. 6–4 BH", "title": "The boycott of the Banū Hāshim", "chapter": "Ch. 8", "desc": "Unable to obtain the Prophet ﷺ from Abū Ṭālib, the Quraysh imposed a total economic, social and marital boycott on the Banū Hāshim, formalised in a treaty hung inside the Kaʿbah. The clan endured two to three years of near-starvation in a valley outside Mecca, surviving on shrubbery and rainwater, while sympathisers like Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy and Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām secretly sent food.", "people": ["Abū Ṭālib", "Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy", "Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām", "Abū Lahab", "Abū Jahl"]}, {"year": "619 CE", "ah": "c. 4 BH", "title": "The boycott collapses — the termite miracle", "chapter": "Ch. 8", "desc": "Hishām ibn ʿAmr, Zuhayr ibn Abī Umaymah, Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy and other sympathisers staged a coordinated protest in the Quraysh council, and the Prophet ﷺ informed Abū Ṭālib that Allah had sent termites to devour the boycott treaty except the words 'In the Name of Allah'. Abū Ṭālib staked everything on this challenge; the scroll was found consumed exactly as foretold, and the boycott was lifted.", "people": ["Abū Ṭālib", "Hishām ibn ʿAmr", "Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy", "Zuhayr ibn Abī Umaymah", "Abū Jahl"]}]}, {"id": "search-for-base", "title": "The Search for a Base (619–622 CE)", "events": [{"year": "619 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "Death of Abū Ṭālib", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "Less than six weeks after the boycott ended, Abū Ṭālib — the Prophet's ﷺ lifelong shield — lay dying. Though he knew the truth in his heart, tribal prestige gripped him and he passed away without uttering the testimony of faith, to the Prophet's ﷺ immense grief.", "people": ["Abū Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "619 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "Death of Khadījah — the Year of Sorrow", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "Within a span of forty days of Abū Ṭālib's death, Khadījah also passed away, and the two calamities gave the whole year its name, the Year of Sorrow. Years later the Prophet ﷺ would defend her memory to ʿĀ'ishah: 'She supported me when no-one else supported me... and Allah gave me children through her.'", "people": ["Khadījah bint Khuwaylid", "Abū Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "619 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "Abū Lahab revokes the clan's protection", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "After Abū Ṭālib's death, Abū Lahab became chieftain of the Banū Hāshim and initially extended protection to the Prophet ﷺ, but the Quraysh goaded him — asking where his father ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was, and hearing 'in the Fire' — and he revoked it within a week, leaving the Prophet ﷺ exposed with only Allah's protection.", "people": ["Abū Lahab", "ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib"]}, {"year": "c. 619–620 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "The incident of Ṭā'if", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ walked two days to Ṭā'if with Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and presented Islam to the three ruling brothers of Thaqīf — ʿAbd Yālīl, Masʿūd and Ḥabīb — who rejected him in the vilest manner and finally had the rabble stone him out of the city. He ﷺ later told ʿĀ'ishah it was a day harder than Uḥud; yet, offered the destruction of Ṭā'if by the Angel of the Mountains, he chose mercy, and the Christian servant ʿAddās recognised him as a Prophet over a gift of grapes.", "people": ["Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "ʿAbd Yālīl", "ʿAddās", "ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah", "Shaybah ibn Rabīʿah"]}, {"year": "c. 620 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "The jinn of Nakhlah and the protection of Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "Returning from Ṭā'if, the Prophet ﷺ prayed tahajjud in the valley of Nakhlah, where a party of jinn listened to the Qur'an and returned to their people as warners — recorded in Sūrah al-Aḥqāf. Needing political cover to re-enter Mecca, he ﷺ was refused by al-Akhnas ibn Shurayq and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr before Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy sent his four armoured sons and publicly proclaimed his protection at the Kaʿbah.", "people": ["Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy", "Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "Abū Sufyān", "Suhayl ibn ʿAmr"]}, {"year": "c. 620 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "Marriage to Sawdah and betrothal to ʿĀ'ishah", "chapter": "Ch. 9", "desc": "After the loss of Khadījah, Khawlah bint Ḥakīm suggested new marriages for the grieving Prophet ﷺ. He married the widow Sawdah bint Zamʿah and was betrothed to ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr, who had earlier been engaged to Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdiyy's son before that arrangement was called off.", "people": ["Sawdah bint Zamʿah", "ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "Khawlah bint Ḥakīm", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq"]}, {"year": "c. 621 CE", "ah": "c. 1–2 BH", "title": "Al-Isrā' wa al-Miʿrāj — the Night Journey and Ascension", "chapter": "Ch. 10", "desc": "In the last 18 months of the Meccan era — after Khadījah's death, as the book demonstrates — the Prophet ﷺ was carried on al-Burāq from Mecca to Jerusalem, led all the Prophets in prayer, and ascended through the seven heavens, meeting Ādam, ʿĪsā, Yaḥyā, Yūsuf, Idrīs, Hārūn, Mūsā and Ibrāhīm. In Allah's direct presence the prayer was obligated at fifty times daily and, on Mūsā's counsel, reduced to five with the reward of fifty; Abū Bakr's unhesitating belief the next morning sealed his title al-Ṣiddīq.", "people": ["Jibrīl", "Mūsā", "Ibrāhīm", "ʿĪsā", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq"]}, {"year": "620 CE", "ah": "c. 3 BH", "title": "Six men of the Khazraj accept Islam", "chapter": "Ch. 11", "desc": "In the tenth year of the daʿwah, after fruitless approaches to the great tribes at Mina, the Prophet ﷺ sat with six young men of the Khazraj near ʿAqabah during Hajj and preached Islam to them. Softened by the Battle of Buʿāth and familiar with Prophethood from their Jewish neighbours, they accepted — the breakthrough from which the Anṣār would come.", "people": ["Asʿad ibn Zurārah"]}, {"year": "621 CE", "ah": "c. 2 BH", "title": "The First Covenant of ʿAqabah (Covenant of Women)", "chapter": "Ch. 11", "desc": "In the twelfth year of Prophethood, twelve men of the Aws and Khazraj formally converted and pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ at ʿAqabah — an apolitical, purely theological oath, hence its name. At their request he ﷺ sent Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr to teach in Yathrib, where within weeks forty converted, the first Friday prayer in Islam was held, and the conversions of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh and Usayd ibn Huḍayr brought the entire Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal into Islam.", "people": ["Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr", "ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit", "Asʿad ibn Zurārah", "Saʿd ibn Muʿādh", "Usayd ibn Huḍayr"]}, {"year": "622 CE", "ah": "c. 1 BH", "title": "The Second Covenant of ʿAqabah (Covenant of War)", "chapter": "Ch. 11", "desc": "In the thirteenth year of Prophethood, 73 men and two women — Asmā' bint ʿAmr and Nusaybah bint Kaʿb — met the Prophet ﷺ secretly before Fajr on the last night of Hajj, with his uncle al-ʿAbbās speaking first. They pledged to hear and obey, to spend, to command good, and to protect him as their own family; al-Barā' ibn Maʿrūr stretched forth his hand, Asʿad ibn Zurārah warned them of the swords to come, and the Prophet ﷺ promised them Paradise.", "people": ["al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "al-Barā' ibn Maʿrūr", "Asʿad ibn Zurārah", "Abū al-Haytham ibn Tayyahān", "Nusaybah bint Kaʿb", "Asmā' bint ʿAmr", "Kaʿb ibn Mālik"]}, {"year": "622 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "The Companions emigrate to Yathrib", "chapter": "Ch. 12", "desc": "Hijrah was commanded, and the Muslims began slipping away to Yathrib — Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad the first to leave — abandoning homes and wealth under Qurashī harassment. Soon only the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿAlī, and the weak and imprisoned remained, while Abū Bakr prepared two camels and waited months for permission to accompany the Prophet ﷺ.", "people": ["Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "622 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "The assassination plot and the night of the Hijrah", "chapter": "Ch. 12", "desc": "At Dār al-Nadwah the Quraysh resolved that one youth from every clan should strike the Prophet ﷺ simultaneously, but Allah informed him and gave permission to emigrate — news that made Abū Bakr weep for joy. ʿAlī lay in the Prophet's ﷺ bed as a decoy while he ﷺ slipped out in the night, pausing to address Mecca: 'You are the most blessed land on Earth... were it not that my own people expelled me, I would never have left you.'", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Abū Jahl", "ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr"]}, {"year": "622 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "Cave Thawr and the pursuit of Surāqah", "chapter": "Ch. 12", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr hid three days and nights in Cave Thawr, south of Mecca, served by ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr, Asmā', and the shepherd ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah; when the trackers stood overhead, he ﷺ said, 'What do you think of two men whose third is Allah?' On the road north, the bounty-hunter Surāqah ibn Mālik was thrown from his horse three times, sought written protection instead, and heard the stunning prophecy that he would one day wear the bracelets of Kisrā.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr", "Asmā' bint Abī Bakr", "ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Urayqiṭ", "Surāqah ibn Mālik"]}, {"year": "622 CE (Rabīʿ al-Awwal)", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "Arrival at Qubā' and entry into Medina", "chapter": "Ch. 12–13", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ reached the settlement of Qubā', where he founded the mosque 'built upon righteousness from the first day' and waited for ʿAlī and Abū Bakr's daughters to arrive. He then delivered the first Friday sermon en route into the city, let his camel choose the blessed spot, and lodged for six months with Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī as all of Yathrib — soon Medina — celebrated his coming.", "people": ["ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "Asmā' bint Abī Bakr"]}]}, {"id": "medina-1-5", "title": "Medina Years 1–5 AH (622–627 CE)", "events": [{"year": "622–623 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "Building the Prophet's ﷺ Mosque", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ purchased the land where his camel had knelt and built his mosque, labouring alongside the Companions. It became the heart of the new state — a place of prayer, learning, government and shelter for the poor of Ahl al-Ṣuffah.", "people": ["Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī", "ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir"]}, {"year": "622–623 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "The brotherhood between Muhājirūn and Anṣār", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ paired each Muhājir with an Anṣārī brother, forging bonds of faith stronger than blood. The Anṣār offered to split their homes, orchards and wealth, and the Muhājirūn graciously declined in favour of working — each side acting, as the book puts it, out of īmān and brotherhood.", "people": ["ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf", "Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ"]}, {"year": "622–623 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "The Treaty (Constitution) of Medina", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ drafted a five-page treaty declaring the believers 'one nation to the exclusion of the rest of mankind', naming every Jewish subtribe as 'one nation along with the believers' with mutual defence obligations, banning any protection for Qurashī pagans, and declaring Medina's interior a sanctified ḥaram. It established the operating philosophy of the first Islamic state.", "people": []}, {"year": "622–623 CE", "ah": "1 AH", "title": "The adhān is instituted", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "Rejecting bells and horns, the community adopted the call to prayer shown to ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd in a dream, which the Prophet ﷺ confirmed and taught to Bilāl for his voice. As Bilāl gave the adhān, ʿUmar rushed out saying he had seen the very same words in his own dream.", "people": ["ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "623–624 CE", "ah": "1–2 AH", "title": "Fasting and zakāh are legislated", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "Nine months after the Hijrah the fast of the 10th of Muḥarram (ʿĀshūrā') was obligated, then the following Ramadan was made the obligatory month of fasting and ʿĀshūrā' became a Sunnah. In that Ramadan Zakāh al-Fiṭr became obligatory, followed a few months later by Zakāh al-Māl — most pillars of Islam were in place within the state's first year.", "people": []}, {"year": "624 CE", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "The qiblah changes from Jerusalem to the Kaʿbah", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ had prayed towards Jerusalem while yearning for the Kaʿbah, turning his face to the sky in duʿā'. Allah then revealed 'Now We will turn you towards a qiblah you will be pleased with', gifting him the change of direction and distinguishing the Ummah from all who came before, despite the taunts of the Jews and Pagans.", "people": []}, {"year": "624 CE (12 Ṣafar)", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "The first expeditions — al-Abwā' and al-ʿUshayrah", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "Nine months after the Hijrah the first expedition, al-Abwā', set out after a Qurashī caravan; no fighting occurred, but the Prophet ﷺ allied with the Banū Ḍamrah and the Islamic state expanded for the first time, while Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ soon shot the first arrow for the sake of Allah. In Ghazwah al-ʿUshayrah the Muslims narrowly missed Abū Sufyān's great Syria-bound caravan — the same caravan whose return would trigger Badr.", "people": ["Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ", "Abū Sufyān"]}, {"year": "624 CE (Rajab)", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "Sariyyah al-Nakhlah and the sacred-month controversy", "chapter": "Ch. 13", "desc": "A reconnaissance party sent by the Prophet ﷺ attacked a small caravan at Nakhlah on the 30th of Rajab, killing ʿAmr al-Ḥaḍramī in the sacred month. The Prophet ﷺ was displeased and refused the booty until Allah revealed that while fighting in the sacred months is a great sin, 'fitnah is far worse than killing' — an accusation the Quraysh had broadcast across Arabia.", "people": ["ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh", "Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ", "ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān"]}, {"year": "624 CE (17 Ramadan)", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "The Battle of Badr", "chapter": "Ch. 14", "desc": "Expecting an easy caravan raid, 313 Muslims — 82 Muhājirūn, 61 Aws and 170 Khazraj — instead met a Qurashī army led by Abū Jahl; the Prophet ﷺ reached the wells of Badr on the 16th of Ramadan, took al-Ḥubāb's counsel on positioning, and pleaded with Allah through the night. Allah sent the angels, and the decisive victory killed the heads of disbelief: Abū Jahl was felled by the two youths Muʿādh and Muʿawwidh and finished by Ibn Masʿūd, while Umayyah ibn Khalaf was cut down by Bilāl's supporters.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "al-Ḥubāb ibn al-Mundhir", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd", "Abū Jahl", "Umayyah ibn Khalaf", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ", "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn ʿAwf"]}, {"year": "624 CE (Ramadan)", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "Death of Ruqayyah, the Prophet's ﷺ daughter", "chapter": "Ch. 14", "desc": "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān stayed back from Badr by the Prophet's ﷺ instruction to nurse his gravely ill wife Ruqayyah, and she passed away as the Muslims were at Badr. Joy at the victory was thus mingled with grief in the Prophet's ﷺ own household; ʿUthmān was still counted among the people of Badr and given his share.", "people": ["Ruqayyah bint Muhammad", "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān"]}, {"year": "624 CE", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "Expulsion of the Banū Qaynuqāʿ", "chapter": "Ch. 15", "desc": "The largest Jewish tribe of Medina, the Banū Qaynuqāʿ, were the first to break the Treaty of Medina, provoking the Muslims after Badr. The Prophet ﷺ marched against them and besieged them until they surrendered; interceded for by ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy, they were expelled from Medina rather than punished further.", "people": ["ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl", "ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit"]}, {"year": "624 CE", "ah": "2 AH", "title": "The Battle of Sawīq", "chapter": "Ch. 15", "desc": "Abū Sufyān, sworn to avenge Badr, raided the outskirts of Medina with Qurashī riders after being sheltered and briefed by allies among the Banū Naḍīr, killing two Muslims and burning crops. Pursued by the Prophet ﷺ, his men threw off their sacks of sawīq (barley provisions) to flee faster — giving the raid its name.", "people": ["Abū Sufyān"]}, {"year": "624 CE (Rabīʿ al-Awwal 3 AH)", "ah": "3 AH", "title": "Sariyyah al-Qaradah", "chapter": "Ch. 15", "desc": "With the coastal route to Syria blockaded, the Quraysh sent their yearly trade caravan through the Najd route in Rabīʿ al-Awwal 3 AH. The Prophet ﷺ dispatched Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, who captured the caravan at Qaradah without casualties — a tremendous economic blow to Mecca.", "people": ["Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "Abū Sufyān"]}, {"year": "624 CE (14 Rabīʿ al-Awwal 3 AH)", "ah": "3 AH", "title": "The killing of Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf", "chapter": "Ch. 15", "desc": "Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf, a chieftain-poet who incited the Quraysh against the Muslims and had already violated the Treaty of Medina by aiding Abū Sufyān in the Sawīq raid, was killed on the 14th of Rabīʿ al-Awwal by a party under Muhammad ibn Maslamah after the Prophet ﷺ asked, 'Who will take care of Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf?'", "people": ["Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf", "Muhammad ibn Maslamah"]}, {"year": "625 CE (Shaʿbān 3 AH)", "ah": "3 AH", "title": "Marriages to Ḥafṣah and Zaynab bint Khuzaymah", "chapter": "Ch. 15", "desc": "In Shaʿbān 3 AH the Prophet ﷺ married Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar, widowed daughter of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, after ʿUthmān and Abū Bakr had tactfully declined ʿUmar's offer knowing the Prophet's ﷺ intention. A month later he married Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, widow of a martyr — a message that the families of martyrs would be cared for — though she passed away only months into the marriage.", "people": ["Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "Zaynab bint Khuzaymah"]}, {"year": "625 CE (15 Shawwāl 3 AH)", "ah": "3 AH", "title": "The Battle of Uḥud", "chapter": "Ch. 16", "desc": "The Quraysh marched out on the 7th of Shawwāl 3 AH to avenge Badr; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy deserted with 300 men, leaving 700 Muslims to face 3,000. Battle was joined at sunrise on the 15th: the Muslims prevailed until 40 of the 50 archers abandoned the mountain pass for the spoils, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd's cavalry executed a devastating pincer in which the Prophet ﷺ himself was wounded and around 70 Muslims — including Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr — were martyred.", "people": ["ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl", "Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr", "Usayd ibn Huḍayr", "Abū Sufyān", "Nusaybah bint Kaʿb", "Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib"]}, {"year": "625 CE (15 Shawwāl 3 AH)", "ah": "3 AH", "title": "Martyrdom of Ḥamzah", "chapter": "Ch. 16", "desc": "Waḥshī, promised his freedom by his master and riches by Hind bint ʿUtbah — whose father Ḥamzah had killed at Badr — stalked the Lion of Allah through the battlefield of Uḥud and killed him with a thrown spear. The Prophet ﷺ grieved for his uncle more than for any other loss at Uḥud; Waḥshī later accepted Islam and atoned by killing Musaylamah the Liar.", "people": ["Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Waḥshī", "Hind bint ʿUtbah", "Abū Sufyān"]}, {"year": "625 CE", "ah": "4 AH", "title": "The massacre of Bi'r Maʿūnah", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ sent 70 learned Companions, most of them from Ahl al-Ṣuffah, to teach Qur'an at the invitation of a chieftain. Betrayed, they were surrounded at the well of Maʿūnah by 400–500 tribesmen of ʿUṣayyah, Riʿl and Dhakwān and slaughtered; the Prophet ﷺ was so aggrieved that he made qunūt against the treacherous tribes for a month.", "people": ["ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah"]}, {"year": "625 CE", "ah": "4 AH", "title": "The massacre of al-Rajīʿ and the execution of Khubayb", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "On the very day news of Bi'r Maʿūnah arrived, the Prophet ﷺ learned that ten Companions sent under ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit had been ambushed by a hundred men at the well of al-Rajīʿ. Khubayb, sold to Mecca and sentenced to death, asked only to pray two rakʿahs — saying he would have prolonged them but for being thought a coward — and became an emblem of steadfastness at the point of execution.", "people": ["ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit", "Khubayb ibn ʿAdiyy", "Zayd ibn al-Dathinnah"]}, {"year": "625 CE", "ah": "4 AH", "title": "Expulsion of the Banū Naḍīr", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "When the Banū Naḍīr plotted to drop a boulder on the Prophet ﷺ as he sat by their wall, revelation exposed the plan and he ordered them to leave Medina. Emboldened by ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy's empty promises and Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab's defiance, they locked themselves in their fortresses, but surrendered after a siege of seven to ten days and were expelled, many — including Ḥuyayy — relocating to Khaybar.", "people": ["Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq"]}, {"year": "626 CE (Jumādā al-Ākhirah 4 AH)", "ah": "4 AH", "title": "Death of Abū Salamah and marriage to Umm Salamah", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "Abū Salamah, wounded at Uḥud, died of his injuries six months later in Jumādā al-Ākhirah 4 AH, and Umm Salamah recited the duʿā' he had taught her: 'O Allah, compensate me with better.' The Prophet ﷺ himself later proposed, and Umm Salamah — wise and learned — became a Mother of the Believers.", "people": ["Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad", "Umm Salamah"]}, {"year": "c. 626 CE", "ah": "4–5 AH", "title": "The Expedition of Dhāt al-Riqāʿ", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ led 700 Companions against the raiding Ghaṭafān, who declined battle. On the return, the Bedouin Ghawrath seized the Prophet's ﷺ sword as he slept and demanded, 'Who will protect you from me?' — 'Allah,' he ﷺ replied, and the trembling man dropped the sword and was forgiven, returning to his people saying, 'I have come from the best of mankind.'", "people": ["Ghawrath", "Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh"]}, {"year": "c. 626–627 CE (Shaʿbān)", "ah": "5 AH (per the book)", "title": "Birth of Ḥasan and Ḥusayn", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "Amidst a sea of trials the Prophet's ﷺ first grandson Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was born — in Shaʿbān 5 AH according to the book — and his brother Ḥusayn followed ten months later. The Prophet ﷺ renamed the newborn from 'Ḥarb' (War) to Ḥasan (Beauty) and declared, 'Ḥasan and Ḥusayn are the leaders of the youth in Paradise.'", "people": ["Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī", "Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Fāṭimah bint Muhammad"]}, {"year": "c. 627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "Marriage to Zaynab bint Jaḥsh", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, the Prophet's ﷺ cousin, had been married at his insistence to his freedman Zayd ibn Ḥārithah; when the marriage failed and Zayd divorced her, Allah Himself commanded the Prophet ﷺ to marry her — one of two marriages ordained directly by revelation — abolishing thereby the Jāhilī taboo around adopted sons' former wives.", "people": ["Zaynab bint Jaḥsh", "Zayd ibn Ḥārithah"]}, {"year": "c. 627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "The Expedition of al-Muraysīʿ and marriage to Juwayriyyah", "chapter": "Ch. 17", "desc": "Learning that the Banū al-Muṣṭaliq were mustering at the pond of al-Muraysīʿ, the Prophet ﷺ verified the report through Buraydah ibn al-Ḥaṣīb, struck first after Fajr, and won swiftly. He then married Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith, the chieftain's captured daughter, whereupon the Anṣār freed a hundred families of her tribe as the Prophet's ﷺ in-laws — no woman, it was said, brought more blessing to her people.", "people": ["Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith", "Buraydah ibn al-Ḥaṣīb", "al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār"]}, {"year": "c. 627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "The Ifk — the slander of ʿĀ'ishah", "chapter": "Ch. 18", "desc": "Left behind at a camp on the return from al-Muraysīʿ while searching for her necklace, ʿĀ'ishah was brought back by Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy seized on it to fabricate a vile slander that convulsed Medina for a month while ʿĀ'ishah lay sick and unaware. Allah then revealed her innocence in Sūrah al-Nūr; Abū Bakr resumed his stipend to the repentant Misṭaḥ, and the affair became a permanent lesson on gossip and honour.", "people": ["ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl", "Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "Zaynab bint Jaḥsh", "Usāmah ibn Zayd", "Barīrah"]}, {"year": "627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "The Battle of the Trench (Khandaq)", "chapter": "Ch. 19", "desc": "Instigated by the exiled Banū Naḍīr, some 10,000 Confederates marched on Medina; on Salmān al-Fārisī's suggestion the Muslims dug a trench in under ten days, the Prophet ﷺ splitting the boulder none could break with three strikes and three glad tidings. The Banū Qurayẓah treacherously joined the enemy, ʿAlī slew the champion ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Wudd, the new convert Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd sowed distrust between the allies, and Allah sent a wind that broke the siege.", "people": ["Salmān al-Fārisī", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Wudd", "Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd", "Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab", "Saʿd ibn Muʿādh", "Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah", "Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām"]}, {"year": "627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "The judgement on the Banū Qurayẓah", "chapter": "Ch. 19", "desc": "Immediately after the Confederates fled, the Muslims besieged the Banū Qurayẓah for their treachery during the Trench. They agreed to accept the arbitration of their old ally Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, who — wounded and carried in — judged them by their own law: the fighting men were executed, and the Prophet ﷺ declared that Saʿd had judged with the judgement of Allah.", "people": ["Saʿd ibn Muʿādh", "Kaʿb ibn Asad", "Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab"]}, {"year": "627 CE", "ah": "5 AH", "title": "Death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh", "chapter": "Ch. 19", "desc": "Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, struck in the arm's artery during the Trench, had prayed that Allah not take his soul until he was pleased regarding the Banū Qurayẓah; his wound reopened after the judgement and he died a martyr's death. The Prophet ﷺ said the Throne of the Most Merciful shook at his passing — the book notes there can be no greater praise for a man.", "people": ["Saʿd ibn Muʿādh"]}]}, {"id": "medina-6-8", "title": "Medina Years 6–8 AH (627–630 CE)", "events": [{"year": "628 CE (Dhū al-Qaʿdah 6 AH)", "ah": "6 AH", "title": "The march to Ḥudaybiyyah", "chapter": "Ch. 20", "desc": "After the Prophet ﷺ dreamt of performing ṭawāf in iḥrām, 1,400 Companions left Medina on the 1st of Dhū al-Qaʿdah 6 AH intending only ʿUmrah. His camel al-Qaṣwā' knelt and refused to advance at the plain of Ḥudaybiyyah — 'restrained by He Who restrained the elephant' — and the near-empty well there overflowed miraculously for the entire camp.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "628 CE (Dhū al-Qaʿdah 6 AH)", "ah": "6 AH", "title": "ʿUthmān's embassy and the Pledge of Riḍwān", "chapter": "Ch. 20", "desc": "ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān was sent to negotiate in Mecca because of his clan's protection, and when rumour spread that he had been killed, the Prophet ﷺ took the pledge of the Companions under the tree — Bayʿat al-Riḍwān — clasping his own left hand and saying, 'This is for ʿUthmān.' Allah revealed that He was pleased with the believers who pledged, and the shaken Quraysh returned ʿUthmān and sued for terms.", "people": ["ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "628 CE (Dhū al-Qaʿdah 6 AH)", "ah": "6 AH", "title": "The Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah", "chapter": "Ch. 20", "desc": "Suhayl ibn ʿAmr dictated humiliating terms — the basmalah and the title 'Messenger of Allah' struck from the document ʿAlī was writing, ʿUmrah deferred a year, and Muslim refugees from Mecca to be returned, a clause tested at once by the shackled Abū Jandal. The Companions were devastated, but Sūrah al-Fatḥ was revealed calling it 'a clear triumph', and the ensuing peace became the first opening through which Islam spread unimpeded.", "people": ["Suhayl ibn ʿAmr", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "Abū Jandal", "Umm Salamah", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq"]}, {"year": "628 CE", "ah": "7 AH", "title": "The Battle of Khaybar", "chapter": "Ch. 21", "desc": "Marching secretly and arriving after Fajr, the Prophet ﷺ caught the fortified oasis of Khaybar — the new base of the exiled Banū Naḍīr — completely off-guard, proclaiming 'Allāhu Akbar! Khaybar has been destroyed!' He gave the banner to ʿAlī, cured of his eye ailment by the Prophet's ﷺ saliva, who slew the champion Mirḥab in a duel; fortress after fortress fell, and the survivors were allowed to remain as farmers for half the produce.", "people": ["ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "Mirḥab", "ʿĀmir ibn al-Akwaʿ", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "628 CE", "ah": "7 AH", "title": "Return of Jaʿfar and the Abyssinian emigrants", "chapter": "Ch. 21", "desc": "As the spoils of Khaybar were distributed, Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib arrived with the Muslims who had spent some fourteen years in Abyssinia. The Prophet ﷺ embraced him, saying he did not know which pleased him more — the conquest of Khaybar or the return of Jaʿfar — and granted the returnees a share of the spoils.", "people": ["Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "628 CE", "ah": "7 AH", "title": "Marriage to Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy", "chapter": "Ch. 21", "desc": "Ṣafiyyah, daughter of the Naḍīrite chieftain Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, was captured at Khaybar; the Prophet ﷺ offered her the choice of freedom and return to her people or Islam and marriage to him. She chose Allah and His Messenger ﷺ — the seed of īmān having been planted when, as a child, she overheard her father recognise and then reject the true Prophet.", "people": ["Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy", "Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab"]}, {"year": "628 CE", "ah": "6–7 AH", "title": "Letters to the kings and emperors", "chapter": "Ch. 22", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ sent more than twenty letters inviting rulers to Islam: the Negus of Abyssinia accepted; Heraclius interrogated Abū Sufyān and privately recognised the signs of Prophethood; Khosrow of Persia tore the letter, and the Prophet ﷺ said, 'Allah will thoroughly tear up his kingdom'; and the Muqawqis of Egypt replied politely with gifts, including Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah.", "people": ["the Negus", "Heraclius", "Abū Sufyān", "Khosrow", "the Muqawqis", "Diḥyah al-Kalbī", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥudhāfah", "Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah"]}, {"year": "629 CE (Dhū al-Qaʿdah 7 AH)", "ah": "7 AH", "title": "ʿUmrah al-Qaḍā' and marriage to Maymūnah", "chapter": "Ch. 23", "desc": "As stipulated at Ḥudaybiyyah, the Prophet ﷺ and the Companions performed the make-up ʿUmrah in Dhū al-Qaʿdah 7 AH while the Quraysh vacated the city for three days and watched from the hills. During this journey al-ʿAbbās arranged the Prophet's ﷺ marriage to his sister-in-law, the widow Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith — the last of his marriages.", "people": ["Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith", "al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ"]}, {"year": "629 CE", "ah": "early 8 AH", "title": "Conversions of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah", "chapter": "Ch. 23", "desc": "Between ʿUmrah al-Qaḍā' and the Conquest, three pillars of Qurashī society travelled together to Medina and embraced Islam: Khālid ibn al-Walīd the master of war, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ the shrewd statesman, and ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah the keeper of the Kaʿbah's keys. The Prophet ﷺ had already remarked of Khālid that a man of his intelligence could not long remain ignorant of Islam.", "people": ["Khālid ibn al-Walīd", "ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah"]}, {"year": "629 CE (Jumādā al-Ūlā 8 AH)", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The Battle of Mu'tah", "chapter": "Ch. 24", "desc": "In Jumādā al-Ūlā 8 AH the Prophet ﷺ sent 3,000 Companions against the Ghassanids and their Roman backers, appointing Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, then Jaʿfar, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah should each fall — and each was martyred in turn exactly so, Jaʿfar losing both arms and gaining, the Prophet ﷺ said, two wings in Paradise. Khālid ibn al-Walīd, Muslim less than a month, took the flag, broke nine swords, and extracted the vastly outnumbered army — earning the title 'a sword among the swords of Allah'.", "people": ["Zayd ibn Ḥārithah", "Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib", "ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah", "Khālid ibn al-Walīd", "Usāmah ibn Zayd"]}, {"year": "629 CE", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The Expedition of Dhāt al-Salāsil", "chapter": "Ch. 24", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ — the newest of converts, honoured with command — against the Banū Quḍāʿah who had aided the Ghassanids. The campaign succeeded, and ʿAmr's famous question afterwards, 'Who is most beloved to you?', drew the answer 'ʿĀ'ishah', and among men, 'her father'.", "people": ["ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr"]}, {"year": "629–630 CE", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The treaty is violated — the raid on the Banū Khuzāʿah", "chapter": "Ch. 25", "desc": "The Banū Bakr, armed covertly by Qurashī leaders, massacred over twenty men of the Prophet's ﷺ allies the Banū Khuzāʿah, even killing within the Ḥaram. The Khuzāʿī chieftain rode to Medina for justice; Abū Sufyān's damage-control mission to Medina failed utterly — even his daughter Umm Ḥabībah, the Prophet's ﷺ wife, folded away the Prophet's ﷺ blanket rather than let 'an impure polytheist' sit on it.", "people": ["Abū Sufyān", "Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān", "Budayl ibn Warqā'"]}, {"year": "630 CE", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The Conquest of Mecca", "chapter": "Ch. 25", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ marched with over 10,000 Companions in secrecy; at Marr al-Ẓahrān, al-ʿAbbās brought Abū Sufyān to accept Islam, and his house was declared a sanctuary. The Prophet ﷺ entered his hometown with his forehead nearly touching his camel in humility, reciting Sūrah al-Fatḥ, toppled the 360 idols around the Kaʿbah, and granted a general amnesty to the people who had persecuted him for twenty years: the climax of the sīrah, a conquest of hearts and minds.", "people": ["Abū Sufyān", "al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Khālid ibn al-Walīd", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ", "ʿUthmān ibn Ṭalḥah", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "Abū Quḥāfah"]}, {"year": "630 CE (10 Shawwāl 8 AH)", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The Battle of Ḥunayn", "chapter": "Ch. 26", "desc": "The Hawāzin and Thaqīf massed against the Muslims, and the Prophet ﷺ left Mecca on the 6th of Shawwāl with 12,000 men — some murmuring that such a host could never be defeated. Ambushed in the valley on the 10th, the army initially broke and fled, but the Prophet ﷺ stood firm on his mule, al-ʿAbbās called out to the people of the pledge, and the rallied Muslims won the richest spoils in the entire sīrah.", "people": ["al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Mālik ibn ʿAwf", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "Khālid ibn al-Walīd"]}, {"year": "630 CE (Shawwāl 8 AH)", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The Siege of Ṭā'if", "chapter": "Ch. 26", "desc": "The fleeing Thaqīf shut themselves in the stocked fortresses of Ṭā'if, and after almost two weeks of an unsuccessful siege the Muslims withdrew. Asked to curse the Thaqīf who had once stoned him, the Prophet ﷺ instead raised his hands: 'O Allah, guide Thaqīf! O Allah, bring the people of Thaqīf [to us]!' — a prayer answered the following year.", "people": ["Mālik ibn ʿAwf"]}, {"year": "630 CE", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "The spoils at Jiʿrānah and the winning of hearts", "chapter": "Ch. 26", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ waited nearly a month at Jiʿrānah for the Hawāzin, then distributed the enormous spoils, giving lavishly to the newly freed Meccans to reconcile their hearts. When some young Anṣār murmured, he ﷺ gathered them and asked whether they were not content to return home with the Messenger of Allah while others returned with sheep and camels — and they wept and were content.", "people": ["Mālik ibn ʿAwf", "Abū Sufyān"]}, {"year": "630 CE (Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH)", "ah": "8 AH", "title": "Birth of Ibrāhīm", "chapter": "Ch. 28", "desc": "In Dhū al-Ḥijjah 8 AH, Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah — the Coptic servant gifted by the Muqawqis of Egypt — bore the Prophet ﷺ a son. He ﷺ entered the mosque beaming: 'Last night a son was born to me, and I have named him after my father, Ibrāhīm.'", "people": ["Ibrāhīm ibn Muhammad", "Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah"]}]}, {"id": "medina-9-11", "title": "Medina Years 9–11 AH (630–632 CE)", "events": [{"year": "630 CE (Rajab 9 AH)", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "The Expedition of Tabūk — the Army of Hardship", "chapter": "Ch. 27", "desc": "In Rajab 9 AH the Prophet ﷺ mustered the largest army of the sīrah — sources claim over 30,000 — for a pre-emptive march against Roman forces, in blazing summer at harvest time, making attendance a personal obligation. ʿUthmān equipped the army with his entire returning trade caravan, the thirsty army was relieved by miraculous rain, and the Romans never appeared; the bloodless expedition sealed Muslim supremacy in Arabia.", "people": ["ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "630–631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "The three who stayed behind", "chapter": "Ch. 27", "desc": "Kaʿb ibn Mālik and two other sincere Companions failed to join Tabūk with no excuse, and rather than lie, Kaʿb confessed. The Prophet ﷺ ordered a total boycott of the three for fifty days — until Kaʿb, refusing even the king of Ghassān's tempting letter, heard a cry from Mount Selaʿ that Allah had revealed their forgiveness in the Qur'an.", "people": ["Kaʿb ibn Mālik", "Hilāl ibn Umayyah", "Murārah ibn al-Rabīʿ"]}, {"year": "631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "Ṭā'if accepts Islam", "chapter": "Ch. 28–29", "desc": "In 9 AH — the Prophet's ﷺ duʿā' for Thaqīf answered — the people of Ṭā'if, isolated after Tabūk subdued the region, sent their delegation and accepted Islam. The city that had stoned him ﷺ a decade earlier entered his religion, and idolatry's last urban bastion in the Hijaz fell without a sword.", "people": []}, {"year": "631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "The Year of Delegations", "chapter": "Ch. 28", "desc": "Though delegations had trickled in since the ʿAbd al-Qays came first in 5 AH, the year 9 AH became known as the Year of Delegations as tribe after tribe arrived in Medina every few weeks to accept Islam or seek treaties — among them the Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr, whose blunt envoy Ḍimām, and emigrant-delegates like Ṭufayl's people. Arabia was uniting under the Prophet ﷺ.", "people": ["Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr al-Dawsī"]}, {"year": "631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "Musaylamah claims prophethood", "chapter": "Ch. 28", "desc": "Musaylamah ibn al-Ḥabīb of the Banū Ḥanīfah came with his tribe's delegation, then wrote to the Prophet ﷺ as 'Musaylamah, the Messenger of Allah', proposing to split the earth between them. The Prophet ﷺ replied to 'Musaylamah the Liar' that the earth belongs to Allah, and told his envoys that were emissaries not immune, he would have executed them; Waḥshī later killed Musaylamah in the Riddah wars, atoning for Ḥamzah.", "people": ["Musaylamah the Liar", "Waḥshī"]}, {"year": "631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "The Najrān delegation and the mubāhalah", "chapter": "Ch. 28", "desc": "Sixty Christians of Najrān came to Medina and debated the nature of ʿĪsā with the Prophet ﷺ, who received them in his mosque; Allah revealed that 'the example of ʿĪsā with Allah is like that of Ādam' and that Ibrāhīm was neither Jew nor Christian. Challenged to the mutual imprecation (mubāhalah), they declined, chose to keep their faith, and accepted a treaty under Islamic protection.", "people": []}, {"year": "631 CE", "ah": "9 AH", "title": "The Hajj of Abū Bakr and the proclamation of Barā'ah", "chapter": "Ch. 29", "desc": "Mecca was liberated in 8 AH, but the Prophet ﷺ delayed his own pilgrimage while pagan practices persisted at the Ḥaram. In 9 AH he sent Abū Bakr to lead the Hajj, and ʿAlī was dispatched to proclaim Sūrah Barā'ah — the discharge from all obligations to the treaty-breaking polytheists — so that by 10 AH the Ḥaram was finally free of non-Islamic practices.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "631–632 CE", "ah": "10 AH", "title": "Death of Ibrāhīm and the eclipse", "chapter": "Ch. 28", "desc": "Ibrāhīm died before reaching a year and a half, and the Prophet ﷺ wept, saying, 'The eyes tear and the heart grieves, but we only say what pleases our Lord — O Ibrāhīm, we are grieved by your departure.' When a solar eclipse occurred within hours and people called it a cosmic mourning, he ﷺ corrected them: the sun and moon are signs of Allah that do not eclipse for anyone's death or life.", "people": ["Ibrāhīm ibn Muhammad", "Māriyyah al-Qibṭiyyah", "Anas ibn Mālik"]}, {"year": "632 CE (25 Dhū al-Qaʿdah 10 AH)", "ah": "10 AH", "title": "Departure for the Farewell Hajj", "chapter": "Ch. 29", "desc": "The Prophet ﷺ announced his intention to perform Hajj — the first and only Hajj of his life ﷺ — and left Medina on the 25th of Dhū al-Qaʿdah 10 AH with tens of thousands more joining along the way, up to 100,000 by some estimates. The Companions only later understood that this was his farewell to his Ummah.", "people": ["ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "Ibn ʿUmar"]}, {"year": "632 CE (9 Dhū al-Ḥijjah 10 AH)", "ah": "10 AH", "title": "The Farewell Sermon at ʿArafah", "chapter": "Ch. 29", "desc": "Before the greatest audience of his life, the Prophet ﷺ declared blood and wealth sacred, abolished the blood-vengeance and usury of Jāhiliyyah beginning with his own family — the usury of al-ʿAbbās — and commanded that women be treated with kindness and that the Ummah hold fast to the Book of Allah. On that day Allah revealed: 'Today I have perfected your faith for you, completed My favour upon you.'", "people": ["al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ"]}, {"year": "632 CE (18 Dhū al-Ḥijjah 10 AH)", "ah": "10 AH", "title": "Ghadīr Khumm", "chapter": "Ch. 29", "desc": "On the return from Hajj, after complaints had circulated against ʿAlī over his conduct with the spoils of Yemen, the Prophet ﷺ gathered the people at the well of Khumm and passionately defended him: 'If I am a person's protector, then ʿAlī is their protector,' praying for those who supported him.", "people": ["ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib"]}, {"year": "632 CE (Ṣafar 11 AH)", "ah": "11 AH (the book's text reads 'Ṣafar 10 AH')", "title": "The army of Usāmah ibn Zayd", "chapter": "Ch. 30", "desc": "Two months after the Farewell Hajj the Prophet ﷺ mustered an army against the Byzantines and, passing over Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, appointed 18-year-old Usāmah ibn Zayd — sending him towards the region where his father was martyred at Mu'tah. As the army left Medina, news came that the Prophet ﷺ had fallen ill, and Usāmah camped outside the city awaiting word.", "people": ["Usāmah ibn Zayd", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "632 CE (Ṣafar–Rabīʿ al-Awwal 11 AH)", "ah": "11 AH", "title": "The final illness — Abū Bakr leads the prayer", "chapter": "Ch. 30", "desc": "With his wives' permission the ailing Prophet ﷺ moved to ʿĀ'ishah's room, and when he could no longer stand he commanded, over ʿĀ'ishah's tearful objections, 'Go command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer' — repeated three times with emphasis. In his last days he ordered his final seven coins given to the poor, and gazed contentedly at his Ummah praying behind Abū Bakr.", "people": ["ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "Usāmah ibn Zayd"]}, {"year": "632 CE (Rabīʿ al-Awwal 11 AH)", "ah": "11 AH", "title": "The passing of the Prophet ﷺ — the Greatest Calamity", "chapter": "Ch. 30", "desc": "Cradled on ʿĀ'ishah's lap, the Prophet ﷺ softened a miswāk with her help for his final cleaning, then whispered, 'O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, and allow me to be with al-Rafīq al-Aʿlā' — the Loftiest Company — and his soul departed. ʿĀ'ishah counted it among Allah's greatest blessings to her that he ﷺ died in her house, on her day, between her chest and neck.", "people": ["ʿĀ'ishah bint Abī Bakr", "ʿAbdurraḥmān ibn Abī Bakr", "Fāṭimah bint Muhammad"]}, {"year": "632 CE (Rabīʿ al-Awwal 11 AH)", "ah": "11 AH", "title": "Abū Bakr steadies the Ummah", "chapter": "Ch. 30", "desc": "As ʿUmar, in shock, threatened those who said the Prophet ﷺ had died, Abū Bakr kissed the Prophet's ﷺ forehead and addressed the people: 'Whoever used to worship Muhammad, then Muhammad has died; and whoever used to worship Allah, then Allah is alive and never dies,' reciting 'Muhammad is no more than a Messenger.' ʿUmar collapsed to his knees, and it was as if the people had never heard the verse before that day.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb"]}, {"year": "632 CE (Rabīʿ al-Awwal 11 AH)", "ah": "11 AH", "title": "The appointment of Abū Bakr as caliph", "chapter": "Ch. 30", "desc": "Before the burial, the Anṣār gathered to discuss leadership, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar went with a group of the Muhājirūn to join them while ʿAlī, al-ʿAbbās and Zubayr prepared the Prophet's ﷺ body. Abū Bakr's wise and gentle speech — anticipated by the Prophet's ﷺ own command that he lead the prayer — won the community's pledge, and the era of the caliphs began.", "people": ["Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq", "ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb", "ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib", "al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib", "Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām"]}]}]}};
